var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E13_Solvers/#Tutorial-13:-Linear-solvers-for-large-problems","page":"13 · Linear solvers for large problems","text":"Source · Notebook · nbviewer\n\nEvery Newton iteration of a nonlinear simulation solves a linear system with the tangent matrix. The sparse LU factorisation used in the other tutorials is robust and exact, but its cost and memory grow much faster than the number of unknowns: beyond some tens of thousands of degrees of freedom in 3D it is most of the run. This tutorial solves the same hyperelastic problem with the linear solvers that HyperFEM offers for large problems and checks that they all reach the same solution, then does the same for a coupled electro-mechanical problem.\n\nWhat you will learn\n\nplugging a linear solver into NewtonRaphson: LUSolver, the pure-Julia AMGSolver (Krylov method preconditioned by algebraic multigrid) and, with PETSc, PETScSolver (CG + GAMG, CG + hypre's BoomerAMG, MUMPS);\nwhy the rigid-body modes of the displacement space (near_nullspace) matter to algebraic multigrid;\nthe inexact Newton method (forcing = EisenstatWalker()) and lagged preconditioners (reuse);\nblock (field-split) preconditioners for multi-field problems: AMGSolver(U; split), PETScSolver(U; reuse);\nreading the solver counters: num_iterations, linear_history, preconditioner_builds.\n\nMeasured on a Mac M5 Max (Julia 1.13.0, 4 Julia threads, 4 BLAS threads, PETSc serial; HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6, Gridap 0.20.8): FAST (bar with 1 425 dofs, electro-mechanical box with 549 dofs) 56 s with compilation; full run (bar with 68 493 dofs, box with 23 895 dofs) 178 s. Setup + linear solve per Newton iteration on the 68 493-dof bar: LU 2.2 s, CG + AMG with rigid modes 1.05 s (0.37 s with reuse = 3 and the inexact Newton), CG + GAMG 0.51 s, CG + BoomerAMG (nodal coarsening 4, rotations) 0.78 s, MUMPS 1.05 s; one tangent assembly 0.015 s compiled, 0.44 s with Gridap.","title":"Tutorial 13: Linear solvers for large problems"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E13_Solvers/#Problem-statement","page":"13 · Linear solvers for large problems","text":"The neo-Hookean bar Omega = (01)times(002)times(002) (lambda = 10, mu = 1; the energy of tutorial 02) is clamped at x = 0; the face x = 1 is moved by u_z = 005Lambda (bending), with the load factor Lambda going from 0 to 1 in two increments. Trilinear hexahedra (Q1), 5n times n times n cells. The Newton iteration solves, at every iteration k,\n\nboldsymbol K(boldsymbol x_k) Deltaboldsymbol x = -boldsymbol R(boldsymbol x_k)\nqquad boldsymbol x_k+1 = boldsymbol x_k + Deltaboldsymbol x \n\nwhere boldsymbol K is the (symmetric positive definite, along this path) tangent stiffness. A Krylov method (conjugate gradients, CG, for symmetric positive definite systems; GMRES otherwise) only needs products boldsymbol K boldsymbol v and a preconditioner, an approximate inverse of boldsymbol K that is cheap to apply. Algebraic multigrid (AMG) builds that approximation from the matrix alone: a hierarchy of coarser problems that removes the smooth error components a simple smoother cannot.","title":"Problem statement"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E13_Solvers/#Setup","page":"13 · Linear solvers for large problems","text":"using HyperFEM\nusing Gridap\nusing LinearAlgebra, Printf\nusing HyperFEMKernels                          # compiled, threaded assembly (`backend = :compiled`)\nimport AlgebraicMultigrid                      # activates `AMGSolver` (an extension of HyperFEM)\n\nconst FAST = get(ENV, \"HYPERFEM_TUTORIALS_FAST\", \"0\") == \"1\"    # reduced case for the test suite\nconst PETSC = get(ENV, \"HYPERFEM_TUTORIALS_PETSC\", \"1\") == \"1\"  # parts that need PETSc (GridapPETSc)\nif PETSC\n  using GridapPETSc                            # activates `PETScSolver` (PETSc is initialised on first use)\nend\noutdir = joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"data\", \"sims\", \"E13_Solvers\")\nsetupfolder(outdir)                                             # VTK output (not versioned)","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E13_Solvers/#The-mechanical-problem","page":"13 · Linear solvers for large problems","text":"The problem is built by a function of the mesh size n, so that the same code gives a small problem to compile every solver once (the times reported below are then those of the solve, not of Julia's compilation) and the problem of the tutorial. Everything it needs is created inside it.\n\nfunction bar_problem(n)\n  model = CartesianDiscreteModel((0, 1, 0, 0.2, 0, 0.2), (5n, n, n))\n  labels = get_face_labeling(model)\n  add_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"fixed\", CartesianTags.face0YZ⁺)\n  add_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"moved\", CartesianTags.face1YZ⁺)\n  physmodel = NeoHookean3D(λ=10.0, μ=1.0)\n  kinematics = Kinematics(Mechano, Solid)\n  Ω = Triangulation(model)\n  dΩ = Measure(Ω, 2)\n  D = BoundaryConditions(BoundaryCondition(\"fixed\", [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]),\n                         BoundaryCondition(\"moved\", [0.0, 0.0, 0.05], EvolutionFunctions.ramp()))\n  V = TestFESpace(Ω, ReferenceFE(lagrangian, VectorValue{3,Float64}, 1), D, conformity=:H1)\n  U = TrialFESpace(V, D, 0.0)\n  res(Λ) = (u, v) -> residual(physmodel, kinematics, u, v, dΩ)\n  jac(Λ) = (u, du, v) -> jacobian(physmodel, kinematics, u, du, v, dΩ)\n  (; res, jac, U, V, D, Ω)\nend","title":"The mechanical problem"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E13_Solvers/#Running-one-solver","page":"13 · Linear solvers for large problems","text":"solve_with runs the whole simulation (two load increments, no bisection, the Dirichlet projection of tutorial 02) with the linear solver ls and returns the solution and the counters of the run:\n\nnls.log.niters: Newton iterations over the run (num_iterations(nls) and linear_history(nls) give the iterations of the last increment, the latter per Newton iteration);\nnls.log.nlinear: Krylov iterations over the run (0 for a direct solver);\npreconditioner_builds(ns): how many times the preconditioner (the AMG hierarchy, the factorisation) was built. The numerical setup ns of the linear solver lives in the Newton cache of the model. Gridap's LUSolver does not count its factorisations (0 in the tables); MUMPS does (one per tangent);\nnls.log.t_assembly, t_setup, t_linear: the time of the run spent assembling tangents, setting up the linear solver (factorisation, AMG hierarchy) and solving the linear systems.\n\nThe Newton tolerance is tight (10^-10 relative) so that all the solvers stop at the same accuracy. backend = :compiled assembles residual and tangent with the compiled, threaded kernels of HyperFEMKernels instead of Gridap's generic (lazy) evaluation: with an iterative solver the assembly would otherwise be a large part of every Newton iteration.\n\nfunction solve_with(ls, p; forcing=nothing)\n  nls = NewtonRaphson(ls; maxiter=20, atol=1e-14, rtol=1e-10, forcing)\n  m = StaticNonlinearModel(p.res, p.jac, p.U, p.V, p.D; nls, backend=:compiled)\n  t = @elapsed ((x, flag) = solve!(m; stepping=LoadStepping(nsteps=2, maxbisec=0), ProjectDirichlet=true))\n  ns = m.caches.nls_cache.ns\n  log = nls.log\n  (; x=copy(x), ok=(flag == 1), newton=log.niters, krylov=log.nlinear, last=collect(linear_history(nls)),\n     builds=preconditioner_builds(ns), t, t_asm=log.t_assembly, t_setup=log.t_setup, t_lin=log.t_linear, model=m)\nend\n\nThe solvers compared, each as a function of the problem (some need its trial space). Krylov tolerances are relative to the right-hand side.\n\nLUSolver(): Gridap's sparse LU (UMFPACK), the reference.\nAMGSolver(:cg): CG preconditioned by one V-cycle of smoothed-aggregation AMG (AlgebraicMultigrid.jl).\nAMGSolver(:cg; nullspace = near_nullspace(U)): the same, told the six rigid-body modes (three translations, three rotations) of the displacement field. They are the near-kernel of an elasticity operator: the error components the coarse levels must represent. Without them AlgebraicMultigrid uses its default near-null space, a single vector of ones over all the dofs (one translation along (111)), and the iterations grow.\nforcing = EisenstatWalker(): an inexact Newton. Far from the solution the linear system only needs to be solved loosely (its tolerance eta_k follows the reduction of the Newton residual); close to it, tightly. Fewer Krylov iterations, sometimes one more Newton iteration.\nreuse = 3: the AMG hierarchy is rebuilt every three Newton iterations and reused in between (the operator of CG is always the current tangent, only the preconditioner lags).\n\nmech_solvers = [\n  (\"LU\", p -> LUSolver(), nothing),\n  (\"CG + AMG\", p -> AMGSolver(:cg; rtol=1e-10, maxiter=1000), nothing),\n  (\"CG + AMG, rigid modes\", p -> AMGSolver(:cg; rtol=1e-10, maxiter=1000, nullspace=near_nullspace(p.U)), nothing),\n  (\"  + inexact Newton\", p -> AMGSolver(:cg; rtol=1e-10, maxiter=1000, nullspace=near_nullspace(p.U)), EisenstatWalker()),\n  (\"  + inexact, reuse = 3\", p -> AMGSolver(:cg; rtol=1e-10, maxiter=1000, reuse=3, nullspace=near_nullspace(p.U)), EisenstatWalker()),\n]\n\nWith PETSc (using GridapPETSc), the production solvers of HyperFEM for mechanics: CG with PETSc's algebraic multigrid GAMG and the rigid-body modes, and the parallel direct solver MUMPS (the reference when algebraic multigrid struggles, e.g. thin structures with few elements across the thickness).\n\nPETSc also gives access to hypre's BoomerAMG, a classical AMG: it chooses coarse points among the unknowns by the strength of the matrix couplings, instead of aggregating them as GAMG and AlgebraicMultigrid do. Its V-cycle uses the same symmetric smoother on the way down and up (PETSc's default, symmetric SOR/Jacobi), so it is symmetric and CG applies. Extra options go through options; HyperFEM adds the option prefix of the solver, so they are written as on PETSc's command line, without -ksp_type/-pc_type (the constructor sets them). BoomerAMG ignores the near-null space given with nullspace unless both nodal_coarsen and vec_interp_variant are set (PETSc's PCHYPRE manual page). The configurations compared:\n\nthe defaults of PETSc's interface to hypre (Falgout coarsening, classical interpolation; not hypre's own recommended settings, HMIS coarsening with extended interpolation): one scalar AMG over all the dofs, as if the three displacement components were unrelated unknowns;\na configuration for elasticity that uses the rigid-body modes, with blocksize = 3 (the matrix is made of 3 × 3 nodal blocks; the bar has no component-wise Dirichlet masks, so its free dofs come in complete nodes):\nnodal_coarsen 6 coarsens the graph of the nodes instead of the scalar dofs, the strength of the coupling between two nodes being the sum of all the entries of their 3 × 3 block (4 uses the row-sum norm of the block, 1 its Frobenius norm: HYPRE_BoomerAMGSetNodal in hypre's reference manual);\nvec_interp_variant 2 adds the given vectors to the interpolation (hypre's \"GM approach 2\"), so that the coarse levels can represent the rigid-body modes;\ninterp_type ext+i: the extended interpolation, which reaches the neighbours of neighbours and stays accurate on the sparse coarse grids of coarsen_type HMIS (a parallel coarsening that keeps fewer coarse points than the Falgout default);\nstrong_threshold 0.5: a coupling is strong if it is above half of the largest one in its row. The default, 0.25, suits 2D diffusion; hypre's manual suggests 0.5–0.6 for 3D Laplace operators and larger values, such as 0.9, for elasticity, but 0.9 was worse on this bar;\nagg_nl 1: aggressive coarsening on the first level (far fewer coarse points, a cheaper hierarchy). With the vector interpolation it is essential: without it the hierarchy is so dense that a Newton iteration takes about 20 s on the full bar.\nthe same with nodal_coarsen 4; and with nodal_coarsen 4 given only the three rotations, near_nullspace(U)[:, 4:6] (the translations, the first three columns, are already reproduced by the interpolation of every displacement component; this is also what MFEM passes to BoomerAMG for elasticity).\n\nboomeramg_options(nodal) = \"-pc_hypre_boomeramg_nodal_coarsen $nodal -pc_hypre_boomeramg_vec_interp_variant 2 \" *\n  \"-pc_hypre_boomeramg_interp_type ext+i -pc_hypre_boomeramg_coarsen_type HMIS -pc_hypre_boomeramg_strong_threshold 0.5 \" *\n  \"-pc_hypre_boomeramg_agg_nl 1\"\nboomeramg(p, nodal, modes) = PETScSolver(:cg, :boomeramg; rtol=1e-10, maxiter=1000, nullspace=modes,\n                                         blocksize=3, options=boomeramg_options(nodal))\nif PETSC\n  push!(mech_solvers,\n    (\"CG + GAMG (PETSc), rigid modes\", p -> PETScSolver(:cg, :gamg; rtol=1e-10, maxiter=1000, nullspace=near_nullspace(p.U)), nothing))\n  # the PETSc defaults cost about 2 minutes per Newton iteration on the bar of the full run: FAST only\n  FAST && push!(mech_solvers,\n    (\"CG + BoomerAMG, PETSc defaults\", p -> PETScSolver(:cg, :boomeramg; rtol=1e-10, maxiter=1000), nothing))\n  push!(mech_solvers,\n    (\"CG + BoomerAMG, nodal 6, rigid modes\", p -> boomeramg(p, 6, near_nullspace(p.U)), nothing),\n    (\"CG + BoomerAMG, nodal 4, rigid modes\", p -> boomeramg(p, 4, near_nullspace(p.U)), nothing),\n    (\"CG + BoomerAMG, nodal 4, rotations\", p -> boomeramg(p, 4, near_nullspace(p.U)[:, 4:6]), nothing),\n    (\"MUMPS (PETSc)\", p -> PETScSolver(:preonly, :mumps), nothing))\nend","title":"Running one solver"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E13_Solvers/#Solve","page":"13 · Linear solvers for large problems","text":"First every solver on a small bar (compilation), then on the bar of the tutorial.\n\nn = FAST ? 4 : 16\nfor (name, make, forcing) in mech_solvers\n  pw = bar_problem(2)\n  solve_with(make(pw), pw; forcing)\nend\np = bar_problem(n)\nndofs = num_free_dofs(p.U)\nprintln(\"Bar: $(5n)×$(n)×$(n) Q1 hexahedra, $ndofs free dofs\")\nmech = Dict{String,Any}()\nfor (name, make, forcing) in mech_solvers\n  mech[name] = solve_with(make(p), p; forcing)\nend","title":"Solve"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E13_Solvers/#Results","page":"13 · Linear solvers for large problems","text":"Newton iterations, Krylov iterations (total over the run and per Newton iteration), preconditioner builds, time of the run and, per Newton iteration, of the setup of the linear solver plus the linear solve (\"solve/it\"), and the difference from the LU solution.\n\nfunction print_table(results, names, ref)\n  @printf(\"  %-42s %6s %7s %7s %6s %8s %9s %9s\\n\", \"solver\", \"Newton\", \"Krylov\", \"per it\", \"builds\", \"run (s)\", \"solve/it\", \"vs LU\")\n  for name in names\n    r = results[name]\n    err = norm(r.x - ref.x) / norm(ref.x)\n    @printf(\"  %-42s %6d %7d %7.1f %6d %8.2f %9.3f %9.1e\\n\", name, r.newton, r.krylov, r.krylov / r.newton, r.builds, r.t,\n            (r.t_setup + r.t_lin) / r.newton, err)\n  end\nend\nprint_table(mech, first.(mech_solvers), mech[\"LU\"])\n\nA measured example (full run, Mac M5 Max, 4 Julia threads, 4 BLAS threads, PETSc serial; times change with the machine, the iterations do not):\n\nBar: 80×16×16 Q1 hexahedra, 68493 free dofs\n  solver                                     Newton  Krylov  per it builds  run (s)  solve/it     vs LU\n  LU                                              6       0     0.0      0    18.14     2.183   0.0e+00\n  CG + AMG                                        6    1163   193.8      8    21.20     2.583   9.8e-17\n  CG + AMG, rigid modes                           6     203    33.8      8     9.02     1.059   9.6e-17\n    + inexact Newton                              6      73    12.2      8     6.73     0.695   3.1e-13\n    + inexact, reuse = 3                          6      78    13.0      3     4.30     0.377   4.0e-13\n  CG + GAMG (PETSc), rigid modes                  6     212    35.3      8     4.99     0.505   9.3e-17\n  CG + BoomerAMG, nodal 6, rigid modes            6     404    67.3      8    19.40     2.340   9.5e-17\n  CG + BoomerAMG, nodal 4, rigid modes            6     193    32.2      8     9.32     1.082   8.7e-17\n  CG + BoomerAMG, nodal 4, rotations              6     190    31.7      8     6.80     0.784   9.1e-17\n  MUMPS (PETSc)                                   6       6     1.0      8     8.92     1.044   8.1e-17\n\nReading the table (full run):\n\nwithout the rigid-body modes CG + AMG needs about six times more iterations (194 instead of 34 per Newton iteration) and is slower than LU; with them the Newton iterations are those of LU, to round-off;\nthe inexact Newton cuts the Krylov iterations by a factor of about three with the same Newton iterations;\nreuse = 3 builds the hierarchy 3 times instead of 8 (6 Newton iterations plus the 2 Dirichlet projections, which go through the same linear solver);\nat 68 000 dofs the AMG configurations with rigid modes already beat LU per Newton iteration, and the gap widens with the size (the cost of LU grows much faster than the number of dofs; tutorial 14 shows multigrid iterations that do not grow). With the 1 425 dofs of the FAST run LU is the fastest.\nMUMPS, also a direct solver, factorises this matrix faster than UMFPACK here; it is the reference when algebraic multigrid fails.\nBoomerAMG with PETSc's defaults (FAST only) needs 41.5 CG iterations per Newton iteration on the small bar, but its hierarchy is expensive: on the full bar, measured separately, 121 iterations and about 127 s per Newton iteration, against 2.2 s for LU.\nWith nodal_coarsen 6 the elasticity configuration converges, but its iterations grow with the mesh (41.5 in FAST, 67 in the full run). With nodal_coarsen 4 (row-sum norm of the blocks) they stay at about 32 and the time per Newton iteration falls by half; giving only the three rotations keeps the iterations and makes the hierarchy cheaper. That last configuration is the BoomerAMG to use here: about 1.6 times the time of GAMG with the rigid modes, which remains the PETSc choice for mechanics on this bar.\n\nThe compiled assembly runs on Julia's threads; AlgebraicMultigrid's AMGSolver and PETSc run in serial here. The times also depend on the load of the machine: the iterations are what to compare. With PETSc and several Julia threads, GridapPETSc may print Error in running finalizer: AssertionError(\"Threads.threadid() == 1\") and \"objects still not finalized\" at exit: they come from GridapPETSc's clean-up and are harmless.","title":"Results"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E13_Solvers/#Assembly:-compiled-kernels-against-Gridap","page":"13 · Linear solvers for large problems","text":"One tangent assembly at the final state, after a warm-up, with the compiled operator the model built and with Gridap's assemble_matrix on the same weak form. HyperFEMKernels.work_summary tells how every integration group was compiled: :factorised and :linear_maps are the fast paths, :duals the generic one (automatic differentiation at every point), and fallback_* counts parts left to Gridap.\n\nlet r = mech[\"LU\"], op = r.model.caches.op\n  ws = HyperFEMKernels.work_summary(op)\n  println(\"Compiled groups: \", [(g.kind, g.domain, g.path) for g in ws.groups],\n          \"; Gridap fallbacks: $(ws.fallback_residual + ws.fallback_jacobian)\")\n  A = Gridap.Algebra.allocate_jacobian(op, r.x)\n  Gridap.Algebra.jacobian!(A, op, r.x)\n  t_compiled = minimum(@elapsed(Gridap.Algebra.jacobian!(A, op, r.x)) for _ in 1:3)\n  uh = get_state(r.model)\n  a(du, v) = p.jac(1.0)(uh, du, v)\n  Ag = assemble_matrix(a, p.U, p.V)\n  t_gridap = minimum(@elapsed(assemble_matrix(a, p.U, p.V)) for _ in 1:2)\n  @printf(\"One tangent assembly (%d dofs): compiled %.3f s, Gridap %.3f s, same matrix to %.1e\\n\",\n          ndofs, t_compiled, t_gridap, norm(A - Ag) / norm(Ag))\n  global assembly_diff = norm(A - Ag) / norm(Ag)\n  global compiled_paths = [g.path for g in ws.groups]\nend\n\nThe deformed bar computed with LU, for ParaView.\n\nwritevtk(p.Ω, joinpath(outdir, \"bar\"), cellfields=[\"u\" => get_state(mech[\"LU\"].model)])","title":"Assembly: compiled kernels against Gridap"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E13_Solvers/#Multi-field-problems:-block-preconditioners","page":"13 · Linear solvers for large problems","text":"A dielectric elastomer box (01)times(002)times(002) (ElectroMechModel of tutorial 04: neo-Hookean with lambda = 10, mu = 1, ideal dielectric varepsilon = 1), clamped at x = 0, with a potential difference of 0.02 between its bottom and top faces. The unknowns are (boldsymbol u varphi) and the tangent is a symmetric saddle point,\n\nbeginpmatrix boldsymbol K_uu  boldsymbol K_uvarphi  boldsymbol K_varphi u  boldsymbol K_varphivarphi endpmatrix\nqquad boldsymbol K_uu text positive definite quad boldsymbol K_varphivarphi text negative definite\n\non which a single AMG hierarchy fails. A block (field-split) preconditioner uses one AMG per field, with the rigid-body modes on the displacement block: additively (block Jacobi), multiplicatively (block Gauss–Seidel) or through the Schur complement of boldsymbol K_uu, inside GMRES. AMGSolver(U; split) and PETScSolver(U; …) take the field structure from the multi-field trial space U.\n\nfunction em_problem(nx, ny, nz)\n  model = CartesianDiscreteModel((0.0, 1.0, 0.0, 0.2, 0.0, 0.2), (nx, ny, nz))\n  labels = get_face_labeling(model)\n  add_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"fixed\", CartesianTags.face0YZ⁺)\n  add_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"top\", CartesianTags.faceXY1⁺)\n  add_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"bottom\", CartesianTags.faceXY0⁺)\n  physmodel = ElectroMechModel(IdealDielectric(ε=1.0), NeoHookean3D(λ=10.0, μ=1.0))\n  kinematics = (Kinematics(Mechano, Solid), Kinematics(Electro, Solid))\n  Ω = Triangulation(model)\n  dΩ = Measure(Ω, 2)\n  Du = BoundaryConditions(BoundaryCondition(\"fixed\", [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]))\n  Dφ = BoundaryConditions(BoundaryCondition(\"bottom\", 0.0), BoundaryCondition(\"top\", 0.02, EvolutionFunctions.ramp()))\n  D = MultiFieldBC([Du, Dφ])\n  Vu = TestFESpace(Ω, ReferenceFE(lagrangian, VectorValue{3,Float64}, 1), Du, conformity=:H1)\n  Vφ = TestFESpace(Ω, ReferenceFE(lagrangian, Float64, 1), Dφ, conformity=:H1)\n  V = MultiFieldFESpace([Vu, Vφ])\n  U = MultiFieldFESpace([TrialFESpace(Vu, Du, 0.0), TrialFESpace(Vφ, Dφ, 0.0)])\n  res(Λ) = ((u, φ), (v, vφ)) -> residual(physmodel, kinematics, (u, φ), (v, vφ), dΩ)\n  jac(Λ) = ((u, φ), (du, dφ), (v, vφ)) -> jacobian(physmodel, kinematics, (u, φ), (du, dφ), (v, vφ), dΩ)\n  (; res, jac, U, V, D, Ω)\nend\n\nem_solvers = [\n  (\"LU\", p -> LUSolver(), nothing),\n  (\"GMRES + AMG blocks, additive\", p -> AMGSolver(p.U; split=:additive, rtol=1e-10), nothing),\n  (\"GMRES + AMG blocks, Schur\", p -> AMGSolver(p.U; split=:schur, rtol=1e-10), nothing),\n  (\"  + inexact, reuse = 3\", p -> AMGSolver(p.U; split=:schur, rtol=1e-10, reuse=3), EisenstatWalker()),\n]\n\nThe production configuration of HyperFEM for electro- and magneto-mechanics: PETSc's field split with GAMG on every block, rebuilt every three Newton iterations, and the inexact Newton.\n\nif PETSC\n  push!(em_solvers, (\"PETSc field split, reuse = 3, inexact\", p -> PETScSolver(p.U; rtol=1e-10, reuse=3), EisenstatWalker()))\nend\n\nem_cells = FAST ? (10, 2, 4) : (40, 8, 16)\nfor (name, make, forcing) in em_solvers\n  pw = em_problem(5, 1, 2)\n  solve_with(make(pw), pw; forcing)\nend\npem = em_problem(em_cells...)\nndofs_em = num_free_dofs(pem.U)\nprintln(\"Electro-mechanical box: $(join(em_cells, \"×\")) Q1 hexahedra, $ndofs_em free dofs\")\nem = Dict{String,Any}()\nfor (name, make, forcing) in em_solvers\n  em[name] = solve_with(make(pem), pem; forcing)\nend\nprint_table(em, first.(em_solvers), em[\"LU\"])\n\nA measured example (full run, same machine):\n\nElectro-mechanical box: 40×8×16 Q1 hexahedra, 23895 free dofs\n  solver                                     Newton  Krylov  per it builds  run (s)  solve/it     vs LU\n  LU                                              6       0     0.0      0     3.96     0.433   0.0e+00\n  GMRES + AMG blocks, additive                    6     336    56.0      8     3.16     0.389   2.9e-16\n  GMRES + AMG blocks, Schur                       6     330    55.0      8     3.19     0.387   8.3e-16\n    + inexact, reuse = 3                          7     135    19.3      3     1.73     0.143   1.5e-11\n  PETSc field split, reuse = 3, inexact           6     147    24.5      3     1.15     0.078   5.0e-11\n\nThe block preconditioners follow the Newton trajectory of LU; with the inexact Newton and reuse = 3 the AMG blocks are built 3 times instead of 8 (one more Newton iteration here) and the Krylov iterations drop by a factor of about 2.5. The PETSc field split is the fastest configuration on this problem.","title":"Multi-field problems: block preconditioners"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E13_Solvers/#Checks","page":"13 · Linear solvers for large problems","text":"Every solver reaches the LU solution (the Newton tolerance, 10^-10, through the conditioning of the tangent, bounds the difference). The solvers that solve every linear system to 10^-10 follow the Newton trajectory of LU: the same number of Newton iterations. The counters behave as described above.\n\nfor (results, names) in ((mech, first.(mech_solvers)), (em, first.(em_solvers)))\n  ref = results[\"LU\"]\n  for name in names\n    r = results[name]\n    @assert r.ok \"$name: the load stepping did not reach Λ = 1\"\n    @assert norm(r.x - ref.x) ≤ 1e-6 * norm(ref.x) \"$name: solution away from LU\"\n  end\n  for name in names\n    occursin(\"inexact\", name) && continue\n    @assert results[name].newton == ref.newton \"$name: Newton iterations differ from LU\"\n  end\nend\n@assert mech[\"CG + AMG, rigid modes\"].krylov < mech[\"CG + AMG\"].krylov \"the rigid-body modes did not reduce the iterations\"\n@assert mech[\"  + inexact Newton\"].krylov < mech[\"CG + AMG, rigid modes\"].krylov \"the inexact Newton did not reduce the Krylov iterations\"\n@assert mech[\"  + inexact, reuse = 3\"].builds < mech[\"  + inexact Newton\"].builds \"reuse did not reduce the preconditioner builds\"\n@assert em[\"  + inexact, reuse = 3\"].builds < em[\"GMRES + AMG blocks, Schur\"].builds \"reuse did not reduce the preconditioner builds (EM)\"\n@assert assembly_diff < 1e-12 \"the compiled tangent differs from Gridap's\"\n@assert all(!=(:duals), compiled_paths) \"a group of the compiled operator took the generic (dual number) path\"\nif PETSC\n  @assert norm(mech[\"MUMPS (PETSc)\"].x - mech[\"LU\"].x) ≤ 1e-10 * norm(mech[\"LU\"].x) \"MUMPS away from LU\"\n  # BoomerAMG, the recommended configuration: CG iterations per Newton iteration in a band around the measured value\n  bamg_its = mech[\"CG + BoomerAMG, nodal 4, rotations\"].krylov / mech[\"CG + BoomerAMG, nodal 4, rotations\"].newton\n  bamg_band = FAST ? (24.0, 36.0) : (25.0, 40.0)              # measured: 29.7 (FAST), 31.7 (full)\n  bamg6_its = mech[\"CG + BoomerAMG, nodal 6, rigid modes\"].krylov / mech[\"CG + BoomerAMG, nodal 6, rigid modes\"].newton\n  @assert bamg_its < bamg6_its \"BoomerAMG: nodal coarsening 4 with the rotations did not improve on nodal coarsening 6\"\n  @assert bamg_band[1] ≤ bamg_its ≤ bamg_band[2] \"BoomerAMG: $(round(bamg_its, digits=1)) iterations per Newton iteration, out of $bamg_band\"\nend\n\nKrylov iterations per Newton iteration of CG + AMG with the rigid modes, in a band around the measured value (a regression of the preconditioner, not of the solution).\n\namg_per_newton = mech[\"CG + AMG, rigid modes\"].krylov / mech[\"CG + AMG, rigid modes\"].newton\nprintln(\"CG + AMG (rigid modes): $(round(amg_per_newton, digits=1)) iterations per Newton iteration\")\namg_band = FAST ? (17.0, 28.0) : (25.0, 42.0)          # measured: 22.7 (FAST), 33.8 (full)\n@assert amg_band[1] ≤ amg_per_newton ≤ amg_band[2] \"CG + AMG iterations per Newton iteration out of the band $amg_band\"\n\nRegression values of the LU solution (HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6, Gridap 0.20.8, Julia 1.13.0, macOS arm64).\n\nxref_mech, xref_em = FAST ? (0.6610687966879161, 0.10822488398193163) : (4.59603534564497, 0.8492741596170085)\nprintln(\"‖x‖ bar = $(norm(mech[\"LU\"].x)), ‖x‖ box = $(norm(em[\"LU\"].x))\")\n@assert isapprox(norm(mech[\"LU\"].x), xref_mech; rtol=1e-5) \"bar: regression value\"\n@assert isapprox(norm(em[\"LU\"].x), xref_em; rtol=1e-5) \"box: regression value\"","title":"Checks"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E13_Solvers/#Going-further","page":"13 · Linear solvers for large problems","text":"Tutorial 14 replaces algebraic by geometric multigrid on a hierarchy of refined meshes (HMultigridSolver) and by p-multigrid with matrix-free high-order operators.\noptions = \"-ksp_monitor\" (or monitor = true) prints PETSc's residual history; any PETSc option of the solver goes through options. BoomerAMG has many more (smoothers, cycle type): PETSc's PCHYPRE page lists them.\nbackend = CompiledBackend(jacobian = :matrixfree) never assembles the tangent of the Newton: tutorial 14 uses it with p-multigrid, and HyperFEMKernels.LaggedAssembled(PETScSolver(:preonly, :mumps); every = 3) preconditions it with a factorisation renewed every three Newton iterations.\nNewtonRaphson(…; verbose = 2) prints, per Newton iteration, the linear solver, its iterations and the time.\nReferences: Eisenstat and Walker, SIAM J. Sci. Comput. 17 (1996) 16–32, doi:10.1137/0917003 (forcing terms); Vaněk, Mandel and Brezina, Computing 56 (1996) 179–196, doi:10.1007/BF02238511 (smoothed aggregation).","title":"Going further"},{"category":"section","location":"E07_Topology_optimization/#Tutorial-07:-Topology-optimisation-(in-preparation)","page":"07 · Topology optimisation (in preparation)","text":"This tutorial will show the topology optimisation of a hyperelastic structure with HyperShape.jl, the shape and topology optimisation package built on HyperFEM. It will be published when HyperShape's optimisation loop is released.","title":"Tutorial 07: Topology optimisation (in preparation)"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E17_ThermoMechanics/#Tutorial-17:-Thermo-mechanics:-transient-heat-conduction-and-thermal-expansion","page":"17 · Thermo-mechanics: transient heat conduction and thermal expansion","text":"Source · Notebook · nbviewer\n\nOne end of a hyperelastic bar is suddenly heated while the other is kept at the initial temperature. Heat diffuses along the bar and the bar expands, freely: it is held only as much as needed to remove its rigid motions. The temperature has a classical series solution, and the expansion of a free body has two exact properties (the change of length is the integral of the thermal strain, and a linear temperature profile leaves the body free of stress), so every part of the coupled computation can be checked.\n\nMeasured on a Mac (M5 Max, Julia 1.13.0, 4 threads, 4 BLAS threads; HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6, Gridap 0.20.8; compiled backend), whole run including compilation: FAST (752 dofs, 143 time steps) 46 s, full (4 503 dofs, 210 time steps) 49 s; median time per step after compilation 3.9 ms (FAST) and 26 ms (full).\n\nWhat you will learn\n\nthe thermo-mechanical models of HyperFEM: ThermalModel, a ThermalLaw and ThermoMechModel, and the derivatives they return as functions of (boldsymbol F vartheta);\nthe weak form of a coupled displacement–temperature problem written term by term from the derivatives of the free energy: mechanical equilibrium, heat conduction, the coupling and the heat capacity;\na first-order transient problem in TransientModel: a mass form on one field only (heat capacity), quasi-static mechanics, the BDF(2) scheme and time steps controlled by the error (AdaptiveStepping) with exact output times (tstops);\nhow to restrain a free body with symmetry planes and a point, and how to check the compiled backend (work_summary, counters) in a time loop.","title":"Tutorial 17: Thermo-mechanics: transient heat conduction and thermal expansion"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E17_ThermoMechanics/#Problem-statement","page":"17 · Thermo-mechanics: transient heat conduction and thermal expansion","text":"The unknowns are the displacement boldsymbol u and the temperature increment vartheta = theta - theta_r over the reference temperature theta_r. With the Helmholtz free energy Psi(boldsymbol F vartheta), the first Piola stress is boldsymbol P = partial_boldsymbol FPsi, and the heat flux follows Fourier's law in the reference configuration, boldsymbol Q = -knabla_0vartheta. Neglecting inertia and the heat produced by the deformation (the thermo-elastic heating -thetapartial^2_boldsymbol FvarthetaPsidotboldsymbol F: for a free body it is 9Kalpha_th^2theta_rdotvartheta, a fraction 9Kalpha_th^2theta_rc_v approx 57cdot10^-5 of the heat-capacity term with the data below, whatever the amplitude; see «Going further»), the problem reads: find (boldsymbol u vartheta) such that\n\nint_Omega boldsymbol P(boldsymbol F vartheta)  nabla_0 boldsymbol v mathrm dV = 0  qquad\nint_Omega c_vdotvarthetadeltavartheta mathrm dV\n+ int_Omega knabla_0varthetacdotnabla_0deltavartheta mathrm dV = 0\nqquad forall (boldsymbol v deltavartheta) \n\nwith boldsymbol F = boldsymbol I + nabla_0boldsymbol u, the heat capacity per unit volume c_v and the conductivity k. The free energy of ThermoMechModel is\n\nPsi(boldsymbol F vartheta) = f(theta)Psi_m(boldsymbol F) + Psi_t(vartheta)\n- 3alpha(J^2 - 1)vartheta  qquad J = detboldsymbol F \n\nwith the neo-Hookean Psi_m = tfracmu2(boldsymbol Fboldsymbol F - 3) - muln J + tfraclambda2(J-1)^2, the purely thermal Psi_t(vartheta) = c_vvartheta - thetaln(thetatheta_r) (whose -thetapartial^2_varthetavarthetaPsi_t = c_v), the thermal law f(theta) (here f = 1: the stiffness does not depend on the temperature) and the coupling term. Linearising boldsymbol P at boldsymbol F = boldsymbol I, a free body heated uniformly by vartheta expands with the strain varepsilon = 6alphavartheta(3lambda + 2mu): the coefficient of linear thermal expansion is alpha_th = 2alphaK, with the bulk modulus K = lambda + 2mu3. We give alpha_th and set alpha = Kalpha_th2.\n\nThe bar Omega = (0L)times(0W)times(0H) is a quarter of a bar of section 2Wtimes 2H: the planes y = 0 and z = 0 are symmetry planes (no normal displacement), and the origin cannot move along x. These are the minimal restraints: they remove the rigid motions and nothing else. The bar starts at vartheta = 0; the temperature of the end x = 0 rises linearly to Delta T in a short time t_r, vartheta(0 t) = Delta Tg(t) with g(t) = min(tt_r 1), the end x = L is kept at vartheta = 0 and the lateral faces are insulated. (A sudden jump of the end temperature, t_r = 0, is the textbook case, but neither the mesh nor the error control of the time step can follow it: the step collapses to its minimum on fine meshes. A short ramp is also what a real heater does.) The temperature does not depend on the displacement, so it is that of a 1D rod; by Duhamel's principle,\n\nvartheta(x t) = Delta Tg(t)Big(1 - fracxLBig) - sum_nge1 frac2Delta Tnpi\nfrac1 - e^-lambda_n min(t t_r)lambda_n t_r e^-lambda_n max(t - t_r 0) sinfracnpi xL \nqquad lambda_n = fracn^2pi^2 DL^2  quad D = frackc_v \n\n(for t_r to 0 the classical series of the sudden jump), which tends to the linear profile Delta T(1 - xL). For the mechanics, two exact results of linear thermo-elasticity for a free body: (i) by the reciprocal theorem (with a uniform uniaxial stress as the second state), the change of length of the bar, averaged over the end faces, is the integral of the thermal strain, Deltabarell(t) = alpha_thint_Omega varthetamathrm dV  A (A = WH), at every time; (ii) a temperature linear in the coordinates gives a compatible thermal strain, so at steady state the bar is free of stress and Deltabarell = alpha_thDelta T L2.\n\nThe data are dimensionless for the mechanics and the conduction (any consistent set of units), and the temperature is in kelvin: L = 1, W = H = 01, mu = 1, lambda = 15 (nu = 03), c_v = 1, k = 1 (D = 1, diffusion time L^2(pi^2 D) approx 01), t_r = 001, theta_r = 29315, Delta T = 10, alpha_th = 10^-4 (thermal strains of 10^-3 at most: small deformations).","title":"Problem statement"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E17_ThermoMechanics/#Setup","page":"17 · Thermo-mechanics: transient heat conduction and thermal expansion","text":"using HyperFEM\nusing HyperFEMKernels                                          # compiled backend (`backend=:compiled`)\nusing Gridap\nusing Printf\n\nconst FAST = get(ENV, \"HYPERFEM_TUTORIALS_FAST\", \"0\") == \"1\"   # reduced case for the test suite\noutdir = joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"data\", \"sims\", \"E17_ThermoMechanics\")\nsetupfolder(outdir)                                            # VTK output (not versioned)","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E17_ThermoMechanics/#Geometry-and-mesh","page":"17 · Thermo-mechanics: transient heat conduction and thermal expansion","text":"A Cartesian mesh of hexahedra along the bar. The temperature varies along x only, so the section needs few elements. The restraints of the displacement use four tags: the origin (all components fixed through mask below), the edge y = z = 0 (on both symmetry planes), and the two symmetry planes. A node that belongs to several tags takes the first of them in the list of conditions, so the most restrained come first.\n\nconst L, W, H = 1.0, 0.1, 0.1\npartition = FAST ? (16, 1, 1) : (32, 2, 2)\ngeometry = CartesianDiscreteModel((0, L, 0, W, 0, H), partition)\nlabels = get_face_labeling(geometry)\nadd_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"origin\", CartesianTags.corner000)\nadd_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"axis\", CartesianTags.edgeX00⁺)\nadd_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"y0\", CartesianTags.faceX0Z⁺)\nadd_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"z0\", CartesianTags.faceXY0⁺)\nadd_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"hot\", CartesianTags.face0YZ⁺)\nadd_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"cold\", CartesianTags.face1YZ⁺)","title":"Geometry and mesh"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E17_ThermoMechanics/#Constitutive-model","page":"17 · Thermo-mechanics: transient heat conduction and thermal expansion","text":"ThermalModel holds c_v, theta_r, alpha and k; ConstantEnergyLaw() is the law f = 1 (EntropicElasticityLaw(θr=θr, γ=0.0), f = thetatheta_r, would make the stiffness proportional to the absolute temperature, as in rubber). Calling the model returns the energy and its derivatives as functions of (boldsymbol F vartheta): (Psi partial_boldsymbol FPsi partial_varthetaPsi partial^2_boldsymbol Fboldsymbol FPsi partial^2_varthetavarthetaPsi partial^2_boldsymbol FvarthetaPsi eta), with the entropy eta = -partial_varthetaPsi. These functions are all the weak form needs; we compose them below with the kinematics. The mechanical kinematics gives boldsymbol F = boldsymbol I + nabla_0boldsymbol u from the displacement gradient (Gridap's ∇(u) is the transpose of nabla_0boldsymbol u, hence F ∘ ∇(u)'); the temperature enters the functions as it is (no kinematic map: Kinematics(Thermo, Solid) exists but carries no function, so we do not use it).\n\nconst μ, λ = 1.0, 1.5\nconst cv, k = 1.0, 1.0\nconst θr, ΔT, αth = 293.15, 10.0, 1e-4\nconst t_r = 0.01                                               # duration of the ramp of the end temperature\nconst K = λ + 2μ / 3                                           # bulk modulus\nconst E = μ * (3λ + 2μ) / (λ + μ)                              # Young's modulus\nconst D = k / cv                                               # thermal diffusivity\nphysmodel = ThermoMechModel(ThermalModel(Cv=cv, θr=θr, α=K * αth / 2, κ=k), NeoHookean3D(λ=λ, μ=μ),\n                            ConstantEnergyLaw())\nΨ, ∂Ψu, ∂Ψθ, ∂Ψuu, ∂Ψθθ, ∂Ψuθ, η = physmodel()\nF, _, _ = get_Kinematics(Kinematics(Mechano, Solid))","title":"Constitutive model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E17_ThermoMechanics/#Boundary-conditions","page":"17 · Thermo-mechanics: transient heat conduction and thermal expansion","text":"One set per field, gathered by MultiFieldBC. The temperature of the end x = 0 is Delta T times the evolution function EvolutionFunctions.ramp(t_r), g(t) = min(tt_r 1), evaluated by TransientModel at the time of every stage (it is 0 in the initial state, which is therefore consistent with the conditions). The other conditions are constant.\n\nDu = BoundaryConditions(\n  BoundaryCondition(\"origin\", [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]),\n  BoundaryCondition(\"axis\", [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]; mask=(false, true, true)),\n  BoundaryCondition(\"y0\", [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]; mask=(false, true, false)),\n  BoundaryCondition(\"z0\", [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]; mask=(false, false, true)))\nDθ = BoundaryConditions(BoundaryCondition(\"hot\", ΔT, EvolutionFunctions.ramp(t_r)), BoundaryCondition(\"cold\", 0.0))\nD_bc = MultiFieldBC([Du, Dθ])","title":"Boundary conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E17_ThermoMechanics/#Finite-element-spaces","page":"17 · Thermo-mechanics: transient heat conduction and thermal expansion","text":"Quadratic displacements and linear temperatures (Q2/Q1): the thermal strain alpha_thvartheta and the strain of the displacement are then polynomials of the same degree, and the stress-free state of a linear temperature profile is represented exactly.\n\nreffeu = ReferenceFE(lagrangian, VectorValue{3,Float64}, 2)\nreffeθ = ReferenceFE(lagrangian, Float64, 1)\nVu = TestFESpace(geometry, reffeu, D_bc[1], conformity=:H1)\nVθ = TestFESpace(geometry, reffeθ, D_bc[2], conformity=:H1)\nUu = TrialFESpace(Vu, D_bc[1], 0.0)\nUθ = TrialFESpace(Vθ, D_bc[2], 0.0)\nV = MultiFieldFESpace([Vu, Vθ])\nU = MultiFieldFESpace([Uu, Uθ])\nΩ = Triangulation(geometry)\ndΩ = Measure(Ω, 4)\nprintln(\"free dofs: \", num_free_dofs(U), \" (u: \", num_free_dofs(Uu), \", ϑ: \", num_free_dofs(Uθ), \")\")","title":"Finite element spaces"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E17_ThermoMechanics/#Weak-form","page":"17 · Thermo-mechanics: transient heat conduction and thermal expansion","text":"We write the residual and its tangent term by term from the derivatives of the free energy; every term is a Gridap integral of the model's functions composed (∘) with the kinematics at the trial functions. ⊙ contracts two second-order tensors (boldsymbol Aboldsymbol B) and ⋅² the last two indices of a fourth-order tensor with a second-order one, (mathbb Ccdot^2boldsymbol A)_ij = C_ijklA_kl.\n\nMechanical equilibrium. The stress is the derivative of the free energy at the current temperature,\n\nR_u = int_Omega boldsymbol P(boldsymbol F vartheta)  nabla_0boldsymbol v mathrm dV  qquad\nboldsymbol P = partial_boldsymbol FPsi = partial_boldsymbol FPsi_m - 6alpha Jvarthetaboldsymbol H \nqquad boldsymbol H = Jboldsymbol F^-T \n\nwhere the second term is the thermal stress of the coupling energy (f = 1).\n\nr_mech(u, θ, v) = ∫(∇(v)' ⊙ (∂Ψu ∘ (F ∘ ∇(u)', θ)))dΩ\n\nHeat conduction. Fourier's law in the reference configuration, boldsymbol Q = -knabla_0vartheta:\n\nR_vartheta = int_Omega knabla_0varthetacdotnabla_0deltavartheta mathrm dV \n\nr_cond(θ, vθ) = ∫(k * (∇(θ) ⋅ ∇(vθ)))dΩ\n\nTangent. The derivative of R_u + R_vartheta in the direction (deltaboldsymbol u deltavartheta) (trial functions du, dθ) has three blocks. The mechanical block, with the elasticity tensor mathbb C = partial^2_boldsymbol Fboldsymbol FPsi,\n\nK_uu = int_Omega nabla_0boldsymbol v  mathbb C  nabla_0deltaboldsymbol u mathrm dV \n\nthe coupling block, the stress produced by a temperature change, with partial^2_boldsymbol FvarthetaPsi = -6alpha Jboldsymbol H,\n\nK_uvartheta = int_Omega nabla_0boldsymbol v  partial^2_boldsymbol FvarthetaPsideltavartheta mathrm dV \n\nand the conduction block\n\nK_varthetavartheta = int_Omega knabla_0deltavarthetacdotnabla_0deltavartheta^* mathrm dV\n\n(deltavartheta^* the test function vθ). There is no block K_vartheta u: the conduction is written in the reference configuration and the thermo-elastic heating is neglected, so the temperature equation does not depend on the displacement.\n\nj_mech(u, θ, du, v) = ∫(∇(v)' ⊙ ((∂Ψuu ∘ (F ∘ ∇(u)', θ)) ⋅² ∇(du)'))dΩ\nj_coupling(u, θ, dθ, v) = ∫(∇(v)' ⊙ ((∂Ψuθ ∘ (F ∘ ∇(u)', θ)) * dθ))dΩ\nj_cond(dθ, vθ) = ∫(k * (∇(dθ) ⋅ ∇(vθ)))dΩ\n\nHeat capacity. The time derivative enters through the bilinear mass form,\n\nM(dotvartheta deltavartheta) = int_Omega c_vdotvarthetadeltavartheta mathrm dV \nqquad c_v = -thetapartial^2_varthetavarthetaPsi \n\nwith the heat capacity of the model (∂Ψθθ(F, ϑ) = -c_v/θ for ThermalModel and f = 1, so c_v is the constant Cv given to it). The form acts on the temperature only: TransientModel assembles it once into a matrix boldsymbol M and adds boldsymbol Mdotboldsymbol x (with dotboldsymbol x given by the time scheme) to the residual of every stage, and its multiple to the tangent. The displacement has no time derivative: the mechanics is quasi-static, in equilibrium with the temperature of every step.\n\nThe static forms are functions of the time t, as TransientModel expects; here they do not depend on it.\n\nres(t) = ((u, θ), (v, vθ)) -> r_mech(u, θ, v) + r_cond(θ, vθ)\njac(t) = ((u, θ), (du, dθ), (v, vθ)) -> j_mech(u, θ, du, v) + j_coupling(u, θ, dθ, v) + j_cond(dθ, vθ)\nmass = ((du, dθ), (v, vθ)) -> ∫(cv * (dθ * vθ))dΩ\n@assert ∂Ψθθ(one(TensorValue{3,3,Float64}), 0.0) ≈ -cv / θr \"heat capacity of the model differs from cv\"","title":"Weak form"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E17_ThermoMechanics/#Solver-and-computational-model","page":"17 · Thermo-mechanics: transient heat conduction and thermal expansion","text":"BDF(2), the second-order backward differentiation formula, is a natural scheme for diffusion: implicit, stable for any step, and it damps the fast components of the start. The step is chosen by the local error estimate of the scheme (AdaptiveStepping): small at the start, when the temperature changes quickly near the heated end, and growing as the bar approaches the steady state. The estimate uses only the degrees of freedom with a time derivative (the temperature); atol is in kelvin. The tolerances of the step are tied to the mesh, mathrmtol = 001h^2 (relative to Delta T), so that the error of the time integration stays below that of the mesh, which is of order h^2 for linear elements, when the mesh is refined. tstops are the end of the ramp and the times at which we compare with the series solution: the steps are cut to reach them exactly.\n\nbackend=:compiled evaluates the forms with the compiled, threaded kernels of HyperFEMKernels; they are built once, and at every stage the model only rebinds them to the new state (no recompilation). The mechanics is nonlinear (finite strains), so the tangent is updated at every Newton iteration (tangent_update=:iteration, the default).\n\nt_check = (t_r, 0.05, 0.2)\nt_end = 1.5\nh = L / partition[1]\ntol_t = 0.01 * h^2\nstepping = AdaptiveStepping(; Δt=1e-4, t_end, rtol=tol_t, atol=tol_t * ΔT, tstops=t_check)\nnls = NewtonRaphson(LUSolver(); maxiter=20, atol=1e-10, rtol=1e-8, verbose=0)\ncomp_model = TransientModel(res, jac, U, V, D_bc; scheme=BDF(2), mass, nls, backend=:compiled)\nxh = get_state(comp_model)                                     # (u, ϑ) after every accepted step\nuh, θh = xh","title":"Solver and computational model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E17_ThermoMechanics/#Post-processing","page":"17 · Thermo-mechanics: transient heat conduction and thermal expansion","text":"The exact temperature (series above) is a closure: the parameters are captured when it is built, so the function evaluated at the quadrature points reads only its argument and constants. After the ramp the terms are summed until the exponential factor is negligible; during the ramp the terms decay as n^-3, and 4 000 of them leave a truncation error below 10^-6Delta T.\n\nfunction θ_exact(t)\n  X -> begin\n    x = X[1]\n    s = ΔT * min(t / t_r, 1.0) * (1 - x / L)\n    for n in 1:4000\n      λn = (n * π / L)^2 * D\n      e = exp(-λn * max(t - t_r, 0.0))\n      e < 1e-16 && break\n      s -= 2ΔT / (n * π) * (1 - exp(-λn * min(t, t_r))) / (λn * t_r) * e * sin(n * π * x / L)\n    end\n    s\n  end\nend\n\nAfter every accepted step we store the time, the change of length (mean u_x on the end faces) and its estimate from the reciprocal theorem; at the check times also the error of the temperature, lVertvartheta_h - varthetarVert_L^2  (Delta TOmega^12), and the VTK files.\n\nΓ0 = BoundaryTriangulation(geometry, tags=\"hot\")\nΓ1 = BoundaryTriangulation(geometry, tags=\"cold\")\ndΓ0, dΓ1 = Measure(Γ0, 4), Measure(Γ1, 4)\nconst A = W * H\nvolΩ = sum(∫(1.0)dΩ)\nex = VectorValue(1.0, 0.0, 0.0)\nelongation(uh) = (sum(∫(uh ⋅ ex)dΓ1) - sum(∫(uh ⋅ ex)dΓ0)) / A\nelongation_thermal(θh) = αth * sum(∫(θh)dΩ) / A\nθ_error(θh, t) = sqrt(sum(∫(abs2 ∘ (θh - θ_exact(t)))dΩ) / volΩ) / ΔT\nstress_norm(uh, θh) = sqrt(sum(∫((∂Ψu ∘ (F ∘ ∇(uh)', θh)) ⊙ (∂Ψu ∘ (F ∘ ∇(uh)', θh)))dΩ) / volΩ)\n\ntimes, Δℓ, Δℓ_th, wall = Float64[], Float64[], Float64[], Float64[]\nchecks = Dict{Float64,NamedTuple}()\nx_005 = zeros(num_free_dofs(U))                                # state at t = 0.05 (for the check of the tangent)\nwork_2 = Ref(HyperFEMKernels.counters())                       # kernel counters after the second step\ntime_post = Ref(0.0)                                           # time spent in the post-processing\nfunction record(post)\n  time_post[] += @elapsed begin\n    t = post.Λ[post.iter]\n    push!(wall, time())                                        # wall clock, for the time per step\n    post.iter == 2 && (work_2[] = HyperFEMKernels.counters())\n    push!(times, t); push!(Δℓ, elongation(uh)); push!(Δℓ_th, elongation_thermal(θh))\n    tc = findfirst(s -> isapprox(t, s; atol=1e-12), (t_check..., t_end))\n    if tc !== nothing\n      checks[t] = (err=θ_error(θh, t), σ=stress_norm(uh, θh), Δℓ=Δℓ[end], Δℓ_th=Δℓ_th[end])\n      t == 0.05 && (x_005 .= get_free_dof_values(xh))\n      pvd = get_pvd(post)\n      pvd[t] = createvtk(Ω, joinpath(outdir, \"step_$(post.iter).vtu\"), cellfields=[\"u\" => uh, \"theta\" => θh], order=2)\n    end\n  end\nend\npost = PostProcessor(comp_model, record; is_vtk=true, filepath=outdir)","title":"Post-processing"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E17_ThermoMechanics/#Solve","page":"17 · Thermo-mechanics: transient heat conduction and thermal expansion","text":"Most of the run time is compilation: of the compiled kernels when the model is built, of the first Newton iteration, and of the Gridap integrals and the VTK output of the post-processing the first time they run. The median time per step is what a step costs after that: the coupled mechanics and conduction solved together, in one or two Newton iterations (the mechanics is almost linear here). The step stays small during the ramp and grows to about 01 near the steady state.\n\nHyperFEMKernels.reset_counters!()\ntime_solve = @elapsed (x, flag) = solve!(comp_model; stepping, post)\n@assert flag == 1 \"the time loop did not reach t_end\"\nwork = HyperFEMKernels.counters()\nnsteps = length(times)\nΔts = diff([0.0; times])\nstep_times = sort(diff(wall))                                  # wall time between accepted steps\nprintln(@sprintf(\"%d steps, Δt ∈ [%.2e, %.2e]; solve %.1f s (post-processing %.1f s); median time per step %.1f ms\",\n                 nsteps, minimum(Δts), maximum(Δts), time_solve, time_post[], 1e3 * step_times[cld(end, 2)]))","title":"Solve"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E17_ThermoMechanics/#Efficiency","page":"17 · Thermo-mechanics: transient heat conduction and thermal expansion","text":"What the compiled operators do (work_summary): every group of the stage forms and of the mass form takes a fast path (:linear_maps) and nothing falls back to Gridap. The kernels are generated when the model is built; the counters, reset before solve!, show that the time loop rebinds them to the new state at every attempt (update_fast). The moving Dirichlet values of the ramp need one more kernel (their contribution to the inertia term), generated once in the first step (codegen, update_full); after that nothing is generated or rebuilt: the counters after the second step and at the end agree. HyperFEM 0.3.0 has no accessor for the operators of a TransientModel: the two lines below read internal fields, and may change.\n\nfallbacks = 0\nfor (what, o) in ((\"stage forms\", comp_model.caches.op.inner), (\"mass form\", comp_model.caches.opM))\n  ws = HyperFEMKernels.work_summary(o)\n  global fallbacks += ws.fallback_residual + ws.fallback_jacobian\n  println(what, \": \", [(g.kind, g.domain, g.path) for g in ws.groups], \"; Gridap fallbacks: \",\n          ws.fallback_residual + ws.fallback_jacobian)\nend\nprintln(\"counters after the second step: \", work_2[])\nprintln(\"counters of the solve:          \", work)\n@assert fallbacks == 0 && work.fallback_assemblies == 0 \"a form fell back to Gridap\"\n@assert work.codegen == work_2[].codegen && work.update_full == work_2[].update_full \"kernels rebuilt during the time loop\"","title":"Efficiency"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E17_ThermoMechanics/#Checks","page":"17 · Thermo-mechanics: transient heat conduction and thermal expansion","text":"The values at the check times: error of the temperature, stress, change of length and its estimate.\n\nprintln(\"\\n   t        error ϑ                ‖P‖/(E αth ΔT)   Δℓ            Δℓ (reciprocal)\")\nfor t in sort(collect(keys(checks)))\n  c = checks[t]\n  println(@sprintf(\"%6.3f   %.3e (%.2f h²)   %.3e        %.6e   %.6e\", t, c.err, c.err / h^2, c.σ / (E * αth * ΔT), c.Δℓ, c.Δℓ_th))\nend\n@assert all(t -> haskey(checks, t), (t_check..., t_end)) \"a check time was not reached exactly\"\n\nThe tangent written above must be the derivative of the residual written above: Newton converges (more slowly) even with a wrong tangent, so the solution alone does not test it. We compare the tangent applied to a direction boldsymbol d with a central difference of the residual, boldsymbol r(boldsymbol x + epsilonboldsymbol d) - boldsymbol r(boldsymbol x - epsilonboldsymbol d)(2epsilon), at the state of t = 005 (stressed, with a curved temperature profile). The direction moves every displacement and temperature dof, in proportion to its value with a varying factor, so that all the blocks, the coupling included, take part; the difference is O(epsilon^2) plus round-off (10^-10). The rows of the two equations are compared apart: the conduction rows are much larger than the mechanical ones, which carry the coupling block (a sign error in K_uvartheta gives a difference of order one there). The vectors and the matrix are assembled by Gridap from the same res and jac.\n\nr_at(x) = assemble_vector(y -> res(0.05)(FEFunction(U, x), y), V)\nd = x_005 .* (1 .+ 0.5 .* sin.(1:length(x_005)))\nϵ = 1e-6\nJd = assemble_matrix((dx, y) -> jac(0.05)(FEFunction(U, x_005), dx, y), U, V) * d\nfd = (r_at(x_005 .+ ϵ .* d) .- r_at(x_005 .- ϵ .* d)) ./ (2ϵ)\nnu = num_free_dofs(Uu)                                         # the displacement dofs come first\nblockdiff(rows) = sqrt(sum(abs2, fd[rows] .- Jd[rows]) / sum(abs2, Jd[rows]))\ntangent_error = max(blockdiff(1:nu), blockdiff(nu+1:length(d)))   # mechanical and thermal rows apart\nprintln(@sprintf(\"tangent against finite differences of the residual: relative difference %.2e (u rows), %.2e (ϑ rows)\",\n                 blockdiff(1:nu), blockdiff(nu+1:length(d))))\n@assert tangent_error < 1e-6 \"the tangent is not the derivative of the residual ($tangent_error)\"\n\n(a) The temperature against the series solution. The error comes from the mesh (linear elements, h = Ln_x: of order h^2) and from the time integration (whose tolerance is 001h^2). Measured with n_x = 16 32 64 128 (the FAST and full meshes, and two refinements of the full one), the errors are 19h^2 at t = t_r = 001 (the mesh dominates: the steep profile near the heated end), and 030–041h^2 and 014–032h^2 at t = 005 and 02, where the time error, made of more steps on finer meshes, adds a part that grows slowly with n_x. The tolerances, 3h^2, 08h^2 and 06h^2, hold for those four meshes with a margin of at least 1.5. At t = 15 the series has decayed to 10^-6Delta T, and the linear profile is represented exactly by the linear elements.\n\nfor (t, c) in zip(t_check, (3.0, 0.8, 0.6))\n  @assert checks[t].err < c * h^2 \"temperature at t = $t away from the series solution (error $(checks[t].err))\"\nend\n@assert checks[t_end].err < 1e-5 \"temperature at t = $t_end away from the steady linear profile\"\n\n(b) The mechanics. The change of length matches the integral of the thermal strain at every step, and at steady state the linear estimate alpha_thDelta T L2, with the bar free of stress: the stress is 10^-5 of the thermal stress scale Ealpha_thDelta T against 10^-3–4cdot10^-2 during the transient. The remaining differences (2cdot10^-3 relative) are the second-order terms of the finite-strain model, of relative size alpha_thDelta T = 10^-3: with Delta T = 1 they drop tenfold (measured), so they are not a discretisation error.\n\nrel_recip = maximum(abs.(Δℓ .- Δℓ_th) ./ Δℓ_th)\nrel_steady = abs(Δℓ[end] / (αth * ΔT * L / 2) - 1)\nσ_steady = checks[t_end].σ / (E * αth * ΔT)\nprintln(@sprintf(\"reciprocal theorem: max relative difference %.2e; steady state: Δℓ/(αth ΔT L/2) - 1 = %.2e, ‖P‖/(E αth ΔT) = %.2e\",\n                 rel_recip, rel_steady, σ_steady))\n@assert rel_recip < 5 * αth * ΔT \"change of length away from the integral of the thermal strain ($rel_recip)\"\n@assert rel_steady < 5 * αth * ΔT \"steady change of length away from αth ΔT L/2 ($rel_steady)\"\n@assert σ_steady < 1e-4 \"the bar is not free of stress at steady state ($σ_steady)\"\n@assert checks[t_check[1]].σ / (E * αth * ΔT) > 1e-2 \"no thermal stress during the transient\"\n\n(c) Regression values (HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6, Gridap 0.20.8, Julia 1.13.0, macOS arm64).\n\nprintln(@sprintf(\"regression: err(0.05) = %.10e, σ(0.05) = %.10e, Δℓ(0.2) = %.10e, Δℓ(end) = %.10e\",\n                 checks[0.05].err, checks[0.05].σ, checks[0.2].Δℓ, Δℓ[end]))\nreference = FAST ? (err=1.1898517676e-03, σ=1.9909595440e-05, Δℓ02=4.4218746895e-04, Δℓend=5.0102569875e-04) :\n                   (err=3.1409089772e-04, σ=1.9031434554e-05, Δℓ02=4.4183785276e-04, Δℓend=5.0102534280e-04)\n@assert isapprox(checks[0.05].err, reference.err; rtol=1e-5) \"regression: temperature error at t = 0.05\"\n@assert isapprox(checks[0.05].σ, reference.σ; rtol=1e-5) \"regression: stress at t = 0.05\"\n@assert isapprox(checks[0.2].Δℓ, reference.Δℓ02; rtol=1e-5) \"regression: change of length at t = 0.2\"\n@assert isapprox(Δℓ[end], reference.Δℓend; rtol=1e-5) \"regression: final change of length\"","title":"Checks"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E17_ThermoMechanics/#Going-further","page":"17 · Thermo-mechanics: transient heat conduction and thermal expansion","text":"The thermo-elastic heating: the energy balance thetadoteta + nabla_0cdotboldsymbol Q = 0 with eta = -partial_varthetaPsi adds -thetapartial^2_boldsymbol FvarthetaPsidotboldsymbol F to the heat equation; it needs the general level of TransientModel (∂t(ctx, u) on the displacement).\nEntropicElasticityLaw(θr=θr, γ=0.0) makes the shear stiffness proportional to theta, as in rubber; ThermoMech_Bonet and ThermoMech_EntropicPolyconvex are the other thermo-mechanical models.\nClamp the end x = 0 (all components): the bar can no longer expand freely, and the thermal stress appears.","title":"Going further"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E04_ElectroMechanics/#Tutorial-04:-Electro-mechanics-of-a-dielectric-elastomer","page":"04 · Electro-mechanics of a dielectric elastomer","text":"Source · Notebook · nbviewer\n\nA dielectric elastomer plate is clamped at one end and carries two compliant electrodes, one at mid-thickness and one on the top face. A voltage between them squeezes the upper layer across its thickness; being nearly incompressible, the layer elongates, while the lower layer (without field) does not: the plate bends like a bimorph. Mechanics and electrostatics are fully coupled and solved together (monolithic scheme), with the weak form written term by term.\n\nWhat you will learn\n\nelectro-mechanical constitutive models (ElectroMechModel, IdealDielectric) and their derivatives;\nmulti-field spaces (displacement and electric potential) and their conditions (MultiFieldBC);\nthe coupled weak form and its consistent linearisation written by hand with ⊙, ⋅ and ⋅²;\nthe same forms from HyperFEM's helpers residual and jacobian, and how to check that they agree.\n\nMeasured on a Mac M5 Max (Julia 1.13.0, 4 threads, 4 BLAS threads; HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6, Gridap 0.20.8; compiled backend), whole run including compilation: FAST 53 s, full 54 s (20 increments, 113 Newton iterations, 14 s in solve! including its compilation); 1 528 free dofs. One assembly of the tangent: 0.7 ms compiled, 11 ms with Gridap's lazy evaluation.","title":"Tutorial 04: Electro-mechanics of a dielectric elastomer"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E04_ElectroMechanics/#Problem-statement","page":"04 · Electro-mechanics of a dielectric elastomer","text":"The body Omega is described in its reference configuration by the displacement boldsymbol u and the electric potential varphi. With the deformation gradient boldsymbol F = boldsymbol I + nabla_0 boldsymbol u and the nominal electric field boldsymbol E_0 = -nabla_0 varphi, the material is described by a free energy Psi(boldsymbol F boldsymbol E_0): the first Piola stress is boldsymbol P = partial_boldsymbol FPsi and the nominal electric displacement boldsymbol D_0 = -partial_boldsymbol E_0Psi. Equilibrium and Gauss's law, mathrmDivboldsymbol P = boldsymbol 0 and mathrmDivboldsymbol D_0 = 0, in weak form: find (boldsymbol u varphi) such that for all (boldsymbol v deltavarphi)\n\nR = int_Omega nabla_0 boldsymbol v  partial_boldsymbol FPsi mathrm dV\n  - int_Omega nabla_0 deltavarphi cdot partial_boldsymbol E_0Psi mathrm dV = 0 \n\nIts directional derivative along (Deltaboldsymbol u Deltavarphi) is the tangent used by Newton's method,\n\nbeginaligned\nK =   int_Omega nabla_0 boldsymbol v  partial^2_boldsymbol Fboldsymbol FPsi  nabla_0 Deltaboldsymbol u mathrm dV\n  + int_Omega nabla_0 deltavarphi cdot partial^2_boldsymbol E_0boldsymbol E_0Psi  nabla_0 Deltavarphi mathrm dV \n   - int_Omega nabla_0 Deltavarphi cdot partial^2_boldsymbol E_0boldsymbol FPsi  nabla_0 boldsymbol v mathrm dV\n  - int_Omega nabla_0 deltavarphi cdot partial^2_boldsymbol E_0boldsymbol FPsi  nabla_0 Deltaboldsymbol u mathrm dV \nendaligned\n\nThe free energy is the sum of a compressible neo-Hookean energy (tutorial 02) and the energy of an ideal dielectric, written with the cofactor boldsymbol H = Jboldsymbol F^-T and J = detboldsymbol F:\n\nPsi = tfracmu2(boldsymbol Fboldsymbol F - 3) - mu ln J + tfraclambda2(J-1)^2\n     - fracvarepsilon2Jboldsymbol H boldsymbol E_0^2 \n\nwith mu = 1, lambda = 10 and varepsilon = 1 in non-dimensional units (stresses in units of mu, fields in units of sqrtmuvarepsilon). The plate is 8 times 08 times 40; the face z = 0 is clamped, varphi = 0 on the mid-plane y = 04 and varphi = Lambda V on the top face y = 08, with the load factor Lambda going from 0 to 1. The rest of the boundary is free of tractions and of charge.","title":"Problem statement"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E04_ElectroMechanics/#Setup","page":"04 · Electro-mechanics of a dielectric elastomer","text":"using HyperFEM\nusing Gridap\nusing HyperFEMKernels                                          # compiled backend (`backend=:compiled`)\nusing LinearAlgebra: norm\n\nconst FAST = get(ENV, \"HYPERFEM_TUTORIALS_FAST\", \"0\") == \"1\"   # reduced case for the test suite\noutdir = joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"data\", \"sims\", \"E04_ElectroMechanics\")\nsetupfolder(outdir)                                            # VTK output (not versioned)","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E04_ElectroMechanics/#Geometry-and-mesh","page":"04 · Electro-mechanics of a dielectric elastomer","text":"A Gmsh mesh of 266 trilinear hexahedra (7 across the width, 19 along the length, one per layer across the thickness) with the physical groups fixedup (clamped face), midsuf (mid-plane electrode) and topsuf (top electrode). We add a tag for the free end z = 40 from the coordinates of its vertices, to measure the deflection.\n\nmodel = GmshDiscreteModel(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"data\", \"models\", \"test_static_EM.msh\"))\nlabels = get_face_labeling(model)\nadd_tag_from_vertex_filter!(labels, \"tip\", model, x -> abs(x[3] - 40.0) < 1e-8)","title":"Geometry and mesh"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E04_ElectroMechanics/#Constitutive-model","page":"04 · Electro-mechanics of a dielectric elastomer","text":"Calling an electro-mechanical model returns the energy and its derivatives as functions of (boldsymbol F boldsymbol E_0): (Psi partial_boldsymbol FPsi partial_boldsymbol E_0Psi partial^2_boldsymbol Fboldsymbol FPsi partial^2_boldsymbol E_0boldsymbol FPsi partial^2_boldsymbol E_0boldsymbol E_0Psi). The kinematic objects give boldsymbol F(nabla_0boldsymbol u) and boldsymbol E_0(nabla_0varphi).\n\nphysmodel = ElectroMechModel(mechano=NeoHookean3D(λ=10.0, μ=1.0), electro=IdealDielectric(ε=1.0))\nΨ, ∂ΨF, ∂ΨE, ∂ΨFF, ∂ΨEF, ∂ΨEE = physmodel()\nkm = Kinematics(Mechano, Solid)\nke = Kinematics(Electro, Solid)\nF, _, _ = get_Kinematics(km)\nE = get_Kinematics(ke)","title":"Constitutive model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E04_ElectroMechanics/#Boundary-conditions","page":"04 · Electro-mechanics of a dielectric elastomer","text":"One set of conditions per field, gathered by MultiFieldBC. The clamp is constant; the two electrodes follow the load factor (varphi = 0 on midsuf for every Lambda, varphi = Lambda V on topsuf).\n\nV0 = FAST ? 0.1 : 0.3                                          # voltage between the electrodes\nnsteps = FAST ? 5 : 20\nDu = BoundaryConditions(BoundaryCondition(\"fixedup\", [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]))\nDφ = BoundaryConditions(BoundaryCondition(\"midsuf\", 0.0, Λ -> Λ), BoundaryCondition(\"topsuf\", V0, Λ -> Λ))\nD_bc = MultiFieldBC([Du, Dφ])","title":"Boundary conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E04_ElectroMechanics/#Finite-element-spaces","page":"04 · Electro-mechanics of a dielectric elastomer","text":"Trilinear elements for both fields. The multi-field spaces stack the two fields; a function of U is the pair (boldsymbol u varphi).\n\norder = 1\nreffeu = ReferenceFE(lagrangian, VectorValue{3,Float64}, order)\nreffeφ = ReferenceFE(lagrangian, Float64, order)\nVu = TestFESpace(model, reffeu, D_bc[1], conformity=:H1)\nVφ = TestFESpace(model, reffeφ, D_bc[2], conformity=:H1)\nUu = TrialFESpace(Vu, D_bc[1], 0.0)\nUφ = TrialFESpace(Vφ, D_bc[2], 0.0)\nV = MultiFieldFESpace([Vu, Vφ])\nU = MultiFieldFESpace([Uu, Uφ])\nΩ = Triangulation(model)\ndΩ = Measure(Ω, 2 * order)\nprintln(\"free dofs: \", num_free_dofs(U), \" (u: \", num_free_dofs(Uu), \", φ: \", num_free_dofs(Uφ), \")\")","title":"Finite element spaces"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E04_ElectroMechanics/#Weak-form","page":"04 · Electro-mechanics of a dielectric elastomer","text":"The residual and the tangent above, term by term. The constitutive functions are composed (∘) with the kinematics evaluated at the trial functions. ⊙ contracts two second-order tensors (boldsymbol Aboldsymbol B), ⋅ a vector with a vector or a second-order tensor, and ⋅² the last two indices of a higher-order tensor with a second-order one: (mathbb C cdot^2 boldsymbol A)_ij = C_ijklA_kl and (mathcal E cdot^2 boldsymbol A)_i = mathcal E_iklA_kl.\n\nres(Λ) = ((u, φ), (v, vφ)) ->\n  ∫(∇(v)' ⊙ (∂ΨF ∘ (F ∘ ∇(u)', E ∘ ∇(φ))))dΩ -\n  ∫(∇(vφ) ⋅ (∂ΨE ∘ (F ∘ ∇(u)', E ∘ ∇(φ))))dΩ\n\njac(Λ) = ((u, φ), (du, dφ), (v, vφ)) ->\n  ∫(∇(v)' ⊙ ((∂ΨFF ∘ (F ∘ ∇(u)', E ∘ ∇(φ))) ⋅² ∇(du)'))dΩ +\n  ∫(∇(vφ) ⋅ ((∂ΨEE ∘ (F ∘ ∇(u)', E ∘ ∇(φ))) ⋅ ∇(dφ)))dΩ -\n  ∫(∇(dφ) ⋅ ((∂ΨEF ∘ (F ∘ ∇(u)', E ∘ ∇(φ))) ⋅² ∇(v)'))dΩ -\n  ∫(∇(vφ) ⋅ ((∂ΨEF ∘ (F ∘ ∇(u)', E ∘ ∇(φ))) ⋅² ∇(du)'))dΩ\n\nHyperFEM builds exactly these forms from the model and the pair of kinematics: in later tutorials we use the helpers. We check below that both give the same vectors and matrices.\n\nk = (km, ke)\nres_helper(Λ) = (x, y) -> residual(physmodel, k, x, y, dΩ)\njac_helper(Λ) = (x, dx, y) -> jacobian(physmodel, k, x, dx, y, dΩ)","title":"Weak form"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E04_ElectroMechanics/#Solver-and-computational-model","page":"04 · Electro-mechanics of a dielectric elastomer","text":"backend=:compiled (tutorial 01): the hand-written forms are compiled once into threaded kernels.\n\nnls = NewtonRaphson(LUSolver(); maxiter=20, atol=1e-10, rtol=1e-8, verbose=true)\ncomp_model = StaticNonlinearModel(res, jac, U, V, D_bc; nls=nls, backend=:compiled)\nxh = get_state(comp_model)                                     # (u, φ) of the current state\nuh, φh = xh","title":"Solver and computational model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E04_ElectroMechanics/#Post-processing","page":"04 · Electro-mechanics of a dielectric elastomer","text":"After every converged increment: the voltage, the mean deflection u_y of the free end, and the VTK files.\n\nΓtip = BoundaryTriangulation(model, tags=\"tip\")\ndΓtip = Measure(Γtip, 2 * order)\nAtip = sum(∫(1.0)dΓtip)\ney = VectorValue(0.0, 1.0, 0.0)\nvoltage, tip_uy = Float64[], Float64[]\nfunction record(post)\n  Λ = post.Λ[post.iter]\n  push!(voltage, Λ * V0)\n  push!(tip_uy, sum(∫(uh ⋅ ey)dΓtip) / Atip)\n  pvd = get_pvd(post)\n  pvd[Λ] = createvtk(Ω, joinpath(outdir, \"step_$(post.iter).vtu\"), cellfields=[\"u\" => uh, \"φ\" => φh])\nend\npost = PostProcessor(comp_model, record; is_vtk=true, filepath=outdir)","title":"Post-processing"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E04_ElectroMechanics/#Solve","page":"04 · Electro-mechanics of a dielectric elastomer","text":"The potential grows with Lambda; ProjectDirichlet=true (tutorial 02) starts each increment from a linear prediction of the effect of the new electrode voltage on both fields.\n\nx, flag = solve!(comp_model; stepping=LoadStepping(nsteps=nsteps, maxbisec=5), ProjectDirichlet=true, post=post)\n\nfor (vo, w) in zip(voltage, tip_uy)\n  println(\"V = $(round(vo, digits=4))   mean tip deflection u_y = $(round(w, sigdigits=8))\")\nend","title":"Solve"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E04_ElectroMechanics/#Checks","page":"04 · Electro-mechanics of a dielectric elastomer","text":"Hand-written forms against the helpers. At a state away from equilibrium (the solution scaled by 1.1), the residual vectors and the tangent matrices of the two formulations are the same up to round-off.\n\nxt = FEFunction(U, 1.1 .* get_free_dof_values(xh))\nr1 = assemble_vector(y -> res(1.0)(xt, y), V)\nr2 = assemble_vector(y -> res_helper(1.0)(xt, y), V)\nK1 = assemble_matrix((dx, y) -> jac(1.0)(xt, dx, y), U, V)\nK2 = assemble_matrix((dx, y) -> jac_helper(1.0)(xt, dx, y), U, V)\nerr_r = norm(r1 - r2) / norm(r1)\nerr_K = norm(K1 - K2) / norm(K1)\nprintln(\"hand-written vs helper: residual $(err_r), tangent $(err_K)\")\n@assert err_r < 1e-12 \"hand-written residual differs from residual(physmodel, ...)\"\n@assert err_K < 1e-12 \"hand-written tangent differs from jacobian(physmodel, ...)\"\n\nPhysics. The load stepping reached the full voltage. The plate bends towards -y, away from the active layer, by an amount that grows with the voltage; at small voltages the electrostatic stresses, and with them the deflection, grow as V^2: doubling the voltage of the first increment multiplies the deflection by 4. Geometry, material and loads are symmetric with respect to the plane x = 4, and so is the solution: the mean of u_x vanishes.\n\nVol = sum(∫(1.0)dΩ)\nux_mean = sum(∫(uh ⋅ VectorValue(1.0, 0.0, 0.0))dΩ) / Vol\nratio = tip_uy[2] / tip_uy[1]\nprintln(\"tip u_y = $(tip_uy[end])   u_y(2ΔV)/u_y(ΔV) = $(ratio)   mean u_x = $(ux_mean)\")\n@assert flag == 1 \"the load stepping did not reach Λ = 1\"\n@assert all(diff(tip_uy) .< 0) && tip_uy[end] < 0 \"the plate does not bend towards -y\"\n@assert abs(ratio - 4) < 0.05 \"the deflection does not grow as V² at small voltages (ratio $ratio)\"\n@assert abs(ux_mean) < 1e-8 * abs(tip_uy[end]) \"the solution is not symmetric about x = 4\"\n\nRegression (HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6, Gridap 0.20.8, Julia 1.13.0, macOS arm64): norm of the final displacement (free values) and mean deflection of the free end.\n\nnu = norm(get_free_dof_values(uh))\nprintln(\"‖u‖ = $(nu)\")\nnu_ref, tip_ref = FAST ? (45.90961930695172, -4.4581346161107) : (394.05924551948493, -31.60568504003123)\n@assert isapprox(nu, nu_ref; rtol=1e-5) \"‖u‖ = $nu differs from the reference $nu_ref\"\n@assert isapprox(tip_uy[end], tip_ref; rtol=1e-5) \"tip deflection $(tip_uy[end]) differs from the reference $tip_ref\"","title":"Checks"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E04_ElectroMechanics/#Going-further","page":"04 · Electro-mechanics of a dielectric elastomer","text":"Raise the voltage: the stiffness of the plate drops as the field grows and, past a critical voltage, the Newton iterations fail (electro-mechanical instability). In HyperFEM 0.3.0 such a failure can end the run with an uncaught SingularException of the LU factorisation instead of a bisection of the increment. HessianRegularization(physmodel) and the hybrid Newton of tutorial 06 are the tools to go beyond it.\nSolve the two fields one after the other instead of together: StaggeredModel (tutorial 09).\nTutorial 05 adds a fibre family to the same plate; tutorial 13 replaces LUSolver by block-preconditioned Krylov solvers (AMGSolver(U), PETScSolver(U)) for this kind of multi-field problem.","title":"Going further"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E01_Poisson/#Tutorial-01:-Poisson-equation","page":"01 · Poisson equation","text":"Source · Notebook · nbviewer\n\nThe Poisson equation is the simplest elliptic problem and the place to meet the pieces that every HyperFEM simulation shares: a mesh, boundary conditions, finite element spaces, a weak form and a computational model that assembles and solves it. The source term is manufactured from a known solution, so the error can be measured and the order of convergence of the method checked.\n\nWhat you will learn\n\nDirichlet data given by a function of the coordinates (BoundaryConditions, BoundaryCondition);\nfinite element spaces built from the conditions (TestFESpace, TrialFESpace);\na linear problem as a StaticLinearModel, its solution with solve! and the solution field (get_state);\na second right-hand side solved with the same factorised matrix (solve!(model, b; Assembly=false));\nmeasuring the L^2 error and the order of convergence.\n\nMeasured on a Mac M5 Max (Julia 1.13.0, 4 threads, 4 BLAS threads; HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6, Gridap 0.20.8; compiled backend), whole run including compilation: full case (meshes 50×20 and 100×40, 3 900 free dofs on the finest) 26 s, FAST case (20×8 and 40×16) 27 s; almost all of it is compilation (each linear solve takes milliseconds).","title":"Tutorial 01: Poisson equation"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E01_Poisson/#Problem-statement","page":"01 · Poisson equation","text":"Find u in the rectangle Omega = (-pipi)times(-pi2pi2) such that\n\n-Delta u = f  textin  Omega qquad u = u_0  texton  Gamma_D qquad\nnabla ucdotboldsymbol n = g  texton  Gamma_N \n\nwhere Gamma_N is the right side x = pi and Gamma_D the other three. The data come from the manufactured solution u_0(xy) = cos x sin(y+pi): f = -Delta u_0 = 2u_0 and g = nabla u_0cdotboldsymbol n. The weak form reads: find u with u = u_0 on Gamma_D such that\n\na(uv) = int_Omega nabla ucdotnabla v mathrm dOmega\n= int_Omega fv mathrm dOmega + int_Gamma_N gv mathrm dGamma = l(v)\nqquad forall v v = 0 text on  Gamma_D \n\nWith bilinear elements (Q1) and a smooth solution, the L^2 error decreases as h^2.","title":"Problem statement"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E01_Poisson/#Setup","page":"01 · Poisson equation","text":"using HyperFEM\nusing Gridap\nusing HyperFEMKernels                                          # compiled backend (`backend=:compiled`)\n\nconst FAST = get(ENV, \"HYPERFEM_TUTORIALS_FAST\", \"0\") == \"1\"   # reduced case for the test suite\noutdir = joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"data\", \"sims\", \"E01_Poisson\")\nsetupfolder(outdir)                                            # VTK output (not versioned)","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E01_Poisson/#Exact-solution-and-data","page":"01 · Poisson equation","text":"The gradient and the Laplacian are written by hand. VectorValue is Gridap's small vector type.\n\nu₀(x) = cos(x[1]) * sin(x[2] + π)\n∇u₀(x) = VectorValue(-sin(x[1]) * sin(x[2] + π), cos(x[1]) * cos(x[2] + π))\nf(x) = 2 * u₀(x)                                               # f = -Δu₀","title":"Exact solution and data"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E01_Poisson/#Geometry-and-mesh","page":"01 · Poisson equation","text":"A Cartesian mesh of bilinear quadrilaterals. In 2D, Gridap names the four vertices tag_1…tag_4 and the sides tag_5 (y = -pi2), tag_6 (y = pi2), tag_7 (x = -pi) and tag_8 (x = pi). The Dirichlet tag takes the vertices too, the Neumann tag only the open side.\n\ndomain = (-π, π, -π / 2, π / 2)\npartitions = FAST ? [(20, 8), (40, 16)] : [(50, 20), (100, 40)]\nmodel = CartesianDiscreteModel(domain, partitions[1])\nlabels = get_face_labeling(model)\nadd_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"dirichlet\", [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7])\nadd_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"neumann\", [8])","title":"Geometry and mesh"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E01_Poisson/#Boundary-conditions","page":"01 · Poisson equation","text":"A BoundaryCondition is a tag and a value: a number, a vector or, as here, a function of the coordinates. BoundaryConditions groups the conditions of a problem.\n\nD_bc = BoundaryConditions(BoundaryCondition(\"dirichlet\", u₀))","title":"Boundary conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E01_Poisson/#Finite-element-spaces","page":"01 · Poisson equation","text":"The test space vanishes on the Dirichlet tags; the trial space takes the values of the conditions.\n\norder = 1\nreffe = ReferenceFE(lagrangian, Float64, order)\nΩ = Triangulation(model)\ndΩ = Measure(Ω, 2 * order)\nΓ = BoundaryTriangulation(model, tags=\"neumann\")\ndΓ = Measure(Γ, 2 * order)\nnΓ = get_normal_vector(Γ)                                       # outward unit normal on Γ_N\nV = TestFESpace(Ω, reffe, D_bc, conformity=:H1)\nU = TrialFESpace(V, D_bc)\nprintln(\"free dofs: \", num_free_dofs(V))","title":"Finite element spaces"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E01_Poisson/#Weak-form","page":"01 · Poisson equation","text":"Plain Gridap syntax: a and l are Julia functions that return integrals. A function of the coordinates (here f and ∇u₀) can be combined with fields such as the normal nΓ. For this u_0 the flux g on x = pi happens to vanish; the second load case below has a non-zero one.\n\na(u, v) = ∫(∇(u) ⋅ ∇(v))dΩ\nl(v) = ∫(f * v)dΩ + ∫((∇u₀ ⋅ nΓ) * v)dΓ","title":"Weak form"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E01_Poisson/#Computational-model-and-solution","page":"01 · Poisson equation","text":"StaticLinearModel(l, a, U, V, D_bc) assembles the matrix and the right-hand side (with the Dirichlet values moved to it) and factorises the matrix with the default LUSolver(). solve! returns the vector of free values; get_state wraps it as a finite element function. Assembly=false uses the system assembled by the constructor instead of assembling it again. backend=:compiled assembles with the compiled, threaded kernels of HyperFEMKernels (loaded above) instead of Gridap's lazy cell-by-cell evaluation: the weak form is turned into Julia code once, when the model is built. The result is the same. On a mesh this small the gain is invisible, but every tutorial uses it, because it grows with the mesh and matters most in nonlinear problems, which assemble at every Newton iteration.\n\ncomp_model = StaticLinearModel(l, a, U, V, D_bc; backend=:compiled)\nsolve!(comp_model; Assembly=false)\nuh = get_state(comp_model)","title":"Computational model and solution"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E01_Poisson/#Error","page":"01 · Poisson equation","text":"The error is integrated with a higher quadrature degree than the one of the weak form.\n\ndΩe = Measure(Ω, 4 * order)\nl2(e) = sqrt(sum(∫(e * e)dΩe))\nerror_0 = l2(uh - u₀)\nprintln(\"L2 error, u₀: \", error_0, \"   (relative to ‖u₀‖: \", error_0 / l2(CellField(u₀, Ω)), \")\")\nwritevtk(Ω, joinpath(outdir, \"poisson_u0\"), cellfields=[\"uh\" => uh, \"error\" => uh - u₀])","title":"Error"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E01_Poisson/#A-second-right-hand-side-with-the-same-factorisation","page":"01 · Poisson equation","text":"A new load case on the same mesh, with the same Dirichlet data: u_1 = u_0 + w with w = (x+pi)cos y, which vanishes on Gamma_D. Its data are f_1 = f - Delta w = f + (x+pi)cos y and g_1 = nabla u_1cdotboldsymbol n (now cos y on x = pi).\n\nu₁(x) = u₀(x) + (x[1] + π) * cos(x[2])\n∇u₁(x) = ∇u₀(x) + VectorValue(cos(x[2]), -(x[1] + π) * sin(x[2]))\nf₁(x) = f(x) + (x[1] + π) * cos(x[2])\nl₁(v) = ∫(f₁ * v)dΩ + ∫((∇u₁ ⋅ nΓ) * v)dΓ\n\nThe matrix does not change, so only the right-hand side is assembled. It must include the Dirichlet lift, b_1 = l_1(v) - a(u_D v), where u_D = zero(U) is the function of the trial space with zero free values (only its Dirichlet values are non-zero). solve!(model, b; Assembly=false) then solves with the matrix already factorised: no assembly and no new factorisation.\n\nuD = zero(U)\nb₁ = assemble_vector(v -> l₁(v) - a(uD, v), V)\nx₁ = solve!(comp_model, b₁; Assembly=false)\nuh₁ = get_state(comp_model)\n\nget_state wraps the model's own vector of free values, which solve! overwrites: uh above now holds the second solution too (its error and VTK file were taken before). To keep a solution, copy its values: FEFunction(U, copy(get_free_dof_values(uh))).\n\nerror_1 = l2(uh₁ - u₁)\nprintln(\"L2 error, u₁: \", error_1, \"   (relative to ‖u₁‖: \", error_1 / l2(CellField(u₁, Ω)), \")\")\n\nThe same problem assembled and solved from scratch with Gridap's AffineFEOperator, for comparison:\n\nx₁_ref = get_free_dof_values(solve(LUSolver(), AffineFEOperator(a, l₁, U, V)))\nwritevtk(Ω, joinpath(outdir, \"poisson_u1\"), cellfields=[\"uh\" => uh₁, \"error\" => uh₁ - u₁])","title":"A second right-hand side with the same factorisation"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E01_Poisson/#Convergence","page":"01 · Poisson equation","text":"The same steps on a mesh twice as fine in each direction. With h halved, the observed order is log_2(e_h  e_h2).\n\nfunction l2_error_on(partition)\n  model = CartesianDiscreteModel(domain, partition)\n  labels = get_face_labeling(model)\n  add_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"dirichlet\", [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7])\n  add_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"neumann\", [8])\n  Ω = Triangulation(model)\n  dΩ = Measure(Ω, 2 * order)\n  Γ = BoundaryTriangulation(model, tags=\"neumann\")\n  dΓ = Measure(Γ, 2 * order)\n  nΓ = get_normal_vector(Γ)\n  V = TestFESpace(Ω, reffe, D_bc, conformity=:H1)\n  U = TrialFESpace(V, D_bc)\n  a(u, v) = ∫(∇(u) ⋅ ∇(v))dΩ\n  l(v) = ∫(f * v)dΩ + ∫((∇u₀ ⋅ nΓ) * v)dΓ\n  m = StaticLinearModel(l, a, U, V, D_bc; backend=:compiled)\n  solve!(m; Assembly=false)\n  e = get_state(m) - u₀\n  sqrt(sum(∫(e * e)Measure(Ω, 4 * order))), num_free_dofs(V)\nend\nerror_fine, ndofs_fine = l2_error_on(partitions[2])\nrate = log2(error_0 / error_fine)\nprintln(\"L2 error on $(partitions[2]) ($ndofs_fine free dofs): \", error_fine, \"   observed order: \", rate)","title":"Convergence"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E01_Poisson/#Checks","page":"01 · Poisson equation","text":"The order of convergence is the one of Q1 elements and the errors are of the size expected on these meshes. The second load case, solved with the factorisation of the first, is the solution of a fresh assembly to round-off.\n\n@assert abs(rate - 2) < 0.2 \"observed order $rate, expected 2\"\n@assert error_0 < (FAST ? 5e-2 : 1e-2) \"L2 error too large: $error_0\"\n@assert error_1 / l2(CellField(u₁, Ω)) < (FAST ? 2e-2 : 4e-3) \"L2 error of the second load case too large: $error_1\"\n@assert maximum(abs.(x₁ .- x₁_ref)) < 1e-10 * maximum(abs.(x₁_ref)) \"re-used factorisation ≠ fresh solve\"","title":"Checks"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E01_Poisson/#Going-further","page":"01 · Poisson equation","text":"Use order = 2 (biquadratic elements): the observed order becomes 3.\nSolve several load cases at once: StaticLinearModel([l, l₁], a, U, V, D_bc) keeps one matrix for a vector of linear forms (the load vectors are assembled without the Dirichlet lift).\nTutorial 02 moves to nonlinear elasticity: the same spaces and conditions, with a Newton solver.","title":"Going further"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E15_Elastodynamics/#Tutorial-15:-Elastodynamics:-free-vibration-of-a-cantilever","page":"15 · Elastodynamics: free vibration of a cantilever","text":"Source · Notebook · nbviewer\n\nA slender neo-Hookean cantilever is set in motion with an initial velocity that has the shape of its first bending mode, and vibrates freely. With a small amplitude the motion is a single harmonic whose period is given by beam theory, and the time schemes can be told apart by what they do with the energy: the trapezoidal rule and the midpoint rule keep it, the generalised-α scheme removes it in a controlled way. With a large amplitude the response is geometrically nonlinear, and an adaptive time step follows it.\n\nMeasured on a Mac (M5 Max, 4 Julia threads, 4 BLAS threads, Julia 1.13.0, HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6, Gridap 0.20.8): FAST (540 dofs, four runs of 50–100 steps) 38 s, full (3 000 dofs, 100 steps per small-amplitude run) 51 s, including compilation; after the first run, 7 ms (FAST) and 30 ms (full) per time step with two Newton iterations.\n\nWhat you will learn\n\nTransientModel at its structured level: the static forms of StaticNonlinearModel plus a mass form, an initial velocity vh and a consistent initial acceleration (initial_acceleration=:solve);\nthe second-order schemes Newmark(), Midpoint() and GeneralizedAlpha(ρ∞), and what each does with the energy;\nfixed steps with TimeStepping and error-controlled steps with AdaptiveStepping;\nthe energies of the run: energy=, get_energies, get_velocity, get_previous_state;\nthe compiled backend in a time loop, and how to check which path its kernels take.","title":"Tutorial 15: Elastodynamics: free vibration of a cantilever"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E15_Elastodynamics/#Problem-statement","page":"15 · Elastodynamics: free vibration of a cantilever","text":"Find the displacement boldsymbol u(boldsymbol X t) of the beam Omega = (0L)times(0W)times(0H) such that\n\nint_Omega rhoddotboldsymbol ucdotboldsymbol v mathrm dV\n+ int_Omega boldsymbol P(boldsymbol F)  nabla_0 boldsymbol v mathrm dV = 0\nquad forall boldsymbol v  qquad boldsymbol F = boldsymbol I + nabla_0 boldsymbol u \n\nwith the compressible neo-Hookean energy of tutorial 02, Psi = tfracmu2(boldsymbol Fboldsymbol F - 3) - mu ln J + tfraclambda2(J-1)^2, the face x = 0 clamped and the rest free of traction. The beam starts undeformed, boldsymbol u(cdot 0) = boldsymbol 0, with the velocity dotboldsymbol u(boldsymbol X 0) = V_0phi_1(X)phi_1(L)boldsymbol e_z, where\n\nphi_1(x) = coshbeta_1 x - cosbeta_1 x - sigma_1(sinhbeta_1 x - sinbeta_1 x)  qquad\nsigma_1 = fraccoshbeta_1 L + cosbeta_1 Lsinhbeta_1 L + sinbeta_1 L  qquad beta_1 L = 18751ldots\n\nis the first bending mode of an Euler–Bernoulli cantilever. Its angular frequency is\n\nomega_1 = (beta_1 L)^2 sqrtfracE Irho A L^4  qquad I = fracW H^312  quad A = W H \nquad E = fracmu(3lambda + 2mu)lambda + mu \n\nso with a small V_0 the tip moves as u_z(L t) approx (V_0omega_1)sinomega_1 t and the period is T_1 = 2piomega_1. The total energy K + Pi, with K = tfrac12int_Omegarhodotboldsymbol u cdotdotboldsymbol umathrm dV and Pi = int_OmegaPsimathrm dV, is constant in time.\n\nThe data are dimensionless (any consistent set of units): L = 10, W = H = 1, mu = 1, lambda = 15 (nu = 03, E = 26), rho = 1.","title":"Problem statement"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E15_Elastodynamics/#Setup","page":"15 · Elastodynamics: free vibration of a cantilever","text":"using HyperFEM\nusing HyperFEMKernels                                          # compiled backend (`backend=:compiled`)\nusing Gridap\nusing Printf\n\nconst FAST = get(ENV, \"HYPERFEM_TUTORIALS_FAST\", \"0\") == \"1\"   # reduced case for the test suite\noutdir = joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"data\", \"sims\", \"E15_Elastodynamics\")\nsetupfolder(outdir)                                            # VTK output (not versioned)","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E15_Elastodynamics/#Geometry-and-mesh","page":"15 · Elastodynamics: free vibration of a cantilever","text":"A Cartesian mesh of hexahedra, ten times longer than thick. The clamped face includes its edges and vertices (⁺); the tip face is used to measure the deflection.\n\nconst L, W, H = 10.0, 1.0, 1.0\npartition = FAST ? (10, 1, 1) : (20, 2, 2)\ngeometry = CartesianDiscreteModel((0, L, 0, W, 0, H), partition)\nlabels = get_face_labeling(geometry)\nadd_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"clamped\", CartesianTags.face0YZ⁺)\nadd_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"tip\", CartesianTags.face1YZ)","title":"Geometry and mesh"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E15_Elastodynamics/#Constitutive-model","page":"15 · Elastodynamics: free vibration of a cantilever","text":"const λ, μ, ρ = 1.5, 1.0, 1.0\nphysmodel = NeoHookean3D(λ=λ, μ=μ)\nkinematics = Kinematics(Mechano, Solid)\n\nBeam theory: the first bending frequency and the mode shape. The mode shape is evaluated at the nodes (it becomes the initial velocity), so it reads only const globals and its argument.\n\nconst E = μ * (3λ + 2μ) / (λ + μ)\nconst β₁L = 1.8751040687119611\nconst ω₁ = β₁L^2 * sqrt(E * (W * H^3 / 12) / (ρ * W * H * L^4))\nconst T₁ = 2π / ω₁\nconst σ₁ = (cosh(β₁L) + cos(β₁L)) / (sinh(β₁L) + sin(β₁L))\nφ₁(x) = (b = β₁L * x / L; cosh(b) - cos(b) - σ₁ * (sinh(b) - sin(b)))\nmode_velocity(V0) = X -> VectorValue(0.0, 0.0, V0 * φ₁(X[1]) / φ₁(L))   # closure: V0 is captured\nprintln(@sprintf(\"Euler–Bernoulli: ω₁ = %.6e, T₁ = %.4f\", ω₁, T₁))","title":"Constitutive model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E15_Elastodynamics/#Boundary-conditions,-finite-element-spaces-and-forms","page":"15 · Elastodynamics: free vibration of a cantilever","text":"Quadratic hexahedra (Q2): trilinear ones lock in bending and give a beam far too stiff. The static forms are those of tutorial 02; the inertia comes from the bilinear mass form, which the model assembles once into a mass matrix. energy(t) is the stored energy Pi: the model reports it with the kinetic energy.\n\nbc = BoundaryConditions(BoundaryCondition(\"clamped\", [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]))\norder = 2\nreffe = ReferenceFE(lagrangian, VectorValue{3,Float64}, order)\nΩ = Triangulation(geometry)\ndΩ = Measure(Ω, 2 * order)\nΓtip = BoundaryTriangulation(geometry, tags=\"tip\")\ndΓtip = Measure(Γtip, 2 * order)\nez = VectorValue(0.0, 0.0, 1.0)\n\nres(t) = (u, v) -> residual(physmodel, kinematics, u, v, dΩ)\njac(t) = (u, du, v) -> jacobian(physmodel, kinematics, u, du, v, dΩ)\nstored(t) = u -> energy(physmodel, kinematics, u, dΩ)\nmass = (a, v) -> ∫(ρ * (a ⋅ v))dΩ\n\nprintln(\"free dofs: \", num_free_dofs(TestFESpace(Ω, reffe, bc, conformity=:H1)))\n\ntip(uh) = sum(∫(uh ⋅ ez)dΓtip) / (W * H)                      # mean vertical displacement of the tip face\nkinetic(vh) = 0.5 * sum(mass(vh, vh))","title":"Boundary conditions, finite element spaces and forms"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E15_Elastodynamics/#One-free-vibration-run","page":"15 · Elastodynamics: free vibration of a cantilever","text":"The function below builds the model for a time scheme, an initial tip velocity V0 and a stepping, and solves it. vh is the initial velocity; initial_acceleration=:solve takes the initial acceleration from boldsymbol M boldsymbol a_0 = -boldsymbol r(boldsymbol u_0) (zero here, as the beam starts undeformed, but it is the consistent choice and costs one solve).\n\nThe post-processing runs after every accepted step, before the scheme commits it: the state is already boldsymbol u_n+1, while get_previous_state and get_velocity still give boldsymbol u_n and boldsymbol v_n. The energies are therefore recorded one step behind, and the last ones are taken after the solve. With verbose=1 the solver prints one line per step with K, Pi and K + Pi, and get_energies returns the last values (they are tracked only when the solver is verbose). The three integrals of the post-processing are evaluated by Gridap: about 1 ms per step, a few per cent of a step.\n\nbackend=:compiled evaluates the static forms and the mass form with the compiled, threaded kernels of HyperFEMKernels. The forms are built once; at every stage the model only rebinds them to the new time and state (no recompilation), and the mass matrix is assembled once. The problem is nonlinear, so the tangent is updated at every Newton iteration (tangent_update=:iteration, the default); a linear problem would use :Δt (factorise only when the step changes).\n\nfunction free_vibration(scheme, V0, stepping; verbose=0, vtk=false, name=\"run\")\n  V = TestFESpace(Ω, reffe, bc, conformity=:H1)\n  U = TrialFESpace(V, bc, 0.0)\n  vh = interpolate(mode_velocity(V0), U)\n  nls = NewtonRaphson(LUSolver(); maxiter=20, atol=1e-12, rtol=1e-10, verbose=verbose)\n  m = TransientModel(res, jac, U, V, bc; scheme, mass, vh, initial_acceleration=:solve, energy=stored, nls,\n                     backend=:compiled)\n  uh = get_state(m)\n  t, z = [0.0], [0.0]                                          # tip history\n  te, Ke, Πe = Float64[], Float64[], Float64[]                 # energies, one step behind\n  function record(post)\n    push!(t, post.Λ[post.iter]); push!(z, tip(uh))\n    push!(te, post.iter == 1 ? 0.0 : post.Λ[post.iter-1])\n    push!(Ke, kinetic(get_velocity(m))); push!(Πe, sum(stored(0.0)(get_previous_state(m))))\n    if vtk\n      pvd = get_pvd(post)\n      pvd[t[end]] = createvtk(Ω, joinpath(outdir, \"$(name)_$(post.iter).vtu\"), cellfields=[\"u\" => uh], order=2)\n    end\n  end\n  post = PostProcessor(m, record; is_vtk=vtk, filepath=outdir)\n  time = @elapsed (_, flag) = solve!(m; stepping, post)\n  push!(te, t[end]); push!(Ke, kinetic(get_velocity(m))); push!(Πe, sum(stored(0.0)(uh)))\n  (; flag, t, z, te, Ke, Πe, Etot=Ke .+ Πe, m, time, nsteps=length(t) - 1)\nend\n\nThe period from the tip history: the tip starts at zero going up, crosses zero downwards near T_12 and upwards near T_1; the crossings are located by linear interpolation.\n\nfunction period(t, z)\n  k = findfirst(i -> z[i] > 0 && z[i+1] <= 0, 2:length(z)-1) + 1           # downward crossing\n  j = findfirst(i -> z[i] < 0 && z[i+1] >= 0, k:length(z)-1) + k - 1     # next upward crossing\n  t[j] - z[j] * (t[j+1] - t[j]) / (z[j+1] - z[j])\nend","title":"One free-vibration run"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E15_Elastodynamics/#Solve","page":"15 · Elastodynamics: free vibration of a cantilever","text":"Small amplitude: tip amplitude V_0omega_1 = 10^-3 L, so the response is linear in practice. The runs cover 1.25 periods (the period check needs the second zero crossing) with T_140 (FAST) or T_180 steps.\n\nnper = FAST ? 40 : 80\nΔt = T₁ / nper\nt_end = 1.25 * T₁\nV0small = 1e-3 * L * ω₁\nprintln(\"\\n── Newmark (trapezoidal rule), small amplitude ──\")\nnm = free_vibration(Newmark(), V0small, TimeStepping(; Δt, t_end); verbose=1)\nprintln(\"\\n── Midpoint rule, small amplitude ──\")\nHyperFEMKernels.reset_counters!()\nmp = free_vibration(Midpoint(), V0small, TimeStepping(; Δt, t_end))\nwork_mp = HyperFEMKernels.counters()\nprintln(\"── Generalised-α (ρ∞ = 0.5), small amplitude ──\")\nga = free_vibration(GeneralizedAlpha(0.5), V0small, TimeStepping(; Δt, t_end))\n\nLarge amplitude: tip amplitude approx 025 L (linear estimate), one period, generalised-α with a little dissipation (ρ∞ = 0.8), and the step controlled by the local error estimate. The weighted error is measured against mathrmatol + mathrmrtolu_i dof by dof: atol must be set to the scale of the displacements that matter (here 10^-3, 0.04 % of the tip amplitude); with the default 10^-6 the dofs that barely move (the axial ones) would force steps of T_15000.\n\nprintln(\"── Generalised-α (ρ∞ = 0.8), large amplitude, adaptive steps ──\")\nV0large = 0.25 * L * ω₁\nlg = free_vibration(GeneralizedAlpha(0.8), V0large, AdaptiveStepping(; Δt=T₁ / 40, t_end=T₁, rtol=1e-3, atol=1e-3);\n                    vtk=true, name=\"large\")\nΔts = diff(lg.t)\nprintln(@sprintf(\"adaptive run: %d steps, Δt ∈ [%.3f, %.3f] (T₁/%.0f … T₁/%.0f), max tip deflection %.3f\",\n                 length(Δts), minimum(Δts), maximum(Δts), T₁ / minimum(Δts), T₁ / maximum(Δts), maximum(abs.(lg.z))))","title":"Solve"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E15_Elastodynamics/#Efficiency","page":"15 · Elastodynamics: free vibration of a cantilever","text":"What the compiled operators do (work_summary): every group of the stage forms and of the mass form should take a fast path (:linear_maps or :factorised) and nothing should fall back to Gridap. The counters of the midpoint run (after the first run compiled everything) show that the forms are only rebound at every stage (update_fast: one per step, plus one for the initial acceleration), never compiled again (update_full). HyperFEM 0.3.0 has no accessor for the operators of a TransientModel: the two lines below read internal fields, and may change.\n\nop = nm.m.caches.op.inner                                      # static forms (stiffness), internal field\nopM = nm.m.caches.opM                                          # mass form, internal field\nfor (what, o) in ((\"stage forms\", op), (\"mass form\", opM))\n  ws = HyperFEMKernels.work_summary(o)\n  println(what, \": \", [(g.kind, g.domain, g.path) for g in ws.groups], \"; Gridap fallbacks: \",\n          ws.fallback_residual + ws.fallback_jacobian)\nend\nprintln(\"counters of the midpoint run: \", work_mp)\nfor o in (op, opM)\n  ws = HyperFEMKernels.work_summary(o)\n  @assert ws.fallback_residual == 0 && ws.fallback_jacobian == 0 \"part of the forms is assembled by Gridap\"\n  @assert all(g.path !== :duals for g in ws.groups) \"a group is evaluated with dual numbers\"\nend\n@assert work_mp.update_full == 0 && work_mp.fallback_assemblies == 0 \"the compiled forms were compiled again or assembled by Gridap\"\n@assert work_mp.update_fast == mp.nsteps + 1 \"the compiled forms were not rebound once per step (plus the initial acceleration)\"\nfor (name, r) in ((\"Newmark\", nm), (\"midpoint\", mp), (\"generalised-α(0.5)\", ga), (\"large amplitude\", lg))\n  println(@sprintf(\"%-20s %4d steps  %.3f s per step\", name, r.nsteps, r.time / r.nsteps))\nend","title":"Efficiency"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E15_Elastodynamics/#Checks","page":"15 · Elastodynamics: free vibration of a cantilever","text":"(a) The period of the small-amplitude motion against the Euler–Bernoulli value, with a 3 % tolerance. The measured difference (+0.01 % FAST, +0.22 % full) adds up three effects of opposite signs: the model (shear deformation and rotary inertia, which Euler–Bernoulli neglects, lengthen the period by a relative amount of order (HL)^2, as Timoshenko beam theory shows: Han, Benaroya & Wei 1999, doi:10.1006/jsvi.1999.2257; the 3D clamped face, which also blocks the lateral contraction, stiffens the beam), the Q2 mesh (stiffer than the continuum) and the period elongation of the trapezoidal rule, (omega_1Delta t)^212: 2cdot10^-3 with 40 steps per period, 5cdot10^-4 with 80.\n\nTnm = period(nm.t, nm.z)\nTmp = period(mp.t, mp.z)\nprintln(@sprintf(\"\\nperiod: Newmark %.4f, midpoint %.4f, Euler–Bernoulli %.4f (difference %.2f %%)\",\n                 Tnm, Tmp, T₁, 100 * (Tnm - T₁) / T₁))\n@assert all(r.flag == 1 for r in (nm, mp, ga, lg)) \"a run did not reach its final time\"\n@assert abs(Tnm - T₁) < 0.03 * T₁ \"period away from beam theory (Newmark: $Tnm, T₁ = $T₁)\"\n@assert abs(Tmp - T₁) < 0.03 * T₁ \"period away from beam theory (midpoint: $Tmp, T₁ = $T₁)\"\n@assert abs(maximum(nm.z) - V0small / ω₁) < 0.05 * V0small / ω₁ \"tip amplitude away from V₀/ω₁\"\n\n(b) Energy. For a linear problem the trapezoidal rule and the midpoint rule conserve the discrete energy exactly; at this amplitude the problem is linear up to terms of relative size 10^-4. The generalised-α scheme with ρ∞ < 1 dissipates. Its energy at the step points is not monotone (it oscillates by about 10^-4 at twice the frequency of the motion: the quantity that decreases monotonically is a modified energy of the scheme), but it never exceeds the initial one, and most of what it removes in the first steps is the high-frequency content of the initial velocity (the Euler–Bernoulli mode is not exactly a mode of the 3D mesh), which the trapezoidal rule keeps forever. The last energies of the verbose run are also those of get_energies.\n\ndrift(r) = maximum(abs.(r.Etot .- r.Etot[1])) / r.Etot[1]\nloss(r) = 1 - r.Etot[end] / r.Etot[1]\nprintln(@sprintf(\"energy: initial %.6e; relative drift Newmark %.2e, midpoint %.2e; loss generalised-α(0.5) %.2e, large amplitude %.2e\",\n                 nm.Etot[1], drift(nm), drift(mp), loss(ga), loss(lg)))\ne = get_energies(nm.m)\n@assert abs(e.K + e.Π - nm.Etot[end]) < 1e-10 * nm.Etot[1] \"get_energies differs from the recorded energies\"\n@assert abs(nm.Etot[1] - kinetic(interpolate(mode_velocity(V0small), get_trial_space(nm.m)))) < 1e-12 * nm.Etot[1]\n@assert drift(nm) < 1e-6 \"Newmark: energy not conserved (drift $(drift(nm)))\"\n@assert drift(mp) < 1e-6 \"midpoint: energy not conserved (drift $(drift(mp)))\"\nexcess(r) = maximum(r.Etot) / r.Etot[1] - 1\n@assert excess(ga) < 1e-10 && loss(ga) > 1e-3 \"generalised-α(0.5): no dissipation (loss $(loss(ga)), excess $(excess(ga)))\"\n@assert excess(lg) < 1e-10 && loss(lg) > 0 \"large amplitude: energy above the initial one (excess $(excess(lg)))\"\n\n(c) Regression values (HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6, Gridap 0.20.8, Julia 1.13.0, macOS arm64).\n\nprintln(@sprintf(\"regression: Tnm = %.10e, z_end = %.10e, loss_ga = %.10e, zmax(large) = %.10e\",\n                 Tnm, nm.z[end], loss(ga), maximum(lg.z)))\nreference = FAST ? (Tnm=3.8396008005e+02, z_end=9.9400476022e-03, loss_ga=6.9292171059e-03, z_large=2.4515873351e+00) :\n                   (Tnm=3.8476605127e+02, z_end=9.9605466481e-03, loss_ga=4.4195200638e-03, z_large=2.4580846981e+00)\n@assert isapprox(Tnm, reference.Tnm; rtol=1e-5) \"regression: period $Tnm\"\n@assert isapprox(nm.z[end], reference.z_end; rtol=1e-5) \"regression: final tip deflection $(nm.z[end])\"\n@assert isapprox(loss(ga), reference.loss_ga; rtol=1e-5) \"regression: generalised-α energy loss $(loss(ga))\"\n@assert isapprox(maximum(lg.z), reference.z_large; rtol=1e-5) \"regression: large-amplitude maximum tip deflection $(maximum(lg.z))\"","title":"Checks"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E15_Elastodynamics/#Going-further","page":"15 · Elastodynamics: free vibration of a cantilever","text":"HHT(α) and Newmark(β=…, γ=…) are other members of the generalised-α family; Newmark(β=0.3, γ=0.6) dissipates as well, at first order.\nCentralDifference() with mass_lumping=true is the explicit scheme of wave propagation and impact problems: no Newton iterations, but a stable step below 2omega_max (the highest frequency of the mesh), far smaller than the ones used here.\nA damping form, e.g. Rayleigh damping (v, w) -> ∫(c * ρ * (v ⋅ w))dΩ, adds physical dissipation.\nIncrease the amplitude further and compare the period with the small-amplitude one, and the axial displacement of the tip (it moves inwards: geometric nonlinearity). Run the large-amplitude case with Newmark() and compare its energy with that of GeneralizedAlpha(0.8).\nReference: J. Chung, G. M. Hulbert (1993), A time integration algorithm for structural dynamics with improved numerical dissipation: the generalized-α method, J. Appl. Mech. 60(2), 371–375, doi:10.1115/1.2900803.","title":"Going further"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E02_Hyperelastic_stretch/#Tutorial-02:-Uniaxial-stretch-of-a-hyperelastic-bar","page":"02 · Uniaxial stretch of a hyperelastic bar","text":"Source · Notebook · nbviewer\n\nA neo-Hookean bar is stretched to 1.75 times its length while its lateral faces are free to contract. The deformation is homogeneous, so the force–stretch curve has a closed form: the first nonlinear tutorial is also a validation of the whole pipeline (model, weak form, Newton, load stepping).\n\nWhat you will learn\n\nchoosing a constitutive model (NeoHookean3D) and getting its weak form (residual, jacobian);\nDirichlet conditions by components (BoundaryCondition(...; mask)) that grow with the load factor;\nStaticNonlinearModel, NewtonRaphson and the load increments of LoadStepping;\nrecording results at every increment with a PostProcessor.\n\nMeasured on a Mac M5 Max (Julia 1.13.0, 4 threads, 4 BLAS threads; HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6, Gridap 0.20.8; compiled backend), whole run including compilation: FAST 29 s, full 32 s (3 127 free dofs, 5 increments of 3 Newton iterations, 9 s in solve!, almost all of it compilation). One assembly of the tangent on the full mesh: 0.7 ms compiled, 14 ms with Gridap's lazy evaluation.","title":"Tutorial 02: Uniaxial stretch of a hyperelastic bar"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E02_Hyperelastic_stretch/#Problem-statement","page":"02 · Uniaxial stretch of a hyperelastic bar","text":"Find the displacement boldsymbol u of the bar Omega = (0L)times(0W)times(0T) such that\n\nint_Omega boldsymbol P(boldsymbol F)  nabla_0 boldsymbol v mathrm dV = 0 quad forall boldsymbol v \nqquad boldsymbol F = boldsymbol I + nabla_0 boldsymbol u  qquad boldsymbol P = partial_boldsymbol FPsi \n\nwith the compressible neo-Hookean energy Psi = tfracmu2(boldsymbol Fboldsymbol F - 3) - mu ln J + tfraclambda2(J-1)^2, J = det boldsymbol F. The face x = 0 cannot move along x, the faces y = 0 and z = 0 are symmetry planes, and the face x = L is pulled: u_x = Lambdadelta, with the load factor Lambda going from 0 to 1.\n\nWith these conditions boldsymbol F = mathrmdiag(lambda_1 lambda_t lambda_t) is uniform. The lateral faces are free of traction (P_22 = 0), which gives J = lambda_1lambda_t^2 as the positive root of lambda J^2 - (lambda - mulambda_1) J - mu = 0, and the nominal stress P_11 = mu(lambda_1 - Jlambda_1^2).","title":"Problem statement"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E02_Hyperelastic_stretch/#Setup","page":"02 · Uniaxial stretch of a hyperelastic bar","text":"using HyperFEM\nusing Gridap\nusing HyperFEMKernels                                          # compiled backend (`backend=:compiled`)\n\nconst FAST = get(ENV, \"HYPERFEM_TUTORIALS_FAST\", \"0\") == \"1\"   # reduced case for the test suite\noutdir = joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"data\", \"sims\", \"E02_Hyperelastic_stretch\")\nsetupfolder(outdir)                                            # VTK output (not versioned)","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E02_Hyperelastic_stretch/#Geometry-and-mesh","page":"02 · Uniaxial stretch of a hyperelastic bar","text":"A Cartesian mesh of trilinear hexahedra. CartesianTags names the faces of the box; the ⁺ variants include the edges and vertices of the face.\n\nL, W, T = 1.0, 0.2, 0.5                                        # m\npartition = FAST ? (8, 2, 4) : (20, 4, 10)\nmodel = CartesianDiscreteModel((0, L, 0, W, 0, T), partition)\nlabels = get_face_labeling(model)\nadd_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"x0\", CartesianTags.face0YZ⁺)\nadd_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"x1\", CartesianTags.face1YZ⁺)\nadd_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"y0\", CartesianTags.faceX0Z)\nadd_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"z0\", CartesianTags.faceXY0)","title":"Geometry and mesh"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E02_Hyperelastic_stretch/#Constitutive-model","page":"02 · Uniaxial stretch of a hyperelastic bar","text":"Every model of HyperFEM is a small struct; calling it returns the energy and its analytical derivatives (Psi partial_boldsymbol FPsi partial^2_boldsymbol Fboldsymbol FPsi).\n\nλ, μ = 100.0, 1.0                                              # Pa (nearly incompressible, ν ≈ 0.495)\nphysmodel = NeoHookean3D(λ=λ, μ=μ)\nkinematics = Kinematics(Mechano, Solid)","title":"Constitutive model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E02_Hyperelastic_stretch/#Boundary-conditions","page":"02 · Uniaxial stretch of a hyperelastic bar","text":"A BoundaryCondition is a tag, a value and (optionally) a function of the load factor; mask selects the constrained components. Conditions without a function are constant.\n\nδ = 0.75 * L                                                   # final elongation: λ₁ = 1.75\nD_bc = BoundaryConditions(\n  BoundaryCondition(\"x0\", [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]; mask=(true, false, false)),\n  BoundaryCondition(\"x1\", [δ, 0.0, 0.0], EvolutionFunctions.ramp(); mask=(true, false, false)),\n  BoundaryCondition(\"y0\", [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]; mask=(false, true, false)),\n  BoundaryCondition(\"z0\", [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]; mask=(false, false, true)))","title":"Boundary conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E02_Hyperelastic_stretch/#Finite-element-spaces","page":"02 · Uniaxial stretch of a hyperelastic bar","text":"order = 1\nreffe = ReferenceFE(lagrangian, VectorValue{3,Float64}, order)\nΩ = Triangulation(model)\ndΩ = Measure(Ω, 2 * order)\nV = TestFESpace(Ω, reffe, D_bc, conformity=:H1)\nU = TrialFESpace(V, D_bc, 0.0)                                 # Dirichlet values at Λ = 0","title":"Finite element spaces"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E02_Hyperelastic_stretch/#Weak-form","page":"02 · Uniaxial stretch of a hyperelastic bar","text":"residual and jacobian build the forms above from the model. They are functions of the load factor because other problems have loads or material parameters that depend on it; here they do not.\n\nres(Λ) = (u, v) -> residual(physmodel, kinematics, u, v, dΩ)\njac(Λ) = (u, du, v) -> jacobian(physmodel, kinematics, u, du, v, dΩ)","title":"Weak form"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E02_Hyperelastic_stretch/#Solver-and-computational-model","page":"02 · Uniaxial stretch of a hyperelastic bar","text":"backend=:compiled (tutorial 01) compiles the residual and the tangent once into threaded kernels; at every increment the model only rebinds them to the new state. The solution is the same as with Gridap's lazy evaluation.\n\nnls = NewtonRaphson(LUSolver(); maxiter=20, rtol=1e-10, verbose=true)\ncomp_model = StaticNonlinearModel(res, jac, U, V, D_bc; nls=nls, backend=:compiled)\nuh = get_state(comp_model)                                     # FE function of the current state","title":"Solver and computational model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E02_Hyperelastic_stretch/#Post-processing","page":"02 · Uniaxial stretch of a hyperelastic bar","text":"The function below runs after every converged increment: it stores the stretch and the force on the pulled face, int_Gamma (boldsymbol P boldsymbol N)_x mathrm dA, and writes the displacement.\n\n_, ∂Ψ∂F, _ = physmodel()\nF, _, _ = get_Kinematics(kinematics)\nΓ = BoundaryTriangulation(model, tags=\"x1\")\ndΓ = Measure(Γ, 2 * order)\nN = get_normal_vector(Γ)\nstretch, force = Float64[], Float64[]\nfunction record(post)\n  Λ = post.Λ[post.iter]\n  push!(stretch, 1 + Λ * δ / L)\n  push!(force, sum(∫((∂Ψ∂F ∘ (F ∘ ∇(uh)')) ⋅ N)dΓ)[1])\n  pvd = get_pvd(post)\n  pvd[Λ] = createvtk(Ω, joinpath(outdir, \"step_$(post.iter).vtu\"), cellfields=[\"u\" => uh])\nend\npost = PostProcessor(comp_model, record; is_vtk=true, filepath=outdir)","title":"Post-processing"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E02_Hyperelastic_stretch/#Solve","page":"02 · Uniaxial stretch of a hyperelastic bar","text":"Five equal increments; an increment whose Newton iteration fails is halved (three times in a row at most). ProjectDirichlet=true starts every increment from a linear prediction of the effect of the new boundary values; without it Newton starts with only the boundary nodes moved and diverges for increments this large.\n\nx, flag = solve!(comp_model; stepping=LoadStepping(nsteps=5, maxbisec=3), ProjectDirichlet=true, post=post)   # also closes the .pvd","title":"Solve"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E02_Hyperelastic_stretch/#Checks","page":"02 · Uniaxial stretch of a hyperelastic bar","text":"The numerical force–stretch curve against the closed form (the mesh represents the homogeneous deformation exactly, so the difference is the Newton tolerance).\n\nJ_exact(λ₁) = ((λ - μ / λ₁) + sqrt((λ - μ / λ₁)^2 + 4λ * μ)) / (2λ)\nP11_exact(λ₁) = μ * (λ₁ - J_exact(λ₁) / λ₁^2)\nforce_exact = P11_exact.(stretch) .* (W * T)\nfor (s, f, fe) in zip(stretch, force, force_exact)\n  println(\"λ₁ = $(round(s, digits=2))   force = $(round(f, sigdigits=8)) N   exact = $(round(fe, sigdigits=8)) N\")\nend\n@assert flag == 1 \"the load stepping did not reach Λ = 1\"\n@assert length(stretch) == 5 \"an increment was bisected\"\n@assert maximum(abs.(force .- force_exact) ./ force_exact) < 1e-6 \"force–stretch curve away from the closed form\"\ndΩe = Measure(Ω, 4)\nλt = sqrt(J_exact(1 + δ / L) / (1 + δ / L))                     # lateral stretch at the end\nu_exact = let δ = δ, L = L, λt = λt                         # captured values: no non-constant globals at points\n  X -> VectorValue(δ * X[1] / L, (λt - 1) * X[2], (λt - 1) * X[3])\nend\nerr = sqrt(sum(∫((uh - u_exact) ⋅ (uh - u_exact))dΩe))\n@assert err < 1e-6 * δ \"displacement away from the homogeneous solution (error $err)\"","title":"Checks"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E02_Hyperelastic_stretch/#Going-further","page":"02 · Uniaxial stretch of a hyperelastic bar","text":"Clamp both ends (remove the masks of x0 and x1): the deformation is no longer homogeneous and the bar necks; compare the force with the curve above.\nTry MooneyRivlin3D or Yeoh3D instead of NeoHookean3D: nothing else changes.\nLoadStepping(nsteps=50, grow=1.5, ΔΛmax=0.2) starts with small increments and lets them grow.\nTutorial 03 adds fibres and a pressure that follows the deformation; tutorial 13 replaces LUSolver.","title":"Going further"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E08_MagnetoMechanical_beam/#Tutorial-08:-Magneto-mechanical-hMRE-beam","page":"08 · Magneto-mechanical hMRE beam","text":"Source · Notebook · nbviewer\n\nA slender beam of a hard-magnetic magnetorheological elastomer (hMRE: hard magnetic particles in an ultra-soft silicone matrix) is magnetised along its length and clamped at one end. A transverse magnetic field exerts a torque on the magnetisation, which tries to align with the field: the beam bends. This is the simplified model of Perez-Garcia, Ortigosa, Martínez-Frutos and Garcia-Gonzalez (Adv. Mater. 2025): the applied field acts on the solid only, and the field created by the beam itself and the surrounding air are ignored. Tutorial 09 solves the same beam with the magnetic field in the surrounding vacuum.\n\nWhat you will learn\n\ntwo-dimensional (plane strain) models: NonlinearMooneyRivlin2D + TransverseIsotropy2D;\na domain made of one subdomain of a larger mesh (Triangulation(model, tags=...));\na load written by hand and evaluated at the previous converged state: the state pair xh/xh⁻ of StaticNonlinearModel;\nevaluating a finite element function at a point.\n\nMeasured on a Mac M5 Max (Julia 1.13.0, 4 threads, 4 BLAS threads; HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6, Gridap 0.20.8; compiled backend), whole run including compilation: 25 s (FAST and full are the same case), of which 5 s in solve! (20 increments, 137 Newton iterations); 1 232 free dofs.","title":"Tutorial 08: Magneto-mechanical hMRE beam"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E08_MagnetoMechanical_beam/#Problem-statement","page":"08 · Magneto-mechanical hMRE beam","text":"The beam Omega = (0 L)times(-t2 t2), with L = 50 mm and t = 2 mm, is in plane strain. It is clamped on the first 2.5 mm of its lower face and cannot move along x at its left end. Its residual magnetisation has the direction boldsymbol N = (1 0) in the reference configuration and is carried by the rotation boldsymbol R = boldsymbol Fboldsymbol U^-1 of the polar decomposition (boldsymbol U^2 = boldsymbol F^Tboldsymbol F). The applied field boldsymbol B_a = Lambda(0 -B_a) is uniform and grows with the load factor Lambda. The magnetic (Zeeman) energy and its stress, with boldsymbol U^-1 taken as fixed when differentiating, are\n\nPsi_mathrmmag = -fracalpha_rmu_0 boldsymbol B_a cdot boldsymbol R boldsymbol N \nqquad\nboldsymbol P_mathrmmag = -fracalpha_rmu_0 boldsymbol B_a otimes boldsymbol U^-1boldsymbol N \n\nwith mu_0 = 4picdot 10^-7 H/m, the remanent magnetisation alpha_r = 10^-2mu_0 approx 796 kA/m (10 mT divided by mu_0) and B_a = 8cdot 10^-8, the values of the paper's script. In that script, and here, B_a is mu_0 times the applied flux density: the applied field is B_amu_0 approx 64 mT, the energy is the usual -boldsymbol Mcdotboldsymbol B with boldsymbol M = alpha_r boldsymbol Rboldsymbol N, and the magnetic stress scale alpha_r B_amu_0 is about 0.5 kPa. The matrix is an ultra-soft silicone with a slight residual anisotropy along boldsymbol N, fitted in the paper: the sum of a nonlinear Mooney–Rivlin energy and a transversely isotropic one (in Pa),\n\nbeginaligned\nPsi_mathrmMR = fracmu_12alpha_1 3^alpha_1-1(boldsymbol Fboldsymbol F + 1)^alpha_1\n  + fracmu_22alpha_2 3^alpha_2-1(boldsymbol Fboldsymbol F + J^2)^alpha_2\n  - (mu_1 + 2mu_2)ln J + fraclambda2(J-1)^2 + textconst \nPsi_mathrmTI = fracmu2beta_1(I_4^beta_1 - 1) + fracmu2beta_2(I_5^beta_2 - 1) - muln J \nqquad I_4 = boldsymbol Fboldsymbol N^2  I_5 = boldsymbol Hboldsymbol N^2 \nendaligned\n\n(in the 2D models boldsymbol Fboldsymbol F + 1 and boldsymbol Fboldsymbol F + J^2 are the first two invariants of the plane-strain deformation). The weak form is\n\nint_Omega nabla_0boldsymbol v  left(partial_boldsymbol FPsi_mathrmMR + partial_boldsymbol FPsi_mathrmTI\n  + boldsymbol P_mathrmmagright) mathrm dA = 0 quad forall boldsymbol v \n\nThe magnetic stress is evaluated at the displacement of the previous converged increment, boldsymbol u^-, so that within an increment it is a given load and the tangent is that of the elastic energy alone. This explicit treatment of the rotation makes the result depend (slightly) on the size of the increments.","title":"Problem statement"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E08_MagnetoMechanical_beam/#Setup","page":"08 · Magneto-mechanical hMRE beam","text":"using HyperFEM\nusing Gridap\nusing HyperFEMKernels                                          # compiled backend (`backend=:compiled`)\nusing LinearAlgebra: norm\n\nconst FAST = get(ENV, \"HYPERFEM_TUTORIALS_FAST\", \"0\") == \"1\"   # the test suite runs the same case (see *Solve*)\noutdir = joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"data\", \"sims\", \"E08_MagnetoMechanical_beam\")\nsetupfolder(outdir)                                            # VTK output (not versioned)","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E08_MagnetoMechanical_beam/#Geometry-and-mesh","page":"08 · Magneto-mechanical hMRE beam","text":"The mesh of the paper: the beam, a region below it meant for a permanent magnet in other variants of the problem, and the surrounding air, in linear triangles. Here only the beam (physical group Beam) is used; tutorial 09 uses the rest, all of it as vacuum.\n\nmodel = GmshDiscreteModel(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"data\", \"models\", \"Short_magnet.msh\"))\nL = 0.05                                                       # m","title":"Geometry and mesh"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E08_MagnetoMechanical_beam/#Constitutive-model","page":"08 · Magneto-mechanical hMRE beam","text":"Parameters fitted in the paper (Pa); + composes the isotropic and the anisotropic models.\n\nparams = [10.0, 12480.64495232286, 1.0, 2.0, 5195.545287237134, 0.2602717127043121, 2.0]\nmodelmech = NonlinearMooneyRivlin2D(λ=(params[1] + params[2]) * 1e2, μ1=params[1] * 1e3, μ2=params[2],\n                                    α1=params[3], α2=params[4]) +\n            TransverseIsotropy2D(μ=params[5], α1=params[6], α2=params[7])\nkm = Kinematics(Mechano, Solid)\nF, _, _ = get_Kinematics(km)\n\nMagnetic constants. They are read inside Pmag below, a function evaluated at every quadrature point, so they are declared const: a non-constant global read there would make every evaluation dynamic (and slow).\n\nconst μ0 = 4π * 1e-7                                           # H/m\nconst αr = 10e-3 / μ0                                          # A/m, remanent magnetisation\nBanorm = 80.0e-9                                               # μ0 × applied flux density: Banorm/μ0 ≈ 64 mT","title":"Constitutive model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E08_MagnetoMechanical_beam/#Boundary-conditions","page":"08 · Magneto-mechanical hMRE beam","text":"Fixed_Solid (the clamped part of the lower face) in both components; Fixed_Solid_x (the left end) along x only.\n\nDu = BoundaryConditions(\n  BoundaryCondition(\"Fixed_Solid\", [0.0, 0.0]),\n  BoundaryCondition(\"Fixed_Solid_x\", [0.0, 0.0]; mask=(true, false)))","title":"Boundary conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E08_MagnetoMechanical_beam/#Finite-element-spaces","page":"08 · Magneto-mechanical hMRE beam","text":"Spaces on the beam only. Two FE functions hold the current state boldsymbol u and the state of the previous converged increment boldsymbol u^-; the computational model updates both (xh, xh⁻).\n\norder = 1\nreffeu = ReferenceFE(lagrangian, VectorValue{2,Float64}, order)\nΩ = Triangulation(model, tags=[\"Beam\"])\ndΩ = Measure(Ω, 2 * order)\nV = TestFESpace(Ω, reffeu, Du, conformity=:H1)\nU = TrialFESpace(V, Du)\nU⁻ = TrialFESpace(V, Du)\nuh = FEFunction(U, zeros(num_free_dofs(U)))\nuh⁻ = FEFunction(U⁻, zeros(num_free_dofs(U⁻)))\nprintln(\"free dofs: \", num_free_dofs(U), \"   triangles: \", num_cells(Ω))\n\nMagnetisation direction and applied field, as finite element fields on the beam.\n\nVN = TestFESpace(Ω, reffeu)\nNh = interpolate_everywhere(x -> VectorValue(1.0, 0.0), VN)\nBah = interpolate_everywhere(VectorValue(0.0, -Banorm), VN)","title":"Finite element spaces"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E08_MagnetoMechanical_beam/#Weak-form","page":"08 · Magneto-mechanical hMRE beam","text":"boldsymbol U^-1 = (boldsymbol F^Tboldsymbol F)^-12 at a point, with a small shift that keeps the square root well defined, and the magnetic stress. They are plain Julia functions of tensors, composed with FE fields like the derivatives of the constitutive models.\n\nfunction invU(F; ε=1e-12)\n  S = get_array(F)' * get_array(F) + ε * get_array(I2D)\n  TensorValue(inv(sqrt(S)))\nend\nPmag(F, N, Ba) = (-αr / μ0) * (Ba ⊗ (invU(F) * N))\n\nThe elastic terms come from the helpers (Nh is the extra argument of the anisotropic model); the magnetic term is written by hand, with boldsymbol F of uh⁻ and multiplied by the load factor. It does not depend on the unknown boldsymbol u, so it does not enter the tangent.\n\nres(Λ) = (u, v) -> residual(modelmech, km, u, v, dΩ, Λ, Nh) +\n                   Λ * ∫((Pmag ∘ (F ∘ ∇(uh⁻)', Nh, Bah)) ⊙ ∇(v)')dΩ\njac(Λ) = (u, du, v) -> jacobian(modelmech, km, u, du, v, dΩ, Λ, Nh)","title":"Weak form"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E08_MagnetoMechanical_beam/#Solver-and-computational-model","page":"08 · Magneto-mechanical hMRE beam","text":"The paper's script stops Newton at a 1 % relative residual (rtol=1e-2), on the grounds that the magnetic load is lagged by one increment anyway. Here every increment is solved to equilibrium (rtol=1e-8): the result then depends on the increments only, not on where the iterations happened to stop. With backend=:compiled (tutorial 01) the hand-written magnetic term, invU included, is compiled with the rest of the form; at every increment it is only rebound to the new uh⁻ and Lambda.\n\nnls = NewtonRaphson(LUSolver(); maxiter=20, atol=1e-10, rtol=1e-8, verbose=true)\ncomp_model = StaticNonlinearModel(res, jac, U, V, Du; nls=nls, xh=uh, xh⁻=uh⁻, backend=:compiled)","title":"Solver and computational model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E08_MagnetoMechanical_beam/#Post-processing","page":"08 · Magneto-mechanical hMRE beam","text":"After every increment: the displacement of the middle of the free end, (L 0), and the VTK files.\n\ntip = Point(L, 0.0)\nfield, tip_u = Float64[], VectorValue{2,Float64}[]\nfunction record(post)\n  Λ = post.Λ[post.iter]\n  push!(field, Λ * Banorm / μ0)                                # applied flux density, T\n  push!(tip_u, uh(tip))\n  pvd = get_pvd(post)\n  pvd[Λ] = createvtk(Ω, joinpath(outdir, \"step_$(post.iter).vtu\"), cellfields=[\"u\" => uh])\nend\npost = PostProcessor(comp_model, record; is_vtk=true, filepath=outdir)","title":"Post-processing"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E08_MagnetoMechanical_beam/#Solve","page":"08 · Magneto-mechanical hMRE beam","text":"Twenty equal increments, as in the paper; one bisection at most. The beam is so soft that the first increments need many Newton iterations, and with five increments the first one diverges. The whole run takes seconds, so the FAST mode of the test suite runs this same case.\n\nx, flag = solve!(comp_model; stepping=LoadStepping(nsteps=20, maxbisec=1), post=post)\n\nfor (b, w) in zip(field, tip_u)\n  println(\"B = $(round(1e3b, sigdigits=4)) mT   tip displacement = ($(round(w[1]*1e3, sigdigits=6)), $(round(w[2]*1e3, sigdigits=6))) mm\")\nend","title":"Solve"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E08_MagnetoMechanical_beam/#Checks","page":"08 · Magneto-mechanical hMRE beam","text":"Physics. The beam bends towards the field (-y), more at every increment, and its free end stays at a distance from the clamp no larger than the length of the beam (a soft but not extensible bending).\n\ntip_end = tip_u[end]\nprintln(\"tip displacement: \", tip_end, \"   ‖u‖ = \", norm(get_free_dof_values(uh)))\n@assert flag == 1 \"the load stepping did not reach Λ = 1\"\n@assert all(diff([w[2] for w in tip_u]) .< 0) && tip_end[2] < 0 \"the beam does not bend towards the field\"\n@assert norm(VectorValue(L, 0.0) + tip_end) < 1.02 * L \"the free end moved too far\"\n\nReference. The norm of the displacement against the script of the paper, Adv_Mater_Ultra_Soft_Magnet/src/hMRE_2D.jl (HyperFEM 0.3.0, Gridap 0.20.8, Julia 1.13.0, macOS arm64), which stops Newton at a 1 % relative residual: the two agree to 4e-8, so the paper's looser tolerance did not change its result. The tip displacement is a regression value of this script (same versions, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6).\n\nnu = norm(get_free_dof_values(uh))\nnu_ref = 0.7146295685790958                                    # m, paper's script\n@assert isapprox(nu, nu_ref; rtol=1e-6) \"‖u‖ = $nu differs from the reference $nu_ref\"\ntip_ref = [-0.034300973255553015, -0.04289853199660556]        # m\n@assert isapprox(collect(Tuple(tip_end)), tip_ref; rtol=1e-5) \"tip displacement $tip_end differs from the reference $tip_ref\"","title":"Checks"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E08_MagnetoMechanical_beam/#Going-further","page":"08 · Magneto-mechanical hMRE beam","text":"Run with LoadStepping(nsteps=40, maxbisec=1): the change of the tip displacement measures the error of the lagged magnetic load.\nReverse the field, or turn it: boldsymbol B_a along boldsymbol N exerts no torque, and the beam only stretches or shortens along its axis.\nTutorial 09 computes the magnetic field in the beam and in the surrounding vacuum, driven by a magnetic potential difference across the box, and solves magnetics and mechanics one after the other (StaggeredModel).\nReference: C. Perez-Garcia, R. Ortigosa, J. Martínez-Frutos, D. Garcia-Gonzalez, Topology and material optimization in ultra-soft magnetoactive structures: making advantage of residual anisotropies, Adv. Mater. (2025) e18489, doi:10.1002/adma.202518489.","title":"Going further"},{"category":"section","location":"running/#Running-and-testing","page":"Running and testing","text":"","title":"Running and testing"},{"category":"section","location":"running/#Running-a-tutorial","page":"Running and testing","text":"From the root of a clone of the repository, after julia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()':\n\njulia -t 4 --project=. src/E02_Hyperelastic_stretch.jl\n\nEvery script is self-contained. Results (VTK files, CSV tables) are written to data/sims/<tutorial>/, which is not versioned; open the .pvd collections with ParaView. Several tutorials assemble or solve with threads: start Julia with -t 4 or -t auto.\n\nFrom the Julia REPL the same can be done with\n\nusing HyperFEMTutorials\nrun_tutorial(2)              # or run_tutorial(\"E02\")\nrun_tutorial(2; fast=true)   # reduced case","title":"Running a tutorial"},{"category":"section","location":"running/#Reduced-cases","page":"Running and testing","text":"With the environment variable HYPERFEM_TUTORIALS_FAST=1 every tutorial runs a reduced case (coarser mesh, fewer steps) that finishes in a few minutes at most. The test suite uses them.","title":"Reduced cases"},{"category":"section","location":"running/#Notebooks","page":"Running and testing","text":"Each tutorial page links to a Jupyter notebook generated from the same script. To open them install IJulia and generate the notebooks inside your clone, so that the relative paths to data/ work:\n\njulia --project=docs -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\njulia --project=docs docs/make.jl notebooks     # writes notebooks/E<NN>_<Name>.ipynb\n\nThe notebooks are not executed: run the cells in order. Select a Julia kernel with threads (for example created with IJulia.installkernel(\"Julia 4 threads\", env=Dict(\"JULIA_NUM_THREADS\" => \"4\"))) and activate the project of the repository in the first cell (using Pkg; Pkg.activate(\"..\")).","title":"Notebooks"},{"category":"section","location":"running/#Tests","page":"Running and testing","text":"julia -t 4 --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'\n\nruns every tutorial in its reduced case in a fresh module; the checks of each tutorial (its @asserts) are the tests. Environment variables: HYPERFEM_TUTORIALS_GROUP (basics, coupled, contact, fluids, solvers, dynamics), HYPERFEM_TUTORIALS_ONLY (comma-separated prefixes, e.g. E02,E10), HYPERFEM_TUTORIALS_FAST=0 (the documented cases) and HYPERFEM_TUTORIALS_PETSC=0 (skip the parts that use PETSc).","title":"Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"running/#PETSc","page":"Running and testing","text":"The solver tutorials use PETSc through GridapPETSc. The project pins PETSc_jll to 3.22.1, the version these tutorials are tested with (newer builds failed to load on macOS with GridapPETSc 0.5.8); with HYPERFEM_TUTORIALS_PETSC=0 those parts are skipped.","title":"PETSc"},{"category":"section","location":"running/#Threads-of-the-linear-algebra","page":"Running and testing","text":"Julia's OpenBLAS starts one thread per core by default. When several Julia processes run at the same time (for example several tutorials, or the test groups in parallel), the sparse LU factorisations compete for the cores and can become many times slower. Limit the BLAS threads in that case, e.g.\n\nOPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=4 julia -t 4 --project=. src/E13_Solvers.jl\n\nThe times written in the headers of the tutorials were measured with 4 Julia threads and 4 BLAS threads (Apple M5 Max).","title":"Threads of the linear algebra"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E03_Fibre_reinforced_cylinder/#Tutorial-03:-Fibre-reinforced-cylinder-under-internal-pressure","page":"03 · Fibre-reinforced cylinder under internal pressure","text":"Source · Notebook · nbviewer\n\nA thick-walled tube, clamped at both ends, is inflated by an internal pressure. Its wall is a soft isotropic matrix reinforced by four families of collagen-like fibres (axial, circumferential and two helical ones), the arrangement used to model arteries. Two features of soft-tissue problems appear here: anisotropic models with fibre directions that vary in space, and a pressure that follows the deformed surface, whose weak form and tangent are written by hand.\n\nWhat you will learn\n\na quarter of a tube from a Cartesian mesh and a polar map, with symmetry planes imposed by mask;\nsumming an isotropic and an anisotropic model (MooneyRivlin3D() + HGO_4Fibers());\nfibre directions as finite element fields, passed to the model as extra arguments;\na follower pressure: its weak form with the cofactor boldsymbol H and its consistent tangent;\nchecking the setup against the Lamé solution before trusting the nonlinear results.\n\nMeasured on a Mac M5 Max (Julia 1.13.0, 4 threads, 4 BLAS threads; HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6, Gridap 0.20.8; compiled backend), whole run including compilation: full case (triquadratic Q2 elements, mesh 2×12×30, 21 535 free dofs, 20 increments of 4 Newton iterations) 51 s, of which 16 s in solve! (0.8 s per increment, mostly the LU factorisations, 0.16 s each); FAST case (1×8×20, 5 733 free dofs, 4 increments to 20 % of the pressure) 40 s. One assembly of the tangent on the full mesh: 0.016 s compiled, 0.49 s with Gridap's lazy evaluation.","title":"Tutorial 03: Fibre-reinforced cylinder under internal pressure"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E03_Fibre_reinforced_cylinder/#Problem-statement","page":"03 · Fibre-reinforced cylinder under internal pressure","text":"The tube has inner radius R_i, outer radius R_o and length L, both ends are clamped and the inner surface Gamma_i carries the pressure p in the deformed configuration. Find the displacement boldsymbol u such that\n\nint_Omega boldsymbol P(boldsymbol F)  nabla_0 boldsymbol v mathrm dV\n+ Lambda p int_Gamma_i (boldsymbol H boldsymbol N)cdot boldsymbol v mathrm dA = 0\nquad forall boldsymbol v  qquad boldsymbol H = operatornamecofboldsymbol F = Jboldsymbol F^-T\n\nwith the load factor Lambda going from 0 to 1. The second term is minus the virtual work of the pressure, +int_gamma_i pboldsymbol ncdotboldsymbol vmathrm da (the pressure acts along -boldsymbol n), pulled back to the reference surface with Nanson's formula boldsymbol nmathrm da = boldsymbol Hboldsymbol Nmathrm dA; boldsymbol N is the outward normal of the solid (it points to the axis on Gamma_i). Because boldsymbol H depends on boldsymbol u, the load contributes to the tangent: with Dboldsymbol Hdeltaboldsymbol u = boldsymbol F times nabla_0deltaboldsymbol u (the tensor cross product of Bonet, Gil and Ortigosa, × in HyperFEM),\n\nLambda p int_Gamma_i big((boldsymbol Ftimesnabla_0deltaboldsymbol u)boldsymbol Nbig)cdotboldsymbol v mathrm dA \n\nThe strain energy is a Mooney–Rivlin matrix plus four fibre families of Holzapfel–Gasser–Ogden type (Holzapfel, Gasser and Ogden 2000, doi:10.1023/A:1010835316564),\n\nPsi = fracmu_12boldsymbol Fboldsymbol F + fracmu_22boldsymbol Hboldsymbol H\n- (mu_1+2mu_2)ln J + fraclambda2(J-1)^2 - frac32(mu_1+mu_2)\n+ sum_a=1^4 fracc_1a4c_2aleft(e^c_2a(I_4a-1)^2 - 1right)\nqquad I_4a = boldsymbol Fboldsymbol M_a^2 \n\nwith unit fibre directions boldsymbol M_a: axial boldsymbol e_z, circumferential boldsymbol e_theta and the two helices (boldsymbol e_theta pm boldsymbol e_z)sqrt 2. Units: cm and Pa.","title":"Problem statement"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E03_Fibre_reinforced_cylinder/#Setup","page":"03 · Fibre-reinforced cylinder under internal pressure","text":"using HyperFEM\nusing Gridap\nusing HyperFEMKernels                                          # compiled backend (`backend=:compiled`)\n\nconst FAST = get(ENV, \"HYPERFEM_TUTORIALS_FAST\", \"0\") == \"1\"   # reduced case for the test suite\noutdir = joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"data\", \"sims\", \"E03_Fibre_reinforced_cylinder\")\nsetupfolder(outdir)                                            # VTK output (not versioned)","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E03_Fibre_reinforced_cylinder/#Geometry-and-mesh","page":"03 · Fibre-reinforced cylinder under internal pressure","text":"The material and the loads are symmetric with respect to the planes x = 0 and y = 0 (the two helical families are mirror images of each other), so a quarter of the tube is enough. It is the box (R_iR_o)times(0pi2)times(0L) in coordinates (rthetaz), mapped to Cartesian coordinates by map. The map moves the vertices: the cells stay trilinear, so the cross-section is a polygon, and the quadratic elements below do not curve it.\n\nRi, Ro, L = 1.0, 1.1, 4.4                                       # cm\npolar(X) = Point(X[1] * cos(X[2]), X[1] * sin(X[2]), X[3])\npartition = FAST ? (1, 8, 20) : (2, 12, 30)                    # cells along r, θ, z\nmodel = CartesianDiscreteModel((Ri, Ro, 0, π / 2, 0, L), partition; map=polar)\n\nCartesianTags names the faces, edges and corners of the box (X = r, Y = theta, Z = z). Overlapping tags would leave unclear which mask applies at the shared nodes, so the tags are chosen disjoint: the clamped ends take their edges and corners (⁺), the symmetry planes only their interior and their edges along z.\n\nT = CartesianTags\nlabels = get_face_labeling(model)\nadd_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"inner\", T.face0YZ)                  # r = Ri\nadd_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"clamped\", [T.faceXY0⁺; T.faceXY1⁺]) # z = 0 and z = L\nadd_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"sym_y\", [T.faceX0Z; T.edge00Z; T.edge10Z])   # θ = 0, plane y = 0\nadd_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"sym_x\", [T.faceX1Z; T.edge01Z; T.edge11Z])   # θ = π/2, plane x = 0","title":"Geometry and mesh"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E03_Fibre_reinforced_cylinder/#Constitutive-model","page":"03 · Fibre-reinforced cylinder under internal pressure","text":"Parameters of the original HyperFEM example (artery-like: mu_1+mu_2 = 74.4 kPa, nearly incompressible). The first two fibre families (axial, circumferential) are very weak; the helices carry the load. Summing an isotropic and an anisotropic model gives a model whose functions take boldsymbol F followed by the four fibre directions.\n\nμ1, μ2 = 37.2e3, 37.2e3                                        # Pa\nλ = 100 * (μ1 + μ2)\nc1 = [4.68e-3, 2.9e-2, 28.16e3, 28.16e3]                       # Pa (axial, circumferential, helix +, helix −)\nc2 = [2.97e-7, 1.68e-6, 3.48, 3.48]                            # dimensionless\nmatrix = MooneyRivlin3D(λ=λ, μ1=μ1, μ2=μ2)\nphysmodel = matrix + HGO_4Fibers(c1=c1, c2=c2)\nkinematics = Kinematics(Mechano, Solid)\nF, H, _ = get_Kinematics(kinematics)                           # F(∇u'), H(F) = cof F","title":"Constitutive model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E03_Fibre_reinforced_cylinder/#Finite-element-spaces","page":"03 · Fibre-reinforced cylinder under internal pressure","text":"Q2 elements: the material is nearly incompressible, and trilinear elements lock (they are about 1.5 % too stiff in the Lamé check below on a mesh like this one).\n\norder = 2\nreffe = ReferenceFE(lagrangian, VectorValue{3,Float64}, order)\nΩ = Triangulation(model)\ndΩ = Measure(Ω, 2 * order)\nΓi = BoundaryTriangulation(model, tags=\"inner\")\ndΓi = Measure(Γi, 2 * order)\nN = get_normal_vector(Γi)                                       # reference outward normal on Γi","title":"Finite element spaces"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E03_Fibre_reinforced_cylinder/#Fibre-fields","page":"03 · Fibre-reinforced cylinder under internal pressure","text":"The directions are interpolated in a vector space without boundary conditions and passed to the model as finite element functions (the model normalises them at the quadrature points).\n\neθ(x) = VectorValue(-x[2], x[1], 0.0) / sqrt(x[1]^2 + x[2]^2)\nez(x) = VectorValue(0.0, 0.0, 1.0)\nVf = FESpace(model, reffe)\nfibres = (interpolate_everywhere(ez, Vf),                         # M₁: axial\n          interpolate_everywhere(eθ, Vf),                         # M₂: circumferential\n          interpolate_everywhere(x -> (eθ(x) + ez(x)) / √2, Vf),  # M₃: helix +45°\n          interpolate_everywhere(x -> (-eθ(x) + ez(x)) / √2, Vf)) # M₄: helix −45°","title":"Fibre fields"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E03_Fibre_reinforced_cylinder/#Weak-form","page":"03 · Fibre-reinforced cylinder under internal pressure","text":"The weak form of the model comes from residual/jacobian, with the fibres after the load factor; the pressure terms are written as in the equations above. ⋅ between a tensor and a vector is the matrix–vector product.\n\np = 5e3                                                         # Pa (≈ 37.5 mmHg)\np_final = FAST ? 0.2 * p : p\npressure_res(u, v, pΛ) = pΛ * ∫(((H ∘ (F ∘ ∇(u)')) ⋅ N) ⋅ v)dΓi\npressure_jac(u, du, v, pΛ) = pΛ * ∫((((F ∘ ∇(u)') × ∇(du)') ⋅ N) ⋅ v)dΓi\nres(Λ) = (u, v) -> residual(physmodel, kinematics, u, v, dΩ, Λ, fibres...) + pressure_res(u, v, Λ * p_final)\njac(Λ) = (u, du, v) -> jacobian(physmodel, kinematics, u, du, v, dΩ, Λ, fibres...) + pressure_jac(u, du, v, Λ * p_final)","title":"Weak form"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E03_Fibre_reinforced_cylinder/#Points-where-the-solution-is-read","page":"03 · Fibre-reinforced cylinder under internal pressure","text":"Three points of the inner surface at mid-length, at theta = 0 pi4 pi2. A finite element function can be evaluated at a vector of points (one search for all of them).\n\nθs = [0.0, π / 4, π / 2]\nprobe = [polar(Point(Ri, θ, L / 2)) for θ in θs]\ner(θ) = VectorValue(cos(θ), sin(θ), 0.0)\nradial(uh) = [u ⋅ er(θ) for (u, θ) in zip(uh(probe), θs)]","title":"Points where the solution is read"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E03_Fibre_reinforced_cylinder/#A-check-first:-the-isotropic-tube-at-a-small-pressure","page":"03 · Fibre-reinforced cylinder under internal pressure","text":"With the matrix alone, a small pressure and ends that can slide radially but not axially, the solution is Lamé's plane-strain solution u_r = A r + Br, where\n\nB = fracp2mu(R_i^-2 - R_o^-2) qquad A = fracmu B(lambda_L+mu) R_o^2\nqquad mu = mu_1+mu_2 qquad lambda_L = lambda + 2mu_2 \n\nwith the Lamé constants of the Mooney–Rivlin energy linearised at boldsymbol F = boldsymbol I. It checks the mesh, the symmetry conditions and the pressure term in a few seconds. The sliding ends need u_z = 0 and, on the edges they share with the symmetry planes, the symmetry component as well: three more tags.\n\nadd_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"ends\", [T.faceXY0; T.faceXY1; T.edge0Y0; T.edge1Y0; T.edge0Y1; T.edge1Y1])\nadd_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"ends_y\", [T.edgeX00⁺; T.edgeX01⁺])\nadd_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"ends_x\", [T.edgeX10⁺; T.edgeX11⁺])\nzero3 = [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]\nD_lame = BoundaryConditions(\n  BoundaryCondition(\"ends\", zero3; mask=(false, false, true)),\n  BoundaryCondition(\"ends_y\", zero3; mask=(false, true, true)),\n  BoundaryCondition(\"ends_x\", zero3; mask=(true, false, true)),\n  BoundaryCondition(\"sym_y\", zero3; mask=(false, true, false)),\n  BoundaryCondition(\"sym_x\", zero3; mask=(true, false, false)))\nV_lame = TestFESpace(Ω, reffe, D_lame, conformity=:H1)\nU_lame = TrialFESpace(V_lame, D_lame)\np_small = 50.0                                                  # Pa: strains of 0.2 %\nres_lame(Λ) = (u, v) -> residual(matrix, kinematics, u, v, dΩ) + pressure_res(u, v, Λ * p_small)\njac_lame(Λ) = (u, du, v) -> jacobian(matrix, kinematics, u, du, v, dΩ) + pressure_jac(u, du, v, Λ * p_small)\nlame_model = StaticNonlinearModel(res_lame, jac_lame, U_lame, V_lame, D_lame;\n  nls=NewtonRaphson(LUSolver(); maxiter=10, rtol=1e-10, verbose=false), backend=:compiled)\n_, flag_lame = solve!(lame_model; stepping=LoadStepping(nsteps=1))\nμL, λL = μ1 + μ2, λ + 2μ2\nB = p_small / (2μL * (1 / Ri^2 - 1 / Ro^2))\nA = μL * B / ((λL + μL) * Ro^2)\nur_lame = radial(get_state(lame_model))\nprintln(\"Lamé check: u_r(Ri) = \", ur_lame[2], \" cm, exact \", A * Ri + B / Ri, \" cm\")","title":"A check first: the isotropic tube at a small pressure"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E03_Fibre_reinforced_cylinder/#Boundary-conditions-of-the-clamped-tube","page":"03 · Fibre-reinforced cylinder under internal pressure","text":"D_bc = BoundaryConditions(\n  BoundaryCondition(\"clamped\", zero3),\n  BoundaryCondition(\"sym_y\", zero3; mask=(false, true, false)),\n  BoundaryCondition(\"sym_x\", zero3; mask=(true, false, false)))\nV = TestFESpace(Ω, reffe, D_bc, conformity=:H1)\nU = TrialFESpace(V, D_bc)\nprintln(\"free dofs: \", num_free_dofs(V))","title":"Boundary conditions of the clamped tube"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E03_Fibre_reinforced_cylinder/#Solver-and-computational-model","page":"03 · Fibre-reinforced cylinder under internal pressure","text":"backend=:compiled (tutorial 01) compiles the residual and the tangent, the pressure terms included, once; at every increment the model only rebinds them to the new load factor, without compiling again.\n\nnls = NewtonRaphson(LUSolver(); maxiter=20, rtol=1e-10, verbose=true)\ncomp_model = StaticNonlinearModel(res, jac, U, V, D_bc; nls=nls, backend=:compiled)\nuh = get_state(comp_model)","title":"Solver and computational model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E03_Fibre_reinforced_cylinder/#Post-processing","page":"03 · Fibre-reinforced cylinder under internal pressure","text":"After every converged increment: the pressure and the radial displacement at the three probes, and the displacement and fibre fields in a VTK file.\n\npressures, ur_history = Float64[], Vector{Float64}[]\nfunction record(post)\n  Λ = post.Λ[post.iter]\n  push!(pressures, Λ * p_final)\n  push!(ur_history, radial(uh))\n  pvd = get_pvd(post)\n  pvd[Λ] = createvtk(Ω, joinpath(outdir, \"step_$(post.iter).vtu\"),\n    cellfields=[\"u\" => uh, \"M_axial\" => fibres[1], \"M_circ\" => fibres[2], \"M_helix+\" => fibres[3], \"M_helix-\" => fibres[4]])\nend\npost = PostProcessor(comp_model, record; is_vtk=true, filepath=outdir)","title":"Post-processing"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E03_Fibre_reinforced_cylinder/#Solve","page":"03 · Fibre-reinforced cylinder under internal pressure","text":"The pressure is applied in equal increments; an increment whose Newton iteration fails is halved (up to five times in a row). The load does not enter through Dirichlet values, so ProjectDirichlet is not needed here.\n\nnsteps = FAST ? 4 : 20\nx, flag = solve!(comp_model; stepping=LoadStepping(nsteps=nsteps, maxbisec=5), post=post)   # also closes the .pvd\nfor (pk, ur) in zip(pressures, ur_history)\n  println(\"p = $(round(pk, digits=1)) Pa   u_r(Ri, L/2) = $(round(ur[2], sigdigits=10)) cm\")\nend","title":"Solve"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E03_Fibre_reinforced_cylinder/#Checks","page":"03 · Fibre-reinforced cylinder under internal pressure","text":"(a) The isotropic tube matches Lamé's solution within 0.5 % (measured: 0.13 % FAST, 0.19 % full; the rest is discretisation and the small geometric nonlinearity; trilinear elements, about 1.5 % too stiff, would fail). (b) The discrete problem is invariant under rotations about the axis, so the radial displacement is the same at the three probes and there is no twist. (c) A regression value of the final radial displacement (HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6, Gridap 0.20.8, Julia 1.13.0, macOS arm64).\n\n@assert flag_lame == 1 && flag == 1 \"the load stepping did not reach Λ = 1\"\n@assert abs(ur_lame[2] / (A * Ri + B / Ri) - 1) < 0.005 \"isotropic tube away from Lamé's solution\"\nur_end = ur_history[end]\n@assert maximum(abs.(ur_end .- ur_end[2])) < 1e-8 * abs(ur_end[2]) \"radial displacement not axisymmetric\"\nu45 = uh(probe[2])\n@assert abs(u45 ⋅ VectorValue(-sin(π / 4), cos(π / 4), 0.0)) < 1e-8 * abs(ur_end[2]) \"the tube twists\"\nur_ref = FAST ? 0.0393139021903048 : 0.260654941449461                        # cm, regression values\n@assert isapprox(ur_end[2], ur_ref; rtol=1e-5) \"radial displacement $(ur_end[2]) ≠ regression value $ur_ref\"","title":"Checks"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E03_Fibre_reinforced_cylinder/#Going-further","page":"03 · Fibre-reinforced cylinder under internal pressure","text":"Change the helix angle (here ±45°) or remove the helices: the tube becomes softer and, with unbalanced families, twists (the quarter model then no longer applies: mesh the whole tube with (0, 2π) in the map and remove the symmetry tags).\nIncrease the pressure: the exponential fibres stiffen and the pressure–radius curve turns up.\nTutorial 05 uses a single fibre family in an electro-active solid; tutorial 13 replaces LUSolver for larger meshes.","title":"Going further"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E12_Aorta_blood_flow/#Tutorial-12:-Pulsatile-blood-flow-in-a-patient-aorta","page":"12 · Pulsatile blood flow in a patient aorta","text":"Source · Notebook · nbviewer\n\nBlood is pumped by the heart into the aorta in short pulses and leaves it through the descending aorta and the three supra-aortic branches. We compute one cardiac cycle of the incompressible Navier–Stokes flow in a patient-specific (rigid) aorta, with the formulation of tutorial 11, a pulsatile inflow and three-element Windkessel models at the four outlets: each outlet sees the resistance and the compliance of the arterial tree downstream, which gives physiological pressures. The 3D flow and the four 0D models are coupled at every time step.\n\nMeasured on a Mac (M5 Max, 4 Julia threads, 4 BLAS threads, Julia 1.13.0, HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6, Gridap 0.20.8): 16 659 free dofs (P1–P1 on 33 643 tetrahedra). FAST (10 time steps) 92 s and full (one cycle, 200 time steps) 170 s, including compilation (the first step takes about 24 s); 0.21 s per time step after that. One assembly of the step Jacobian (the check at the end): 0.06 s with the compiled backend, 0.65 s with Gridap.\n\nWhat you will learn\n\nboundary terms and lumped (0D) models in a TransientModel: Robin outlet conditions and backflow stabilisation in the weak form, and an explicit 3D–0D coupling through the presolver and internal hooks;\na time-dependent Dirichlet condition, BoundaryCondition(tag, profile, t -> Q(t));\nthe compiled backend of HyperFEMKernels (backend=:compiled) for a 3D stabilised flow problem, and how to check that it takes its fast paths (HyperFEMKernels.work_summary);\nmass-conservation checks of a 3D–0D model and a comparison with a pure 0D model.","title":"Tutorial 12: Pulsatile blood flow in a patient aorta"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E12_Aorta_blood_flow/#Problem-statement","page":"12 · Pulsatile blood flow in a patient aorta","text":"Find the velocity boldsymbol u and the pressure p in the lumen Omega such that\n\nrholeft(partial_tboldsymbol u + (nablaboldsymbol u)boldsymbol uright) - nablacdotboldsymbolsigma = boldsymbol 0 \nqquad nablacdotboldsymbol u = 0  qquad\nboldsymbolsigma = -pboldsymbol I + 2muboldsymbolvarepsilon(boldsymbol u) \n\nwith blood as a Newtonian fluid, rho = 1050 kg/m³ and mu = 35 mPa s. We work in mm, s and g: the density is 105cdot 10^-3 g/mm³, the viscosity 35cdot 10^-3 g/(mm s) = Pa s, and pressures come out in Pa.\n\nInlet. The flow rate is a half sine during systole (period T = 1 s, systole T_s = 034 s, stroke volume V_s = 833 ml) and zero in diastole:\n\nQ(t) = fracpi V_s2T_ssinfracpi tT_s quad (0 le t le T_s) qquad Q(t) = 0 quad (T_s  t  T) \n\nThe velocity at the inlet is boldsymbol u = Q(t)boldsymbol g(boldsymbol x), with boldsymbol g a plug profile along the mean inward normal that vanishes on the rim of the inlet (no slip) and carries a unit flow rate. Wall: no slip, boldsymbol u = boldsymbol 0 (rigid wall).\n\nOutlets. At each outlet Gamma_i (descending aorta, brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid and left subclavian arteries) a three-element Windkessel (RCR) model relates the flow rate Q_i = int_Gamma_iboldsymbol ucdotboldsymbol nmathrm dGamma and the pressure: a proximal resistance Z_i in series with a capacitor C_i (the compliance of the arteries downstream) that discharges through a distal resistance R_i,\n\nboldsymbolsigmaboldsymbol n = -left(P_ci + Z_i Q_iright)boldsymbol n \nqquad C_i fracmathrm dP_cimathrm dt = Q_i - fracP_ciR_i \n\nThe compliance pressure P_ci is advanced with backward Euler using the flow rate of the previous step (explicit coupling), and the proximal term enters the weak form implicitly as a Robin term, int_Gamma_i Z_i A_i(boldsymbol ucdotboldsymbol n)(boldsymbol vcdotboldsymbol n)mathrm dGamma, which equals Z_i Q_iint_Gamma_iboldsymbol vcdotboldsymbol nmathrm dGamma when the normal velocity is uniform on the outlet (area A_i). The values of Z_i, C_i, R_i are those of the aorta example of HyperFEMKernels.jl (examples/aorta_cfd/aorta_flow.jl): the descending aorta from Mousavi et al. (2021, Table 6, case «Aneurysmal 1») and the three supra-aortic branches B1–B3 from Youssefi et al. (2018, Table 1, subject A).\n\nBackflow. During deceleration and diastole blood flows back into the domain through parts of the outlets, and the kinetic energy that enters there can make the computation blow up. The backflow stabilisation -rhoint_Gamma_i(boldsymbol wcdotboldsymbol n)_-boldsymbol ucdotboldsymbol vmathrm dGamma, with (a)_- = min(a 0), acts only where the flow enters (Bazilevs et al. 2009, DOI 10.1016/j.cma.2009.04.015; Esmaily-Moghadam et al. 2011, DOI 10.1007/s00466-011-0599-0).\n\nDiscretisation. As in tutorial 11: linear velocity and pressure (P1–P1) with SUPG, PSPG and LSIC stabilisation, BDF2 in time with an extrapolated advecting velocity boldsymbol w = 2boldsymbol u^n - boldsymbol u^n-1 (an Oseen problem: one linear system per step), Delta t = 5 ms. The element size is the edge of the regular tetrahedron with the volume of the cell, h = (6sqrt2K)^13.","title":"Problem statement"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E12_Aorta_blood_flow/#Setup","page":"12 · Pulsatile blood flow in a patient aorta","text":"using HyperFEM\nusing HyperFEMKernels      # the compiled backend (`backend=:compiled`) and its introspection\nusing Gridap\nusing GridapGmsh\nusing LinearAlgebra\nusing Printf\n\nconst FAST = get(ENV, \"HYPERFEM_TUTORIALS_FAST\", \"0\") == \"1\"   # reduced case for the test suite\noutdir = joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"data\", \"sims\", \"E12_Aorta_blood_flow\")\nsetupfolder(outdir)                                            # VTK output (not versioned)\n\nThe constants read by functions evaluated at quadrature points (the stabilisation parameters) or by the inflow Q(t) are const: a non-constant global read inside such a function is looked up dynamically at every evaluation, which makes the compiled Jacobian several times slower.\n\nconst ρ = 1050e-6                                              # g/mm³\nconst μ = 3.5e-3                                               # Pa s\nconst Tc = 1.0                                                 # s: cardiac period\nconst Ts = 0.34                                                # s: systole\nconst Vs = 83.3e3                                              # mm³: stroke volume (83.3 ml)\nconst Qmax = π * Vs / (2Ts)                                    # mm³/s: peak flow rate\nQin(t) = (τ = mod(t, Tc); τ <= Ts ? Qmax * sin(π * τ / Ts) : 0.0)\nconst mmHg = 133.322                                           # Pa","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E12_Aorta_blood_flow/#Geometry-and-mesh","page":"12 · Pulsatile blood flow in a patient aorta","text":"A patient aorta (ascending aorta, arch with three branches and descending aorta), 33 643 linear tetrahedra, with the physical surfaces inlet, wall, outlet (descending aorta), B1, B2, B3 (branches). The mesh is coarse: most of its nodes lie on the wall, so the boundary layers are not resolved; it is meant to show the method, not to give converged wall shear stresses.\n\nmodel = GmshDiscreteModel(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"data\", \"models\", \"aorta_fluido_h1p5_3p0.msh\"))\n\nGridapGmsh gives each physical surface its triangles, but an edge or a vertex shared by two surfaces belongs to only one of them. A Dirichlet condition needs all the nodes of its faces, so we add the edges and vertices of the faces of inlet and then of wall to their tags; the shared ones (the rim of the inlet) end up in the last tag, wall: the inflow profile vanishes on the rim.\n\nfunction close_boundary_tags!(model, names)\n  labels = get_face_labeling(model)\n  topo = Gridap.Geometry.get_grid_topology(model)\n  D = num_cell_dims(model)\n  for name in names\n    tag = findfirst(==(name), labels.tag_to_name)\n    faces = findall(Gridap.Geometry.get_face_mask(labels, name, D - 1))\n    for d in 0:D-2\n      f_to_d = Gridap.Geometry.get_faces(topo, D - 1, d)\n      ents = labels.d_to_dface_to_entity[d+1]\n      newent = maximum(maximum(e) for e in labels.d_to_dface_to_entity) + 1\n      for f in faces, e in f_to_d[f]\n        ents[e] = newent\n      end\n      push!(labels.tag_to_entities[tag], newent)\n    end\n  end\n  model\nend\nclose_boundary_tags!(model, [\"inlet\", \"wall\"])\n\nΩ = Triangulation(model)\ndΩ = Measure(Ω, 3)\nhe = CellField(map(v -> (6sqrt(2) * v)^(1 / 3), collect(get_cell_measure(Ω))), Ω)   # element size\nΓin = BoundaryTriangulation(model, tags=\"inlet\")\ndΓin = Measure(Γin, 2)\nnin = get_normal_vector(Γin)                                   # outward normal of the fluid\nAin = sum(∫(1.0)dΓin)\nn̄ = sum(∫(nin)dΓin) / Ain                                      # mean outward normal of the inlet","title":"Geometry and mesh"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E12_Aorta_blood_flow/#Windkessel-parameters","page":"12 · Pulsatile blood flow in a patient aorta","text":"The values are given in SI units (Pa s/m³ and m³/Pa) and converted to mm–s–g. ZA is Z_i A_i, the coefficient of the Robin term.\n\nouts = [\"outlet\", \"B1\", \"B2\", \"B3\"]\nSIR, SIC = 1e-9, 1e9                                           # Pa s/m³ → Pa s/mm³, m³/Pa → mm³/Pa\nZ = [1.5e7, 1.36e8, 2.46e8, 1.74e8] .* SIR\nC = [1.05e-8, 4.83e-10, 2.92e-10, 3.93e-10] .* SIC\nR = [2.5e8, 9.23e8, 1.53e9, 1.13e9] .* SIR\nΓo = [BoundaryTriangulation(model, tags=o) for o in outs]\ndΓo = [Measure(Γ, 2) for Γ in Γo]\nno = [get_normal_vector(Γ) for Γ in Γo]\nZA = [Z[i] * sum(∫(1.0)dΓo[i]) for i in eachindex(outs)]\nflux(uh, i) = sum(∫(uh ⋅ no[i])dΓo[i])                         # outflow rate through outlet i\n\nThe same four Windkessel models attached to a rigid, inviscid aorta (a single node with pressure P) give a 0D model of the whole circulation downstream: Q(t) = sum_i (P - P_ci)Z_i and C_idot P_ci = (P - P_ci)Z_i - P_ciR_i. Integrated over 30 cycles (RK4) it reaches its periodic state, which gives the compliance pressures at t = 0 (the initial values of the 3D computation: the flow starts at rest, at the end of diastole) and a reference for the pressures and the split of the flow.\n\nfunction windkessel_0d(Qin, Z, C, R; T, h=1e-4, ncycles=30)\n  P0(t, Pc) = (Qin(t) + sum(Pc ./ Z)) / sum(1 ./ Z)\n  f(t, Pc) = ((P0(t, Pc) .- Pc) ./ Z .- Pc ./ R) ./ C\n  rk4(t, y) = (k1 = f(t, y); k2 = f(t + h / 2, y + h / 2 * k1); k3 = f(t + h / 2, y + h / 2 * k2);\n               k4 = f(t + h, y + h * k3); y + h / 6 * (k1 + 2k2 + 2k3 + k4))\n  nper = round(Int, T / h)\n  Qm = sum(Qin(k * h) for k in 0:nper-1) / nper\n  Pc = R ./ (Z .+ R) .* (Qm / sum(1 ./ (Z .+ R)))             # mean state as the starting point\n  for k in 0:ncycles*nper-1\n    Pc = rk4(k * h, Pc)\n  end\n  Pc0 = copy(Pc)\n  ts, P, Q = Float64[], Float64[], Vector{Float64}[]           # one period of the periodic state\n  for k in 0:nper\n    t = k * h; p = P0(t, Pc)\n    push!(ts, t); push!(P, p); push!(Q, (p .- Pc) ./ Z)\n    Pc = rk4(t, Pc)\n  end\n  (; Pc0, ts, P, Q)\nend\nref0d = windkessel_0d(Qin, Z, C, R; T=Tc)\n@printf(\"0D model: P from %.1f to %.1f mmHg, Pc(0) = %s mmHg\\n\", minimum(ref0d.P) / mmHg, maximum(ref0d.P) / mmHg,\n        string(round.(ref0d.Pc0 ./ mmHg, digits=1)))","title":"Windkessel parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E12_Aorta_blood_flow/#Boundary-conditions-and-finite-element-spaces","page":"12 · Pulsatile blood flow in a patient aorta","text":"The plug profile is the interpolation of -barboldsymbol n on the inlet with zero on the wall (and so on the rim); its discrete flow rate q_1 normalises it, so that the imposed flow rate is exactly Q(t). The profile and the time function are given separately: BoundaryCondition(\"inlet\", g, Qin) imposes boldsymbol g(boldsymbol x)Q(t). The profile is built by a function that returns a closure, so that the pointwise function reads its own captured value and not a global variable.\n\nreffeu = ReferenceFE(lagrangian, VectorValue{3,Float64}, 1)\nreffep = ReferenceFE(lagrangian, Float64, 1)\nuniform(a) = x -> a\nVaux = TestFESpace(model, reffeu; conformity=:H1, dirichlet_tags=[\"inlet\", \"wall\"])\nu1 = FEFunction(TrialFESpace(Vaux, [uniform(-n̄), uniform(zero(n̄))]), zeros(num_free_dofs(Vaux)))\nq1 = -sum(∫(u1 ⋅ nin)dΓin)\nD_bc = MultiFieldBC([\n  BoundaryConditions(\n    BoundaryCondition(\"inlet\", uniform(-n̄ / q1), Qin),\n    BoundaryCondition(\"wall\", [0.0, 0.0, 0.0])),\n  NothingBC()])\nVu = TestFESpace(model, reffeu, D_bc[1], conformity=:H1)\nVp = TestFESpace(model, reffep, D_bc[2], conformity=:H1)\nV = MultiFieldFESpace([Vu, Vp])\nU = MultiFieldFESpace([TrialFESpace(Vu, D_bc[1], 0.0), TrialFESpace(Vp, D_bc[2], 0.0)])","title":"Boundary conditions and finite element spaces"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E12_Aorta_blood_flow/#Weak-form","page":"12 · Pulsatile blood flow in a patient aorta","text":"The volume terms are those of tutorial 11 (with h of tetrahedra); the outlets add the Robin term of the proximal resistances and the backflow stabilisation, which are part of the operator, and the compliance pressures P_ci, which are known numbers of the step and only enter the residual. Pc holds the compliance pressures of the step being computed and Pcn those of the last accepted step.\n\nPcn = copy(ref0d.Pc0)\nPc = copy(Pcn)\nτsupg(u, h) = ((2 * norm(u) / h)^2 + 9 * (4μ / (ρ * h^2))^2)^(-1 / 2)\nτpspg(u, h) = τsupg(u, h) / ρ\nτlsic(u, h) = ρ * norm(u) * h * 0.5\nε(∇u) = 0.5 * (∇u + ∇u')\nnegpart(a) = 0.5 * (a - abs(a))\nfunction advecting(cu)\n  a, b = cu.step == 1 ? (1.0, 0.0) : (2.0, 1.0)\n  a * cu.u⁻ - b * cu.u⁻⁻\nend\n\nThe bilinear part of a step, with the advecting velocity w, the velocity un of the previous step (for the tau's) and the time derivative ut of the unknown:\n\nfunction step_form(w, un, (u, p), ut, (v, q))\n  τs = τsupg ∘ (un, he); τp = τpspg ∘ (un, he); τl = τlsic ∘ (un, he)\n  Ru = ρ * ut + ρ * (∇(u)' ⋅ w)\n  a = ∫(ρ * (ut ⋅ v))dΩ + ∫(2μ * (ε ∘ (∇(u))) ⊙ (ε ∘ (∇(v))))dΩ + ∫(ρ * ((∇(u)' ⋅ w) ⋅ v))dΩ -\n      ∫(p * (∇ ⋅ v))dΩ - ∫(q * (∇ ⋅ u))dΩ +\n      ∫(τs * ((∇(v)' ⋅ w) ⋅ Ru))dΩ + ∫(τs * ((∇(v)' ⋅ w) ⋅ ∇(p)))dΩ -\n      ∫(τp * (∇(q) ⋅ Ru))dΩ - ∫(τp * (∇(q) ⋅ ∇(p)))dΩ +\n      ∫(τl * (∇ ⋅ v) * (∇ ⋅ u))dΩ\n  for i in eachindex(outs)\n    a = a + ∫(ZA[i] * (u ⋅ no[i]) * (v ⋅ no[i]))dΓo[i] - ∫(ρ * (negpart ∘ (w ⋅ no[i])) * (u ⋅ v))dΓo[i]\n  end\n  a\nend\nfunction res(ctx)\n  cu = ctx[1]\n  un = cu.u⁻; w = advecting(cu)\n  ((u, p), (v, q)) -> begin\n    r = step_form(w, un, (u, p), ∂t(cu, u), (v, q))\n    for i in eachindex(outs)\n      r = r + ∫(Pc[i] * (v ⋅ no[i]))dΓo[i]\n    end\n    r\n  end\nend\nfunction jac(ctx)\n  cu = ctx[1]\n  un = cu.u⁻; w = advecting(cu)\n  ((u, p), (du, dp), (v, q)) -> step_form(w, un, (du, dp), δ∂t(cu, du), (v, q))\nend","title":"Weak form"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E12_Aorta_blood_flow/#Solver-and-computational-model","page":"12 · Pulsatile blood flow in a patient aorta","text":"backend=:compiled assembles with the compiled, threaded kernels of HyperFEMKernels. The operator is compiled in the first step; in the next steps only the numbers that change (the BDF coefficients, the compliance pressures, the Dirichlet values) are read again from the weak form, and the compiled code, the sparsity pattern and the tables are reused. The step system has 16 659 unknowns: a sparse LU factorisation costs about 0.06 s, as much as one assembly of the Jacobian, so a direct solver is the right choice at this size (tutorial 13 shows the iterative solvers for larger problems).\n\nThe 0D models are advanced with two hooks. presolver(t, Δt) runs before every attempt of a step: it computes the compliance pressures of the attempt from the flow rates of the last accepted state. internal(xh, xh⁻) runs when the step is accepted: it makes them the new accepted values. With a fixed time step there are no rejected attempts, but the pair Pc/Pcn keeps the coupling right if a step were halved.\n\nnls = NewtonRaphson(LUSolver(); verbose=0)\naccept_windkessel(xh, xh⁻) = (Pcn .= Pc)\nflow = TransientModel(res, jac, U, V, D_bc; scheme=BDF(2), tangent_update=:step, nls=nls,\n                      backend=:compiled, internal=accept_windkessel)\n\nThe flow rates of the last accepted state are computed once, by the post-processing function below (which runs after every accepted step), and kept in Qn (zero at rest, before the first step).\n\nQn = zeros(length(outs))\nfunction windkessel!(t, Δt)\n  for i in eachindex(outs)\n    Pc[i] = (Pcn[i] + Δt * Qn[i] / C[i]) / (1 + Δt / (R[i] * C[i]))\n  end\nend","title":"Solver and computational model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E12_Aorta_blood_flow/#Post-processing","page":"12 · Pulsatile blood flow in a patient aorta","text":"After every step we store the inflow rate (measured on the solution), the four outflow rates, the compliance pressures, the outlet pressures P_ci + Z_iQ_i, the mean pressure on the inlet and the largest velocity component; the flow is written to VTK a few times per cycle.\n\nΔt = 0.005\nnsteps = FAST ? 10 : round(Int, Tc / Δt)\nvtk_every = FAST ? nsteps : 20\nhist = (t=Float64[], qin=Float64[], qout=Vector{Float64}[], Pc=Vector{Float64}[], Pout=Vector{Float64}[],\n        pin=Float64[], umax=Float64[], wall=Float64[])\ntclock = Ref(time())\nfunction record(post)\n  uh, ph = get_state(flow)\n  t = post.Λ[end]\n  q = [flux(uh, i) for i in eachindex(outs)]\n  Qn .= q\n  push!(hist.t, t); push!(hist.qin, -sum(∫(uh ⋅ nin)dΓin)); push!(hist.qout, q); push!(hist.Pc, copy(Pc))\n  push!(hist.Pout, Pc .+ Z .* q); push!(hist.pin, sum(∫(ph)dΓin) / Ain)\n  push!(hist.umax, maximum(abs, get_free_dof_values(uh))); push!(hist.wall, time() - tclock[])\n  tclock[] = time()\n  if FAST || post.iter % 10 == 0\n    @printf(\"step %3d  t = %.3f s  Q_in = %6.1f ml/s  Q_out = %s ml/s  P_in = %5.1f mmHg  %.2f s\\n\", post.iter, t,\n            hist.qin[end] / 1e3, string(round.(q ./ 1e3, digits=1)), hist.pin[end] / mmHg, hist.wall[end])\n  end\n  if post.iter % vtk_every == 0\n    get_pvd(post)[t] = createvtk(Ω, joinpath(outdir, \"aorta_$(post.iter)\"), cellfields=[\"u\" => uh, \"p\" => ph])\n  end\nend\npost = PostProcessor(flow, record; is_vtk=true, filepath=outdir)","title":"Post-processing"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E12_Aorta_blood_flow/#Solve","page":"12 · Pulsatile blood flow in a patient aorta","text":"println(\"$(num_cells(Ω)) tetrahedra, $(num_free_dofs(U)) free dofs, $nsteps steps of $Δt s\")\ntclock[] = time()\nx, flag = solve!(flow; stepping=TimeStepping(; Δt=Δt, t_end=nsteps * Δt), post=post, presolver=windkessel!)\nsteady = hist.wall[3:end]                                       # the first two steps compile\n@printf(\"mean time per step after the first two: %.3f s\\n\", sum(steady) / length(steady))","title":"Solve"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E12_Aorta_blood_flow/#Checks","page":"12 · Pulsatile blood flow in a patient aorta","text":"Mass conservation. The pressure test functions include the constants, and with q = 1 the weak form says int_Omeganablacdotboldsymbol umathrm dOmega = 0 (the stabilisation terms contain nabla q): the inflow equals the sum of the outflows at every step, to the precision of the linear solver. The measured inflow is the imposed Q(t). The tolerance of these checks and of the volume budget below is 10^-10 relative to the peak flow: the LU solution is accurate to about 10^-12 (we measured 9cdot 10^-13 for the mass balance), and the budget inherits that error.\n\nqout_sum = [sum(q) for q in hist.qout]\nmass_err = maximum(abs.(hist.qin .- qout_sum)) / Qmax\ninflow_err = maximum(abs.(hist.qin .- Qin.(hist.t))) / Qmax\nprintln(\"max |Q_in - ΣQ_out| / Q_max = $mass_err,  max |Q_in - Q(t)| / Q_max = $inflow_err\")\n@assert flag == 1 \"the time loop did not complete\"\n@assert length(hist.t) == nsteps \"a time step was bisected\"\n@assert mass_err < 1e-10 \"mass is not conserved: max |Q_in - ΣQ_out| / Q_max = $mass_err\"\n@assert inflow_err < 1e-10 \"the inflow rate differs from Q(t): $inflow_err\"\n\nVolume budget of the 3D–0D system. The volume that entered up to the previous step is stored in the compliances or has left through the distal resistances: summing the update of P_ci over the steps, Delta tsum_kNQ_in^k = sum_i C_i(P_ci^N - P_ci^0) + Delta tsum_kle Nsum_i P_ci^kR_i (the flow rate is lagged by one step, and Q^0 = 0: the flow starts at rest).\n\nVin = Δt * sum(hist.qin[1:end-1])\nVstored = sum(C .* (hist.Pc[end] .- ref0d.Pc0))\nVdrained = Δt * sum(sum(Pk ./ R) for Pk in hist.Pc)\nbudget_err = abs(Vin - Vstored - Vdrained) / (Δt * nsteps * Qmax)\n@printf(\"volume budget: in %.1f ml = stored %.1f ml + drained %.1f ml  (relative error %.1e)\\n\",\n        Vin / 1e3, Vstored / 1e3, Vdrained / 1e3, budget_err)\n@assert budget_err < 1e-10 \"the volume budget of the Windkessel models does not close: $budget_err\"\n\nPhysiological range. The pressures at the outlets and at the inlet stay in the physiological range of the systemic arteries, and the velocity stays bounded (the backflow stabilisation does its job).\n\nPall = reduce(vcat, hist.Pout)\n@printf(\"outlet pressures %.1f–%.1f mmHg, inlet %.1f–%.1f mmHg, max velocity component %.0f mm/s\\n\",\n        minimum(Pall) / mmHg, maximum(Pall) / mmHg, minimum(hist.pin) / mmHg, maximum(hist.pin) / mmHg, maximum(hist.umax))\n@assert all(p -> 50mmHg < p < 180mmHg, Pall) \"outlet pressure out of the physiological range\"\n@assert all(p -> 50mmHg < p < 180mmHg, hist.pin) \"inlet pressure out of the physiological range\"\n@assert maximum(hist.umax) < 3000 \"velocity above 3 m/s\"\n\nRegression values of the final state (HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6, Gridap 0.20.8, Julia 1.13.0, macOS arm64): the norm of the vector of free values (dominated by the pressures, in Pa) and the mean inlet pressure (Pa), after 10 steps (FAST) and after one cycle.\n\nprintln(\"regression: ‖x‖ = $(norm(x)), Pc = $(hist.Pc[end]), P_in = $(hist.pin[end])\")\nif FAST\n  @assert isapprox(norm(x), 1.5565908708897454e6, rtol=1e-5) \"regression: ‖x‖ = $(norm(x))\"\n  @assert isapprox(hist.pin[end], 16656.492743907616, rtol=1e-5) \"regression: P_in = $(hist.pin[end])\"\nelse\n  @assert isapprox(norm(x), 1.0728389663079288e6, rtol=1e-5) \"regression: ‖x‖ = $(norm(x))\"\n  @assert isapprox(hist.pin[end], 10492.936373140392, rtol=1e-5) \"regression: P_in = $(hist.pin[end])\"\nend","title":"Checks"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E12_Aorta_blood_flow/#Comparison-with-the-0D-model-(full-run)","page":"12 · Pulsatile blood flow in a patient aorta","text":"Over one cycle the 3D–0D model and the 0D model see the same inflow and the same Windkessel models; they differ by the resistance and the inertia of the aorta itself and by the spatial distribution of the pressure at the outlets. We compare the volume that leaves through each outlet in the cycle and the extreme pressures of the descending aorta. We measured differences of 0.2–2.7 % in the volumes (the branches, whose flow depends more on the 3D pressure distribution, differ most) and of about 1 % in the extreme pressures; the tolerances are 5 % and 3 %.\n\nif !FAST\n  vol3d = Δt .* reduce(+, hist.qout)\n  w0 = [k == 1 || k == length(ref0d.ts) ? 0.5 : 1.0 for k in eachindex(ref0d.ts)]\n  vol0d = (ref0d.ts[2] - ref0d.ts[1]) .* reduce(+, w0 .* ref0d.Q)\n  Pdesc = [P[1] for P in hist.Pout]\n  println(\"outlet                volume 3D (ml)   volume 0D (ml)\")\n  for i in eachindex(outs)\n    @printf(\"  %-8s           %10.2f       %10.2f\\n\", outs[i], vol3d[i] / 1e3, vol0d[i] / 1e3)\n  end\n  @printf(\"descending aorta: P 3D %.1f–%.1f mmHg, 0D %.1f–%.1f mmHg\\n\", minimum(Pdesc) / mmHg, maximum(Pdesc) / mmHg,\n          minimum(ref0d.P) / mmHg, maximum(ref0d.P) / mmHg)\n  @assert all(isapprox.(vol3d ./ sum(vol3d), vol0d ./ sum(vol0d), rtol=0.05)) \"flow split away from the 0D model\"\n  @assert isapprox(maximum(Pdesc), maximum(ref0d.P), rtol=0.03) \"systolic pressure away from the 0D model\"\n  @assert isapprox(minimum(Pdesc), minimum(ref0d.P), rtol=0.03) \"diastolic pressure away from the 0D model\"\nend","title":"Comparison with the 0D model (full run)"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E12_Aorta_blood_flow/#Efficiency-of-the-compiled-backend-(full-run)","page":"12 · Pulsatile blood flow in a patient aorta","text":"With Gridap's assembly (backend=:lazy) the Jacobian alone takes 0.65 s and dominates the step; with the compiled backend a step of 0.21 s splits into Jacobian 0.06 s, residual 0.04 s, LU 0.06 s and the rest (see below). Running this tutorial with backend=:lazy gives the same solution (‖x‖ equal to 1e-13 in FAST) at about 1.0 s per step. We build, on the final state, the Jacobian of a BDF2 step with the same forms, assemble it with Gridap and with a KernelOperator of HyperFEMKernels (what backend=:compiled does inside the model), and check that both give the same matrix and that the compiled one is at least four times faster: we measured eleven times, and a non-constant global read by a stabilisation parameter (see the setup) makes the compiled Jacobian about eight times slower, which this margin catches. HyperFEMKernels.work_summary lists the path each group of integrals takes: :factorised or :linear_maps are the fast paths; :duals (automatic differentiation at every point) would point at a weak form or a pointwise function the compiler could not analyse. We measured (4 threads) 0.06 s for the compiled Jacobian and 0.65 s for Gridap's, with the volume group and the four outlet groups all :factorised and nothing left to Gridap. Inside the model a step costs about 0.21 s: Jacobian 0.06 s, residual 0.04 s, LU factorisation 0.06 s, and the rest is the update of the operator with the numbers of the step and the post-processing.\n\nif !FAST\n  uN = get_state(flow)[1]\n  c0 = 1.5 / Δt\n  kres(x, y) = step_form(uN, uN, x, c0 * x[1], y)\n  kjac(x, dx, y) = step_form(uN, uN, dx, c0 * dx[1], y)\n  x0 = zeros(num_free_dofs(U))\n  gop = Gridap.FESpaces.get_algebraic_operator(FEOperator(kres, kjac, U, V))\n  kop = KernelOperator(kres, kjac, U, V)\n  Ag = Gridap.Algebra.allocate_jacobian(gop, x0)\n  Ak = Gridap.Algebra.allocate_jacobian(kop, x0)\n  Gridap.Algebra.jacobian!(Ag, gop, x0); Gridap.Algebra.jacobian!(Ak, kop, x0)   # warm-up (compilation)\n  tg = minimum(@elapsed(Gridap.Algebra.jacobian!(Ag, gop, x0)) for _ in 1:3)\n  tk = minimum(@elapsed(Gridap.Algebra.jacobian!(Ak, kop, x0)) for _ in 1:3)\n  ws = HyperFEMKernels.work_summary(kop)\n  for g in ws.groups\n    g.kind === :jacobian && println(\"  Jacobian group $(g.group): $(g.domain), $(g.cells) cells, path $(g.path)\")\n  end\n  @printf(\"Jacobian assembly: compiled %.3f s, Gridap %.3f s (%d threads); fallback groups: %d\\n\", tk, tg,\n          Threads.nthreads(), ws.fallback_jacobian)\n  @assert norm(Ak - Ag) < 1e-10 * norm(Ag) \"the compiled Jacobian differs from Gridap's\"\n  @assert ws.fallback_jacobian == 0 \"part of the Jacobian falls back to Gridap\"\n  @assert all(g -> g.path !== :duals, filter(g -> g.kind === :jacobian, ws.groups)) \"a Jacobian group takes the slow :duals path\"\n  @assert tk < tg / 4 \"the compiled Jacobian ($tk s) is not four times faster than Gridap's ($tg s)\"\nend","title":"Efficiency of the compiled backend (full run)"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E12_Aorta_blood_flow/#Going-further","page":"12 · Pulsatile blood flow in a patient aorta","text":"Two or more cycles: nsteps = 2 * round(Int, Tc / Δt). The compliance pressures start from the periodic state of the 0D model, so the second cycle differs little from the first.\nTaylor–Hood P2–P1 (99 141 dofs on this mesh) needs no PSPG and resolves the flow better; with quadratic velocities the SUPG residual also contains the viscous term -muDeltaboldsymbol u^n, which must be computed (for instance by projecting nablaboldsymbol u^n onto discontinuous P1 functions).\nA realistic inflow profile, from the solution of a Poisson problem on the inlet plus an eccentric valve jet, is in examples/aorta_cfd/aorta_flow.jl of HyperFEMKernels.jl, which also compares the assembly of that P2–P1 problem with Gridap and with HyperFEMKernels.\nbackend=:lazy assembles with Gridap: same solution, slower steps.\nTutorial 11 validates the same formulation against the cylinder benchmark.\nReferences: I. E. Vignon-Clementel et al., Outflow boundary conditions for three-dimensional finite element modeling of blood flow and pressure in arteries, Comput. Methods Appl. Mech. Eng. 195 (2006) 3776–3796, DOI 10.1016/j.cma.2005.04.014; T. E. Tezduyar, Stabilized finite element formulations for incompressible flow computations, Adv. Appl. Mech. 28 (1992) 1–44, DOI 10.1016/S0065-2156(08)70153-4; S. J. Mousavi, R. Jayendiran, S. Farzaneh, S. Campisi, M. Viallon, P. Croisille, S. Avril, Coupling hemodynamics with mechanobiology in patient-specific computational models of ascending thoracic aortic aneurysms, Comput. Methods Programs Biomed. 205 (2021) 106107, DOI 10.1016/j.cmpb.2021.106107 (Windkessel parameters of the descending aorta); P. Youssefi, A. Gomez, C. Arthurs, R. Sharma, M. Jahangiri, C. A. Figueroa, Impact of patient-specific inflow velocity profile on hemodynamics of the thoracic aorta, J. Biomech. Eng. 140 (2018) 011002, DOI 10.1115/1.4037857 (Windkessel parameters of the branches).","title":"Going further"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E05_ElectroMechanics_anisotropic/#Tutorial-05:-Anisotropic-electro-mechanics","page":"05 · Anisotropic electro-mechanics","text":"Source · Notebook · nbviewer\n\nThe dielectric elastomer plate of tutorial 04, now reinforced by a family of stiff fibres lying in the plane of the plate at 45° between its width and its length. The fibres resist the in-plane elongation of the active layer along their direction, so the plate no longer deforms symmetrically: besides bending, it shears and twists. The constitutive model is built by adding three models, and the fibre direction enters the weak form as an extra field.\n\nWhat you will learn\n\ncomposing constitutive models with + (isotropic matrix, fibres, dielectric);\npassing a field of material directions to an anisotropic model (the extra arguments vars...);\nthe field-by-field helpers residual(model, Mechano, ...), residual(model, Electro, ...) and the coupling tangent jacobian(model, ElectroMechano, ...), which accept those extra arguments;\ndetecting the effect of anisotropy through a broken symmetry.\n\nMeasured on a Mac M5 Max (Julia 1.13.0, 4 threads, 4 BLAS threads; HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6, Gridap 0.20.8; compiled backend), whole run including compilation: FAST 45 s, full 46 s (20 increments, 109 Newton iterations, 16 s in solve! including its compilation); 1 528 free dofs. One assembly of the tangent: 0.7 ms compiled, 13 ms with Gridap's lazy evaluation.","title":"Tutorial 05: Anisotropic electro-mechanics"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E05_ElectroMechanics_anisotropic/#Problem-statement","page":"05 · Anisotropic electro-mechanics","text":"Geometry, boundary conditions and weak form are those of tutorial 04. The free energy adds to the neo-Hookean and ideal-dielectric energies the Holzapfel–Gasser–Ogden energy of one family of fibres with unit direction boldsymbol N in the reference configuration,\n\nPsi = Psi_mathrmNH(boldsymbol F) + fracc_14 c_2left(e^c_2 (I_4 - 1)^2 - 1right)\n     - fracvarepsilon2Jboldsymbol H boldsymbol E_0^2 \nqquad I_4 = boldsymbol Fboldsymbol N cdot boldsymbol Fboldsymbol N \n\nwith c_1 = 10, c_2 = 20 (in units of mu = 1) and boldsymbol N = (1 0 1)sqrt 2: the fibres lie in the plane of the plate, at 45° between the width x and the length z. I_4 is the squared stretch of the fibres. (This form of the energy acts in compression too: fibres that can only be pulled would need a switch on I_4  1.)\n\nReflecting the plate about its mid-plane x = 4 maps boldsymbol N to (-1 0 1)sqrt 2, a different material: the symmetry of tutorial 04 is lost.","title":"Problem statement"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E05_ElectroMechanics_anisotropic/#Setup","page":"05 · Anisotropic electro-mechanics","text":"using HyperFEM\nusing Gridap\nusing HyperFEMKernels                                          # compiled backend (`backend=:compiled`)\nusing LinearAlgebra: norm\n\nconst FAST = get(ENV, \"HYPERFEM_TUTORIALS_FAST\", \"0\") == \"1\"   # reduced case for the test suite\noutdir = joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"data\", \"sims\", \"E05_ElectroMechanics_anisotropic\")\nsetupfolder(outdir)                                            # VTK output (not versioned)","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E05_ElectroMechanics_anisotropic/#Geometry-and-mesh","page":"05 · Anisotropic electro-mechanics","text":"model = GmshDiscreteModel(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"data\", \"models\", \"test_static_EM.msh\"))\nlabels = get_face_labeling(model)\nadd_tag_from_vertex_filter!(labels, \"tip\", model, x -> abs(x[3] - 40.0) < 1e-8)","title":"Geometry and mesh"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E05_ElectroMechanics_anisotropic/#Constitutive-model","page":"05 · Anisotropic electro-mechanics","text":"+ composes models: an isotropic and an anisotropic mechanical model give an anisotropic mechanical model, and a mechanical and an electrical model give an ElectroMechModel. The derivatives of the composition are the sums of the derivatives; they now take the fibre direction as a third argument, (boldsymbol F boldsymbol E_0 boldsymbol N).\n\nphysmodel = NeoHookean3D(λ=10.0, μ=1.0) + HGO_1Fiber(c1=10.0, c2=20.0) + IdealDielectric(ε=1.0)\nk = (Kinematics(Mechano, Solid), Kinematics(Electro, Solid))","title":"Constitutive model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E05_ElectroMechanics_anisotropic/#Boundary-conditions","page":"05 · Anisotropic electro-mechanics","text":"The full case stops at 0.25 instead of the 0.3 of tutorial 04: with the fibres, Newton's iterations diverge at about 0.27 (the iterations of the previous increments already converge erratically) and, in HyperFEM 0.3.0, the run ends with an uncaught SingularException of the LU factorisation instead of a bisection.\n\nV0 = FAST ? 0.1 : 0.25                                         # voltage between the electrodes\nnsteps = FAST ? 5 : 20\nDu = BoundaryConditions(BoundaryCondition(\"fixedup\", [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]))\nDφ = BoundaryConditions(BoundaryCondition(\"midsuf\", 0.0, Λ -> Λ), BoundaryCondition(\"topsuf\", V0, Λ -> Λ))\nD_bc = MultiFieldBC([Du, Dφ])","title":"Boundary conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E05_ElectroMechanics_anisotropic/#Finite-element-spaces","page":"05 · Anisotropic electro-mechanics","text":"order = 1\nreffeu = ReferenceFE(lagrangian, VectorValue{3,Float64}, order)\nreffeφ = ReferenceFE(lagrangian, Float64, order)\nVu = TestFESpace(model, reffeu, D_bc[1], conformity=:H1)\nVφ = TestFESpace(model, reffeφ, D_bc[2], conformity=:H1)\nUu = TrialFESpace(Vu, D_bc[1], 0.0)\nUφ = TrialFESpace(Vφ, D_bc[2], 0.0)\nV = MultiFieldFESpace([Vu, Vφ])\nU = MultiFieldFESpace([Uu, Uφ])\nΩ = Triangulation(model)\ndΩ = Measure(Ω, 2 * order)\nprintln(\"free dofs: \", num_free_dofs(U), \" (u: \", num_free_dofs(Uu), \", φ: \", num_free_dofs(Uφ), \")\")","title":"Finite element spaces"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E05_ElectroMechanics_anisotropic/#Fibre-field","page":"05 · Anisotropic electro-mechanics","text":"The direction is a finite element field, so it could vary in space (read from a file, or computed, as the fibres of tutorial 03). Here it is uniform. We give it unit length; HGO_1Fiber also normalises it at every quadrature point, so only its direction matters.\n\nVN = FESpace(model, reffeu)                                    # no boundary conditions\nNh = interpolate_everywhere(VectorValue(1.0, 0.0, 1.0) / sqrt(2.0), VN)","title":"Fibre field"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E05_ElectroMechanics_anisotropic/#Weak-form","page":"05 · Anisotropic electro-mechanics","text":"The forms of tutorial 04 with Nh appended to the arguments of every constitutive function. The helpers do it for us: every one of them takes the extra arguments of the model after the load factor Lambda. The residual is the sum of the mechanical and electrical equations, and the tangent the sum of the two diagonal blocks and the two coupling blocks (ElectroMechano).\n\nres(Λ) = ((u, φ), (v, vφ)) ->\n  residual(physmodel, Mechano, k, (u, φ), v, dΩ, Λ, Nh) +\n  residual(physmodel, Electro, k, (u, φ), vφ, dΩ, Λ, Nh)\n\njac(Λ) = ((u, φ), (du, dφ), (v, vφ)) ->\n  jacobian(physmodel, Mechano, k, (u, φ), du, v, dΩ, Λ, Nh) +\n  jacobian(physmodel, Electro, k, (u, φ), dφ, vφ, dΩ, Λ, Nh) +\n  jacobian(physmodel, ElectroMechano, k, (u, φ), (du, dφ), (v, vφ), dΩ, Λ, Nh)","title":"Weak form"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E05_ElectroMechanics_anisotropic/#Solver-and-computational-model","page":"05 · Anisotropic electro-mechanics","text":"The compiled backend (tutorial 01) also accepts the fibre field: Nh is read at the quadrature points like the state.\n\nnls = NewtonRaphson(LUSolver(); maxiter=20, atol=1e-10, rtol=1e-8, verbose=true)\ncomp_model = StaticNonlinearModel(res, jac, U, V, D_bc; nls=nls, backend=:compiled)\nxh = get_state(comp_model)\nuh, φh = xh","title":"Solver and computational model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E05_ElectroMechanics_anisotropic/#Post-processing","page":"05 · Anisotropic electro-mechanics","text":"The mean displacement of the free end at every increment, and the VTK files (with the fibre field).\n\nΓtip = BoundaryTriangulation(model, tags=\"tip\")\ndΓtip = Measure(Γtip, 2 * order)\nAtip = sum(∫(1.0)dΓtip)\nvoltage, tip_u = Float64[], VectorValue{3,Float64}[]\nfunction record(post)\n  Λ = post.Λ[post.iter]\n  push!(voltage, Λ * V0)\n  push!(tip_u, sum(∫(uh)dΓtip) / Atip)\n  pvd = get_pvd(post)\n  pvd[Λ] = createvtk(Ω, joinpath(outdir, \"step_$(post.iter).vtu\"), cellfields=[\"u\" => uh, \"φ\" => φh, \"N\" => Nh])\nend\npost = PostProcessor(comp_model, record; is_vtk=true, filepath=outdir)","title":"Post-processing"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E05_ElectroMechanics_anisotropic/#Solve","page":"05 · Anisotropic electro-mechanics","text":"x, flag = solve!(comp_model; stepping=LoadStepping(nsteps=nsteps, maxbisec=5), ProjectDirichlet=true, post=post)\n\nfor (vo, w) in zip(voltage, tip_u)\n  println(\"V = $(round(vo, digits=4))   mean tip displacement (u_x, u_y, u_z) = $(round.(Tuple(w), sigdigits=6))\")\nend","title":"Solve"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E05_ElectroMechanics_anisotropic/#Checks","page":"05 · Anisotropic electro-mechanics","text":"Broken symmetry. In tutorial 04 the problem is symmetric about the plane x = 4: the displacement at a point and at its mirror image (8 - x y z) are mirror images of each other, u_x with the opposite sign and u_y, u_z equal. We measure the largest departure from that, at a set of points of the plate, relative to the deflection of the free end. It is zero to round-off in tutorial 04; the fibres make it a visible fraction of the deflection. (The mean of u_x would also do, but it changes sign along the load path.)\n\nmirror(p) = Point(8.0 - p[1], p[2], p[3])\npts = [Point(x, y, z) for x in (0.5, 1.5, 2.5, 3.5) for y in (0.2, 0.6) for z in (10.0, 20.0, 30.0, 39.5)]\nasym = maximum(norm(VectorValue(a[1] + b[1], a[2] - b[2], a[3] - b[3])) for (a, b) in zip(uh(pts), uh(mirror.(pts))))\ntip_uy = tip_u[end][2]\nprintln(\"asymmetry max|u(p) - mirrored u(p')| = $(asym)   tip u = $(tip_u[end])\")\n@assert flag == 1 \"the load stepping did not reach Λ = 1\"\n@assert tip_uy < 0 \"the plate does not bend towards -y\"\n@assert asym > 1e-2 * abs(tip_uy) \"the fibres do not break the symmetry about x = 4\"\n\nRegression (HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6, Gridap 0.20.8, Julia 1.13.0, macOS arm64).\n\nnu = norm(get_free_dof_values(uh))\nprintln(\"‖u‖ = $(nu)\")\nnu_ref, tip_ref = FAST ? (41.0162661692127, [0.03549296225574539, -3.9131266697605196, -0.08863170134311517]) :\n                         (273.70414034301757, [11.101859070803826, -20.15049709580238, -9.803265915334809])\n@assert isapprox(nu, nu_ref; rtol=1e-5) \"‖u‖ = $nu differs from the reference $nu_ref\"\n@assert isapprox(collect(Tuple(tip_u[end])), tip_ref; rtol=1e-5) \"tip displacement $(tip_u[end]) differs from the reference $tip_ref\"","title":"Checks"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E05_ElectroMechanics_anisotropic/#Going-further","page":"05 · Anisotropic electro-mechanics","text":"Turn the fibres: along the length, VectorValue(0.0, 0.0, 1.0), or across the width, VectorValue(1.0, 0.0, 0.0), they keep the symmetry about x = 4 (the mean of u_x vanishes again); compare the deflections. A fibre field that varies in space only needs a function in interpolate_everywhere.\nAdd a second family with another HGO_1Fiber and another field (the arguments then follow the order of the models in the sum), or use HGO_4Fibers as in tutorial 03.\nCompare with tutorial 04: the fibres reduce the deflection at the same voltage.","title":"Going further"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E18_Explicit_wave_propagation/#Tutorial-18:-Explicit-dynamics:-wave-propagation-in-a-bar","page":"18 · Explicit dynamics: wave propagation in a bar","text":"Source · Notebook · nbviewer\n\nA short pressure pulse hits one end of a long bar. A compression wave runs along the bar at the speed of sound of the material, reflects at the free far end as a tension wave and throws the end forward. Waves like this one are the domain of explicit time integration: every step costs one evaluation of the internal forces and a division by a diagonal mass, with no tangent and no Newton iteration, but the step must stay below the time a wave needs to cross one element. The tutorial measures that limit, shows what happens above it, and compares the explicit scheme with an implicit one on the same problem.\n\nMeasured on a Mac (M5 Max, 4 Julia threads, 4 BLAS threads, Julia 1.13.0, HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6): FAST (404 dofs, 223 explicit steps) 36 s, full (1 604 dofs, 889 explicit steps) 40–42 s, including compilation; after it, 1.3 ms per explicit step (of which 0.03–0.09 ms are the internal forces) and 2–3 ms (FAST) or 3.9 ms (full) per implicit step.\n\nWhat you will learn\n\nTransientModel with the explicit CentralDifference() scheme and a lumped (diagonal) mass (mass_lumping=true);\nthe critical time step Delta t_mathrmcrit = 2omega_max: its estimate hc and its value from the largest eigenvalue of the mesh, and what a step above it does;\na time-dependent traction (NeumannConditions with a load function of time) and the energy balance of the run: work of the load = kinetic + stored energy;\nexplicit against implicit (Newmark()): accuracy (dispersion) and cost per step;\nthe compiled backend in an explicit loop, and how to check that no matrix is assembled at each step.","title":"Tutorial 18: Explicit dynamics: wave propagation in a bar"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E18_Explicit_wave_propagation/#Problem-statement","page":"18 · Explicit dynamics: wave propagation in a bar","text":"Find the displacement boldsymbol u(boldsymbol X t) of the bar Omega = (0L)times(0W)times(0H) such that\n\nint_Omega rhoddotboldsymbol ucdotboldsymbol v mathrm dV\n+ int_Omega boldsymbol P(boldsymbol F)  nabla_0 boldsymbol v mathrm dV\n= int_Gamma_0 boldsymbol t(t)cdotboldsymbol v mathrm dA quad forall boldsymbol v \n\nwith the compressible neo-Hookean energy of tutorial 02 and the bar at rest at t = 0. The face Gamma_0 (X = 0) receives the pressure pulse\n\nboldsymbol t(t) = sigma_0 p(t) boldsymbol e_x  qquad\np(t) = sin^2(pi t  t_p)  text for  0 le t le t_p  quad p(t) = 0  text afterwards \n\nthe face X = L is free, and the four lateral faces are rollers (u_y = u_z = 0). The rollers make the motion one-dimensional (uniaxial strain), so the wave travels at the dilatational (P-wave) speed\n\nc = sqrtfraclambda + 2murho \n\nnot at the bar speed sqrtErho of a bar with free lateral faces (which is reached only for pulses much longer than the thickness, and disperses otherwise). For small strains the stress of the wave is sigma = rho c v: the pulse travels with particle velocity v = sigma_0 p(rho c), reaches the far end at t = Lc and reflects there as a tension pulse; the free end moves with twice the particle velocity, so its displacement grows by\n\nu_L^infty = frac2rho cint_0^t_psigma_0 p(t)mathrm dt = fracsigma_0 t_prho c \n\nand reaches half of it at t_12 = Lc + t_p2 (the pulse is symmetric). The work of the load, W = int_0^tint_Gamma_0boldsymbol tcdotdotboldsymbol umathrm dAmathrm dt, becomes kinetic energy K plus stored energy Pi; while the pulse acts the loaded face moves with v = sigma(rho c), so the pulse puts in\n\nW_infty = fracArho cint_0^t_psigma_0^2 p^2mathrm dt = frac3sigma_0^2 t_p A8rho c  qquad A = WH \n\nThe momentum of the bar is the impulse of the load, sigma_0 t_p A2.\n\nThe data are dimensionless (any consistent set of units): L = 10, W = H = 1, mu = 1, lambda = 15 (nu = 03), rho = 1, so c = sqrt35 = 1871; the pulse lasts t_p = 02Lc (it is 02L = 2 long) and its amplitude sigma_0 = 10^-3(lambda + 2mu) gives strains of 10^-3: the material is nonlinear, the response is linear in practice.","title":"Problem statement"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E18_Explicit_wave_propagation/#Setup","page":"18 · Explicit dynamics: wave propagation in a bar","text":"using HyperFEM\nusing HyperFEMKernels                                          # compiled backend (`backend=:compiled`)\nusing Gridap\nusing LinearAlgebra\nusing Printf\n\nconst FAST = get(ENV, \"HYPERFEM_TUTORIALS_FAST\", \"0\") == \"1\"   # reduced case for the test suite\noutdir = joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"data\", \"sims\", \"E18_Explicit_wave_propagation\")\nsetupfolder(outdir)                                            # VTK output (not versioned)","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E18_Explicit_wave_propagation/#Geometry-and-mesh","page":"18 · Explicit dynamics: wave propagation in a bar","text":"One element across the section: with the rollers on the lateral faces the problem is one-dimensional, and the 3D mesh only carries it. h = LN is the element length along the bar.\n\nconst L, W, H = 10.0, 1.0, 1.0\nN = FAST ? 100 : 400\nh = L / N\ngeometry = CartesianDiscreteModel((0, L, 0, W, 0, H), (N, 1, 1))\nlabels = get_face_labeling(geometry)\nadd_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"lateral\", [CartesianTags.faceX0Z⁺; CartesianTags.faceX1Z⁺;\n                                       CartesianTags.faceXY0⁺; CartesianTags.faceXY1⁺])\nadd_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"loaded\", CartesianTags.face0YZ)\nadd_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"far\", CartesianTags.face1YZ)","title":"Geometry and mesh"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E18_Explicit_wave_propagation/#Constitutive-model-and-wave-data","page":"18 · Explicit dynamics: wave propagation in a bar","text":"const λ, μ, ρ = 1.5, 1.0, 1.0\nphysmodel = NeoHookean3D(λ=λ, μ=μ)\nkinematics = Kinematics(Mechano, Solid)\nconst c = sqrt((λ + 2μ) / ρ)                                   # dilatational wave speed\nconst σ0 = 1e-3 * (λ + 2μ)                                     # pulse amplitude (strain 10⁻³)\nconst tp = 0.2 * L / c                                         # pulse duration\nconst uL∞ = σ0 * tp / (ρ * c)                                  # final displacement of the far end\nconst W∞ = 3 * σ0^2 * tp * W * H / (8 * ρ * c)                 # work of the whole pulse\nprintln(@sprintf(\"wave speed c = %.6f, pulse duration tp = %.4f, arrival L/c = %.4f, h/c = %.5f\", c, tp, L / c, h / c))","title":"Constitutive model and wave data"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E18_Explicit_wave_propagation/#Boundary-conditions","page":"18 · Explicit dynamics: wave propagation in a bar","text":"The rollers are a Dirichlet condition on the components y z (mask). The pulse is a Neumann condition: a constant vector times a function of the load parameter, which in a TransientModel is the time (load = identity). The function is evaluated once per stage, not at the quadrature points; it is built by a function so that it captures tp instead of reading a global.\n\npulse(tp) = t -> 0.0 <= t <= tp ? sin(π * t / tp)^2 : 0.0\ndirichlet = BoundaryConditions(BoundaryCondition(\"lateral\", [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]; mask=(false, true, true)))\ntraction = BoundaryConditions(BoundaryCondition(\"loaded\", [σ0, 0.0, 0.0], pulse(tp)))\nneumann = NeumannConditions(geometry, traction, 2)","title":"Boundary conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E18_Explicit_wave_propagation/#Finite-element-spaces-and-forms","page":"18 · Explicit dynamics: wave propagation in a bar","text":"Trilinear hexahedra (Q1), the usual partner of a lumped mass in explicit dynamics: one dof per node and the largest stable step for a given node spacing. (Row-sum lumping also works for the tensor-product Q2 elements of Gridap, whose row sums are all positive; it fails, with zero or negative vertex masses, for serendipity hexahedra and quadratic simplices.) The static forms are those of tutorial 02; mass is the bilinear inertia form, which TransientModel assembles once and, with mass_lumping=true, replaces by the diagonal of its row sums.\n\nreffe = ReferenceFE(lagrangian, VectorValue{3,Float64}, 1)\nΩ = Triangulation(geometry)\ndΩ = Measure(Ω, 2)\nV = TestFESpace(Ω, reffe, dirichlet, conformity=:H1)\nU = TrialFESpace(V, dirichlet, 0.0)\nprintln(\"free dofs: \", num_free_dofs(V))\n\nres(t) = (u, v) -> residual(physmodel, kinematics, u, v, dΩ)\njac(t) = (u, du, v) -> jacobian(physmodel, kinematics, u, du, v, dΩ)\nmass = (a, v) -> ∫(ρ * (a ⋅ v))dΩ\nstored(u) = sum(energy(physmodel, kinematics, u, dΩ))\n\nThe mean axial displacement of a face is a linear functional of the dofs, ell^mathsf Tboldsymbol x  A: its vector is assembled once, and every step only takes a dot product.\n\nex = VectorValue(1.0, 0.0, 0.0)\nface_functional(tag) = (Γ = BoundaryTriangulation(geometry, tags=tag); dΓ = Measure(Γ, 2);\n                        assemble_vector(v -> ∫(v ⋅ ex)dΓ, V) ./ (W * H))\nℓ0, ℓL = face_functional(\"loaded\"), face_functional(\"far\")","title":"Finite element spaces and forms"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E18_Explicit_wave_propagation/#The-critical-time-step","page":"18 · Explicit dynamics: wave propagation in a bar","text":"The central-difference scheme is stable when Delta t le 2omega_max, with omega_max^2 the largest eigenvalue of boldsymbol M_L^-1boldsymbol K. For a chain of linear elements with lumped mass omega_max approx 2ch, which gives the familiar Courant condition Delta t le hc: a wave may not cross more than one element per step. Here the mesh is small, so omega_max is computed exactly from the stiffness at the undeformed state and the lumped mass (a dense eigenvalue problem; on a large mesh one takes the estimate hc with the smallest element, or a few power iterations). For this mesh the estimate is exact: the highest mode is the one in which neighbouring sections move in opposite directions, and its frequency is 2ch.\n\nuh0 = FEFunction(U, zeros(num_free_dofs(U)))\nK = assemble_matrix((du, v) -> jac(0.0)(uh0, du, v), U, V)\nmL = vec(sum(assemble_matrix(mass, U, V); dims=2))             # lumped mass: row sums\nd = 1 ./ sqrt.(mL)\nωmax = sqrt(eigmax(Symmetric(Matrix(d .* K .* d'))))\nΔtcrit = 2 / ωmax\nprintln(@sprintf(\"ω_max = %.6f, Δt_crit = 2/ω_max = %.6f = %.4f h/c\", ωmax, Δtcrit, Δtcrit / (h / c)))","title":"The critical time step"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E18_Explicit_wave_propagation/#One-run","page":"18 · Explicit dynamics: wave propagation in a bar","text":"The function below builds the model for a scheme, a step Δt and a final time, and solves it. For the explicit scheme the stage unknown is the acceleration, boldsymbol M_Lboldsymbol a_n+1 = boldsymbol f_mathrmext(t_n+1) - boldsymbol r(boldsymbol u_n+1), with boldsymbol u_n+1 known from the previous step: the model evaluates the internal forces once and solves with the mass, which it factorises once for the whole run (it changes only with Δt); the Newton parameters are not used. The implicit run uses the consistent mass and Newton with the full tangent at every iteration.\n\nThe post-processing runs after every accepted step: it records the mean displacement of the two end faces (two dot products) and, optionally, the stored and kinetic energies (energies=true, one Gridap integral each, for the short unstable run: the stored energy of the new state plus the kinetic energy of the previous step, enough to see it grow) and a VTK frame every vtk_every steps. Its time is measured apart, so that the time per step is that of the solver. The bar starts at rest and unloaded, so the initial acceleration is zero (initial_acceleration=:zero, the default).\n\nfunction wave_run(scheme, Δt, t_end; lumped, energies=false, vtk_every=0, name=\"run\")\n  U = TrialFESpace(V, dirichlet, 0.0)\n  nls = NewtonRaphson(LUSolver(); maxiter=20, atol=1e-13, rtol=1e-10, verbose=0)\n  m = TransientModel(res, jac, U, V, dirichlet; scheme, mass, mass_lumping=lumped, neumann, nls, backend=:compiled)\n  uh = get_state(m)\n  x = get_free_dof_values(uh)\n  t, s0, sL, E = [0.0], [0.0], [0.0], [0.0]\n  tpost = Ref(0.0)\n  function record(post)\n    tpost[] += @elapsed begin\n      push!(t, post.Λ[post.iter]); push!(s0, dot(ℓ0, x)); push!(sL, dot(ℓL, x))\n      energies && push!(E, stored(uh) + 0.5 * sum(mass(get_velocity(m), get_velocity(m))))   # Π(uₙ₊₁) + K(vₙ)\n      if vtk_every > 0 && post.iter % vtk_every == 0\n        pvd = get_pvd(post)\n        pvd[t[end]] = createvtk(Ω, joinpath(outdir, \"$(name)_$(post.iter).vtu\"), cellfields=[\"u\" => uh])\n      end\n    end\n  end\n  post = PostProcessor(m, record; is_vtk=vtk_every > 0, filepath=outdir)\n  time = @elapsed (_, flag) = solve!(m; stepping=TimeStepping(; Δt, t_end), post)\n  nsteps = length(t) - 1\n  (; flag, t, s0, sL, E, m, lumped, nsteps, time, time_per_step=(time - tpost[]) / nsteps)\nend\n\nWork of the load over the run, by the trapezoidal rule in time on the recorded displacement of the loaded face (the face moves rigidly: one element across), the energies and the momentum at the end, and the impulse of the load as both schemes see it: the trapezoidal rule on the load at the step times.\n\nfunction balance(r)\n  P = pulse(tp)\n  Wext = sum(0.5 * σ0 * (P(r.t[k]) + P(r.t[k+1])) * W * H * (r.s0[k+1] - r.s0[k]) for k in 1:r.nsteps)\n  vh = get_velocity(r.m)\n  v = get_free_dof_values(vh)\n  Kin = r.lumped ? 0.5 * dot(v, mL .* v) : 0.5 * sum(mass(vh, vh))\n  Π = stored(get_state(r.m))\n  momentum = sum(∫(ρ * (vh ⋅ ex))dΩ)\n  impulse = sum(0.5 * σ0 * (P(r.t[k]) + P(r.t[k+1])) * W * H * (r.t[k+1] - r.t[k]) for k in 1:r.nsteps)\n  (; Wext, Kin, Π, error=(Wext - Kin - Π) / Wext, momentum, impulse)\nend\n\nArrival time: the far end reaches half of its final displacement at t_12 = Lc + t_p2 (linear interpolation between steps).\n\nfunction half_time(r)\n  k = findfirst(i -> r.sL[i] < uL∞ / 2 <= r.sL[i+1], 1:length(r.sL)-1)\n  r.t[k] + (uL∞ / 2 - r.sL[k]) * (r.t[k+1] - r.t[k]) / (r.sL[k+1] - r.sL[k])\nend","title":"One run"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E18_Explicit_wave_propagation/#Above-the-stability-limit","page":"18 · Explicit dynamics: wave propagation in a bar","text":"A step 10 % above the limit. Nothing fails at once: the scheme is explicit, there is no iteration to diverge. But the highest mode of the mesh, which the sudden start of the load excites a little, is amplified at every step: by 2.43 in amplitude while the motion is small (the root of lambda^2 - (2 - omega_max^2Delta t^2)lambda + 1 = 0 with omega_maxDelta t = 22), and much faster once the strains are large and the neo-Hookean material stiffens. Within twenty steps the energy is many orders of magnitude above the energy of the whole pulse, and soon it overflows to Inf (nothing throws: the run simply produces garbage, which is why the limit must be respected by construction).\n\nprintln(\"\\n── Central differences, Δt = 1.1 Δt_crit ──\")\nunstable = wave_run(CentralDifference(), 1.1 * Δtcrit, 60 * 1.1 * Δtcrit; lumped=true, energies=true)\nEu = unstable.E ./ W∞                                          # energy / energy of the whole pulse\nfor k in 11:10:length(Eu)\n  println(@sprintf(\"  step %2d: energy / energy of the whole pulse = %.2e\", k - 1, Eu[k]))\nend","title":"Above the stability limit"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E18_Explicit_wave_propagation/#Explicit-run","page":"18 · Explicit dynamics: wave propagation in a bar","text":"Central differences at 90 % of the limit, until the wave has come back to the loaded end (t = 2Lc).\n\nprintln(\"\\n── Central differences, Δt = 0.9 Δt_crit, lumped mass ──\")\nHyperFEMKernels.reset_counters!()\nΔtex = 0.9 * Δtcrit\nex_run = wave_run(CentralDifference(), Δtex, 2L / c; lumped=true, vtk_every=FAST ? 0 : 80, name=\"explicit\")\nwork_ex = HyperFEMKernels.counters()","title":"Explicit run"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E18_Explicit_wave_propagation/#Implicit-run","page":"18 · Explicit dynamics: wave propagation in a bar","text":"The trapezoidal rule (Newmark()) is unconditionally stable: here it takes a step five times larger than the explicit one (4.5 times the Courant limit). A first, two-step run compiles the implicit path, so that the time per step of the second one is that of the solver.\n\nprintln(\"── Newmark (trapezoidal rule), Δt = 5 Δt_explicit, consistent mass ──\")\nΔtim = 5 * Δtex\nwave_run(Newmark(), Δtim, 2Δtim; lumped=false)\nim_run = wave_run(Newmark(), Δtim, 2L / c; lumped=false)","title":"Implicit run"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E18_Explicit_wave_propagation/#Efficiency","page":"18 · Explicit dynamics: wave propagation in a bar","text":"work_summary of the compiled operators (internal fields of the model: HyperFEM 0.3.0 has no accessor for them, so these lines may change): the volume groups of the stage forms and the mass form take the fast :linear_maps path and nothing falls back to Gridap (the traction is a small boundary group on the general :duals path). The counters of the explicit run show that at every step the forms are only rebound to the new time of the traction (update_fast, one per step), never compiled again (update_full = 0).\n\nNo matrix is assembled or factorised inside the explicit loop: the explicit stage has no stiffness term (its coefficient s_K is zero), so its tangent is the lumped mass alone, assembled and factorised once for the run (again only if Δt changes), and every step is one evaluation of the internal forces plus one solve with the factors of that diagonal matrix. HyperFEM 0.3.0 does this with the linear solver of nls (here LUSolver) on a matrix with the sparsity pattern of the stiffness: negligible here, but on a large mesh the factorisation costs as much as many steps (0.6 s for a 3D mesh of 26 000 dofs, about a hundred steps, measured on the same machine), so keep Δt fixed in an explicit run.\n\nThe time of one evaluation of the internal forces is measured below for comparison: on a mesh this small most of a step is the fixed cost of rebinding the forms to the new time (about a millisecond: the forms are captured again, the Jacobian included, although the explicit stage does not use it); on larger meshes the internal forces take the larger share.\n\nop = ex_run.m.caches.op.inner                                  # stage forms, internal field\nopM = ex_run.m.caches.opM                                      # mass form, internal field\nfallbacks = 0\nfor (what, o) in ((\"stage forms\", op), (\"mass form\", opM))\n  ws = HyperFEMKernels.work_summary(o)\n  global fallbacks += ws.fallback_residual + ws.fallback_jacobian\n  println(what, \": \", [(g.kind, g.domain, g.path) for g in ws.groups], \"; Gridap fallbacks: \",\n          ws.fallback_residual + ws.fallback_jacobian)\nend\nML = ex_run.m.caches.op.M                                      # the mass matrix of the model, internal field\nprintln(\"counters of the explicit run: \", work_ex)\nprintln(\"lumped mass diagonal: \", isdiag(ML))\nrint = zeros(num_free_dofs(V))\nxend = get_free_dof_values(get_state(ex_run.m))\nGridap.Algebra.residual!(rint, op, xend)\nt_res = @elapsed (for _ in 1:100; Gridap.Algebra.residual!(rint, op, xend); end)\nprintln(@sprintf(\"one evaluation of the internal forces: %.3f ms\", 10 * t_res))\nfor (name, r) in ((\"central differences\", ex_run), (\"Newmark\", im_run))\n  println(@sprintf(\"%-20s %5d steps  %.3f ms per step  %.2f s in all\", name, r.nsteps, 1e3 * r.time_per_step,\n                   r.time_per_step * r.nsteps))\nend\n\nOn this problem the explicit run costs more in all than the implicit one (about 0.3 s against 0.1 s in the FAST case, 1.2 s against 0.7 s in the full one), although it is the more accurate of the two. The reason is the time per step: about 1.3 ms, almost all of it fixed overhead of HyperFEM 0.3.0 (the stage forms, residual and Jacobian, are rebound at every step, and the diagonal mass is solved with the LU factors of a matrix with the pattern of the stiffness), against 0.03–0.09 ms for the internal forces that an explicit step really needs. A 1D bar of a few hundred dofs is also the best case for the implicit scheme: its factorisations are tiny. Explicit integration pays off on large 3D meshes, where a factorisation of the stiffness costs far more than an evaluation of the internal forces, on stiff materials and short events (impacts, waves) that need small steps anyway for accuracy, and on strongly nonlinear problems where Newton struggles; and its advantage grows once the driver divides by the lumped diagonal and skips the unused Jacobian.","title":"Efficiency"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E18_Explicit_wave_propagation/#Checks","page":"18 · Explicit dynamics: wave propagation in a bar","text":"(a) The wave speed from the arrival at the far end, and the final displacement of the far end, against the closed forms. The explicit scheme with a lumped mass is almost free of dispersion near the stability limit (in one dimension the two errors cancel exactly at Delta t = hc); the implicit run with a step of 4.5 hc and a consistent mass delays and spreads the pulse.\n\ncex = L / (half_time(ex_run) - tp / 2)\ncim = L / (half_time(im_run) - tp / 2)\nprintln(@sprintf(\"\\nwave speed: exact %.5f, explicit %.5f (%+.3f %%), implicit %.5f (%+.3f %%)\",\n                 c, cex, 100 * (cex - c) / c, cim, 100 * (cim - c) / c))\nprintln(@sprintf(\"far-end displacement: exact %.6e, explicit %.6e, implicit %.6e\", uL∞, ex_run.sL[end], im_run.sL[end]))\n@assert ex_run.flag == 1 && im_run.flag == 1 \"a run did not reach its final time\"\n@assert abs(Δtcrit - h / c) < 1e-8 * h / c \"critical step away from h/c\"\n@assert abs(cex - c) < 0.005 * c \"explicit: wave speed away from √((λ+2μ)/ρ) ($cex)\"\n@assert abs(ex_run.sL[end] - uL∞) < 0.01 * uL∞ \"explicit: far-end displacement away from σ₀tₚ/(ρc)\"\n@assert abs(cim - c) < 0.05 * c \"implicit: wave speed away from √((λ+2μ)/ρ) ($cim)\"\n\n(b) Invariants. Momentum: the internal forces add up to zero, so both schemes (whose velocity update is the trapezoidal rule on the accelerations) give the bar exactly the impulse of the sampled load, and that impulse approaches sigma_0 t_p A2 as the pulse is resolved by more steps (the implicit run samples it with about 4 steps in the FAST case, 18 in the full one). Energy: the work of the load is the kinetic plus the stored energy, to the accuracy of the time integration: the trapezoidal rule conserves the energy of a linear problem exactly; central differences only up to O(Delta t^2) (3cdot10^-3 in the FAST run, 2cdot10^-4 in the full one, whose step is four times smaller). And a step above the limit makes the energy explode.\n\nbex, bim = balance(ex_run), balance(im_run)\nimpulse = σ0 * tp / 2 * W * H\nprintln(@sprintf(\"energy: closed form W∞ = %.6e; explicit W = %.6e, K + Π = %.6e (error %.2e); implicit W = %.6e, K + Π = %.6e (error %.2e)\",\n                 W∞, bex.Wext, bex.Kin + bex.Π, bex.error, bim.Wext, bim.Kin + bim.Π, bim.error))\nprintln(@sprintf(\"momentum: impulse %.6e; explicit %.6e (sampled impulse %.6e), implicit %.6e (sampled %.6e)\",\n                 impulse, bex.momentum, bex.impulse, bim.momentum, bim.impulse))\n@assert abs(bex.Wext - W∞) < 1e-2 * W∞ \"explicit: work of the load away from 3σ₀²tₚA/(8ρc)\"\n@assert abs(bex.error) < 1e-2 \"explicit: energy balance error $(bex.error)\"\n@assert abs(bim.error) < 1e-3 \"implicit: energy balance error $(bim.error)\"\n@assert abs(bex.momentum - bex.impulse) < 1e-8 * impulse \"explicit: momentum ≠ impulse of the load\"\n@assert abs(bim.momentum - bim.impulse) < 1e-8 * impulse \"implicit: momentum ≠ impulse of the load\"\n@assert abs(bex.momentum - impulse) < 1e-3 * impulse \"explicit: momentum away from σ₀tₚA/2\"\n@assert unstable.flag == 1 \"the unstable run did not reach its final time\"\n@assert Eu[21] > 1e6 && Eu[21] > 1e3 * Eu[11] \"Δt above the limit did not blow up (step 10: $(Eu[11]), step 20: $(Eu[21]))\"\n@assert fallbacks == 0 \"a part of the forms fell back to Gridap\"\n@assert isdiag(ML) \"the lumped mass is not diagonal\"\n@assert work_ex.update_full == 0 && work_ex.update_fast == ex_run.nsteps \"the explicit loop compiled forms again\"\n\n(c) Regression values (HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6, Gridap 0.20.8, Julia 1.13.0, macOS arm64).\n\nprintln(@sprintf(\"regression: Δtcrit = %.10e, t½ ex = %.10e, t½ im = %.10e, uL ex = %.10e\",\n                 Δtcrit, half_time(ex_run), half_time(im_run), ex_run.sL[end]))\nreference = FAST ? (Δtcrit=5.3452248382e-02, t_ex=5.8812024050e+00, t_im=6.0252310222e+00, uL_ex=1.9999358513e-03) :\n                   (Δtcrit=1.3363062096e-02, t_ex=5.8791509873e+00, t_im=5.9081377630e+00, uL_ex=2.0000005438e-03)\n@assert isapprox(Δtcrit, reference.Δtcrit; rtol=1e-5) \"regression: critical step $Δtcrit\"\n@assert isapprox(half_time(ex_run), reference.t_ex; rtol=1e-5) \"regression: explicit arrival time $(half_time(ex_run))\"\n@assert isapprox(half_time(im_run), reference.t_im; rtol=1e-5) \"regression: implicit arrival time $(half_time(im_run))\"\n@assert isapprox(ex_run.sL[end], reference.uL_ex; rtol=1e-5) \"regression: explicit far-end displacement $(ex_run.sL[end])\"","title":"Checks"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E18_Explicit_wave_propagation/#Going-further","page":"18 · Explicit dynamics: wave propagation in a bar","text":"Run the explicit scheme at exactly Delta t = hc: in one dimension the lumped mass and the central differences then propagate the pulse without any dispersion (their phase errors cancel), and a hair above it the run blows up.\nmass_lumping=false keeps the consistent mass: every step solves with it, and the limit drops to h(sqrt3c) (compute omega_max with the consistent mass to see it).\nFree the lateral faces (remove the rollers) and use a few elements across the section: the pulse now travels at about the bar speed sqrtErho and disperses (Pochhammer–Chree); the critical step is set by the smallest element and the dilatational speed.\nRaise the amplitude (sigma_0 = 01(lambda + 2mu)): the speed of the neo-Hookean material depends on the strain, and the pulse changes shape as it travels; the explicit step needs no change, the implicit one needs more Newton iterations.\nTutorial 15 treats the implicit schemes (GeneralizedAlpha, AdaptiveStepping) and the energy of a free vibration.\nReferences: R. Courant, K. Friedrichs, H. Lewy (1928), Über die partiellen Differenzengleichungen der mathematischen Physik, Math. Ann. 100, 32–74, doi:10.1007/BF01448839; T. Belytschko, W. K. Liu, B. Moran, K. Elkhodary (2014), Nonlinear Finite Elements for Continua and Structures, 2nd ed., Wiley, ch. 6 (explicit time integration and its stability).","title":"Going further"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E09_hMRE_vacuum_staggered/#Tutorial-09:-hMRE-beam-in-vacuum:-staggered-solution","page":"09 · hMRE beam in vacuum: staggered solution","text":"Source · Notebook · nbviewer\n\nA thin beam of hard-magnetic soft elastomer (hMRE: an ultra-soft silicone filled with magnetised particles) bends when an external magnetic field is applied across its remanent magnetisation. Here the beam is surrounded by an explicit layer of vacuum (air) that carries the magnetic field and deforms with the beam, so that the field, the Maxwell stress on the beam and the beam itself are computed together. The coupled problem is split into three problems solved one after another at every load step (a staggered, or partitioned, solution): this is how the simulations of Perez-Garcia et al. (Adv. Mater. 2025) were run.\n\nWhat you will learn\n\nmagneto-mechanical models: HardMagnetic2D inside a MagnetoMechModel, IdealMagnetic2D for the vacuum, and a regularised fictitious material (HessianRegularization) for the motion of the vacuum mesh;\nfinite element spaces on parts of a mesh (solid, vacuum, their interface) and a field on an interface;\nStaggeredModel and StaggeredStepping: three StaticNonlinearModels solved in turn, with the states of the other fields read between their previous and current values (StageBlend);\nprescribing the state of another problem on a boundary, BoundaryCondition(tag, Coupling(...));\nmoving nodal values between meshes with Transfer/transfer! (the Maxwell traction of the vacuum on the beam);\nthe record of a staggered solve, get_log.\n\nMeasured on a Mac M5 Max (Julia 1.13.0, 4 threads, 4 BLAS threads; HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6, Gridap 0.20.8; compiled backend), whole run including compilation: FAST 74 s, full 80 s (20 steps of 2 passes, 28 s in solve!, of which about 11 s compile the three problems at the first pass). 25 375 unknowns: 8 563 magnetic potential, 1 232 solid displacement, 15 580 vacuum displacement. One assembly of the tangent, compiled against Gridap: magnetic 3.9 ms / 27 ms, vacuum 6.0 ms / 29 ms.","title":"Tutorial 09: hMRE beam in vacuum: staggered solution"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E09_hMRE_vacuum_staggered/#Problem-statement","page":"09 · hMRE beam in vacuum: staggered solution","text":"The mesh is half of the problem (x ge 0; x = 0 is a plane of symmetry): a beam Omega_s = (0 50) times (-1 1) mm, clamped on its lower face for x  25 mm, inside a box of vacuum Omega_v of 350 × 450 mm. The magnetic scalar potential varphi is prescribed on the bottom and top of the box, varphi = Lambdavarphi_b and varphi = 0, which applies a vertical field mathcal H_0 approx Lambdavarphi_b  045 A/m; the load factor Lambda goes from 0 to 1.\n\nAll the unknowns are written in the reference configuration: the displacement boldsymbol u (of the beam and, fictitiously, of the vacuum), boldsymbol F = boldsymbol I + nabla_0 boldsymbol u, boldsymbol H = operatornamecof boldsymbol F, J = det boldsymbol F, and the Lagrangian magnetic field boldsymbolmathcal H_0 = -nabla_0 varphi. In the vacuum the energy is that of free space mapped to the reference configuration,\n\nPsi_v(boldsymbol F boldsymbolmathcal H_0) = -fracmu_02J\n  lvert boldsymbol H boldsymbolmathcal H_0 rvert^2 \n\nand in the beam it adds the elastic energy and the energy of the remanent field boldsymbolmathcal H_r = alpha_r boldsymbol N along the magnetisation direction boldsymbol N = (1 0):\n\nPsi_s(boldsymbol F boldsymbolmathcal H_0 boldsymbol N) = Psi_mathrmmec(boldsymbol F boldsymbol N)\n  - fracmu_02Jlvert boldsymbol H boldsymbolmathcal H_0 rvert^2\n  + fracmu_0 (1 + chi_t)J(boldsymbol H boldsymbolmathcal H_0) cdot (boldsymbol H boldsymbolmathcal H_r) \n\nHere chi_t = 0 (the keyword χt of HardMagnetic2D defaults to the susceptibility χe = 0); the constructor's χr only enters a further term weighted by βmok, which is zero by default, so it is not set. The last term is the torque on the magnetised particles: a field across boldsymbol N rotates the beam. In the undeformed state the induction is boldsymbolmathcal B_0 = -partialPsi_spartialboldsymbolmathcal H_0 = mu_0(boldsymbolmathcal H_0 - (1+chi_t)boldsymbolmathcal H_r): with this sign convention the remanent magnetisation points along -boldsymbol N, so an upward field gives a clockwise torque and the free end of the beam moves down.\n\nThe three problems, solved in this order at every load step, are:\n\nMagnetic potential on Omega_s cup Omega_v (Gauss's law for the magnetic induction boldsymbolmathcal B_0 = -partial Psi  partial boldsymbolmathcal H_0) with the displacements fixed:\n-int_Omega_s fracpartial Psi_spartial boldsymbolmathcal H_0 cdot nabla_0 deltavarphi mathrm dV\n-int_Omega_v fracpartial Psi_vpartial boldsymbolmathcal H_0 cdot nabla_0 deltavarphi mathrm dV = 0 \nMechanics of the beam with the magnetic field fixed, loaded on the interface Gamma by the Maxwell stress boldsymbol P_v = partial Psi_v  partial boldsymbol F of the vacuum:\nint_Omega_s fracpartial Psi_spartial boldsymbol F  nabla_0 deltaboldsymbol u mathrm dV\n+ int_Gamma boldsymbol t_M cdot deltaboldsymbol u mathrm dA = 0 \nwhere boldsymbol t_M reproduces at the nodes of Gamma the nodal forces int_Omega_v boldsymbol P_v  nabla_0 deltaboldsymbol u mathrm dV of the vacuum (see Maxwell traction on the interface).\nMotion of the vacuum: a fictitious hyperelastic problem on Omega_v with boldsymbol u_v = boldsymbol u_s on Gamma, whose only purpose is to let the vacuum mesh follow the beam without inverting its elements.\n\nSplitting the problem this way keeps three small, well-conditioned systems, at the price of a coupling that is only satisfied after enough passes; here every load step makes a fixed number of passes (see Solve).\n\nReference: C. Perez-Garcia, R. Ortigosa, J. Martínez-Frutos, D. Garcia-Gonzalez, Topology and material optimization in ultra-soft magnetoactive structures: making advantage of residual anisotropies, Adv. Mater. (2025), doi:10.1002/adma.202518489.","title":"Problem statement"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E09_hMRE_vacuum_staggered/#Setup","page":"09 · hMRE beam in vacuum: staggered solution","text":"using HyperFEM\nusing Gridap, GridapGmsh\nusing HyperFEMKernels                                          # compiled backend (`backend=:compiled`)\nusing Gridap.FESpaces: assemble_vector!, zero_free_values\nusing Gridap.CellData: get_cell_points\nusing LinearAlgebra, Printf\n\nconst FAST = get(ENV, \"HYPERFEM_TUTORIALS_FAST\", \"0\") == \"1\"   # reduced case for the test suite\noutdir = joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"data\", \"sims\", \"E09_hMRE_vacuum_staggered\")\nsetupfolder(outdir)                                            # VTK output (not versioned)","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E09_hMRE_vacuum_staggered/#Geometry-and-mesh","page":"09 · hMRE beam in vacuum: staggered solution","text":"A Gmsh mesh of linear triangles (lengths in m) with physical groups for the beam (Beam), the vacuum (vacuumb, which includes a region kept for a permanent magnet in other variants of the problem), the interface between them (Interface) and the boundary conditions.\n\ngeomodel = GmshDiscreteModel(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"data\", \"models\", \"Short_magnet.msh\"))","title":"Geometry and mesh"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E09_hMRE_vacuum_staggered/#Constitutive-models","page":"09 · hMRE beam in vacuum: staggered solution","text":"SI units (m, Pa, A). The beam: a nonlinear Mooney–Rivlin matrix plus a transversely isotropic term along boldsymbol N (the residual anisotropy left by the magnetisation process), with the parameters of the paper for a Dowsil-based hMRE (shear moduli of about 10 kPa), and the hard-magnetic coupling with a remanent field mu_0 mathcal H_r = 10 mT. MagnetoMechModel adds the two; calling it returns the energy and its derivatives with respect to boldsymbol F and boldsymbolmathcal H_0, functions of (boldsymbol F boldsymbolmathcal H_0 boldsymbol N).\n\nμ0 = 4π * 1e-7                                                 # H/m\nparams = [10.0, 12480.64495232286, 1.0, 2.0, 5195.545287237134, 0.2602717127043121, 2.0]\nmodel_mech = NonlinearMooneyRivlin2D(λ=(params[1] + params[2]) * 1e2, μ1=params[1] * 1e3, μ2=params[2], α1=params[3], α2=params[4]) +\n             TransverseIsotropy2D(μ=params[5], α1=params[6], α2=params[7])\nmodel_hard = HardMagnetic2D(μ0=μ0, αr=10e-3 / μ0, χe=0.0)\nmodel_solid = MagnetoMechModel(magneto=model_hard, mechano=model_mech)\n\nThe vacuum: free space for the magnetic problem, and for its motion a soft compressible Mooney–Rivlin material with the term lambda (J^gamma + J^-gamma), which grows quickly when an element is compressed or stretched. Its tangent is not positive definite for large distortions; HessianRegularization clamps the eigenvalues of partial^2 Psi  partial boldsymbol F partial boldsymbol F at delta (energy and stress unchanged), so that Newton always moves downhill.\n\nmodel_vacuum_mag = IdealMagnetic2D(μ0=μ0, χe=0.0)\nmodel_vacuum_mech = HessianRegularization(mechano=NonlinearMooneyRivlin2D_CV(λ=1.0, μ1=1.0, μ2=0.0, α1=6.0, α2=1.0, γ=6.0), δ=1e-6)","title":"Constitutive models"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E09_hMRE_vacuum_staggered/#Domains","page":"09 · hMRE beam in vacuum: staggered solution","text":"The magnetic potential lives on the whole mesh, the displacements on the beam and on the vacuum separately. Γs is the boundary of the beam on the Interface tag, seen from the beam.\n\nΩdomain = Triangulation(geomodel, tags=[\"vacuumb\", \"Beam\"])\nΩsolid = Triangulation(geomodel, tags=[\"Beam\"])\nΩvacuum = Triangulation(geomodel, tags=[\"vacuumb\"])\norder = 1\ndΩsolid = Measure(Ωsolid, 2 * order)\ndΩvacuum = Measure(Ωvacuum, 2 * order)\nΓs = BoundaryTriangulation(Ωsolid, tags=\"Interface\")\ndΓs = Measure(Γs, 4 * order)","title":"Domains"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E09_hMRE_vacuum_staggered/#Boundary-conditions","page":"09 · hMRE beam in vacuum: staggered solution","text":"The potential ramps with the load factor at the bottom of the box; the beam is clamped on Fixed_Solid and symmetric on Fixed_Solid_x (only u_x = 0). The vacuum is fixed on the outer box and symmetric on x = 0. FAST applies half of the potential.\n\nφbot = FAST ? 0.2 * 5200 / 2 : 0.2 * 5200                      # A (magnetic scalar potential)\nDφ = BoundaryConditions(BoundaryCondition(\"phibot\", φbot, Λ -> Λ), BoundaryCondition(\"phitop\", 0.0, Λ -> Λ))\nDu_solid = BoundaryConditions(BoundaryCondition(\"Fixed_Solid\", [0.0, 0.0]),\n                              BoundaryCondition(\"Fixed_Solid_x\", [0.0, 0.0]; mask=(true, false)))","title":"Boundary conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E09_hMRE_vacuum_staggered/#Finite-element-spaces-and-states","page":"09 · hMRE beam in vacuum: staggered solution","text":"A staggered solve needs, for every field, the current state (⁺) and its value at the previous pass (⁻), each an FEFunction on its own trial space: the forms of a field read the other fields through them.\n\nreffeφ = ReferenceFE(lagrangian, Float64, order)\nreffeu = ReferenceFE(lagrangian, VectorValue{2,Float64}, order)\n\nVφ = TestFESpace(Ωdomain, reffeφ, Dφ, conformity=:H1)\nUφ⁺ = TrialFESpace(Vφ, Dφ); Uφ⁻ = TrialFESpace(Vφ, Dφ)\nφh⁺ = FEFunction(Uφ⁺, zero_free_values(Uφ⁺)); φh⁻ = FEFunction(Uφ⁻, zero_free_values(Uφ⁻))\n\nVu_solid = TestFESpace(Ωsolid, reffeu, Du_solid, conformity=:H1)\nUu_solid⁺ = TrialFESpace(Vu_solid, Du_solid); Uu_solid⁻ = TrialFESpace(Vu_solid, Du_solid)\nuh_solid⁺ = FEFunction(Uu_solid⁺, zero_free_values(Uu_solid⁺)); uh_solid⁻ = FEFunction(Uu_solid⁻, zero_free_values(Uu_solid⁻))\n\nThe vacuum follows the beam on the interface. StageBlend(uh⁻, uh⁺) is the beam displacement between its two states, boldsymbol u_s(tau) = boldsymbol u_s^- + tau(boldsymbol u_s^+ - boldsymbol u_s^-), with the stage tau in 0 1 of the inner solve; Coupling prescribes it as Dirichlet data on the Interface nodes of the vacuum. The nodes are located once in the mesh of the beam (here they coincide, so the Dirichlet values are a copy of dof values) and updated in place at every solve.\n\nuhsolid = StageBlend(uh_solid⁻, uh_solid⁺)\nDu_vacuum = BoundaryConditions(BoundaryCondition(\"Fixed_Air\", [0.0, 0.0]),\n                               BoundaryCondition(\"Fixed_Air_x\", [0.0, 0.0]; mask=(true, false)),\n                               BoundaryCondition(\"Interface\", Coupling(uhsolid)))\nVu_vacuum = TestFESpace(Ωvacuum, reffeu, Du_vacuum, conformity=:H1)\nUu_vacuum⁺ = TrialFESpace(Vu_vacuum, Du_vacuum); Uu_vacuum⁻ = TrialFESpace(Vu_vacuum, Du_vacuum)\nuh_vacuum⁺ = FEFunction(Uu_vacuum⁺, zero_free_values(Uu_vacuum⁺)); uh_vacuum⁻ = FEFunction(Uu_vacuum⁻, zero_free_values(Uu_vacuum⁻))\n\nuhvacuum(τ) = uh_vacuum⁻ + (uh_vacuum⁺ - uh_vacuum⁻) * τ        # the same blend written as a closure\nφh(τ) = φh⁻ + (φh⁺ - φh⁻) * τ\n\nThe magnetisation direction boldsymbol N = (1 0), as a finite element field on the beam.\n\nNh = interpolate_everywhere(x -> VectorValue(1.0, 0.0), TestFESpace(Ωsolid, reffeu))\n@printf(\"Unknowns: magnetic potential %d, solid displacement %d, vacuum displacement %d (total %d)\\n\",\n        num_free_dofs(Uφ⁺), num_free_dofs(Uu_solid⁺), num_free_dofs(Uu_vacuum⁺),\n        num_free_dofs(Uφ⁺) + num_free_dofs(Uu_solid⁺) + num_free_dofs(Uu_vacuum⁺))","title":"Finite element spaces and states"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E09_hMRE_vacuum_staggered/#Maxwell-traction-on-the-interface","page":"09 · hMRE beam in vacuum: staggered solution","text":"The magnetic stress of the vacuum acts on the beam. Its nodal forces, f_a = int_Omega_v boldsymbol P_v  nabla_0 N_a mathrm dV, are assembled on a vacuum space without Dirichlet conditions; Transfer takes the values at the interface nodes into a space Usolid_int defined only on Gamma (built once, it is a gather of dof values when the nodes coincide), and the solve with the interface mass matrix boldsymbol M turns them into a traction field boldsymbol t_M with int_Gamma boldsymbol t_M cdot deltaboldsymbol u mathrm dA = sum_a f_a cdot deltaboldsymbol u_a. (StaticLinearModel(...)(x) solves boldsymbol M boldsymbol y = boldsymbol x in place, reusing its factorisation.)\n\nV_maxwell = TestFESpace(Ωvacuum, reffeu)\nPmaxw = FEFunction(V_maxwell, zero_free_values(V_maxwell))\nPmaxw_ = get_free_dof_values(Pmaxw)\nUsolid_int = FESpace(Γs, reffeu)\ntmaxw = interpolate_everywhere(Pmaxw, Usolid_int)\ntmaxw_ = get_free_dof_values(tmaxw)\nT_maxwell = Transfer(V_maxwell, Usolid_int)                    # vacuum → interface of the beam\nV_traction = TestFESpace(Γs, reffeu)\nmass_Γ = StaticLinearModel((u, v) -> ∫(u ⋅ v)dΓs, TrialFESpace(V_traction), V_traction, NothingBC(); backend=:compiled)","title":"Maxwell traction on the interface"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E09_hMRE_vacuum_staggered/#Weak-forms","page":"09 · hMRE beam in vacuum: staggered solution","text":"Each problem gets its residual and Jacobian as functions of the stage tau of its inner solve. The fields solved earlier in the same pass are read between their previous and new values (φh(τ), uhsolid(τ)), so that the inner load stepping of a problem applies the change of the other fields gradually (and can bisect it); the fields solved later are read at their last value (there  and  coincide).\n\nΨs, ∂Ψs∂F, ∂Ψs∂ℋ, ∂Ψs∂FF, ∂Ψs∂ℋF, ∂Ψs∂ℋℋ = model_solid()\nΨv, ∂Ψv∂F, ∂Ψv∂ℋ, ∂Ψv∂FF, ∂Ψv∂ℋF, ∂Ψv∂ℋℋ = model_vacuum_mag()\nΨvm, ∂Ψvm∂F, ∂Ψvm∂FF = model_vacuum_mech()\nF, H, J = get_Kinematics(Kinematics(Mechano, Solid))\nℋ₀ = get_Kinematics(Kinematics(Magneto, Solid))               # ∇φ ↦ -∇φ\n\nProblem 1, magnetic potential (boldsymbolmathcal H_0 = -nabla_0varphi gives the signs):\n\nres_mag(τ) = (φ, vφ) ->\n  -1.0 * ∫(∇(vφ) ⋅ (∂Ψs∂ℋ ∘ (F ∘ (∇(uhsolid(τ))'), ℋ₀ ∘ (∇(φ)), Nh)))dΩsolid -\n  ∫(∇(vφ) ⋅ (∂Ψv∂ℋ ∘ (F ∘ (∇(uhvacuum(τ))'), ℋ₀ ∘ (∇(φ)))))dΩvacuum\njac_mag(τ) = (φ, dφ, vφ) ->\n  ∫(∇(vφ) ⋅ ((∂Ψs∂ℋℋ ∘ (F ∘ (∇(uhsolid(τ))'), ℋ₀ ∘ (∇(φ)), Nh)) ⋅ ∇(dφ)))dΩsolid +\n  ∫(∇(vφ) ⋅ ((∂Ψv∂ℋℋ ∘ (F ∘ (∇(uhvacuum(τ))'), ℋ₀ ∘ (∇(φ)))) ⋅ ∇(dφ)))dΩvacuum\n\nProblem 2, mechanics of the beam. Before returning the form, res_mech(τ) updates the Maxwell traction with the current fields (the three steps of the previous section); it is a load, so it is not differentiated in the Jacobian.\n\nres_mech(τ) = begin\n  assemble_vector!(v -> ∫(∇(v)' ⊙ (∂Ψv∂F ∘ (F ∘ (∇(uhvacuum(τ))'), ℋ₀ ∘ (∇(φh(τ))))))dΩvacuum, Pmaxw_, V_maxwell)\n  transfer!(tmaxw_, T_maxwell, Pmaxw)\n  mass_Γ(tmaxw_)\n  (u, v) -> ∫(∇(v)' ⊙ (∂Ψs∂F ∘ (F ∘ (∇(u)'), ℋ₀ ∘ (∇(φh(τ))), Nh)))dΩsolid + ∫(v ⋅ tmaxw)dΓs\nend\njac_mech(τ) = (u, du, v) -> ∫(∇(v)' ⊙ ((∂Ψs∂FF ∘ (F ∘ (∇(u)'), ℋ₀ ∘ (∇(φh(τ))), Nh)) ⋅² (∇(du)')))dΩsolid\n\nProblem 3, motion of the vacuum:\n\nres_vac(τ) = (u, v) -> ∫(∇(v)' ⊙ (∂Ψvm∂F ∘ (F ∘ (∇(u)'))))dΩvacuum\njac_vac(τ) = (u, du, v) -> ∫(∇(v)' ⊙ ((∂Ψvm∂FF ∘ (F ∘ (∇(u)'))) ⋅² (∇(du)')))dΩvacuum","title":"Weak forms"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E09_hMRE_vacuum_staggered/#Solvers-and-computational-models","page":"09 · hMRE beam in vacuum: staggered solution","text":"One StaticNonlinearModel per problem, each solving for its own state (xh=), with the tolerances of the paper. Two of them need no Newton iteration at all, because the Dirichlet projection of ProjectDirichlet=true (see Solve) is a linear solve with the tangent that already does the work:\n\nthe magnetic problem is linear in varphi once the displacements are fixed, so the projection of the new potential on the box gives its solution and Newton converges at iteration 0;\nthe motion of the vacuum is not solved to equilibrium: rtol=2 accepts the starting point, the projection that moves the interface to the new position of the beam (one linear solve per pass), which is all the vacuum needs, a valid mesh that follows the beam (maxiter=1 only bounds what is never used).\n\nThe beam is solved to a 1 % relative residual, as in the paper (the coupling error of a staggered step is larger than that). The regression values in Checks rest on these loosely converged iterates; they reproduce across machines because the number of iterations is fixed by the data: HyperFEM's test suite asserts the FAST values below at rtol = 1e-6 on Linux. All of them, and the interface mass matrix above, assemble with the compiled backend (backend=:compiled, tutorial 01): at every pass the forms are rebound to the new stage and to the current states of the other fields, never compiled again.\n\nnls_mag = NewtonRaphson(LUSolver(); maxiter=20, atol=1e-9, rtol=1e-8, verbose=false)\ncm_mag = StaticNonlinearModel(res_mag, jac_mag, Uφ⁺, Vφ, Dφ; nls=nls_mag, xh=φh⁺, backend=:compiled)\nnls_mech = NewtonRaphson(LUSolver(); maxiter=20, atol=1e-6, rtol=1e-2, verbose=false)\ncm_mech = StaticNonlinearModel(res_mech, jac_mech, Uu_solid⁺, Vu_solid, Du_solid; nls=nls_mech, xh=uh_solid⁺, backend=:compiled)\nnls_vac = NewtonRaphson(LUSolver(); maxiter=1, rtol=2, verbose=false)\ncm_vac = StaticNonlinearModel(res_vac, jac_vac, Uu_vacuum⁺, Vu_vacuum, Du_vacuum; nls=nls_vac, xh=uh_vacuum⁺, backend=:compiled)\n\nStaggeredModel takes the three models (in the order they are solved), their states and the lagged states.\n\ncomp_model = StaggeredModel((cm_mag, cm_mech, cm_vac), (φh⁺, uh_solid⁺, uh_vacuum⁺), (φh⁻, uh_solid⁻, uh_vacuum⁻))","title":"Solvers and computational models"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E09_hMRE_vacuum_staggered/#Post-processing","page":"09 · hMRE beam in vacuum: staggered solution","text":"on_step runs after every accepted step: it writes the deformed beam and the vacuum with the field boldsymbolmathcal H_0 = -nabla_0varphi.\n\npvd_solid = createpvd(joinpath(outdir, \"solid\"))\npvd_vacuum = createpvd(joinpath(outdir, \"vacuum\"))\nnvtk = Ref(0)\nfunction write_step(τ, Δτ, nsub)\n  n = (nvtk[] += 1)\n  pvd_solid[τ] = createvtk(Ωsolid, joinpath(outdir, \"solid_$n\"), cellfields=[\"u\" => uh_solid⁺, \"H0\" => -∇(φh⁺)])\n  pvd_vacuum[τ] = createvtk(Ωvacuum, joinpath(outdir, \"vacuum_$n\"), cellfields=[\"u\" => uh_vacuum⁺, \"H0\" => -∇(φh⁺)])\nend","title":"Post-processing"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E09_hMRE_vacuum_staggered/#Solve","page":"09 · hMRE beam in vacuum: staggered solution","text":"StaggeredStepping controls the outer loop: nsteps equal load steps, and in each one max_substeps passes through the three problems. Without coupling_tol this is the fixed loop: every step makes exactly that number of passes, whatever the remaining coupling error (the paper's setting; see Going further for the converged loop). kargsolve holds the keywords of the inner solve! of each problem: one load increment per pass, bisected up to five times if Newton fails, and ProjectDirichlet=true for the two problems with moving Dirichlet data (the potential and the interface of the vacuum).\n\nstepping = FAST ? StaggeredStepping(nsteps=4, max_substeps=1, maxbisec=1) :\n                  StaggeredStepping(nsteps=20, max_substeps=2, maxbisec=1)\nkargsolve = ((stepping=LoadStepping(nsteps=1, maxbisec=5), ProjectDirichlet=true),\n             (stepping=LoadStepping(nsteps=1, maxbisec=5),),\n             (stepping=LoadStepping(nsteps=1, maxbisec=5), ProjectDirichlet=true))\nx, flag = solve!(comp_model; stepping=stepping, kargsolve=kargsolve, verbose=true, on_step=write_step)\nsavepvd(pvd_solid); savepvd(pvd_vacuum)\n\nget_log returns the record of the solve: per accepted step, the passes, the coupling error of every field after the last pass (the relative change of its free values in that pass) and the time spent in each problem. With one pass per step that change is just the increment of the step; from the second pass on it measures how far the three fields still are from agreeing with each other.\n\nslog = get_log(comp_model)\nprintln(slog)\ntf = reduce(+, slog.times)\n@printf(\"time in the inner solves: magnetic %.1f s, beam %.1f s, vacuum %.1f s\\n\", tf...)\n@printf(\"coupling error after the last pass of the last step: magnetic %.1e, beam %.1e, vacuum %.1e\\n\", slog.errors[end]...)","title":"Solve"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E09_hMRE_vacuum_staggered/#Checks","page":"09 · hMRE beam in vacuum: staggered solution","text":"Displacement of the tip of the beam (x = 50 mm), averaged over the tip face.\n\nχtip = CellField(x -> x[1] > 0.05 - 1e-6 ? 1.0 : 0.0, Γs)\nu_tip = sum(∫(χtip * uh_solid⁺)dΓs) / sum(∫(χtip)dΓs)\n@printf(\"tip displacement: (%.4f, %.4f) mm\\n\", 1e3 * u_tip[1], 1e3 * u_tip[2])\n\n(b) The vacuum follows the beam: on the interface its displacement equals the one of the beam. Both are taken to the interface space with Transfer (the beam's nodes there are free or clamped, the vacuum's are all Dirichlet).\n\nus_Γ = zeros(num_free_dofs(Usolid_int)); uv_Γ = zeros(num_free_dofs(Usolid_int))\ntransfer!(us_Γ, Transfer(Vu_solid, Usolid_int), uh_solid⁺)\ntransfer!(uv_Γ, Transfer(Vu_vacuum, Usolid_int), uh_vacuum⁺)\ngap = maximum(abs.(uv_Γ .- us_Γ)) / maximum(abs.(us_Γ))\nprintln(\"interface gap |u_vacuum - u_solid| / max|u_solid| = $gap\")\n\n(b) Neither mesh is inverted: the smallest J at the quadrature points.\n\nJmin(uh, dΩ) = minimum(minimum, (J ∘ (F ∘ ∇(uh)'))(get_cell_points(dΩ)))\nJmin_s, Jmin_v = Jmin(uh_solid⁺, dΩsolid), Jmin(uh_vacuum⁺, dΩvacuum)\n@printf(\"min J: beam %.4f, vacuum %.4f\\n\", Jmin_s, Jmin_v)\n\nnφ, ns, nv = norm(get_free_dof_values(φh⁺)), norm(get_free_dof_values(uh_solid⁺)), norm(get_free_dof_values(uh_vacuum⁺))\n@printf(\"‖φ‖ = %.10e   ‖u_s‖ = %.10e   ‖u_v‖ = %.10e   u_tip = (%.10e, %.10e)\\n\", nφ, ns, nv, u_tip[1], u_tip[2])\n\n@assert flag == 1 \"an inner solve did not converge\"\n@assert length(slog.τ) == stepping.nsteps && all(==(stepping.max_substeps), slog.nsub) \"unexpected number of steps or passes\"\n@assert gap < 1e-10 \"the vacuum does not follow the beam on the interface (gap $gap)\"\n@assert Jmin_s > 0 && Jmin_v > 0 \"inverted elements\"\n@assert u_tip[2] < 0 && u_tip[1] < 0 \"the tip should move down and towards the clamp (clockwise rotation)\"\n\n(c) Regression. FAST is the reduced case of the test suite of HyperFEM (test/SimulationsTests/HMREVacuumSimulation.jl, half of the potential, 4 steps of one pass); the same values here show that StaggeredStepping reproduces it. The full case: our own values (HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6, Gridap 0.20.8, Julia 1.13.0, macOS arm64).\n\nif FAST\n  @assert isapprox(nφ, 2.0940145799e4; rtol=1e-6) \"‖φ‖ = $nφ differs from the HyperFEM test\"\n  @assert isapprox(ns, 1.0426282896e-1; rtol=1e-6) \"‖u_s‖ = $ns differs from the HyperFEM test\"\n  @assert isapprox(nv, 2.4014448097e-1; rtol=1e-6) \"‖u_v‖ = $nv differs from the HyperFEM test\"\nelse\n  REF_PHI, REF_US, REF_UV, REF_TIP = 4.2100933777e4, 1.9454136102e-1, 4.5495058874e-1, -1.5853670013e-2\n  @assert isapprox(nφ, REF_PHI; rtol=1e-5) \"‖φ‖ = $nφ differs from the reference\"\n  @assert isapprox(ns, REF_US; rtol=1e-5) \"‖u_s‖ = $ns differs from the reference\"\n  @assert isapprox(nv, REF_UV; rtol=1e-5) \"‖u_v‖ = $nv differs from the reference\"\n  @assert isapprox(u_tip[2], REF_TIP; rtol=1e-5) \"tip deflection $(u_tip[2]) differs from the reference\"\nend","title":"Checks"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E09_hMRE_vacuum_staggered/#Going-further","page":"09 · hMRE beam in vacuum: staggered solution","text":"Converged staggered loop. With a coupling tolerance every step makes passes until the relative change of every field is below it, and a step that does not converge in max_substeps passes is rejected and bisected. The plain iteration converges slowly here (the field and the beam pull on each other); a quasi-Newton update of the beam displacement built from the passes of the step, IQNILS(), accelerates it (one relaxation per field):\nstepping = StaggeredStepping(nsteps=20, max_substeps=30, coupling_tol=1e-6, maxbisec=4,\n                             relaxation=(:none, IQNILS(), :none))\nget_log(comp_model) then shows how many passes every step needed. Compare the tip deflection with the fixed loop.\nAitken() is a cheaper scalar relaxation; StaggeredStepping(grow=1.5) lets the steps grow when they converge in few passes.\nTutorial 08 solves the same beam without the vacuum (the field applied directly in the beam), in one problem.\nPaper and original scripts: Perez-Garcia et al., Adv. Mater. (2025), doi:10.1002/adma.202518489; the repository MultiSimOLab/Adv_Mater_Ultra_Soft_Magnet (src/hMRE_Vacuum_2D.jl).","title":"Going further"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E19_Constitutive_models/#Tutorial-19:-Constitutive-models:-energies,-stresses-and-tangents","page":"19 · Constitutive models: energies, stresses and tangents","text":"Source · Notebook · nbviewer\n\nEvery simulation of HyperFEM starts from a constitutive model: a small object that returns the stored energy Psi(boldsymbol F), the first Piola–Kirchhoff stress partial_boldsymbol FPsi and the tangent partial^2_boldsymbol Fboldsymbol FPsi as plain Julia functions. This tutorial uses them without finite elements: it evaluates several hyperelastic models in the three classical homogeneous tests (uniaxial tension, equibiaxial tension and simple shear), checks them against closed-form solutions and against their own derivatives, and writes a new model with the same interface.\n\nWhat you will learn\n\nthe interface of the PhysicalModels: Ψ, ∂Ψ∂F, ∂Ψ∂FF = model(), and the extra arguments of the anisotropic models (the fibre directions);\nthe catalogue of 3D hyperelastic models: NeoHookean3D, MooneyRivlin3D, Yeoh3D, EightChain, IncompressibleNeoHookean3D, TransverseIsotropy3D, HGO_1Fiber, HGO_4Fibers, and how to add models with +;\nthe kinematics object (Kinematics, get_Kinematics, getIsoInvariants);\nhomogeneous tests: the incompressible closed forms and the compressible ones (lateral faces free of traction, found with a scalar Newton iteration that uses the tangent);\nhow to verify a model (derivatives, objectivity, stress-free reference state) and how to write your own;\nwhy the models are fast inside the compiled kernels: they are allocation-free isbits closures.\n\nMeasured on a Mac M5 Max (Julia 1.13.0, 4 Julia threads, 4 BLAS threads; HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6, Gridap 0.20.8): about 7 s including package loading and compilation; there is no FAST variant (the reduced and the full runs are the same, the computation takes milliseconds). No degrees of freedom: only point evaluations.","title":"Tutorial 19: Constitutive models: energies, stresses and tangents"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E19_Constitutive_models/#Problem-statement","page":"19 · Constitutive models: energies, stresses and tangents","text":"A hyperelastic material is defined by a stored energy per unit reference volume Psi(boldsymbol F) of the deformation gradient boldsymbol F = boldsymbol I + nabla_0boldsymbol u. The first Piola–Kirchhoff (nominal) stress and the tangent used by Newton's method are\n\nboldsymbol P = fracpartial Psipartial boldsymbol F qquad\nmathbb A = fracpartial^2 Psipartial boldsymbol Fpartial boldsymbol F qquad\nmathrm dboldsymbol P = mathbb A  mathrm dboldsymbol F \n\nMost models are written with the cofactor boldsymbol H = Jboldsymbol F^-T and the Jacobian J = detboldsymbol F, and with the invariants I_1 = boldsymbol Fboldsymbol F and I_2 = boldsymbol Hboldsymbol H.\n\nHomogeneous deformations, with boldsymbol e_i the Cartesian basis:\n\nuniaxial tension, boldsymbol F = mathrmdiag(lambda lambda_t lambda_t) with P_22 = P_33 = 0;\nequibiaxial tension, boldsymbol F = mathrmdiag(lambda lambda lambda_3) with P_33 = 0;\nsimple shear, boldsymbol F = boldsymbol I + gamma boldsymbol e_1 otimes boldsymbol e_2 (J = 1).\n\nFor an incompressible material (J = 1, lambda_t = lambda^-12, lambda_3 = lambda^-2) with Psi = W(I_1 I_2), W_1 = partial Wpartial I_1 and W_2 = partial Wpartial I_2, the nominal stresses are (Ogden, Non-linear elastic deformations, 1984, §7.2)\n\nP_mathrmuni = 2left(lambda - lambda^-2right)left(W_1 + lambda^-1 W_2right) qquad\nP_mathrmequi = 2left(lambda - lambda^-5right)left(W_1 + lambda^2 W_2right) qquad\nP_12 = 2gamma(W_1 + W_2)\n\nThe models of HyperFEM are compressible: they add volumetric terms of J to W. At J = 1 those terms only contribute stresses proportional to boldsymbol F^-T, which an incompressible material absorbs in its pressure: boldsymbol P = hatboldsymbol P(boldsymbol F) - pboldsymbol F^-T, with p given by the traction-free lateral faces. That is how the closed forms are checked below.","title":"Problem statement"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E19_Constitutive_models/#Setup","page":"19 · Constitutive models: energies, stresses and tangents","text":"using HyperFEM\nusing Gridap\nusing LinearAlgebra\nusing Printf\n\nconst FAST = get(ENV, \"HYPERFEM_TUTORIALS_FAST\", \"0\") == \"1\"   # same case in both modes (point evaluations)\noutdir = joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"data\", \"sims\", \"E19_Constitutive_models\")\nsetupfolder(outdir)                                            # CSV tables (not versioned)\n\nconst e1, e2, e3 = VectorValue(1.0, 0.0, 0.0), VectorValue(0.0, 1.0, 0.0), VectorValue(0.0, 0.0, 1.0)\ndiagF(a, b, c) = TensorValue(a, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, b, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, c)   # column-major storage","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E19_Constitutive_models/#Kinematics","page":"19 · Constitutive models: energies, stresses and tangents","text":"Kinematics(Mechano, Solid) holds the maps used by the weak forms: boldsymbol F(nablaboldsymbol u), boldsymbol H(boldsymbol F) and J(boldsymbol F). In a weak form they are composed with the FE gradient, F ∘ ∇(u)' (Gridap's ∇(u) is the transpose of nabla_0boldsymbol u); here we call them with a tensor. getIsoInvariants gives I_1, I_2 and I_3 = J.\n\nkinematics = Kinematics(Mechano, Solid)\nFmap, Hmap, Jmap = get_Kinematics(kinematics)\nI1map, I2map, I3map = getIsoInvariants(kinematics)\n∇u = TensorValue(0.10, 0.02, 0.00, 0.05, -0.03, 0.01, 0.00, 0.02, 0.04)\nF = Fmap(∇u)\nprintln(\"F = \", F)\nprintln(\"J = \", Jmap(F), \",  I1 = \", I1map(F), \",  I2 = \", I2map(F))\n@assert Fmap(∇u) == one(∇u) + ∇u\n@assert norm(Hmap(F) - det(F) * inv(F)') < 1e-14 * norm(Hmap(F)) \"H is the cofactor J F⁻ᵀ\"\n@assert abs(I2map(F) - Hmap(F) ⊙ Hmap(F)) < 1e-14 * I2map(F)","title":"Kinematics"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E19_Constitutive_models/#A-first-model","page":"19 · Constitutive models: energies, stresses and tangents","text":"A model is a struct with its parameters; calling it with no arguments returns three functions of boldsymbol F (closures that capture the parameters). The stress is a TensorValue{3,3} and the tangent a FourthOrderTensorValue{3}, applied to an increment with the double contraction ⋅², as in the jacobian of tutorial 02.\n\nnh = NeoHookean3D(λ=100.0, μ=1.0)                              # kPa\nΨ, ∂Ψ∂F, ∂Ψ∂FF = nh()\nprintln(\"Ψ(F) = \", Ψ(F), \" kPa\")\nprintln(\"P(F) = \", ∂Ψ∂F(F))\nprintln(typeof(∂Ψ∂FF(F)))\ndF = 1e-6 * TensorValue(1.0, -2.0, 0.5, 0.3, 1.0, -0.7, 0.2, 0.4, -1.0)\nprintln(\"‖P(F + dF) - P(F) - A:dF‖ / ‖A:dF‖ = \", norm(∂Ψ∂F(F + dF) - ∂Ψ∂F(F) - ∂Ψ∂FF(F) ⋅² dF) / norm(∂Ψ∂FF(F) ⋅² dF))","title":"A first model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E19_Constitutive_models/#The-model-catalogue","page":"19 · Constitutive models: energies, stresses and tangents","text":"The 3D hyperelastic models of HyperFEM 0.3.0 (their code is in HyperFEM's src/PhysicalModels/MechanicalModels.jl; some, like ?Yeoh3D or ?EightChain, have docstrings), all with a shear modulus close to 1 kPa and a bulk penalty lambda = 100 kPa (nearly incompressible):\n\nNeoHookean3D(λ, μ): tfracmu2(I_1-3) - muln J + tfraclambda2(J-1)^2;\nMooneyRivlin3D(λ, μ1, μ2): tfracmu_12(I_1-3) + tfracmu_22(I_2-3) - (mu_1+2mu_2)ln J + tfraclambda2(J-1)^2;\nYeoh3D(λ, C10, C20, C30): sum_k C_k0(I_1-3)^k - 2C_10ln J + tfraclambda2(J-1)^2;\nEightChain(μ, N): Arruda–Boyce in the isochoric invariant bar I_1 = J^-23 I_1 only, so it needs a volumetric term: here + VolumetricEnergy(λ), tfraclambda2(J-1)^2;\nIncompressibleNeoHookean3D(λ, μ, δ): neo-Hookean in bar I_1 with a regularised J (parameter delta) and a penalty lambda. HyperFEM has no exactly incompressible (mixed u–p) model: incompressibility is always a penalty;\nfibre models, functions of (boldsymbol F boldsymbol N) with boldsymbol N the fibre direction in the reference configuration: TransverseIsotropy3D(μ, α1, α2), polyconvex in I_4 = boldsymbol Fboldsymbol N^2 and I_5 = boldsymbol Hboldsymbol N^2, and the Holzapfel–Gasser–Ogden fibre energy HGO_1Fiber(c1, c2), tfracc_14c_2left(e^c_2(I_4-1)^2-1right) (HGO_4Fibers has four families). They have no volumetric term of their own and are added to an isotropic matrix.\n\nAdding models. + composes an isotropic model with another isotropic one (the result is isotropic) or with an anisotropic one (the result takes the fibre directions as extra arguments). Gent exists only in 2D (Gent2D, plane strain); a 3D Gent model is written below as the example of a user model.\n\nconst N_fibre = e1                                             # fibres along the loading direction\nconst N4 = (e1, VectorValue(0.0, 1.0, 1.0), VectorValue(1.0, 1.0, 0.0), VectorValue(1.0, 0.0, 1.0))","title":"The model catalogue"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E19_Constitutive_models/#Writing-your-own-model","page":"19 · Constitutive models: energies, stresses and tangents","text":"A model is any subtype of Mechano callable with no arguments that returns (Ψ, ∂Ψ∂F, ∂Ψ∂FF). That is all that residual, jacobian and energy use, so the new model works in every mechanical tutorial (02, 03, 13–15). Subtyping the (non-exported) HyperFEM.PhysicalModels.IsoElastic instead also enables + with the other models.\n\nThe compressible Gent model in 3D, with J_m the limit of I_1 - 3 (finite extensibility):\n\nPsi = -fracmu J_m2lnleft(1 - fracI_1-3J_mright) - muln J + fraclambda2(J-1)^2 \nqquad boldsymbol P = fracmugboldsymbol F + Psi_J boldsymbol H \nqquad g = 1 - fracI_1-3J_m quad Psi_J = -fracmuJ + lambda (J-1)\n\nmathbb A = fracmugmathbb I + frac2muJ_m g^2boldsymbol Fotimesboldsymbol F\n  + left(fracmuJ^2 + lambdaright)boldsymbol Hotimesboldsymbol H + Psi_Jfracpartialboldsymbol Hpartialboldsymbol F \n\n𝕀3D is the fourth-order identity and ×ᵢ⁴(F) is partialboldsymbol Hpartialboldsymbol F (both exported by HyperFEM). The three functions read only their argument and the parameters captured from the struct, never a non-constant global variable (a non-constant global has an unknown type, so every call that reads it is resolved at run time and is many times slower); so they compile to allocation-free code.\n\nstruct Gent3D <: HyperFEM.PhysicalModels.IsoElastic\n  μ::Float64\n  Jm::Float64\n  λ::Float64\nend\nGent3D(; μ::Float64, Jm::Float64, λ::Float64) = Gent3D(μ, Jm, λ)\n\nfunction (obj::Gent3D)()\n  μ, Jm, λ = obj.μ, obj.Jm, obj.λ\n  J(F) = det(F)\n  H(F) = det(F) * inv(F)'\n  g(F) = 1.0 - (tr(F' * F) - 3.0) / Jm\n  ΨJ(F) = -μ / J(F) + λ * (J(F) - 1.0)\n  Ψ(F) = -0.5 * μ * Jm * log(g(F)) - μ * log(J(F)) + 0.5 * λ * (J(F) - 1.0)^2\n  ∂Ψ∂F(F) = (μ / g(F)) * F + ΨJ(F) * H(F)\n  ∂Ψ∂FF(F) = (μ / g(F)) * 𝕀3D + (2.0 * μ / (Jm * g(F)^2)) * (F ⊗ F) +\n             (μ / J(F)^2 + λ) * (H(F) ⊗ H(F)) + ΨJ(F) * ×ᵢ⁴(F)\n  return (Ψ, ∂Ψ∂F, ∂Ψ∂FF)\nend\n\nA second user model: a fibre-reinforced material that adds an isotropic matrix and a fibre model, like +, but with the types of both parts as parameters of the struct and with the fibre arguments declared as Vararg{Any,K}. That declaration makes Julia compile one method per number of fibres; the reason is explained in the Efficiency section.\n\nstruct FibreReinforced{M<:Mechano,A<:Mechano} <: Mechano\n  matrix::M\n  fibres::A\nend\n\nfunction (obj::FibreReinforced)()\n  Ψm, Pm, Am = obj.matrix()\n  Ψf, Pf, Af = obj.fibres()\n  Ψ(F, N::Vararg{Any,K}) where {K} = Ψm(F) + Ψf(F, N...)\n  ∂Ψ∂F(F, N::Vararg{Any,K}) where {K} = Pm(F) + Pf(F, N...)\n  ∂Ψ∂FF(F, N::Vararg{Any,K}) where {K} = Am(F) + Af(F, N...)\n  return (Ψ, ∂Ψ∂F, ∂Ψ∂FF)\nend\n\nThe models of this tutorial: a name, the model and the extra arguments of its functions (the fibres).\n\nmodels = [\n  (name=\"NeoHookean3D\",       model=nh,                                                      args=()),\n  (name=\"MooneyRivlin3D\",     model=MooneyRivlin3D(λ=100.0, μ1=0.7, μ2=0.3),                 args=()),\n  (name=\"Yeoh3D\",             model=Yeoh3D(λ=100.0, C10=0.5, C20=0.02, C30=0.001),            args=()),\n  (name=\"EightChain+Vol\",     model=EightChain(μ=1.0, N=8.0) + VolumetricEnergy(λ=100.0),    args=()),\n  (name=\"IncompNeoHookean3D\", model=IncompressibleNeoHookean3D(λ=100.0, μ=1.0),             args=()),\n  (name=\"Gent3D (user)\",      model=Gent3D(μ=1.0, Jm=20.0, λ=100.0),                         args=()),\n  (name=\"NH+TransvIso\",       model=nh + TransverseIsotropy3D(μ=0.5, α1=1.5, α2=1.3),        args=(N_fibre,)),\n  (name=\"NH+HGO_1Fiber\",      model=nh + HGO_1Fiber(c1=1.0, c2=0.2),                         args=(N_fibre,)),\n  (name=\"NH+HGO_4Fibers\",     model=nh + HGO_4Fibers(c1=[2.0, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0], c2=[0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 0.5]), args=N4),\n  (name=\"FibreReinforced\",    model=FibreReinforced(nh, HGO_4Fibers(c1=[2.0, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0], c2=[0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 0.5])), args=N4),\n]","title":"Writing your own model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E19_Constitutive_models/#Homogeneous-tests","page":"19 · Constitutive models: energies, stresses and tangents","text":"For a compressible model the lateral stretch is unknown. The scalar equation P_22(lambda_t) = 0 is solved by Newton's method with the model's own tangent, mathrm dP_22mathrm dlambda_t = mathbb A  (boldsymbol e_2otimesboldsymbol e_2 + boldsymbol e_3otimesboldsymbol e_3)_22; for the equibiaxial test the unknown is lambda_3 and the equation P_33 = 0. The fibres of the anisotropic models are along boldsymbol e_1, so lambda_2 = lambda_3 still holds in uniaxial tension (with four fibre families it does not: those models are only used in the checks).\n\nfunction lateral_newton(∂Ψ∂F, ∂Ψ∂FF, Fof, dFda, i, a, args; tol=1e-13, maxiter=30)\n  for _ in 1:maxiter\n    F = Fof(a)\n    r = ∂Ψ∂F(F, args...)[i, i]\n    abs(r) < tol && return a, F\n    a -= r / (∂Ψ∂FF(F, args...) ⋅² dFda)[i, i]\n  end\n  error(\"lateral equilibrium did not converge\")\nend\n\nuniaxial(∂Ψ∂F, ∂Ψ∂FF, λ, args) = lateral_newton(∂Ψ∂F, ∂Ψ∂FF, a -> diagF(λ, a, a), e2 ⊗ e2 + e3 ⊗ e3, 2, 1 / sqrt(λ), args)\nequibiaxial(∂Ψ∂F, ∂Ψ∂FF, λ, args) = lateral_newton(∂Ψ∂F, ∂Ψ∂FF, a -> diagF(λ, λ, a), e3 ⊗ e3, 3, 1 / λ^2, args)\nshearF(γ) = one(TensorValue{3,3,Float64}) + γ * (e1 ⊗ e2)\n\nstretches = collect(1.0:0.1:2.0)\nshears = collect(0.0:0.1:1.0)\niso_models = filter(m -> length(m.args) <= 1, models)    # the four-fibre models are only checked\ntable_uni = zeros(length(stretches), length(iso_models))\ntable_lat = zeros(length(stretches), length(iso_models))\ntable_equi = zeros(length(stretches), length(iso_models))\ntable_shear = zeros(length(shears), length(iso_models))\nfor (j, m) in enumerate(iso_models)\n  _, Pm, Am = m.model()\n  for (k, λ) in enumerate(stretches)\n    λt, Fu = uniaxial(Pm, Am, λ, m.args)\n    table_uni[k, j] = Pm(Fu, m.args...)[1, 1]\n    table_lat[k, j] = λt\n    _, Fe = equibiaxial(Pm, Am, λ, m.args)\n    table_equi[k, j] = Pm(Fe, m.args...)[1, 1]\n  end\n  for (k, γ) in enumerate(shears)\n    table_shear[k, j] = Pm(shearF(γ), m.args...)[1, 2]\n  end\nend\n\nfunction print_table(title, xname, x, T, names)\n  println(\"\\n\", title)\n  @printf(\"%6s\", xname)\n  foreach(n -> @printf(\" %19s\", n), names)\n  println()\n  for k in eachindex(x)\n    @printf(\"%6.2f\", x[k])\n    foreach(v -> @printf(\" %19.6f\", v), T[k, :])\n    println()\n  end\nend\nfunction write_csv(file, xname, x, T, names)\n  open(joinpath(outdir, file), \"w\") do io\n    println(io, join([xname; names], \",\"))\n    for k in eachindex(x)\n      println(io, join(string.([x[k]; T[k, :]]), \",\"))\n    end\n  end\nend\ncolnames = [m.name for m in iso_models]\nprint_table(\"Uniaxial tension, lateral faces free: nominal stress P11 (kPa)\", \"λ\", stretches, table_uni, colnames)\nprint_table(\"Equibiaxial tension, P33 = 0: nominal stress P11 (kPa)\", \"λ\", stretches, table_equi, colnames)\nprint_table(\"Simple shear: nominal shear stress P12 (kPa)\", \"γ\", shears, table_shear, colnames)\n\nReading the tables: all the isotropic models share the initial slope (3mu in uniaxial tension, 6mu in equibiaxial tension and mu in shear, with mu = 1 kPa) and differ at large stretch: Mooney–Rivlin softens in uniaxial tension and stiffens in equibiaxial tension, Yeoh, Gent and the eight-chain model stiffen. The fibre models are much stiffer along the fibre; in simple shear the fibre boldsymbol N = boldsymbol e_1 is not stretched (boldsymbol Fboldsymbol e_1 = boldsymbol e_1), so HGO_1Fiber adds nothing, whereas TransverseIsotropy3D does, through I_5.\n\nwrite_csv(\"uniaxial_P11.csv\", \"stretch\", stretches, table_uni, colnames)\nwrite_csv(\"uniaxial_lateral_stretch.csv\", \"stretch\", stretches, table_lat, colnames)\nwrite_csv(\"equibiaxial_P11.csv\", \"stretch\", stretches, table_equi, colnames)\nwrite_csv(\"simple_shear_P12.csv\", \"gamma\", shears, table_shear, colnames)","title":"Homogeneous tests"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E19_Constitutive_models/#Checks","page":"19 · Constitutive models: energies, stresses and tangents","text":"(a) Closed forms. Incompressible tests: the model is evaluated at the isochoric boldsymbol F and the pressure p is eliminated with the traction-free lateral faces. The derivatives W_1, W_2 of the isochoric part of each model are written by hand (neo-Hookean W_1 = mu2; Mooney–Rivlin W_1 = mu_12, W_2 = mu_22; Yeoh W_1 = C_10 + 2C_20(I_1-3) + 3C_30(I_1-3)^2; Gent W_1 = mu(2g)).\n\nfunction incompressible_uniaxial(P, λ)\n  s = 1 / sqrt(λ)\n  P̂ = P(diagF(λ, s, s))\n  p = P̂[2, 2] * s                                              # P22 = P̂22 - p/s = 0\n  P̂[1, 1] - p / λ\nend\nfunction incompressible_equibiaxial(P, λ)\n  s = 1 / λ^2\n  P̂ = P(diagF(λ, λ, s))\n  p = P̂[3, 3] * s\n  P̂[1, 1] - p / λ\nend\nincompressible_shear(P, γ) = P(shearF(γ))[1, 2]                # F⁻ᵀ has no 12 component: p does not enter\n\nclosed_forms = [\n  (\"NeoHookean3D\",   NeoHookean3D(λ=100.0, μ=1.0),                    (I1, I2) -> (0.5, 0.0)),\n  (\"MooneyRivlin3D\", MooneyRivlin3D(λ=100.0, μ1=0.7, μ2=0.3),         (I1, I2) -> (0.35, 0.15)),\n  (\"Yeoh3D\",         Yeoh3D(λ=100.0, C10=0.5, C20=0.02, C30=0.001),    (I1, I2) -> (0.5 + 0.04 * (I1 - 3) + 0.003 * (I1 - 3)^2, 0.0)),\n  (\"Gent3D (user)\",  Gent3D(μ=1.0, Jm=20.0, λ=100.0),                  (I1, I2) -> (0.5 / (1 - (I1 - 3) / 20.0), 0.0)),\n]\nmax_err_a = 0.0\nfor (name, model, W) in closed_forms\n  _, Pm, _ = model()\n  err = 0.0\n  for λ in stretches[2:end]\n    W1, W2 = W(λ^2 + 2 / λ, 2λ + 1 / λ^2)\n    Pex = 2 * (λ - λ^-2) * (W1 + W2 / λ)\n    err = max(err, abs(incompressible_uniaxial(Pm, λ) - Pex) / abs(Pex))\n    W1, W2 = W(2λ^2 + λ^-4, λ^4 + 2 / λ^2)\n    Pex = 2 * (λ - λ^-5) * (W1 + λ^2 * W2)\n    err = max(err, abs(incompressible_equibiaxial(Pm, λ) - Pex) / abs(Pex))\n  end\n  for γ in shears[2:end]\n    W1, W2 = W(3 + γ^2, 3 + γ^2)\n    Pex = 2γ * (W1 + W2)\n    err = max(err, abs(incompressible_shear(Pm, γ) - Pex) / abs(Pex))\n  end\n  @printf(\"closed forms, %-15s max relative error %.2e\\n\", name, err)\n  global max_err_a = max(max_err_a, err)\nend\n@assert max_err_a < 1e-10 \"incompressible closed forms not reproduced (max error $max_err_a)\"\n\nThe compressible neo-Hookean in uniaxial tension also has a closed form (tutorial 02): J is the positive root of lambda J^2 - (lambda - mulambda_1)J - mu = 0 and P_11 = mu(lambda_1 - Jlambda_1^2). The nearly incompressible models are close to the incompressible curve: the difference is of order mulambda.\n\nJ_nh(λ₁; λ=100.0, μ=1.0) = ((λ - μ / λ₁) + sqrt((λ - μ / λ₁)^2 + 4λ * μ)) / (2λ)\nP11_nh(λ₁; μ=1.0) = μ * (λ₁ - J_nh(λ₁) / λ₁^2)\nerr_nh = maximum(abs.(table_uni[2:end, 1] .- P11_nh.(stretches[2:end])) ./ P11_nh.(stretches[2:end]))\nprintln(\"compressible neo-Hookean, uniaxial: max relative error $(@sprintf(\"%.2e\", err_nh))\")\n@assert err_nh < 1e-10 \"compressible neo-Hookean uniaxial curve away from the closed form\"\ngap = maximum(abs.(table_uni[2:end, 1] .- 1.0 .* (stretches[2:end] .- stretches[2:end] .^ -2)) ./ table_uni[2:end, 1])\nprintln(\"compressible (λ = 100 μ) vs incompressible neo-Hookean, uniaxial: max relative difference $(@sprintf(\"%.2e\", gap))\")\n@assert gap < 0.05\n\n(b) Invariants of every model. At three states (small strain, large strain, compression), the stress must be the derivative of the energy and the tangent the derivative of the stress. Both are checked against central finite differences with Richardson extrapolation along the nine directions boldsymbol e_iotimesboldsymbol e_j (relative error of the whole Jacobian, tolerance 1e-6). Also: the tangent has the major symmetry of a second derivative; the energy is objective, Psi(boldsymbol Qboldsymbol F) = Psi(boldsymbol F) and boldsymbol P(boldsymbol Qboldsymbol F) = boldsymbol Qboldsymbol P(boldsymbol F) for a rotation boldsymbol Q (the fibres are referential); and the reference state is free of energy and stress.\n\nconst Fs = (small=one(TensorValue{3,3,Float64}) + 1e-3 * TensorValue(1.0:9.0...),\n            large=TensorValue(1.3, 0.1, 0.0, 0.2, 0.9, -0.1, 0.0, 0.15, 1.1),\n            compression=TensorValue(0.5, 0.02, 0.0, 0.01, 0.6, 0.0, 0.0, 0.03, 0.67))\nconst Ebasis = ntuple(m -> TensorValue(ntuple(n -> n == m ? 1.0 : 0.0, 9)), 9)\nconst Q = let a = VectorValue(1.0, 2.0, 3.0) / sqrt(14.0), θ = 0.7     # rotation (Rodrigues)\n  K = TensorValue(0.0, a[3], -a[2], -a[3], 0.0, a[1], a[2], -a[1], 0.0)\n  one(K) + sin(θ) * K + (1 - cos(θ)) * (K ⋅ K)\nend\n\nfunction fd(f, F, dF; h=1e-4)                                  # Richardson-extrapolated central difference\n  d(δ) = (f(F + δ * dF) - f(F - δ * dF)) / (2δ)\n  (4 * d(h / 2) - d(h)) / 3\nend\nfunction derivative_errors(Ψ, P, A, F, args)\n  eP = eA = nP = nA = 0.0\n  for E in Ebasis\n    dΨ = fd(G -> Ψ(G, args...), F, E)\n    eP += (P(F, args...) ⊙ E - dΨ)^2\n    nP += dΨ^2\n    dP = fd(G -> P(G, args...), F, E)\n    eA += norm(A(F, args...) ⋅² E - dP)^2\n    nA += norm(dP)^2\n  end\n  sqrt(eP / nP), sqrt(eA / nA)\nend\nfunction symmetry_error(A, F, args)\n  B, C = TensorValue(1.0:9.0...), TensorValue(0.3, -1.0, 2.0, 0.5, 0.1, -0.4, 1.5, 0.2, -0.6)\n  AF = A(F, args...)\n  abs(C ⊙ (AF ⋅² B) - B ⊙ (AF ⋅² C)) / abs(C ⊙ (AF ⋅² B))\nend\n\nprintln(\"\\nmodel                 P vs dΨ/dF   A vs dP/dF   A symmetry   objectivity  Ψ(I), |P(I)|\")\nfor m in models\n  Ψm, Pm, Am = m.model()\n  eP = eA = es = eo = 0.0\n  for (state, Fm) in pairs(Fs)\n    a, b = derivative_errors(Ψm, Pm, Am, Fm, m.args)\n    eP, eA = max(eP, a), max(eA, b)\n    es = max(es, symmetry_error(Am, Fm, m.args))\n    state == :small && continue                                # Ψ ≈ 1e-6: relative round-off too large\n    eo = max(eo, abs(Ψm(Q ⋅ Fm, m.args...) - Ψm(Fm, m.args...)) / abs(Ψm(Fm, m.args...)),\n                 norm(Pm(Q ⋅ Fm, m.args...) - Q ⋅ Pm(Fm, m.args...)) / norm(Pm(Fm, m.args...)))\n  end\n  Id = one(TensorValue{3,3,Float64})\n  rest = max(abs(Ψm(Id, m.args...)), norm(Pm(Id, m.args...)))\n  @printf(\"%-20s  %.2e     %.2e     %.2e     %.2e     %.2e\\n\", m.name, eP, eA, es, eo, rest)\n  @assert eP < 1e-6 \"$(m.name): stress is not the derivative of the energy ($eP)\"\n  @assert eA < 1e-6 \"$(m.name): tangent is not the derivative of the stress ($eA)\"\n  @assert es < 1e-12 \"$(m.name): tangent without major symmetry ($es)\"\n  @assert eo < 1e-12 \"$(m.name): not objective ($eo)\"\n  @assert rest < 1e-12 \"$(m.name): energy or stress at F = I ($rest)\"\nend\n\nThe user composition FibreReinforced and the library's + are the same material:\n\nlet (Ψa, Pa, Aa) = models[end-1].model(), (Ψb, Pb, Ab) = models[end].model(), F = Fs.large\n  @assert Ψa(F, N4...) ≈ Ψb(F, N4...) && Pa(F, N4...) ≈ Pb(F, N4...) && Aa(F, N4...) ≈ Ab(F, N4...)\nend\n\n(c) Regression values (HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6, Gridap 0.20.8, Julia 1.13.0, macOS arm64): the nominal stress at lambda = 2 in uniaxial tension with free lateral faces. Point arithmetic without BLAS: the tolerance is tighter than in the FE tutorials.\n\nP11_ref = Dict(\"NeoHookean3D\" => 1.748762315869, \"MooneyRivlin3D\" => 1.487635363752,\n               \"Yeoh3D\" => 2.071539451067, \"EightChain+Vol\" => 1.844431004853,\n               \"IncompNeoHookean3D\" => 1.731210061009, \"Gent3D (user)\" => 1.943697469481,\n               \"NH+TransvIso\" => 3.705533075008, \"NH+HGO_1Fiber\" => 38.046647102346)\nfor (j, m) in enumerate(iso_models)\n  @printf(\"%-20s  P11(λ = 2) = %.12f kPa   λt = %.12f\\n\", m.name, table_uni[end, j], table_lat[end, j])\n  @assert isapprox(table_uni[end, j], P11_ref[m.name]; rtol=1e-8) \"$(m.name): regression value\"\nend","title":"Checks"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E19_Constitutive_models/#Efficiency","page":"19 · Constitutive models: energies, stresses and tangents","text":"The FE forms call these functions at every quadrature point (∂Ψ∂F ∘ (F ∘ ∇(u)', args...)), and the compiled backend of HyperFEMKernels captures the forms, closures included, in type-specialised loops. That is fast because a model is an immutable struct of numbers and its functions are closures over those numbers: isbits values that the compiler inlines, with no heap allocation per evaluation. The check below measures the allocations of one stress and one tangent evaluation after compilation (in a function, so that the measurement does not include the dynamic call from global scope) and the time per evaluation. Both are printed, not asserted: whether a closure is inlined depends on the compiler's heuristics, which change between Julia versions and platforms (the table below was measured with Julia 1.13 on macOS arm64), so a zero-allocation assertion could fail on another machine for reasons that have nothing to do with the model. Composite models (+) and HGO_4Fibers, whose parameters are Vectors, are not isbits structs, but their stress functions still do not allocate, with one exception (measured).\n\nThe exception: + with four fibre families. In HyperFEM 0.3.0, nh + HGO_4Fibers(...) allocates at every evaluation: 720 bytes per stress and 1920 bytes per tangent, and its tangent takes about twice as long as that of the same material written as FibreReinforced (663 ns against 327 ns in our measurement). This is a known issue of the library, and a fix is pending in HyperFEM; the model is correct (it passes every check above) and remains a valid choice. + builds (F, N...) -> Ψ1(F) + Ψ2(F, N...), and Julia does not specialise a function on a Vararg argument that it only passes on to another function (Julia manual, Performance tips, «Be aware of when Julia avoids specializing»): the call with four fibres goes through a generic method. With one fibre the compiler still inlines it. The lesson for your own models is in FibreReinforced above: declaring N::Vararg{Any,K} forces the specialisation, with the same energy and stresses and no allocation. Writing the sum of the two models with explicit arguments has the same effect.\n\nfunction allocations(f, F, args)\n  f(F, args...)\n  @allocated f(F, args...)\nend\nfunction ns_per_eval(f, F, args; n=200_000)\n  s = 0.0\n  f(F, args...)\n  t = @elapsed for i in 1:n\n    s += first(Tuple(f(F + (1e-12 * i) * one(F), args...)))\n  end\n  1e9 * t / n, s\nend\nprintln(\"\\nmodel                 isbits(model)  isbits(P)  alloc P  alloc A   ns/P    ns/A\")\nfor m in models\n  _, Pm, Am = m.model()\n  aP, aA = allocations(Pm, Fs.large, m.args), allocations(Am, Fs.large, m.args)\n  tP, _ = ns_per_eval(Pm, Fs.large, m.args)\n  tA, _ = ns_per_eval(Am, Fs.large, m.args)\n  @printf(\"%-20s  %-13s  %-9s  %7d  %7d  %6.1f  %6.1f\\n\", m.name, isbits(m.model), isbits(Pm), aP, aA, tP, tA)\nend","title":"Efficiency"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E19_Constitutive_models/#Going-further","page":"19 · Constitutive models: energies, stresses and tangents","text":"SecondPiola(model) gives Psi, boldsymbol S and partialboldsymbol Spartialboldsymbol C as functions of boldsymbol C = boldsymbol F^Tboldsymbol F for the models that implement it (IsochoricNeoHookean3D in 0.3.0).\nCoupled models follow the same pattern with more arguments: ElectroMechModel returns derivatives with respect to (boldsymbol F boldsymbol E) (tutorials 04 and 05), MagnetoMechModel with respect to (boldsymbol F boldsymbolmathcal H) (tutorials 08 and 09), and the viscous models take the previous deformation and an internal variable (tutorial 16).\nPut the user model in a simulation: replace NeoHookean3D by Gent3D(μ=1.0, Jm=20.0, λ=100.0) in tutorial 02; for a homogeneous bar the force–stretch curve is the uniaxial column above.\nThe derivative checks of the library itself are in HyperFEM's test/ConstitutiveModelsTests/DerivativeConsistencyTests.jl.","title":"Going further"},{"category":"section","location":"#HyperFEM-tutorials","page":"Home","text":"Tutorials of HyperFEM.jl, a library of the Gridap ecosystem for the finite-element simulation of multiphysics problems with hyperelastic materials: thermo-, electro- and magneto-mechanics at finite strains, contact, fluids, transient problems and solvers for large systems.\n\nEvery tutorial is a self-contained Julia script in the src/ folder of the repository. Its page explains the problem, the equations and every step of the code, and links to a Jupyter notebook generated from the same script. Each script ends with quantitative checks of its result (exact solutions, published benchmarks, conservation laws), which are also the test suite of the repository.","title":"HyperFEM tutorials"},{"category":"section","location":"#Getting-started","page":"Home","text":"git clone https://github.com/MultiSimOLab/HyperFEMTutorials.git\ncd HyperFEMTutorials\njulia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\njulia -t 4 --project=. src/E02_Hyperelastic_stretch.jl\n\nThe tutorials need Julia 1.12 or later and use HyperFEM 0.3, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6 and Gridap 0.20.8. See Running and testing for reduced cases, notebooks and tests.","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"#Tutorials","page":"Home","text":"Nº Tutorial What it shows\n01 Poisson equation linear problems, Dirichlet and Neumann conditions, convergence under refinement\n02 Uniaxial stretch of a hyperelastic bar constitutive models, load stepping, Newton–Raphson, post-processing\n03 Fibre-reinforced cylinder under internal pressure anisotropic models, fibre fields, follower pressure\n04 Electro-mechanics of a dielectric elastomer multi-field problems, coupled weak forms\n05 Anisotropic electro-mechanics composition of models, extra arguments of the constitutive functions\n06 Third-medium contact contact through a soft medium, injectivity line search, hybrid Newton\n07 Topology optimisation in preparation (HyperShape)\n08 Magneto-mechanical hMRE beam hard-magnetic soft materials, lagged loads\n09 hMRE beam in vacuum: staggered solution staggered models, coupling between meshes, transfers\n10 Stokes flow around a cylinder mixed and stabilised formulations\n11 Transient Navier–Stokes flow around a cylinder time integration with TransientModel, benchmarks\n12 Pulsatile blood flow in a patient aorta 3D transient flow, Windkessel outlet conditions\n13 Linear solvers for large problems direct, Krylov and algebraic multigrid solvers, inexact Newton\n14 Geometric h- and p-multigrid mesh hierarchies, matrix-free p-multigrid\n15 Elastodynamics: free vibration of a cantilever structural dynamics, energy conservation\n16 Visco-electro-mechanics: creep of a dielectric bimorph generalised Maxwell viscoelasticity, internal variables, quasi-static time stepping\n17 Thermo-mechanics: transient heat conduction and thermal expansion coupled thermal and mechanical fields, weak forms from the energy, adaptive BDF2\n18 Explicit dynamics: wave propagation in a bar central differences, lumped mass, critical time step, wave speed\n19 Constitutive models: energies, stresses and tangents the constitutive-model API, homogeneous tests, writing your own model","title":"Tutorials"},{"category":"section","location":"#How-to-cite","page":"Home","text":"If you use HyperFEM, please cite\n\nC. Perez-Garcia, R. Ortigosa, J. Martínez-Frutos and D. Garcia-Gonzalez, Topology and material optimization in ultra-soft magnetoactive structures: making advantage of residual anisotropies, Adv. Mater. (2025) e18489, https://doi.org/10.1002/adma.202518489,\n\ntogether with the citations requested by Gridap.","title":"How to cite"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E10_Stokes/#Tutorial-10:-Stokes-flow-around-a-cylinder","page":"10 · Stokes flow around a cylinder","text":"Source · Notebook · nbviewer\n\nCreeping (Stokes) flow is the simplest incompressible flow problem and already contains its main difficulty: velocity and pressure are coupled by a saddle-point system, and not every pair of finite element spaces gives a stable pressure. We first check the pipeline on Poiseuille flow in a straight channel, where the stable Taylor–Hood element reproduces the exact solution, and then compute the flow around the cylinder of the DFG benchmark (Schäfer & Turek 1996) with Taylor–Hood (P2–P1) and with equal-order P1–P1 elements stabilised by PSPG. Tutorial 11 adds inertia and time to the same geometry.\n\nMeasured on a Mac (M5 Max, 4 Julia threads, 4 BLAS threads, Julia 1.13.0, HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6, Gridap 0.20.8): FAST and full runs are the same case (the problem is linear and small), 45 s including compilation and package loading; 335 dofs (Poiseuille, Q2–Q1), 32 878 dofs (P2–P1) and 10 972 dofs (P1–P1) on the cylinder mesh. After compilation, assembling and solving P2–P1 takes 0.085 s; one assembly of its matrix takes 4 ms with the compiled backend and 49 ms with Gridap's (P1–P1 + PSPG: 2.5 ms and 20 ms).\n\nWhat you will learn\n\nmixed velocity–pressure spaces: MultiFieldFESpace and MultiFieldBC([Du, NothingBC()]);\na Dirichlet inflow profile given as a function, and the natural (do-nothing) outflow condition;\nStaticLinearModel for a multi-field linear problem;\ninf–sup stability: Taylor–Hood elements, and equal-order elements with PSPG stabilisation;\npost-processing of a flow: drag and lift on a body, pressure drop and mass balance;\nwhy a function evaluated at points must not read non-constant global variables;\nassembling with the compiled backend of HyperFEMKernels (backend=:compiled).","title":"Tutorial 10: Stokes flow around a cylinder"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E10_Stokes/#Problem-statement","page":"10 · Stokes flow around a cylinder","text":"Find the velocity boldsymbol u and the pressure p in the channel Omega such that\n\n-mu Delta boldsymbol u + nabla p = boldsymbol 0  qquad nablacdotboldsymbol u = 0 quad textin  Omega \n\nwith a parabolic velocity boldsymbol u = (4U_m y (H-y)H^2 0) on the inlet x = 0, no slip (boldsymbol u = boldsymbol 0) on the walls and on the cylinder, and the do-nothing condition mu partial_boldsymbol nboldsymbol u - pboldsymbol n = boldsymbol 0 on the outlet. The weak form: find (boldsymbol u p) such that, for all (boldsymbol v q) with boldsymbol v = boldsymbol 0 on the Dirichlet boundary,\n\nint_Omega mu nablaboldsymbol u  nablaboldsymbol v mathrm dOmega\n- int_Omega p nablacdotboldsymbol v mathrm dOmega\n- int_Omega q nablacdotboldsymbol u mathrm dOmega = 0 \n\nThe do-nothing condition is the natural condition of this form; it is not imposed anywhere. (With the symmetric form 2muboldsymbolvarepsilon(boldsymbol u)boldsymbolvarepsilon(boldsymbol v), used in tutorial 11, the natural condition is (2muboldsymbolvarepsilon(boldsymbol u) - pboldsymbol I)boldsymbol n = boldsymbol 0 instead, which a Poiseuille profile does not satisfy at the outlet.)\n\nThe parameters are those of the DFG benchmark: H = 041 m, U_m = 03 m/s, mu = 10^-3 Pa s, rho = 1 kg/m³ (rho only enters the definition of the force coefficients).","title":"Problem statement"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E10_Stokes/#Setup","page":"10 · Stokes flow around a cylinder","text":"using HyperFEM\nusing Gridap\nusing GridapGmsh\nusing HyperFEMKernels                                          # enables `backend=:compiled`\n\nconst FAST = get(ENV, \"HYPERFEM_TUTORIALS_FAST\", \"0\") == \"1\"   # reduced case for the test suite (same case here)\noutdir = joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"data\", \"sims\", \"E10_Stokes\")\nsetupfolder(outdir)                                            # VTK output (not versioned)\n\nThe inflow profile u_in is a Julia function of the position, evaluated at every node of the inlet and, in the error of part 0, at every quadrature point. A function evaluated at points may only read its arguments and const globals. A non-constant global, such as Um written without const, can change its type at any time, so Julia cannot specialise the function on it: every one of the thousands of evaluations looks the variable up and dispatches dynamically. The result is the same, but slower; in the stabilised blood flow of tutorial 12 a non-constant μ inside the stabilisation parameter made the compiled Jacobian 8 times slower. Hence the const below. (A closure built by a function, as inflow(Um, H) in tutorial 11, is the other way.) A number that only multiplies a term of an integral, such as μ in ∫(μ * …)dΩ, is read once when the form is built and does not need to be constant.\n\nconst μ = 1e-3                                                 # Pa s\nconst ρ = 1.0                                                  # kg/m³\nconst Um = 0.3                                                 # m/s, peak inlet velocity\nconst H = 0.41                                                 # m, channel height\nu_in(x) = VectorValue(4Um * x[2] * (H - x[2]) / H^2, 0.0)       # parabolic inflow profile","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E10_Stokes/#Part-0:-Poiseuille-flow-in-a-straight-channel","page":"10 · Stokes flow around a cylinder","text":"In a channel without obstacle the solution is known: the inflow profile is carried unchanged to the outlet and the pressure falls linearly, p = 8mu U_m (L - x)H^2. The velocity is quadratic in y and the pressure linear in x, so the Taylor–Hood pair Q2–Q1 (biquadratic velocity, bilinear pressure) contains the exact solution: the error must be at the level of round-off.","title":"Part 0: Poiseuille flow in a straight channel"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E10_Stokes/#Geometry-and-mesh","page":"10 · Stokes flow around a cylinder","text":"The default tags of a 2D Cartesian model are 1–4 for the corners and 5–8 for the sides (y = 0, y = H, x = 0, x = L). The walls take the corners.\n\nconst L0 = 1.0                                                 # m, channel length (read by p_exact below)\nmodel0 = CartesianDiscreteModel((0, L0, 0, H), (10, 4))\nlabels0 = get_face_labeling(model0)\nadd_tag_from_tags!(labels0, \"inlet\", [7])\nadd_tag_from_tags!(labels0, \"wall\", [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6])","title":"Geometry and mesh"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E10_Stokes/#Boundary-conditions-and-spaces","page":"10 · Stokes flow around a cylinder","text":"The velocity has Dirichlet conditions; the pressure has none (NothingBC()): it is fixed by the outlet condition. MultiFieldBC keeps one set of conditions per field, in the order of the fields.\n\nDu0 = BoundaryConditions(\n  BoundaryCondition(\"inlet\", u_in),\n  BoundaryCondition(\"wall\", [0.0, 0.0]))\nD0 = MultiFieldBC([Du0, NothingBC()])\n\nreffeu = ReferenceFE(lagrangian, VectorValue{2,Float64}, 2)    # velocity: order 2\nreffep = ReferenceFE(lagrangian, Float64, 1)                   # pressure: order 1\nVu0 = TestFESpace(model0, reffeu, D0[1], conformity=:H1)\nVp0 = TestFESpace(model0, reffep, D0[2], conformity=:H1)\nV0 = MultiFieldFESpace([Vu0, Vp0])\nU0 = MultiFieldFESpace([TrialFESpace(Vu0, D0[1], 1.0), TrialFESpace(Vp0, D0[2], 1.0)])","title":"Boundary conditions and spaces"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E10_Stokes/#Weak-form-and-solution","page":"10 · Stokes flow around a cylinder","text":"StaticLinearModel(l, a, U, V, D) assembles the bilinear form a and the linear form l (with the Dirichlet lift) and factorises the matrix when it is built; solve! then solves the system. backend=:compiled assembles with HyperFEMKernels: the forms are written as for Gridap and compiled into one kernel per element (backend=:lazy, the default, assembles with Gridap and gives the same solution). On the cylinder mesh below the compiled assembly of the P2–P1 matrix takes 4 ms and Gridap's 49 ms; the sparse LU factorisation, the right solver at this size, dominates the solve either way.\n\nΩ0 = Triangulation(model0)\ndΩ0 = Measure(Ω0, 4)\nf = VectorValue(0.0, 0.0)                                       # body force\na0((u, p), (v, q)) = ∫(μ * (∇(u) ⊙ ∇(v)))dΩ0 - ∫(p * (∇ ⋅ v))dΩ0 - ∫(q * (∇ ⋅ u))dΩ0\nl0((v, q)) = ∫(f ⋅ v)dΩ0\npoiseuille = StaticLinearModel(l0, a0, U0, V0, D0; backend=:compiled)\nsolve!(poiseuille; Assembly=false)\nuh0, ph0 = get_state(poiseuille)\n\np_exact(x) = 8μ * Um * (L0 - x[1]) / H^2\nnormL2(w) = sqrt(sum(∫(w ⊙ w)dΩ0))\neu0 = normL2(uh0 - u_in) / normL2(CellField(u_in, Ω0))\nep0 = normL2(ph0 - p_exact) / normL2(CellField(p_exact, Ω0))\nprintln(\"Poiseuille (Q2–Q1, $(num_free_dofs(U0)) dofs): relative L2 errors u $(eu0), p $(ep0)\")\nwritevtk(Ω0, joinpath(outdir, \"poiseuille\"), cellfields=[\"u\" => uh0, \"p\" => ph0])","title":"Weak form and solution"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E10_Stokes/#Part-1:-flow-around-a-cylinder","page":"10 · Stokes flow around a cylinder","text":"","title":"Part 1: flow around a cylinder"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E10_Stokes/#Geometry-and-mesh-2","page":"10 · Stokes flow around a cylinder","text":"The channel of the DFG benchmark: 22 times 041 m with a cylinder of diameter D = 01 m centred at (02 02), slightly below the axis of the channel. 7 402 linear triangles, refined around the cylinder; the mesh is made by data/models/turek.jl (Gmsh) with tags inlet, outlet, wall, cylinder.\n\nmodel = GmshDiscreteModel(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"data\", \"models\", \"turek2d.msh\"))\nDc = 0.1                                                       # m, cylinder diameter\nŪ = 2Um / 3                                                    # mean inflow velocity\nΩ = Triangulation(model)\n\nD_bc = MultiFieldBC([\n  BoundaryConditions(\n    BoundaryCondition(\"inlet\", u_in),\n    BoundaryCondition(\"wall\", [0.0, 0.0]),\n    BoundaryCondition(\"cylinder\", [0.0, 0.0])),\n  NothingBC()])","title":"Geometry and mesh"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E10_Stokes/#Post-processing-quantities","page":"10 · Stokes flow around a cylinder","text":"The force of the fluid on the cylinder is boldsymbol F = -int_Gamma_c boldsymbolsigmaboldsymbol n mathrm dGamma with boldsymbolsigma = -pboldsymbol I + mu(nablaboldsymbol u + nablaboldsymbol u^T) and boldsymbol n the outward normal of the fluid domain (pointing into the cylinder); the drag and lift coefficients are C_DL = 2F_xy(rho bar U^2 D). The pressure difference Delta p = p(015 02) - p(025 02) between the front and the back of the cylinder is the other output of the benchmark. The volume flow rates through the inlet and the outlet must be equal: the pressure test function q = 1 turns the discrete continuity equation into int_partialOmegaboldsymbol ucdotboldsymbol nmathrm dGamma = 0, for both discretisations below.\n\nΓc = BoundaryTriangulation(model, tags=\"cylinder\")\ndΓc = Measure(Γc, 4)\nnc = get_normal_vector(Γc)\nΓin, Γout = BoundaryTriangulation(model, tags=\"inlet\"), BoundaryTriangulation(model, tags=\"outlet\")\ndΓin, dΓout = Measure(Γin, 4), Measure(Γout, 4)\nnin, nout = get_normal_vector(Γin), get_normal_vector(Γout)\n\nfunction flow_outputs(uh, ph)\n  σ = μ * (∇(uh) + ∇(uh)') - ph * I2D\n  F = -sum(∫(σ ⋅ nc)dΓc)\n  cD, cL = 2F[1] / (ρ * Ū^2 * Dc), 2F[2] / (ρ * Ū^2 * Dc)\n  Δp = ph(Point(0.15, 0.2)) - ph(Point(0.25, 0.2))\n  Qin, Qout = -sum(∫(uh ⋅ nin)dΓin), sum(∫(uh ⋅ nout)dΓout)\n  (; cD, cL, Δp, Qin, Qout)\nend\n\nEach solve writes its velocity and pressure through a PostProcessor (called once by solve! of a linear model).\n\nfunction vtk_writer(name, model_)\n  record(post) = (get_pvd(post)[0.0] = createvtk(Ω, joinpath(outdir, name),\n                    cellfields=[\"u\" => get_state(model_)[1], \"p\" => get_state(model_)[2]]))\n  PostProcessor(model_, record; is_vtk=true, filepath=outdir)\nend","title":"Post-processing quantities"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E10_Stokes/#Taylor–Hood-P2–P1","page":"10 · Stokes flow around a cylinder","text":"The same element as in part 0, now on triangles. It satisfies the discrete inf–sup condition, so the plain weak form above is stable.\n\ndΩ2 = Measure(Ω, 4)\nVu2 = TestFESpace(model, reffeu, D_bc[1], conformity=:H1)\nVp2 = TestFESpace(model, reffep, D_bc[2], conformity=:H1)\nV2 = MultiFieldFESpace([Vu2, Vp2])\nU2 = MultiFieldFESpace([TrialFESpace(Vu2, D_bc[1], 1.0), TrialFESpace(Vp2, D_bc[2], 1.0)])\na2((u, p), (v, q)) = ∫(μ * (∇(u) ⊙ ∇(v)))dΩ2 - ∫(p * (∇ ⋅ v))dΩ2 - ∫(q * (∇ ⋅ u))dΩ2\nl2((v, q)) = ∫(f ⋅ v)dΩ2\nstokes_th = StaticLinearModel(l2, a2, U2, V2, D_bc; backend=:compiled)\nsolve!(stokes_th; Assembly=false, post=vtk_writer(\"cylinder_P2P1\", stokes_th))\nth = flow_outputs(get_state(stokes_th)...)\nprintln(\"P2–P1 ($(num_free_dofs(U2)) dofs): C_D = $(th.cD), C_L = $(th.cL), Δp = $(th.Δp), Q_in = $(th.Qin), Q_out = $(th.Qout)\")","title":"Taylor–Hood P2–P1"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E10_Stokes/#Equal-order-P1–P1-with-PSPG","page":"10 · Stokes flow around a cylinder","text":"Linear velocity and pressure on the same mesh are cheaper and simpler (one set of nodes), but violate the inf–sup condition: the plain weak form admits spurious pressure oscillations. The pressure-stabilised Petrov–Galerkin method (PSPG; Hughes, Franca & Balestra 1986, DOI 10.1016/0045-7825(86)90025-3) adds the residual of the momentum equation tested with -taunabla q to the continuity equation:\n\nldots - int_Omega qnablacdotboldsymbol umathrm dOmega\n- sum_K int_K tau nabla q cdot (-muDeltaboldsymbol u + nabla p)mathrm dOmega = 0 \nqquad tau = frach_K^212mu \n\nThe method is consistent (the exact solution makes the added term vanish) and, with linear velocities, Deltaboldsymbol u = boldsymbol 0 inside each element, so only taunabla qcdotnabla p remains. h_K = sqrt2K is the size of each triangle, a piecewise constant CellField; tau is the value of the stabilisation parameter of tutorial 11 at zero velocity.\n\nreffeu1 = ReferenceFE(lagrangian, VectorValue{2,Float64}, 1)\ndΩ1 = Measure(Ω, 2)\nVu1 = TestFESpace(model, reffeu1, D_bc[1], conformity=:H1)\nVp1 = TestFESpace(model, reffep, D_bc[2], conformity=:H1)\nV1 = MultiFieldFESpace([Vu1, Vp1])\nU1 = MultiFieldFESpace([TrialFESpace(Vu1, D_bc[1], 1.0), TrialFESpace(Vp1, D_bc[2], 1.0)])\nhK = CellField(map(a -> sqrt(2a), collect(get_cell_measure(Ω))), Ω)\nτ = hK * hK / (12μ)\na1((u, p), (v, q)) = ∫(μ * (∇(u) ⊙ ∇(v)))dΩ1 - ∫(p * (∇ ⋅ v))dΩ1 - ∫(q * (∇ ⋅ u))dΩ1 -\n                     ∫(τ * (∇(q) ⋅ ∇(p)))dΩ1\nl1((v, q)) = ∫(f ⋅ v)dΩ1\nstokes_pspg = StaticLinearModel(l1, a1, U1, V1, D_bc; backend=:compiled)\nsolve!(stokes_pspg; Assembly=false, post=vtk_writer(\"cylinder_P1P1_PSPG\", stokes_pspg))\nps = flow_outputs(get_state(stokes_pspg)...)\nprintln(\"P1–P1 + PSPG ($(num_free_dofs(U1)) dofs): C_D = $(ps.cD), C_L = $(ps.cL), Δp = $(ps.Δp), Q_in = $(ps.Qin), Q_out = $(ps.Qout)\")","title":"Equal order P1–P1 with PSPG"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E10_Stokes/#Checks","page":"10 · Stokes flow around a cylinder","text":"Part 0 reproduces the exact solution; in part 1 the flow rate is conserved by both elements (up to round-off), the two elements agree on the drag and the pressure drop, and the Taylor–Hood values are kept as regression values (HyperFEM 0.3.0, Gridap 0.20.8, Julia 1.13.0, macOS arm64). The two elements give C_D approx 313 and 310, Delta p approx 00456 and 00453 Pa: without inertia the drag is well below the C_D = 558 of the same inflow with the Navier–Stokes equations (tutorial 11).\n\n@assert eu0 < 1e-9 && ep0 < 1e-9 \"Poiseuille flow not reproduced (errors $eu0, $ep0)\"\n@assert abs(th.Qin - 2Um * H / 3) < 1e-10 \"P2–P1: inflow rate different from the exact 2 Um H / 3\"\nfor r in (th, ps)\n  @assert abs(r.Qout - r.Qin) < 1e-9 * r.Qin \"outflow rate different from the inflow rate\"\nend\n@assert isapprox(ps.cD, th.cD, rtol=0.05) \"P1–P1 + PSPG drag differs from P2–P1 by more than 5 %\"\n@assert isapprox(ps.Δp, th.Δp, rtol=0.05) \"P1–P1 + PSPG pressure drop differs from P2–P1 by more than 5 %\"\n@assert isapprox(th.cD, 3.1347333364256826, rtol=1e-5) \"regression: C_D = $(th.cD) (P2–P1)\"\n@assert isapprox(th.Δp, 0.04555520370905265, rtol=1e-5) \"regression: Δp = $(th.Δp) (P2–P1)\"","title":"Checks"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E10_Stokes/#Going-further","page":"10 · Stokes flow around a cylinder","text":"Remove the PSPG term (τ = 0): the P1–P1 pressure is polluted by oscillations (look at p in ParaView) and the pressure drop is wrong, although the velocity may look reasonable.\nReplace munablaboldsymbol unablaboldsymbol v by the symmetric form 2muboldsymbolvarepsilon(boldsymbol u)boldsymbolvarepsilon(boldsymbol v) in part 0: the solution is no longer the exact Poiseuille flow near the outlet (see the problem statement).\nThe Stokes flow is symmetric in time: reverse the inflow and the flow pattern reverses exactly. With inertia it does not; tutorial 11 solves the Navier–Stokes equations on this mesh and compares with the published values of the benchmark: M. Schäfer and S. Turek, Benchmark computations of laminar flow around a cylinder, Notes on Numerical Fluid Mechanics 52 (1996) 547–566, DOI 10.1007/978-3-322-89849-4_39.\nA finer mesh: julia --project=. data/models/turek.jl 0.01 writes data/models/turek2d_h0.01.msh.","title":"Going further"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E11_NavierStokes_transient/#Tutorial-11:-Transient-Navier–Stokes-flow-around-a-cylinder","page":"11 · Transient Navier–Stokes flow around a cylinder","text":"Source · Notebook · nbviewer\n\nThe flow around a cylinder in a channel is the classical benchmark of incompressible flow solvers (Schäfer & Turek 1996). At a Reynolds number of 20 the flow is steady, with two attached vortices behind the cylinder; at 100 the wake becomes unstable and sheds vortices periodically (the von Kármán street). We march the Navier–Stokes equations in time with a second-order BDF scheme on the mesh of tutorial 10, reach the steady state of the case 2D-1 and compare the drag coefficient and the pressure drop with the published values; the full run then computes the periodic case 2D-2.\n\nMeasured on a Mac (M5 Max, 4 Julia threads, 4 BLAS threads, Julia 1.13.0, HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6, Gridap 0.20.8): 10 972 dofs; FAST (case 2D-1, 12 time steps) 67 s and full (2D-1 with both backends, then 2D-2 with 800 steps) 145 s, including compilation and package loading. A step of 2D-2 (assembly and LU) takes 0.037 s; one Jacobian assembly takes 5.6 ms with the compiled backend and 58 ms with Gridap's.\n\nWhat you will learn\n\nTransientModel at its general level: the residual and the tangent are written with the time context of the step (∂t, δ∂t, the previous states u⁻, u⁻⁻), here with BDF(2);\ntangent_update=:step: a problem linear in the unknown of the step (Oseen linearisation), assembled and solved once per step;\nresidual-based stabilisation of equal-order elements (SUPG, PSPG, LSIC);\nTimeStepping, and recording forces at every step with a PostProcessor;\nassembling with the compiled backend of HyperFEMKernels (backend=:compiled), and checking that it is faster than Gridap's assembly;\nvalidation against a published benchmark.","title":"Tutorial 11: Transient Navier–Stokes flow around a cylinder"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E11_NavierStokes_transient/#Problem-statement","page":"11 · Transient Navier–Stokes flow around a cylinder","text":"Find the velocity boldsymbol u and the pressure p such that\n\nrholeft(partial_tboldsymbol u + (nablaboldsymbol u)boldsymbol uright) - nablacdotboldsymbolsigma = boldsymbol 0 \nqquad nablacdotboldsymbol u = 0  qquad\nboldsymbolsigma = -pboldsymbol I + 2muboldsymbolvarepsilon(boldsymbol u) \n\nin the channel of tutorial 10, starting from rest, with the parabolic inflow boldsymbol u = (4U_m y(H-y)H^2 0) (H = 041 m) imposed from t = 0, no slip on the walls and on the cylinder, and boldsymbolsigmaboldsymbol n = boldsymbol 0 on the outlet (the natural condition of the symmetric form below). rho = 1 kg/m³, mu = 10^-3 Pa s, and the Reynolds number is Re = bar U Dnu with the mean inflow velocity bar U = 2U_m3 and the cylinder diameter D = 01 m: U_m = 03 m/s gives Re = 20 (case 2D-1) and U_m = 15 m/s gives Re = 100 (2D-2).\n\nTime discretisation. BDF2 replaces the time derivative at t_n+1 by partial_tboldsymbol u approx (3boldsymbol u - 4boldsymbol u^n + boldsymbol u^n-1)(2Delta t) (backward Euler, (boldsymbol u - boldsymbol u^n)Delta t, in the first step, which has no boldsymbol u^n-1). Linearisation. The advecting velocity of the convective term is extrapolated from the history, boldsymbol w = 2boldsymbol u^n - boldsymbol u^n-1 (boldsymbol u^n in the first step): the convective term (nablaboldsymbol u)boldsymbol w becomes linear in the unknown (an Oseen problem), the extrapolation error is O(Delta t^2) like that of BDF2, and every step is one linear solve.\n\nStabilisation. Linear velocity and pressure need PSPG (tutorial 10), and convection-dominated flow needs SUPG (streamline upwind Petrov–Galerkin). With the residual of the momentum equation in each element, boldsymbol R = rhopartial_tboldsymbol u + rho(nablaboldsymbol u)boldsymbol w + nabla p (the viscous term vanishes inside linear elements), the weak form of a step is\n\nbeginaligned\nint_Omega rhopartial_tboldsymbol ucdotboldsymbol v\n+ 2muboldsymbolvarepsilon(boldsymbol u)boldsymbolvarepsilon(boldsymbol v)\n+ rho(nablaboldsymbol u)boldsymbol wcdotboldsymbol v\n- pnablacdotboldsymbol v - qnablacdotboldsymbol u mathrm dOmega \nquad + sum_Kint_K tau_mathrmSUPG(nablaboldsymbol v)boldsymbol wcdotboldsymbol R\n- tau_mathrmPSPGnabla qcdotboldsymbol R\n+ tau_mathrmLSIC(nablacdotboldsymbol v)(nablacdotboldsymbol u) mathrm dOmega = 0 \nendaligned\n\nwith tau_mathrmSUPG = left((2boldsymbol u^nh)^2 + 9(4mu(rho h^2))^2right)^-12, tau_mathrmPSPG = tau_mathrmSUPGrho and tau_mathrmLSIC = rhoboldsymbol u^n h2 (Tezduyar 1992, DOI 10.1016/S0065-2156(08)70153-4), evaluated with the velocity of the previous step and the element size h = sqrt2K.","title":"Problem statement"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E11_NavierStokes_transient/#Setup","page":"11 · Transient Navier–Stokes flow around a cylinder","text":"using HyperFEM\nusing Gridap\nusing GridapGmsh\nusing LinearAlgebra\nusing HyperFEMKernels                                          # enables `backend=:compiled`\nusing Gridap.Algebra: allocate_jacobian\n\nconst FAST = get(ENV, \"HYPERFEM_TUTORIALS_FAST\", \"0\") == \"1\"   # reduced case for the test suite\noutdir = joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"data\", \"sims\", \"E11_NavierStokes_transient\")\nsetupfolder(outdir)                                            # VTK output (not versioned)\n\nThe material constants are const because the stabilisation parameters below read them at every quadrature point (tutorial 10 explains why a function evaluated at points must read only its arguments and const globals).\n\nconst ρ = 1.0                                                  # kg/m³\nconst μ = 1e-3                                                 # Pa s (ν = μ/ρ = 1e-3 m²/s)\nH, Dc = 0.41, 0.1                                              # m: channel height, cylinder diameter","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E11_NavierStokes_transient/#Geometry-and-mesh","page":"11 · Transient Navier–Stokes flow around a cylinder","text":"The DFG channel of tutorial 10 (7 402 linear triangles, generator data/models/turek.jl).\n\nmodel = GmshDiscreteModel(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"data\", \"models\", \"turek2d.msh\"))\nΩ = Triangulation(model)\ndΩ = Measure(Ω, 3)\nhe = CellField(map(a -> sqrt(2a), collect(get_cell_measure(Ω))), Ω)   # element size (triangles)","title":"Geometry and mesh"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E11_NavierStokes_transient/#Boundary-conditions-and-finite-element-spaces","page":"11 · Transient Navier–Stokes flow around a cylinder","text":"Equal-order P1–P1. The inflow profile is a function of the position; it has no time function, so it is applied in full from the first step (an impulsive start). The third argument of TrialFESpace is the time at which the Dirichlet values are first evaluated: the initial time, 0 (with a time function on the inflow, as in tutorial 12, the value matters). The two cases of this tutorial have different inflow velocities, so the profile is not a function of constants as in tutorial 10: inflow(Um, H) builds it as a closure, which stores the values of Um and H it was built with (the other way to keep a function evaluated at points free of non-constant globals).\n\nUm = 0.3                                                       # m/s: Re = 20\ninflow(Um, H) = x -> VectorValue(4Um * x[2] * (H - x[2]) / H^2, 0.0)\nD_bc = MultiFieldBC([\n  BoundaryConditions(\n    BoundaryCondition(\"inlet\", inflow(Um, H)),\n    BoundaryCondition(\"wall\", [0.0, 0.0]),\n    BoundaryCondition(\"cylinder\", [0.0, 0.0])),\n  NothingBC()])\nreffeu = ReferenceFE(lagrangian, VectorValue{2,Float64}, 1)\nreffep = ReferenceFE(lagrangian, Float64, 1)\nVu = TestFESpace(model, reffeu, D_bc[1], conformity=:H1)\nVp = TestFESpace(model, reffep, D_bc[2], conformity=:H1)\nV = MultiFieldFESpace([Vu, Vp])\nU = MultiFieldFESpace([TrialFESpace(Vu, D_bc[1], 0.0), TrialFESpace(Vp, D_bc[2], 0.0)])\nprintln(\"P1–P1: $(num_free_dofs(U)) dofs\")","title":"Boundary conditions and finite element spaces"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E11_NavierStokes_transient/#Weak-form","page":"11 · Transient Navier–Stokes flow around a cylinder","text":"At the general level of TransientModel the residual and the tangent are functions of the time context ctx of the step, which returns the forms. ctx[1] is the context of the velocity field: ∂t(cu, u) is the BDF time derivative c_0boldsymbol u - (c_1boldsymbol u^n - c_2boldsymbol u^n-1) of the unknown u (backward Euler in the first step), δ∂t(cu, du) its derivative in the direction du (c_0deltaboldsymbol u), cu.u⁻ and cu.u⁻⁻ are boldsymbol u^n and boldsymbol u^n-1, and cu.step is the number of the step. The time derivative also enters the SUPG and PSPG terms through the residual boldsymbol R.\n\nτsupg(u, h) = ((2 * norm(u) / h)^2 + 9 * (4μ / (ρ * h^2))^2)^(-1 / 2)\nτpspg(u, h) = τsupg(u, h) / ρ\nτlsic(u, h) = ρ * norm(u) * h * 0.5\nε(∇u) = 0.5 * (∇u + ∇u')\n\nThe advecting velocity boldsymbol w is written as one expression for every step (the operator of the model is rebuilt every step and must keep its type).\n\nfunction advecting(cu)\n  a, b = cu.step == 1 ? (1.0, 0.0) : (2.0, 1.0)\n  a * cu.u⁻ - b * cu.u⁻⁻\nend\nfunction res(ctx)\n  cu = ctx[1]\n  un = cu.u⁻; w = advecting(cu)\n  τs = τsupg ∘ (un, he); τp = τpspg ∘ (un, he); τl = τlsic ∘ (un, he)\n  ((u, p), (v, q)) -> begin\n    ut = ∂t(cu, u)\n    Ru = ρ * ut + ρ * (∇(u)' ⋅ w)\n    ∫(ρ * (ut ⋅ v))dΩ + ∫(2μ * (ε ∘ (∇(u))) ⊙ (ε ∘ (∇(v))))dΩ + ∫(ρ * ((∇(u)' ⋅ w) ⋅ v))dΩ -\n    ∫(p * (∇ ⋅ v))dΩ - ∫(q * (∇ ⋅ u))dΩ +\n    ∫(τs * ((∇(v)' ⋅ w) ⋅ Ru))dΩ + ∫(τs * ((∇(v)' ⋅ w) ⋅ ∇(p)))dΩ -\n    ∫(τp * (∇(q) ⋅ Ru))dΩ - ∫(τp * (∇(q) ⋅ ∇(p)))dΩ +\n    ∫(τl * (∇ ⋅ v) * (∇ ⋅ u))dΩ\n  end\nend\nfunction jac(ctx)\n  cu = ctx[1]\n  un = cu.u⁻; w = advecting(cu)\n  τs = τsupg ∘ (un, he); τp = τpspg ∘ (un, he); τl = τlsic ∘ (un, he)\n  ((u, p), (du, dp), (v, q)) -> begin\n    dut = δ∂t(cu, du)\n    dRu = ρ * dut + ρ * (∇(du)' ⋅ w)\n    ∫(ρ * (dut ⋅ v))dΩ + ∫(2μ * (ε ∘ (∇(du))) ⊙ (ε ∘ (∇(v))))dΩ + ∫(ρ * ((∇(du)' ⋅ w) ⋅ v))dΩ -\n    ∫(dp * (∇ ⋅ v))dΩ - ∫(q * (∇ ⋅ du))dΩ +\n    ∫(τs * ((∇(v)' ⋅ w) ⋅ dRu))dΩ + ∫(τs * ((∇(v)' ⋅ w) ⋅ ∇(dp)))dΩ -\n    ∫(τp * (∇(q) ⋅ dRu))dΩ - ∫(τp * (∇(q) ⋅ ∇(dp)))dΩ +\n    ∫(τl * (∇ ⋅ v) * (∇ ⋅ du))dΩ\n  end\nend","title":"Weak form"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E11_NavierStokes_transient/#Solver-and-computational-model","page":"11 · Transient Navier–Stokes flow around a cylinder","text":"The step problem is linear in the unknown, with a tangent that depends on the history (through boldsymbol w and the tau's): tangent_update=:step assembles it once per step and solves once from the zero guess, with no Newton iterations. With 11 000 dofs a sparse LU is the right linear solver (tutorial 13 shows the iterative solvers for larger problems).\n\nbackend=:compiled assembles the forms with HyperFEMKernels instead of Gridap: the weak form is compiled once into a kernel per element, and at every later step the model only re-captures the forms of the new step (the history fields and the BDF coefficients) without compiling again. The forms are written exactly as for Gridap; backend=:lazy (the default) gives the same solution, assembled by Gridap.\n\nnls = NewtonRaphson(LUSolver(); verbose=0)\nflow = TransientModel(res, jac, U, V, D_bc; scheme=BDF(2), tangent_update=:step, nls=nls, backend=:compiled)","title":"Solver and computational model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E11_NavierStokes_transient/#Post-processing","page":"11 · Transient Navier–Stokes flow around a cylinder","text":"The force on the cylinder, boldsymbol F = -int_Gamma_cboldsymbolsigmaboldsymbol nmathrm dGamma (boldsymbol n the outward normal of the fluid), gives the drag and lift coefficients C_DL = 2F_xy(rhobar U^2 D); Delta p = p(015 02) - p(025 02) is the pressure difference between the front and the back of the cylinder.\n\nΓc = BoundaryTriangulation(model, tags=\"cylinder\")\ndΓc = Measure(Γc, 3)\nnc = get_normal_vector(Γc)\nfunction coefficients(xh, Ū)\n  uh, ph = xh\n  σ = 2μ * (ε ∘ (∇(uh))) - ph * I2D\n  F = -sum(∫(σ ⋅ nc)dΓc)\n  cD, cL = 2 * F[1] / (ρ * Ū^2 * Dc), 2 * F[2] / (ρ * Ū^2 * Dc)\n  Δp = ph(Point(0.15, 0.2)) - ph(Point(0.25, 0.2))\n  (; cD, cL, Δp)\nend\n\nAfter every step the drag coefficient is stored (post.Λ holds the times of the accepted steps) and the last state is written to VTK.\n\nŪ = 2Um / 3\nΔt, nsteps = 0.5, 12\ncD_history = Float64[]\nfunction record(post)\n  xh = get_state(flow)\n  push!(cD_history, coefficients(xh, Ū).cD)\n  t = post.Λ[end]\n  println(\"step $(post.iter)  t = $t s  C_D = $(cD_history[end])\")\n  if post.iter == nsteps\n    get_pvd(post)[t] = createvtk(Ω, joinpath(outdir, \"re20\"), cellfields=[\"u\" => xh[1], \"p\" => xh[2]])\n  end\nend\npost = PostProcessor(flow, record; is_vtk=true, filepath=outdir)","title":"Post-processing"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E11_NavierStokes_transient/#Solve:-case-2D-1-(Re-20)","page":"11 · Transient Navier–Stokes flow around a cylinder","text":"The flow starts from rest and reaches the steady state in a few seconds; the time step is large (Delta t = 05 s) because only the final state matters here.\n\nHyperFEMKernels.reset_counters!()\nt2d1 = @elapsed x, flag = solve!(flow; stepping=TimeStepping(; Δt=Δt, t_end=nsteps * Δt), post=post)\nre20 = coefficients(get_state(flow), Ū)\nprintln(\"2D-1: C_D = $(re20.cD), C_L = $(re20.cL), Δp = $(re20.Δp)  (reference 5.5795, 0.0106, 0.1175)\")\nprintln(\"2D-1: $nsteps steps in $(round(t2d1, digits=2)) s (the first ones include compilation)\")\n\nWhat did the compiled backend do? HyperFEMKernels.work_summary lists the groups of integrals of the operator of the last step and the path each one takes: the Jacobian of this form is :factorised (test factor times trial factor at every point, the fastest path); :duals would mean automatic differentiation with nested dual numbers. HyperFEMKernels.counters() counts the work done since reset_counters!(): update_full the steps whose forms had to be compiled again (one, during the start-up of the scheme: the first step, backward Euler with a single previous state, differs from the BDF2 steps that follow) and update_fast the steps that only re-captured the new history.\n\nws = HyperFEMKernels.work_summary(flow.caches.op.inner)\nforeach(g -> println(\"  \", g.kind, \" group \", g.group, \": \", g.cells, \" cells × \", g.points, \" points, path \", g.path), ws.groups)\ncounts = HyperFEMKernels.counters()\nprintln(\"  Gridap fallbacks: residual $(ws.fallback_residual), Jacobian $(ws.fallback_jacobian); updates: $(counts.update_fast) fast, $(counts.update_full) full\")","title":"Solve: case 2D-1 (Re = 20)"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E11_NavierStokes_transient/#Checks-(2D-1)","page":"11 · Transient Navier–Stokes flow around a cylinder","text":"Reference values: C_D = 55795, C_L = 00106, Delta p = 01175 (rounded from the high-order computation of Nabh 1998, Heidelberg preprint 42/98, listed with the FEATFLOW benchmark results; all within the intervals of Schäfer & Turek 1996). The tolerances, 3 % and 2 %, are those of the test of this simulation in HyperFEM; the regression value boldsymbol x is that of the same test (HyperFEM 0.3.0, Gridap 0.20.8, Julia 1.13.0, macOS arm64). The lift is not checked: at Re = 20 it is tiny (0.2 % of the drag), and the computed C_L = -0037 differs from 0.0106 by about 1 % of the force on the cylinder, the size of the drag error (−1.8 %); P1–P1 on this mesh does not resolve it.\n\nThe compiled backend is checked here in both modes without timings: the Jacobian takes the :factorised path, nothing falls back to Gridap and the forms are not compiled again in the loop. The timing check (next section) runs only in the full run.\n\n@assert flag == 1 \"the time loop did not complete\"\n@assert length(cD_history) == nsteps \"a time step was bisected\"\n@assert isapprox(re20.cD, 5.5795, rtol=0.03) \"C_D = $(re20.cD) away from the benchmark value 5.5795\"\n@assert isapprox(re20.Δp, 0.1175, rtol=0.02) \"Δp = $(re20.Δp) away from the benchmark value 0.1175\"\n@assert abs(cD_history[end] - cD_history[end-1]) < 1e-3 * cD_history[end] \"the flow is not steady at t = $(nsteps * Δt) s\"\n@assert isapprox(norm(x), 13.67687986714, rtol=1e-5) \"regression: ‖x‖ = $(norm(x))\"\n@assert all(g.path === :factorised for g in ws.groups if g.kind === :jacobian) \"the compiled Jacobian is not factorised\"\n@assert ws.fallback_residual == 0 && ws.fallback_jacobian == 0 \"part of the forms is assembled by Gridap\"\n@assert counts.update_full <= 1 \"the compiled forms were compiled again during the time loop\"","title":"Checks (2D-1)"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E11_NavierStokes_transient/#Compiled-or-Gridap-assembly?","page":"11 · Transient Navier–Stokes flow around a cylinder","text":"The same model with backend=:lazy gives the same solution. We time one assembly of the Jacobian of the last step with each backend, on the same state, after a warm-up call (flow.caches.op is the operator of the step that the solver assembles, flow.caches.x the state). The compiled Jacobian must be at least twice as fast (we measured 5 to 10 times): this check catches a slow pattern in the forms, such as a function evaluated at points that reads a non-constant global (tutorial 10), which turns every evaluation at a point into a dynamic call. With a non-constant mu inside τsupg we measured 42 ms for the compiled Jacobian instead of 6 ms, against 67 ms for Gridap's.\n\nfunction jacobian_seconds(m)\n  op, xm = m.caches.op, m.caches.x\n  A = allocate_jacobian(op, xm)\n  Gridap.Algebra.jacobian!(A, op, xm)                          # warm-up (compilation)\n  minimum(@elapsed(Gridap.Algebra.jacobian!(A, op, xm)) for _ in 1:5)\nend\nif !FAST\n  Ul = MultiFieldFESpace([TrialFESpace(Vu, D_bc[1], 0.0), TrialFESpace(Vp, D_bc[2], 0.0)])\n  flow_lazy = TransientModel(res, jac, Ul, V, D_bc; scheme=BDF(2), tangent_update=:step, nls=nls, backend=:lazy)\n  tlazy = @elapsed xl, _ = solve!(flow_lazy; stepping=TimeStepping(; Δt=Δt, t_end=nsteps * Δt))\n  tj_compiled, tj_lazy = jacobian_seconds(flow), jacobian_seconds(flow_lazy)\n  println(\"2D-1 with Gridap's assembly: $nsteps steps in $(round(tlazy, digits=2)) s, ‖x - x_lazy‖/‖x‖ = $(norm(x - xl) / norm(x))\")\n  println(\"one Jacobian: compiled $(round(1e3 * tj_compiled, digits=2)) ms, Gridap $(round(1e3 * tj_lazy, digits=2)) ms\")\n  @assert norm(x - xl) < 1e-8 * norm(x) \"the two backends give different solutions\"\n  @assert tj_compiled < tj_lazy / 2 \"the compiled Jacobian ($tj_compiled s) is not twice as fast as Gridap's ($tj_lazy s)\"\nend","title":"Compiled or Gridap assembly?"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E11_NavierStokes_transient/#Case-2D-2-(Re-100):-vortex-shedding","page":"11 · Transient Navier–Stokes flow around a cylinder","text":"With U_m = 15 m/s the wake is unstable. The cylinder is slightly below the axis of the channel, so the symmetric start is disturbed from the beginning and the shedding develops by itself: the lift oscillates with its final amplitude after some 3 s. Only the inflow changes: new boundary conditions, spaces and model, with the same res and jac. The time step resolves the shedding period (about 0.33 s) with some 30 steps. We record the force coefficients and the pressure difference at every step, and write the flow every few steps during the last period.\n\nif !FAST\n  Um2 = 1.5                                                    # m/s: Re = 100\n  Ū2 = 2Um2 / 3\n  D2 = MultiFieldBC([\n    BoundaryConditions(\n      BoundaryCondition(\"inlet\", inflow(Um2, H)),\n      BoundaryCondition(\"wall\", [0.0, 0.0]),\n      BoundaryCondition(\"cylinder\", [0.0, 0.0])),\n    NothingBC()])\n  Vu2 = TestFESpace(model, reffeu, D2[1], conformity=:H1)\n  Vp2 = TestFESpace(model, reffep, D2[2], conformity=:H1)\n  V2 = MultiFieldFESpace([Vu2, Vp2])\n  U2 = MultiFieldFESpace([TrialFESpace(Vu2, D2[1], 0.0), TrialFESpace(Vp2, D2[2], 0.0)])\n  flow2 = TransientModel(res, jac, U2, V2, D2; scheme=BDF(2), tangent_update=:step, nls=nls, backend=:compiled)\n\n  Δt2, t_end2 = 0.01, 8.0\n  ts, cDs, cLs, Δps = Float64[], Float64[], Float64[], Float64[]\n  function record2(post)\n    xh = get_state(flow2)\n    c = coefficients(xh, Ū2)\n    t = post.Λ[end]\n    push!(ts, t); push!(cDs, c.cD); push!(cLs, c.cL); push!(Δps, c.Δp)\n    post.iter % 50 == 0 && println(\"t = $(round(t, digits=2)) s  C_D = $(c.cD)  C_L = $(c.cL)\")\n    if t > t_end2 - 0.34 && post.iter % 3 == 0\n      get_pvd(post)[t] = createvtk(Ω, joinpath(outdir, \"re100_$(post.iter)\"), cellfields=[\"u\" => xh[1], \"p\" => xh[2]])\n    end\n  end\n  post2 = PostProcessor(flow2, record2; is_vtk=true, filepath=outdir)\n  t2d2 = @elapsed x2, flag2 = solve!(flow2; stepping=TimeStepping(; Δt=Δt2, t_end=t_end2), post=post2)\n  println(\"2D-2: $(length(ts)) steps in $(round(t2d2, digits=1)) s, $(round(t2d2 / length(ts), digits=3)) s per step\")\nend","title":"Case 2D-2 (Re = 100): vortex shedding"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E11_NavierStokes_transient/#Shedding-frequency","page":"11 · Transient Navier–Stokes flow around a cylinder","text":"The Strouhal number St = f Dbar U from the period 1f between the upward zero crossings of C_L - overlineC_L in the last 3 s, and the maxima of C_D and C_L there. Delta p is reported, as in the benchmark, half a period after the maximum of C_L.\n\nif !FAST\n  win = ts .>= t_end2 - 3.0\n  tw, cLw, cDw, Δpw = ts[win], cLs[win], cDs[win], Δps[win]\n  c = cLw .- sum(cLw) / length(cLw)\n  crossings = [tw[i] - c[i] * (tw[i+1] - tw[i]) / (c[i+1] - c[i]) for i in 1:length(c)-1 if c[i] < 0 <= c[i+1]]\n  periods = diff(crossings)\n  T = sum(periods) / length(periods)\n  St = Dc / (Ū2 * T)\n  i0 = findlast(t -> t <= tw[end] - T, tw)                     # maximum of C_L in the last full period\n  i0 = findmax(cLw[i0-round(Int, T / Δt2):i0])[2] + i0 - round(Int, T / Δt2) - 1\n  thalf = tw[i0] + T / 2\n  j = findlast(t -> t <= thalf, tw)\n  Δp_half = Δpw[j] + (Δpw[j+1] - Δpw[j]) * (thalf - tw[j]) / (tw[j+1] - tw[j])\n  println(\"2D-2 (Re = 100, Δt = $Δt2 s, P1–P1, 7 402 triangles)   reference (Schäfer & Turek 1996)\")\n  println(\"  St             = $(round(St, digits=4))       0.2950 – 0.3050\")\n  println(\"  max C_D        = $(round(maximum(cDw), digits=4))       3.2200 – 3.2400\")\n  println(\"  max C_L        = $(round(maximum(cLw), digits=4))       0.9900 – 1.0100\")\n  println(\"  Δp(t₀ + T/2)   = $(round(Δp_half, digits=4))       2.4600 – 2.5000\")\n  spread = (maximum(periods) - minimum(periods)) / T\n  println(\"  $(length(periods)) periods in the last 3 s, relative spread $(spread), C_L amplitude $((maximum(cLw) - minimum(cLw)) / 2)\")\nend","title":"Shedding frequency"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E11_NavierStokes_transient/#Checks-(2D-2)","page":"11 · Transient Navier–Stokes flow around a cylinder","text":"The shedding is periodic (several periods of equal length, a lift that oscillates with a finite amplitude) and its frequency is that of the benchmark: we measured St = 03007 and assert it within 3 % of 0.30. On this mesh with linear elements the force amplitudes are further from the reference values (we measured max C_D = 3187 and max C_L = 0952, 1 % and 4 % below the reference intervals; Delta p = 2475 is inside its interval): they are only reported.\n\nif !FAST\n  @assert flag2 == 1 \"the time loop of 2D-2 did not complete\"\n  @assert length(periods) >= 5 && spread < 0.02 \"no periodic vortex shedding (periods $periods)\"\n  @assert (maximum(cLw) - minimum(cLw)) / 2 > 0.3 \"lift amplitude too small for vortex shedding\"\n  @assert isapprox(St, 0.30, rtol=0.03) \"Strouhal number $St away from the benchmark value 0.30\"\nend","title":"Checks (2D-2)"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E11_NavierStokes_transient/#Going-further","page":"11 · Transient Navier–Stokes flow around a cylinder","text":"scheme=BDF(1) is the backward Euler method: first order, more numerical damping. Try it on 2D-2 with the same time step: the shedding is weaker or delayed. A time-dependent inflow, such as the case 2D-3 of the benchmark (U_m = 15sin(pi t8)), is a time function of the inlet condition: BoundaryCondition(\"inlet\", inflow(1.5, H), t -> sin(π * t / 8)).\nThe Oseen linearisation solves one linear system per step. A full Newton method on the convective term (tangent_update=:iteration with the consistent tangent of rho(nablaboldsymbol u)boldsymbol u) converges to the fully implicit BDF2 solution at the cost of several solves per step.\nAdd a non-constant global to τsupg (write μ2 = 1e-3 without const and use it instead of μ): the solution does not change, but the compiled Jacobian becomes about seven times slower and the check above fails.\nTaylor–Hood P2–P1 (tutorial 10) needs no PSPG and gets closer to the reference values on the same mesh; with quadratic velocities the SUPG residual also contains the viscous term -muDeltaboldsymbol u.\nTutorial 12 uses the same formulation for the pulsatile blood flow in an aorta, with resistance–capacitance outlet conditions.\nReference: M. Schäfer and S. Turek, Benchmark computations of laminar flow around a cylinder, Notes on Numerical Fluid Mechanics 52 (1996) 547–566, DOI 10.1007/978-3-322-89849-4_39.","title":"Going further"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E14_Multigrid/#Tutorial-14:-Geometric-h-and-p-multigrid","page":"14 · Geometric h- and p-multigrid","text":"Source · Notebook · nbviewer\n\nAlgebraic multigrid (tutorial 13) guesses the coarse levels from the matrix. When the mesh comes from refining a coarse one, the coarse levels are known exactly: geometric multigrid uses them and its Krylov iterations stay constant however fine the mesh, so the cost per Newton iteration grows only linearly with the number of unknowns. The same idea across polynomial degrees (p-multigrid) preconditions high-order elements whose tangent is never assembled.\n\nWhat you will learn\n\nbuilding a hierarchy of uniformly refined meshes: MeshHierarchy, fine_model, level_model;\nHMultigridSolver: CG preconditioned by a geometric V-cycle, with lagged rebuilds (reuse) and an inexact Newton;\nchecking grid independence: the Krylov iterations do not grow with the number of levels;\np-multigrid for Q2 elements with a matrix-free tangent: level_spaces, HyperFEMKernels' PMultigrid inside a MatrixFreeSolver, with backend = CompiledBackend(jacobian = :matrixfree).\n\nMeasured on a Mac M5 Max (Julia 1.13.0, 4 Julia threads, 4 BLAS threads; HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6, Gridap 0.20.8): FAST (levels 2–4, up to 9 477 dofs; Q2 with 675 dofs) 48 s with compilation; full run (levels 3–5, up to 68 493 dofs; Q2 with 9 477 dofs) 82 s. Setup + linear solve per Newton iteration at 68 493 dofs: LU 2.2 s, CG + hMG 0.10 s, CG + hMG with reuse = 3 and the inexact Newton 0.04 s, CG + AMG 1.06 s; Q2 with 9 477 dofs: GMRES + p-multigrid on the matrix-free tangent 0.069 s, LU 0.077 s.","title":"Tutorial 14: Geometric h- and p-multigrid"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E14_Multigrid/#Problem-statement","page":"14 · Geometric h- and p-multigrid","text":"The bar of tutorial 13: neo-Hookean (lambda = 10, mu = 1) Omega = (01)times(002)times(002), clamped at x = 0, the face x = 1 moved by u_z = 005Lambda in two load increments. The coarse mesh has 5times1times1 hexahedra; level ell of the hierarchy is that mesh refined ell - 1 times (every hexahedron split into 8), so level 5 has 80times16times16 cells and 68 493 free dofs with Q1 elements.\n\nOne V-cycle on the fine tangent boldsymbol K_L: a few Chebyshev smoothing steps (on the 3times3 nodal blocks), restriction of the residual to the next coarser level with boldsymbol P^T, the same cycle there on the Galerkin operator boldsymbol K_ell-1 = boldsymbol P^T boldsymbol K_ell boldsymbol P, prolongation of the correction with boldsymbol P (the exact interpolation between the nested Q1 spaces) and smoothing again; the coarsest level is solved by any linear solver (LU here). The cycle is symmetric, so it preconditions CG.","title":"Problem statement"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E14_Multigrid/#Setup","page":"14 · Geometric h- and p-multigrid","text":"using HyperFEM\nusing Gridap\nusing HyperFEMKernels                          # compiled assembly; `MatrixFreeSolver`, `PMultigrid`\nusing LinearAlgebra, Printf\nimport AlgebraicMultigrid                      # activates `AMGSolver` (for the comparison)\n\nconst FAST = get(ENV, \"HYPERFEM_TUTORIALS_FAST\", \"0\") == \"1\"    # reduced case for the test suite\noutdir = joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"data\", \"sims\", \"E14_Multigrid\")\nsetupfolder(outdir)                                             # VTK output (not versioned)","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E14_Multigrid/#Mesh-hierarchy-and-problem","page":"14 · Geometric h- and p-multigrid","text":"MeshHierarchy(coarse, L) refines the coarse model L - 1 times; the simulation runs on fine_model(h), and the multigrid solver builds the same finite element space on every level_model(h, ℓ). That is why the space is given as a function of the model, space(m). The Dirichlet tags must be closed sets (a face with its edges and vertices: the ⁺ tags), so that every level has the same Dirichlet boundary; refined models inherit the tags.\n\nfunction hmg_problem(nlevels)\n  coarse = CartesianDiscreteModel((0, 1, 0, 0.2, 0, 0.2), (5, 1, 1))\n  labels = get_face_labeling(coarse)\n  add_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"fixed\", CartesianTags.face0YZ⁺)\n  add_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"moved\", CartesianTags.face1YZ⁺)\n  h = MeshHierarchy(coarse, nlevels)\n  model = fine_model(h)\n  physmodel = NeoHookean3D(λ=10.0, μ=1.0)\n  kinematics = Kinematics(Mechano, Solid)\n  Ω = Triangulation(model)\n  dΩ = Measure(Ω, 2)\n  D = BoundaryConditions(BoundaryCondition(\"fixed\", [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]),\n                         BoundaryCondition(\"moved\", [0.0, 0.0, 0.05], EvolutionFunctions.ramp()))\n  space(m) = TestFESpace(Triangulation(m), ReferenceFE(lagrangian, VectorValue{3,Float64}, 1), D, conformity=:H1)\n  V = space(model)\n  U = TrialFESpace(V, D, 0.0)\n  res(Λ) = (u, v) -> residual(physmodel, kinematics, u, v, dΩ)\n  jac(Λ) = (u, du, v) -> jacobian(physmodel, kinematics, u, du, v, dΩ)\n  (; res, jac, U, V, D, h, space, Ω)\nend\n\nsolve_with is a variant of the function of tutorial 13, with the backend as an argument: the whole simulation (two increments, Dirichlet projection) with the linear solver ls, assembled by the compiled kernels of HyperFEMKernels (backend), and the counters of the run.\n\nfunction solve_with(ls, p; forcing=nothing, backend=:compiled)\n  nls = NewtonRaphson(ls; maxiter=20, atol=1e-14, rtol=1e-10, forcing)\n  m = StaticNonlinearModel(p.res, p.jac, p.U, p.V, p.D; nls, backend)\n  t = @elapsed ((x, flag) = solve!(m; stepping=LoadStepping(nsteps=2, maxbisec=0), ProjectDirichlet=true))\n  log = nls.log\n  (; x=copy(x), ok=(flag == 1), newton=log.niters, krylov=log.nlinear, lin=collect(linear_history(nls)),\n     builds=preconditioner_builds(m.caches.nls_cache.ns), t, t_solve=(log.t_setup + log.t_linear) / log.niters, model=m)\nend","title":"Mesh hierarchy and problem"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E14_Multigrid/#The-solvers","page":"14 · Geometric h- and p-multigrid","text":"HMultigridSolver(h, space; krylov = :cg): CG with one V-cycle per iteration, coarsest level by LU (the coarse keyword takes any linear solver, e.g. AMGSolver(:cg; nullspace = near_nullspace(Uc)) or PETSc's GAMG).\nthe same with reuse = 3 and forcing = EisenstatWalker(): the coarse levels are rebuilt every three Newton iterations (the fine smoother always follows the current tangent) and the linear tolerance follows the Newton residual. The configuration HyperFEM recommends for production.\nCG + AMG with the rigid-body modes (tutorial 13), for comparison.\n\nhmg_solvers = [\n  (\"LU\", p -> LUSolver(), nothing),\n  (\"CG + hMG\", p -> HMultigridSolver(p.h, p.space; krylov=:cg, rtol=1e-10, maxiter=200), nothing),\n  (\"CG + hMG, reuse = 3, inexact\", p -> HMultigridSolver(p.h, p.space; krylov=:cg, rtol=1e-10, maxiter=200, reuse=3), EisenstatWalker()),\n  (\"CG + AMG, rigid modes\", p -> AMGSolver(:cg; rtol=1e-10, maxiter=1000, nullspace=near_nullspace(p.U)), nothing),\n]","title":"The solvers"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E14_Multigrid/#Solve-on-3-levels-of-refinement","page":"14 · Geometric h- and p-multigrid","text":"Every solver is first run on a two-level hierarchy (compilation), then on three hierarchies.\n\nlevels = FAST ? (2, 3, 4) : (3, 4, 5)\nfor (name, make, forcing) in hmg_solvers\n  pw = hmg_problem(2)\n  solve_with(make(pw), pw; forcing)\nend\nhmg = Dict{Tuple{String,Int},Any}()\nndofs = Dict{Int,Int}()\nfor L in levels\n  p = hmg_problem(L)\n  ndofs[L] = num_free_dofs(p.U)\n  for (name, make, forcing) in hmg_solvers\n    hmg[(name, L)] = solve_with(make(p), p; forcing)\n  end\n  L == last(levels) && writevtk(p.Ω, joinpath(outdir, \"bar_L$L\"), cellfields=[\"u\" => get_state(hmg[(\"LU\", L)].model)])\nend","title":"Solve on 3 levels of refinement"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E14_Multigrid/#Results","page":"14 · Geometric h- and p-multigrid","text":"Newton iterations, Krylov iterations per Newton iteration, time of the run, time of setup + linear solve per Newton iteration (\"solve/it\") and difference from LU.\n\n@printf(\"  %6s %7s  %-30s %6s %7s %8s %9s %9s\\n\", \"levels\", \"dofs\", \"solver\", \"Newton\", \"per it\", \"run (s)\", \"solve/it\", \"vs LU\")\nfor L in levels, (name, _, _) in hmg_solvers\n  r = hmg[(name, L)]\n  err = norm(r.x - hmg[(\"LU\", L)].x) / norm(hmg[(\"LU\", L)].x)\n  @printf(\"  %6d %7d  %-30s %6d %7.1f %8.2f %9.3f %9.1e\\n\", L, ndofs[L], name, r.newton, r.krylov / r.newton, r.t, r.t_solve, err)\nend\n\nA measured example (full run, Mac M5 Max, 4 Julia threads, 4 BLAS threads; the iterations are reproducible, the times depend on the machine):\n\n  levels    dofs  solver                         Newton  per it  run (s)  solve/it     vs LU\n       3    1425  LU                                  6     0.0     0.23     0.006   0.0e+00\n       3    1425  CG + hMG                            6    15.5     0.66     0.011   2.3e-16\n       3    1425  CG + hMG, reuse = 3, inexact        6     5.7     0.33     0.004   2.5e-13\n       3    1425  CG + AMG, rigid modes               6    22.7     0.11     0.012   2.2e-16\n       4    9477  LU                                  6     0.0     0.62     0.061   0.0e+00\n       4    9477  CG + hMG                            6    17.2     0.95     0.024   1.2e-16\n       4    9477  CG + hMG, reuse = 3, inexact        6     6.2     0.50     0.009   2.3e-13\n       4    9477  CG + AMG, rigid modes               6    28.2     0.95     0.109   1.3e-16\n       5   68493  LU                                  6     0.0    17.90     2.172   0.0e+00\n       5   68493  CG + hMG                            6    17.8     2.20     0.104   8.5e-17\n       5   68493  CG + hMG, reuse = 3, inexact        6     6.7     1.31     0.041   9.6e-14\n       5   68493  CG + AMG, rigid modes               6    33.8     8.96     1.062   9.6e-17\n\nThe CG iterations of geometric multigrid stay nearly constant from level to level (15.5, 17.2, 17.8); those of AMG grow (22.7, 28.2, 33.8). Each refinement multiplies the dofs by about 7: the time per Newton iteration of hMG grows by less than that (2.2 and 4.3 times), that of LU by 10 and then 35 times. With 1 425 dofs LU is the fastest; with 68 000, CG + hMG is 20 times faster than LU per Newton iteration, and 50 times with reuse = 3 and the inexact Newton. The run times also include the two Dirichlet projections (one per increment) and the assembly. hMG and the compiled assembly run on Julia's threads, AlgebraicMultigrid's AMGSolver in serial: part of the gap between them is threads against serial code; the iteration counts are not.","title":"Results"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E14_Multigrid/#p-multigrid-with-a-matrix-free-tangent","page":"14 · Geometric h- and p-multigrid","text":"For Q2 (and higher) hexahedra the tangent need not be assembled: HyperFEMKernels applies it matrix-free with sum factorisation, at the cost of a sparse product or less and a fraction of its memory. PMultigrid preconditions it with a V-cycle across polynomial degrees on the same mesh: Chebyshev smoothing on the matrix-free Q2 tangent, transfer to the Q1 space, where the tangent is rediscretised at the interpolated state, assembled and solved (coarse). level_spaces(U, V, D) builds the Q1 space with the same boundary conditions. CompiledBackend(jacobian = :matrixfree) makes the Newton hand the linear solver the matrix-free operator; MatrixFreeSolver runs GMRES (Krylov.jl) on it.\n\nfunction q2_problem(cells)\n  model = CartesianDiscreteModel((0, 1, 0, 0.2, 0, 0.2), cells)\n  labels = get_face_labeling(model)\n  add_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"fixed\", CartesianTags.face0YZ⁺)\n  add_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"moved\", CartesianTags.face1YZ⁺)\n  physmodel = NeoHookean3D(λ=10.0, μ=1.0)\n  kinematics = Kinematics(Mechano, Solid)\n  Ω = Triangulation(model)\n  dΩ = Measure(Ω, 4)\n  D = BoundaryConditions(BoundaryCondition(\"fixed\", [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]),\n                         BoundaryCondition(\"moved\", [0.0, 0.0, 0.05], EvolutionFunctions.ramp()))\n  V = TestFESpace(Ω, ReferenceFE(lagrangian, VectorValue{3,Float64}, 2), D, conformity=:H1)\n  U = TrialFESpace(V, D, 0.0)\n  res(Λ) = (u, v) -> residual(physmodel, kinematics, u, v, dΩ)\n  jac(Λ) = (u, du, v) -> jacobian(physmodel, kinematics, u, du, v, dΩ)\n  (; res, jac, U, V, D, Ω)\nend\n\nfunction pmg_solver(p)\n  spaces = level_spaces(p.U, p.V, p.D)            # [(U, V) in Q2, (U1, V1) in Q1]\n  MatrixFreeSolver(; krylov=:gmres, rtol=1e-10, maxiter=200, preconditioner=PMultigrid(spaces; coarse=LUSolver()))\nend\nconst MATRIXFREE = CompiledBackend(jacobian=:matrixfree)\n\nq2_cells = FAST ? (5, 2, 2) : (20, 4, 4)\nlet pw = q2_problem((5, 1, 1))                   # compilation\n  solve_with(LUSolver(), pw)\n  solve_with(pmg_solver(pw), pw; backend=MATRIXFREE)\nend\npq = q2_problem(q2_cells)\nndofs_q2 = num_free_dofs(pq.U)\nq2_lu = solve_with(LUSolver(), pq)\nq2_pmg = solve_with(pmg_solver(pq), pq; backend=MATRIXFREE)\nws = HyperFEMKernels.work_summary(q2_pmg.model.caches.op)\nprintln(\"Q2 bar, $(join(q2_cells, \"×\")) cells, $ndofs_q2 dofs\")\n@printf(\"  LU (assembled):          Newton %d, run %.2f s, setup + solve per Newton iteration %.3f s\\n\", q2_lu.newton, q2_lu.t, q2_lu.t_solve)\n@printf(\"  GMRES + p-MG (matrix-free): Newton %d, GMRES per Newton iteration %s, run %.2f s, %.3f s per iteration, vs LU %.1e\\n\",\n        q2_pmg.newton, string(q2_pmg.lin), q2_pmg.t, q2_pmg.t_solve, norm(q2_pmg.x - q2_lu.x) / norm(q2_lu.x))\nprintln(\"  matrix-free operator: jacobian = $(ws.jacobian), pattern built: $(ws.nnz ≥ 0), groups \",\n        [(g.kind, g.path) for g in ws.groups], \", Gridap fallbacks $(ws.fallback_residual + ws.fallback_jacobian)\")\n\nOn a mesh this small p-multigrid is about as fast as LU; it is shown as a working configuration (the same Newton trajectory as LU, bounded GMRES iterations). HyperFEMKernels' README reports its scaling on larger problems.","title":"p-multigrid with a matrix-free tangent"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E14_Multigrid/#Checks","page":"14 · Geometric h- and p-multigrid","text":"(b) Every multigrid run reaches the LU solution, and the solvers with exact linear solves follow the Newton trajectory of LU. Grid independence: the CG iterations per Newton iteration of hMG grow by at most 50 % over the three hierarchies (the criterion of HyperFEM's test suite), and stay below 30.\n\nfor L in levels, (name, _, _) in hmg_solvers\n  r, ref = hmg[(name, L)], hmg[(\"LU\", L)]\n  @assert r.ok \"$name, $L levels: the load stepping did not reach Λ = 1\"\n  @assert norm(r.x - ref.x) ≤ 1e-6 * norm(ref.x) \"$name, $L levels: solution away from LU\"\n  occursin(\"inexact\", name) || @assert r.newton == ref.newton \"$name, $L levels: Newton iterations differ from LU\"\nend\nits_hmg = [hmg[(\"CG + hMG\", L)].krylov / hmg[(\"CG + hMG\", L)].newton for L in levels]\nprintln(\"CG + hMG iterations per Newton iteration on $(levels) levels: \", round.(its_hmg, digits=1))\n@assert maximum(its_hmg) ≤ 1.5 * minimum(its_hmg) \"hMG iterations grow with the refinement\"\n@assert maximum(its_hmg) ≤ 30 \"hMG iterations above 30\"\n@assert hmg[(\"CG + hMG, reuse = 3, inexact\", last(levels))].builds < hmg[(\"CG + hMG\", last(levels))].builds \"reuse did not reduce the builds\"\n@assert q2_pmg.ok && q2_lu.ok \"Q2: the load stepping did not reach Λ = 1\"\n@assert q2_pmg.newton == q2_lu.newton \"p-MG: Newton iterations differ from LU\"\n@assert norm(q2_pmg.x - q2_lu.x) ≤ 1e-6 * norm(q2_lu.x) \"p-MG: solution away from LU\"\n@assert all(≤(40), q2_pmg.lin) \"p-MG: more than 40 GMRES iterations in a Newton iteration\"\n@assert ws.jacobian === :matrixfree \"the p-MG run assembled the tangent\"\n\n(c) Regression values of the LU solutions (HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6, Gridap 0.20.8, Julia 1.13.0, macOS arm64). The finest Q1 mesh of the full run is the bar of tutorial 13: the same value.\n\nxref_hmg, xref_q2 = FAST ? (1.7082045249535511, 0.4520311508708429) : (4.59603534564497, 1.707304750168487)\nprintln(\"‖x‖ Q1 finest = $(norm(hmg[(\"LU\", last(levels))].x)), ‖x‖ Q2 = $(norm(q2_lu.x))\")\n@assert isapprox(norm(hmg[(\"LU\", last(levels))].x), xref_hmg; rtol=1e-5) \"Q1: regression value\"\n@assert isapprox(norm(q2_lu.x), xref_q2; rtol=1e-5) \"Q2: regression value\"","title":"Checks"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E14_Multigrid/#Going-further","page":"14 · Geometric h- and p-multigrid","text":"Multi-field problems: FieldSplitSolver applies one V-cycle per field of a saddle point, e.g. for the electro-mechanical box of tutorial 13 on a hierarchy, FieldSplitSolver(U, (HMultigridSolver(h, space_u; krylov=:cg, reuse=3), HMultigridSolver(h, space_φ; krylov=:cg, reuse=3)); split=:schur), with forcing = EisenstatWalker() in the Newton.\nThe coarsest level of HMultigridSolver with PETSc's algebraic multigrid: coarse = PETScSolver(:preonly, :gamg; nullspace = near_nullspace(Uc), blocksize = 3) with Uc = TrialFESpace(space(level_model(h, 1))) (using GridapPETSc).\nPMultigrid(spaces; coarse = AMGSolver(:cg; nullspace = near_nullspace(spaces[2][1])), coarse_every = 3) replaces the coarse LU by AMG and rebuilds it every three updates; level_spaces(U, V, D, (2, 1)) gives three levels for Q3 elements.\nHyperFEMKernels.LaggedAssembled(solver; every = k) is another preconditioner slot of MatrixFreeSolver: an assembled tangent factorised every k Newton iterations, applied to the exact matrix-free tangent.\nReference: Trottenberg, Oosterlee and Schüller, Multigrid, Academic Press (2001).","title":"Going further"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E06_ThirdMedium_contact/#Tutorial-06:-Third-medium-contact","page":"06 · Third-medium contact","text":"Source · Notebook · nbviewer\n\nA slender column, clamped at both ends, is compressed by a rigid piston inside a box. It buckles, bends sideways until it touches the walls of the box, and keeps folding against them. The contact is not detected by any search algorithm: the empty space around the column is meshed and filled with a third medium, a very soft hyperelastic material that transmits almost no force while it is open and stiffens strongly when it is squeezed to zero thickness. This is the piston benchmark of Faltus, Amato and Horák (2026, Sec. 3.3), a hard test for the nonlinear solver because of the buckling and the snaps.\n\nWhat you will learn\n\nmeshes with several subdomains (Triangulation(model, tags=...)) and a different material in each;\nthe third-medium contact method: ARAP2D + LogBarrier and the skeleton regularisation of the gradient jump (SkeletonTriangulation, jump, mean);\na robust Newton for problems that lose stability: the injectivity-preserving line search InjectivityLS with Armijo on the energy (energy=, injectivity=), and the hybrid Newton (HybridTangent, HessianRegularization sharing a TangentSwitch);\nincrements that grow when Newton converges easily (LoadStepping(grow=...));\nreaction forces computed from the residual, and a force–displacement history written to a CSV file.\n\nMeasured on a Mac M5 Max (Julia 1.13.0, 4 threads, 4 BLAS threads; HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6, Gridap 0.20.8; compiled backend), whole run including compilation: FAST 51 s (mesh Piston_1.msh, 1 340 free dofs, 15 increments, 22 s of them in solve!, mostly compilation); full 107 s (mesh Piston_0.5.msh, 5 494 free dofs, 400 increments, 900 Newton iterations, 78 s in solve!: 0.2 s per increment: a third in Newton, the rest in the Dirichlet projection, the updates of the compiled forms and the post-processing). One assembly of the tangent on the full mesh: 5.8 ms compiled, 31 ms with Gridap's lazy evaluation.","title":"Tutorial 06: Third-medium contact"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E06_ThirdMedium_contact/#Problem-statement","page":"06 · Third-medium contact","text":"The box is (01 09)times(01 19) (plane strain, dimensionless lengths). The column is the strip 045 le x le 055 of thickness d = 01 and length L = 18; the two regions on its sides are the third medium. The bottom of the column is clamped. The top of the box is the piston: it moves down by u_y = Lambdabar u, with the top of the column clamped to it and the third medium free to slide along it. The third medium slides on the bottom and on the side walls of the box.\n\nFind boldsymbol u such that, for all boldsymbol v,\n\nint_Omega_s partial_boldsymbol FPsi_s  nabla_0 boldsymbol v mathrm dA\n+ int_Omega_tm partial_boldsymbol FPsi_tm  nabla_0 boldsymbol v mathrm dA\n+ int_Gamma_tm fracsigmabetah_F nabla_0 boldsymbol u  nabla_0 boldsymbol v mathrm ds = 0 \n\nwhich is the stationarity condition of the total energy\n\nPi(boldsymbol u) = int_Omega_s Psi_s mathrm dA + int_Omega_tm Psi_tm mathrm dA\n+ frac12 int_Gamma_tm fracsigmabetah_F nabla_0 boldsymbol u  nabla_0 boldsymbol u mathrm ds \n\nSolid. A compressible Mooney–Rivlin material written for plane strain (mu_1 = mu_2 = mu):\n\nPsi_s = fracmu_12left(boldsymbol Fboldsymbol F + 1 - 3right) + fracmu_22left(boldsymbol Fboldsymbol F + J^2 - 3right)\n- (mu_1 + 2mu_2)ln J + fraclambda2(J - 1)^2 \n\nwith mu = 10^5 and lambda = 6mu. Linearised, its shear modulus is mu_1 + mu_2 = 2mu and its first Lamé constant lambda + 2mu (Poisson's ratio 0.4 in plane strain, as in the paper).\n\nThird medium (Wriggers, Schröder and Schwarz 2013). The MIPS energy (called ARAP2D in HyperFEM), which penalises the distortion of the elements but not their size, plus a barrier that grows without bound when the area of an element goes to zero:\n\nPsi_tm = fracmu_tm2left(fracboldsymbol Fboldsymbol FJ - 2right) + frackappa2p(ln J)^2p \nqquad mu_tm = f_cmu quad kappa = mu_tm quad p = 2 quad f_c = 10^-6 \n\nWhile the gap is open the third medium is a million times softer than the column; when the column presses it against a wall, J to 0 and the barrier transmits the contact pressure.\n\nRegularisation (Bluhm, Sigmund and Poulios 2021). A soft medium that is squeezed develops spurious zig-zag modes. Bluhm et al. add the energy tfracbeta2int_Omega_tm nablanablaboldsymbol u vdots nablanablaboldsymbol u, which penalises the curvature of the deformation of the third medium. With linear triangles nablanablaboldsymbol u = 0 inside every element and the curvature sits on the facets, as the jump of the gradient: the skeleton term above (interior facets Gamma_tm of the third medium, facet length h_F, beta = 10^-5mu, sigma = 1) is its discrete counterpart, as in interior-penalty methods.\n\nReactions. The piston force is reported as ff_cr, with the Euler load of the double-clamped column in plane strain, f_cr = pi^2 E I  (L2)^2, I = d^312, E = E(1 - nu^2) of the linearised solid (the normalisation of Horák's curves).","title":"Problem statement"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E06_ThirdMedium_contact/#Setup","page":"06 · Third-medium contact","text":"using HyperFEM\nusing Gridap\nusing HyperFEMKernels                                          # compiled backend (`backend=:compiled`)\nusing Gridap.CellData: get_array                               # cell-wise values of an integral\nusing GridapGmsh\nusing LinearAlgebra\nusing Printf\n\nconst FAST = get(ENV, \"HYPERFEM_TUTORIALS_FAST\", \"0\") == \"1\"   # reduced case for the test suite\noutdir = joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"data\", \"sims\", \"E06_ThirdMedium_contact\")\nsetupfolder(outdir)                                            # VTK and CSV output (not versioned)","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E06_ThirdMedium_contact/#Geometry-and-mesh","page":"06 · Third-medium contact","text":"Gmsh meshes of linear triangles with two (Piston_1.msh, FAST) or four (Piston_0.5.msh, full) elements across the column. Their physical groups name the two subdomains, domain (the column) and third (the third medium), and the boundary pieces used below.\n\nmeshfile = FAST ? \"Piston_1.msh\" : \"Piston_0.5.msh\"\ngeomodel = GmshDiscreteModel(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"data\", \"models\", meshfile))\nd, Lb = 0.1, 1.8                                               # thickness and length of the column","title":"Geometry and mesh"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E06_ThirdMedium_contact/#Constitutive-models","page":"06 · Third-medium contact","text":"The hybrid Newton (see Solver below) needs two tangents of the same energy: the consistent one, and a projected one, positive definite at every quadrature point (the eigenvalues of partial^2_boldsymbol Fboldsymbol FPsi clamped from below at delta). HessianRegularization wraps a model and returns the projected tangent while the shared TangentSwitch is on; the energy and the stress are not changed.\n\nμ = 1e5                                                        # Pa\nfc = 1e-6                                                      # stiffness of the third medium relative to μ\nhrs = 1e-6                                                     # projection threshold relative to the stiffness\nswitch = TangentSwitch()\nsolid = HessianRegularization(NonlinearMooneyRivlin2D(λ=6μ, μ1=μ, μ2=μ, α1=1.0, α2=1.0); δ=hrs * μ, switch=switch)\nmedium = HessianRegularization(ARAP2D(μ=fc * μ) + LogBarrier(κ=fc * μ, p=2); δ=hrs * fc * μ, switch=switch)\nΨs, ∂Ψs∂F, ∂Ψs∂FF = solid()\nΨm, ∂Ψm∂F, ∂Ψm∂FF = medium()\nF, _, _ = get_Kinematics(Kinematics(Mechano, Solid))\nFu(u) = F ∘ (∇(u)')                                            # deformation gradient of a displacement field","title":"Constitutive models"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E06_ThirdMedium_contact/#Boundary-conditions","page":"06 · Third-medium contact","text":"The tags come from the mesh. Imposed_Y_fixed_X is the top of the column, glued to the piston; Imposed_Y_free_X is the top of the third medium, which follows the piston but slides along it.\n\nū = FAST ? 0.15 : 0.40                                         # final piston displacement: 1.5 d (FAST) or 4 d\nD_bc = BoundaryConditions(\n  BoundaryCondition(\"Fixed_XY\", [0.0, 0.0]),\n  BoundaryCondition(\"Fixed_Y\", [0.0, 0.0]; mask=(false, true)),\n  BoundaryCondition(\"Fixed_X\", [0.0, 0.0]; mask=(true, false)),\n  BoundaryCondition(\"Imposed_Y_free_X\", [0.0, -ū], EvolutionFunctions.ramp(); mask=(false, true)),\n  BoundaryCondition(\"Imposed_Y_fixed_X\", [0.0, -ū], EvolutionFunctions.ramp()))","title":"Boundary conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E06_ThirdMedium_contact/#Finite-element-spaces","page":"06 · Third-medium contact","text":"One displacement field over the whole box (column and third medium together), and one measure per subdomain.\n\norder = 1\nreffe = ReferenceFE(lagrangian, VectorValue{2,Float64}, order)\nΩ = Triangulation(geomodel, tags=[\"domain\", \"third\"])\nΩs = Triangulation(geomodel, tags=[\"domain\"])\nΩm = Triangulation(geomodel, tags=[\"third\"])\ndΩ, dΩs, dΩm = Measure(Ω, 2order), Measure(Ωs, 2order), Measure(Ωm, 2order)\nV = TestFESpace(Ω, reffe, D_bc, conformity=:H1)\nU = TrialFESpace(V, D_bc, 0.0)\nuh = FEFunction(U, zeros(num_free_dofs(U)))\nprintln(\"free dofs: \", num_free_dofs(V))","title":"Finite element spaces"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E06_ThirdMedium_contact/#Weak-form","page":"06 · Third-medium contact","text":"The skeleton of the third medium: its interior facets, where jump(∇(u)) is the jump of the gradient between the two triangles of a facet. h_F is computed as the integral of 1 over each facet.\n\nΓ = SkeletonTriangulation(geomodel, tags=[\"third\"])\ndΓ = Measure(Γ, 2order)\nhF = CellField(get_array(∫(1)dΓ), Γ)\nβ, σ = 1e-5 * μ, 1.0\ncJ = (σ * β) / hF\nqJ(a, b) = jump(∇(a)) ⊙ jump(∇(b))\n\nres(Λ) = (u, v) -> ∫(∇(v)' ⊙ (∂Ψs∂F ∘ Fu(u)))dΩs +\n                   ∫(∇(v)' ⊙ (∂Ψm∂F ∘ Fu(u)))dΩm +\n                   ∫(cJ * qJ(u, v))dΓ\njac(Λ) = (u, du, v) -> ∫(∇(v)' ⊙ ((∂Ψs∂FF ∘ Fu(u)) ⋅² ∇(du)'))dΩs +\n                       ∫(∇(v)' ⊙ ((∂Ψm∂FF ∘ Fu(u)) ⋅² ∇(du)'))dΩm +\n                       ∫(cJ * qJ(du, v))dΓ\n\nThe energy Pi, used by the line search below. Its first variation is the residual above.\n\nen(Λ) = u -> ∫(Ψs ∘ Fu(u))dΩs + ∫(Ψm ∘ Fu(u))dΩm + ∫(0.5 * cJ * qJ(u, u))dΓ","title":"Weak form"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E06_ThirdMedium_contact/#Solver-and-computational-model","page":"06 · Third-medium contact","text":"Three ingredients make Newton robust here.\n\nInjectivity. The barrier is infinite at J = 0 and the energies are not defined for J le 0: a full Newton step that inverts one triangle of the third medium is useless. InjectivityLS computes the largest step alpha that keeps det(boldsymbol F + alphanabla_0deltaboldsymbol u) ge J_min at every quadrature point of injectivity (a quadratic equation per point in 2D, solved exactly) and starts the line search from 0999999alpha.\nArmijo on the energy. From there the step is halved until the energy decreases enough, Pi(boldsymbol u + alphadeltaboldsymbol u) le Pi(boldsymbol u) + c_1alphaboldsymbol Rcdotdeltaboldsymbol u (armijo=true, the energy given with energy=).\nHybrid Newton. After the buckling the consistent tangent is indefinite and its Newton direction may not be a descent direction, so the line search fails. HybridTangent then switches the TangentSwitch on and continues with the projected tangent, which always gives a descent direction; it goes back to the consistent tangent (quadratic convergence) once boldsymbol R has dropped by hybback. In projected mode the convergence test is absolute, boldsymbol R  10^-3 f_cr.\n\nWith the increments used below the consistent tangent turns out to be enough: the solver log reports 0 % of the iterations in projected mode, in FAST and in the full run. The hybrid tangent is the safety net for larger increments; with the growing increments of Going further it takes over in about a fifth of the iterations.\n\nWith backend=:compiled (tutorial 01) the residual, the tangent, the energy of the line search and the injectivity step are all evaluated by compiled kernels, which matters most for the line search: it evaluates the energy several times per Newton iteration.\n\nμl, λl = 2μ, 6μ + 2μ                                           # linearised solid\nEps = 4μl * (λl + μl) / (λl + 2μl)                             # plane-strain modulus E/(1 - ν²)\nfcr = π^2 * Eps * (d^3 / 12) / (0.5Lb)^2                       # Euler load of the double-clamped column\n\nlinesearch = InjectivityLS(jmin=1e-6, armijo=true, β=1 - 1e-6, ρ=0.5, maxiter=20, αmin=1e-16)\ntangent = HybridTangent(hybback=1e-2, atol_projected=1e-3 * fcr)\n\nNewton stops at boldsymbol R  10^-6 (about 10^-9 f_cr): every increment ends at an equilibrium, so the results (and the regression values below) do not depend on where the iterations happen to stop.\n\nnls = NewtonRaphson(LUSolver(); maxiter=500, atol=1e-6, rtol=1e-10, verbose=1, linesearch=linesearch, tangent=tangent)\ncomp_model = StaticNonlinearModel(res, jac, U, V, D_bc; nls=nls, xh=uh, injectivity=dΩ, energy=en, tangent_switch=switch,\n                                backend=:compiled)","title":"Solver and computational model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E06_ThirdMedium_contact/#Post-processing","page":"06 · Third-medium contact","text":"Reaction forces from the residual. At a converged state the residual vanishes on the free dofs, and on the Dirichlet dofs it is the reaction. Testing the residual with a function boldsymbolvarphi that is boldsymbol e_y on the nodes of a boundary and zero elsewhere gives the vertical force on that boundary, f = boldsymbol R(boldsymbol u)cdotboldsymbolvarphi. Such a boldsymbolvarphi is a finite element function whose free values are zero and whose Dirichlet values on the chosen tags are (0 1).\n\nfunction unit_y_on(tags)\n  Vr = TestFESpace(Ω, reffe, conformity=:H1, dirichlet_tags=tags, dirichlet_masks=[(false, true) for _ in tags])\n  FEFunction(TrialFESpace(Vr, [VectorValue(0.0, 1.0) for _ in tags]), zeros(num_free_dofs(Vr)))\nend\nφpiston = unit_y_on([\"Imposed_Y_fixed_X\", \"Imposed_Y_free_X\"])\nφclamp = unit_y_on([\"Fixed_XY\"])\n\nAfter every converged increment we store the piston displacement and both reactions (the piston pushes down, so its force is -boldsymbol Rcdotboldsymbolvarphi) and write the displacement and J every few increments.\n\nuy_d, fpiston, fclamp = Float64[], Float64[], Float64[]\nvtkevery = FAST ? 5 : 25\nfunction record(post)\n  Λ = post.Λ[post.iter]\n  push!(uy_d, Λ * ū / d)\n  push!(fpiston, -sum(res(Λ)(uh, φpiston)) / fcr)\n  push!(fclamp, sum(res(Λ)(uh, φclamp)) / fcr)\n  if post.iter % vtkevery == 0\n    pvd = get_pvd(post)\n    pvd[Λ] = createvtk(Ω, joinpath(outdir, \"step_$(post.iter).vtu\"), cellfields=[\"u\" => uh, \"J\" => det ∘ Fu(uh)])\n  end\nend\npost = PostProcessor(comp_model, record; is_vtk=true, filepath=outdir)","title":"Post-processing"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E06_ThirdMedium_contact/#Solve","page":"06 · Third-medium contact","text":"FAST: 15 equal increments up to 15d, just past the buckling of the column. Full: 400 increments up to 4d, through the first contact and the first snap; a bisected increment is allowed to grow back by 1.5 after an increment that converged in at most 10 Newton iterations. ProjectDirichlet=true predicts every increment from the linearised effect of the new piston position (tutorial 02), scaled down if needed so that no element is inverted.\n\nstepping = FAST ? LoadStepping(nsteps=15, maxbisec=5) : LoadStepping(nsteps=400, maxbisec=5, grow=1.5)\nnbisect = Ref(0)\nt_solve = @elapsed x, flag = solve!(comp_model; stepping=stepping, ProjectDirichlet=true, post=post,\n                                    on_bisection=(Λ, ΔΛ) -> nbisect[] += 1)   # also closes the .pvd\n@printf(\"solve: %.1f s, %d increments, %d bisections\\n\", t_solve, length(uy_d), nbisect[])\n\nopen(joinpath(outdir, \"force_displacement.csv\"), \"w\") do io\n  println(io, \"uy_over_d,f_piston_over_fcr,f_clamp_over_fcr\")\n  for (a, b, c) in zip(uy_d, fpiston, fclamp)\n    println(io, a, \",\", b, \",\", c)\n  end\nend","title":"Solve"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E06_ThirdMedium_contact/#Checks","page":"06 · Third-medium contact","text":"Equilibrium. The third medium is so soft that the piston force must reach the bottom clamp through the column: both reactions agree within 1 % at every increment.\n\n@assert flag == 1 \"the load stepping did not reach Λ = 1\"\n@assert all(abs.(fpiston .- fclamp) .< 1e-2 .* abs.(fpiston)) \"piston and clamp reactions are not in equilibrium\"\n\nBuckling. Without buckling the force would grow linearly with the shortening of the column (by a factor of 3 from 05d to 15d); a buckled column carries an almost constant force, the plateau of the curve.\n\ni05, i15 = argmin(abs.(uy_d .- 0.5)), argmin(abs.(uy_d .- 1.5))\n@assert fpiston[i15] < 1.1 * fpiston[i05] \"the column did not buckle before u_y = 0.5 d\"\n\nComparison with the paper (printed, not asserted). Horák's curve is computed with quadratic elements. Linear triangles are too stiff in bending when there are few of them across the column: with two (FAST mesh) the buckling plateau comes out about 60 % above the paper's; with four (full mesh) about 7 % above, and the snap (370f_cr at 296d, against 343f_cr at 30d in the paper) is in the right place.\n\nfunction horak_curve(file, key)\n  s = read(file, String)\n  block = match(Regex(\"\\\"$key\\\": \\\\[(.*?)\\\\]\\\\]\"), s).captures[1]\n  pts = [parse.(Float64, (m[1], m[2])) for m in eachmatch(r\"\\[(-?[0-9.eE+-]+), (-?[0-9.eE+-]+)\\]\", block * \"]\")]\n  first.(pts), last.(pts)\nend\nhx, hy = horak_curve(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"data\", \"reference\", \"horak2026_piston_f_vs_uy.json\"), \"piston\")\ninterp(xs, ys, x) = (i = clamp(searchsortedlast(xs, x), 1, length(xs) - 1); ys[i] + (ys[i+1] - ys[i]) * (x - xs[i]) / (xs[i+1] - xs[i]))\nfor x in (FAST ? (0.5, 1.0, 1.5) : (0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 3.5, 4.0))\n  i = argmin(abs.(uy_d .- x))\n  @printf(\"u_y/d = %.3f   f/f_cr = %.4f   (Horák %.4f)\\n\", uy_d[i], fpiston[i], interp(hx, hy, uy_d[i]))\nend\nimax = argmax(fpiston)\n@printf(\"maximum force f/f_cr = %.4f at u_y/d = %.3f\\n\", fpiston[imax], uy_d[imax])\n@printf(\"‖u‖ = %.10f   f/f_cr at the end = %.10f\\n\", norm(get_free_dof_values(uh)), fpiston[end])\n\nRegression (FAST; HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6, Gridap 0.20.8, Julia 1.13.0, macOS arm64), and, in the full run, the first snap: the column touches the walls, the force rises steeply and then drops when the column snaps into a new shape: the maximum of the force is reached before 34d, and the force at 4d is below it.\n\nif FAST\n  @assert length(uy_d) == 15 && nbisect[] == 0 \"an increment was bisected\"\n  @assert isapprox(norm(get_free_dof_values(uh)), 3.8344902564, rtol=1e-5) \"regression: ‖u‖\"\n  @assert isapprox(fpiston[end], 1.7420291165, rtol=1e-5) \"regression: piston force at 1.5 d\"\nelse\n  @assert uy_d[imax] < 3.4 && fpiston[end] < fpiston[imax] \"no snap before u_y = 3.4 d\"\nend","title":"Checks"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E06_ThirdMedium_contact/#Going-further","page":"06 · Third-medium contact","text":"Run the whole benchmark, up to 15d: ū = 1.5 and LoadStepping(nsteps=1000, maxbisec=5, grow=1.5) (several minutes); the column folds twice more against the walls. Compare with the full curve in data/reference/horak2026_piston_f_vs_uy.json (keys piston and clamp).\nLet the increments grow beyond the initial one: LoadStepping(nsteps=400, maxbisec=5, grow=1.5, ΔΛmax=0.01, neasy=2) reaches 4d in about 100 increments. On Piston_1.msh it gives the same curve as 400 fixed increments (maximum 578f_cr at 332d), and the hybrid Newton is now at work: 21 % of the Newton iterations use the projected tangent (5 switches). mode_history(nls) lists the mode of every iteration of the last Newton solve.\nRemove the barrier (ARAP2D(μ=fc * μ) alone) or the skeleton term, and compare the force curve and the field J of the third medium under the column.\nBluhm et al. also weight the regularisation with a function w(boldsymbol F) that grows with compression and distortion; the residual is then no longer the derivative of an energy, and the line search must use InjectivityLS(armijo=false) (backtracking on boldsymbol Rcdotdeltaboldsymbol u) instead of Armijo.\nConsistentTangent() instead of HybridTangent: a failed line search now fails the increment, and the driver has to bisect it; compare the number of bisections after the buckling.\n\nReferences. O. Faltus, M. Amato, M. Horák (2026), Deformation gradient averaging regularization for third medium contact, arXiv:2603.13063 (piston benchmark, Sec. 3.3, Fig. 3b). P. Wriggers, J. Schröder, A. Schwarz (2013), A finite element method for contact using a third medium, Comput. Mech. 52, 837–847, doi:10.1007/s00466-013-0848-5. G. L. Bluhm, O. Sigmund, K. Poulios (2021), Internal contact modeling for finite strain topology optimization, Comput. Mech. 67, 1099–1114, doi:10.1007/s00466-021-01974-x.","title":"Going further"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E16_ViscoElectroMechanics/#Tutorial-16:-Visco-electro-mechanics:-creep-of-a-dielectric-bimorph","page":"16 · Visco-electro-mechanics: creep of a dielectric bimorph","text":"Source · Notebook · nbviewer\n\nSoft dielectric elastomers are viscoelastic: under a constant voltage an actuator does not stop where it is when the voltage is reached, but keeps deforming for seconds or minutes as the polymer network relaxes. We model the material with a generalised Maxwell model (an elastic equilibrium branch plus two viscous branches with relaxation times of about 1 s and 10 s), apply a voltage ramp to a bimorph cantilever and hold it: the tip deflection creeps towards the value given by the equilibrium branch alone, which we compute separately. The creep turns out to be much slower than the relaxation times suggest, and we predict by how much.\n\nMeasured on a Mac (M5 Max, 4 Julia threads, 4 BLAS threads, Julia 1.13.0, HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6, Gridap 0.20.8; compiled backend), whole run including compilation: FAST 54 s (1 089 free dofs, 41 time steps, 35 ms per step), full 62 s (3 615 free dofs, 87 time steps, 0.10 s per step; the first step takes 7 s, mostly compilation). In a step of the full case the LU factorisation (17 ms, once per Newton iteration) dominates; one assembly of the tangent takes 4.9 ms compiled and 76 ms with Gridap's lazy evaluation.\n\nWhat you will learn\n\ngeneralised Maxwell viscoelasticity (GeneralizedMaxwell, ViscousPolyconvex) inside an electro-mechanical model (ElectroMechModel);\ninternal variables stored at the quadrature points (CellState) and updated once per accepted time step;\nTransientModel with the QuasiStatic scheme: a time loop without inertia, where time enters through the material (Delta t in the viscous update) and through a time-dependent voltage;\ngraded time steps with TimeStepping(; grow, Δtmax, tstops) to cover two relaxation times in a few steps;\nchecking a viscous computation against its long-time (equilibrium) limit, and checking that the compiled backend is not compiled again at every step.","title":"Tutorial 16: Visco-electro-mechanics: creep of a dielectric bimorph"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E16_ViscoElectroMechanics/#Problem-statement","page":"16 · Visco-electro-mechanics: creep of a dielectric bimorph","text":"The plate Omega = (0L)times(0W)times(0H) (L = 50 mm, W = 5 mm, H = 1 mm) is clamped at x = 0. It carries two compliant electrodes: one on the bottom face z = 0 (grounded) and one on the mid-plane z = H2, whose potential follows a ramp of duration t_r = 1 s up to V_0 and is then held. The electric field lives in the lower layer only: it squeezes the layer across its thickness and, the material being nearly incompressible, the layer elongates. The upper layer resists, and the plate bends upwards (+z).\n\nAs in tutorial 04, the unknowns are the displacement boldsymbol u and the electric potential varphi, with boldsymbol F = boldsymbol I + nabla_0boldsymbol u and boldsymbol E_0 = -nabla_0varphi. The free energy is now the sum of three mechanical branches and the ideal dielectric,\n\nPsi(boldsymbol F boldsymbol E_0 boldsymbol C_v1 boldsymbol C_v2) = Psi_infty(boldsymbol F)\n  + sum_a=1^2 Psi_a(boldsymbol C boldsymbol C_va)\n  - fracvarepsilon2Jboldsymbol H boldsymbol E_0^2 \nqquad boldsymbol C = boldsymbol F^Tboldsymbol F \n\nwith the compressible neo-Hookean equilibrium branch of tutorial 02, Psi_infty = tfracmu_infty2(boldsymbol Fboldsymbol F - 3) - mu_infty ln J + tfraclambda2(J-1)^2, and two viscous (Maxwell) branches, each a neo-Hookean spring in series with a dashpot, written in terms of a viscous strain boldsymbol C_va (detboldsymbol C_va = 1):\n\nPsi_a = fracmu_a2left(boldsymbol C  boldsymbol C_va^-1 - 3 J^23right) \n\nThe viscous strains are internal variables: they are not unknowns of the finite element problem but evolve at every quadrature point by a flow rule with relaxation time tau_a, which drives boldsymbol C_va towards the isochoric part of boldsymbol C. Integrated with a backward-Euler step from t_n to t_n+1 = t_n + Delta t it has a closed form (no local Newton iteration):\n\nboldsymbol C_va^-1big_n+1 = fracboldsymbol B(detboldsymbol B)^13  qquad\nboldsymbol B = fracDelta ttau_aboldsymbol C_n+1^-1 + boldsymbol C_va^-1big_n \n\nFor Delta t ll tau_a the viscous strain does not move and the branch acts as an extra spring of modulus mu_a: the instantaneous response is that of all branches together, mu_infty + mu_1 + mu_2. Under a constant boldsymbol C, boldsymbol C_va tends to J^-23boldsymbol C, the energy and the stress of the branch vanish, and the long-time response is that of the equilibrium branch alone.\n\nRelaxation and creep. tau_a is the time with which the stress of branch a relaxes under a fixed deformation. Under a fixed load the deformation creeps with other times, the retardation times lambda: in the small-strain (linear) version of the model they are the roots of\n\nmu_infty lambda^2 - left(mu_infty(tau_1 + tau_2) + mu_1tau_1 + mu_2tau_2right)lambda\n  + tau_1tau_2(mu_infty + mu_1 + mu_2) = 0\n\n(the zeros of the relaxation modulus mu_infty + sum_a mu_a stau_a(1 + stau_a) in the Laplace variable, at s = -1lambda). With a soft equilibrium branch they are much longer than the relaxation times: the dashpot of a stiff branch must stretch a long way before the soft branch takes over the load. With the material below, lambda_1 = 167 s and lambda_2 = 391 s (for tau_1 = 082 s and tau_2 = 107 s).\n\nThe problem at t_n+1 is the electro-mechanical problem of tutorial 04 with the stress of the step (equilibrium branch, viscous branches with boldsymbol C_va given by the update above, and Maxwell stress): find (boldsymbol u varphi) such that for all (boldsymbol v deltavarphi)\n\nint_Omega nabla_0 boldsymbol v  partial_boldsymbol FPsi mathrm dV\n  - int_Omega nabla_0 deltavarphi cdot partial_boldsymbol E_0Psi mathrm dV = 0 \n\nwith partial_boldsymbol FPsi evaluated at (boldsymbol F_n+1 boldsymbol E_0n+1) and depending on the internal variables of the previous step, boldsymbol C_va_n, through the update. There is no inertia: the time only enters through the dashpots and the voltage (a quasi-static time loop). Once the step has converged, the internal variables are updated, and the next step starts from them.\n\nMaterial (from the HyperFEM test of this simulation): mu_infty = 14 kPa, lambda = 1 MPa, mu_1 = 56 kPa with tau_1 = 082 s, mu_2 = 34 kPa with tau_2 = 107 s. The instantaneous shear modulus is 7.4 times the equilibrium one. The permittivity is varepsilon = 1 in scaled units: the solution depends on varepsilon and the voltage only through varepsilon V_0^2, so V_0 = 0008 below is 0008sqrtvarepsilon volts for a material of permittivity varepsilon (about 1.2 kV for an acrylic elastomer with varepsilon approx 42cdot 10^-11 F/m).","title":"Problem statement"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E16_ViscoElectroMechanics/#Setup","page":"16 · Visco-electro-mechanics: creep of a dielectric bimorph","text":"using HyperFEM\nusing Gridap\nusing HyperFEMKernels                                          # compiled backend (`backend=:compiled`)\nusing LinearAlgebra: norm\nusing Printf\n\nconst FAST = get(ENV, \"HYPERFEM_TUTORIALS_FAST\", \"0\") == \"1\"   # reduced case for the test suite\noutdir = joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"data\", \"sims\", \"E16_ViscoElectroMechanics\")\nsetupfolder(outdir)                                            # VTK output (not versioned)","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E16_ViscoElectroMechanics/#Geometry-and-mesh","page":"16 · Visco-electro-mechanics: creep of a dielectric bimorph","text":"A Cartesian mesh of the plate with two layers of elements across the thickness, so that the mid-plane is a mesh plane and can carry the electrode. add_tag_from_vertex_filter! tags the faces, edges and vertices whose vertices all satisfy the filter (the filter captures the height of the plane in a let, rather than reading a global variable). The bimorph bends, and trilinear hexahedra are much too stiff in bending when they are this thin (shear locking): we use triquadratic ones. Even so, the meshes are coarse: the equilibrium tip deflection is 5.45 mm with the FAST mesh and 5.77 mm with the full one (6 % apart; 5.9 mm, from a static run not shown here, with 40×2×4 elements). The checks compare results on the same mesh, so they hold for any of them.\n\nL, W, H = 0.050, 0.005, 0.001                                  # m\npartition = FAST ? (10, 1, 2) : (20, 2, 2)\nmodel = CartesianDiscreteModel((0, L, 0, W, 0, H), partition)\nlabels = get_face_labeling(model)\nadd_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"fixed\", CartesianTags.face0YZ⁺)\nadd_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"bottom\", CartesianTags.faceXY0⁺)\nadd_tag_from_vertex_filter!(labels, \"mid\", model, let zm = H / 2; x -> abs(x[3] - zm) < 1e-12 end)\nadd_tag_from_tags!(labels, \"tip\", CartesianTags.face1YZ)","title":"Geometry and mesh"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E16_ViscoElectroMechanics/#Constitutive-model","page":"16 · Visco-electro-mechanics: creep of a dielectric bimorph","text":"GeneralizedMaxwell(equilibrium, branches...) adds viscous branches to an elastic model; ViscousPolyconvex is the branch above. Calling the electro-mechanical model returns the energy and its derivatives as functions of (boldsymbol F boldsymbol E_0 boldsymbol F_n boldsymbol A_1 boldsymbol A_2): the deformation gradient of the previous step and one internal variable per branch (here boldsymbol A_a = boldsymbol C_va_n) come after the arguments of tutorial 04. boldsymbol F_n is part of the interface of the viscous models (it is passed to every branch); the update of ViscousPolyconvex above does not use it. The stress is the algorithmic stress, with the viscous strains given by the update of the step, and the tangent is its consistent derivative.\n\nμ∞, λ = 1.4e4, 1.0e6                                           # Pa\nμ₁, τ₁ = 5.6e4, 0.82                                           # Pa, s\nμ₂, τ₂ = 3.4e4, 10.7                                           # Pa, s\nε = 1.0                                                        # scaled permittivity\nviscoelastic = GeneralizedMaxwell(NeoHookean3D(λ=λ, μ=μ∞), ViscousPolyconvex(μ=μ₁, τ=τ₁), ViscousPolyconvex(μ=μ₂, τ=τ₂))\nphysmodel = ElectroMechModel(mechano=viscoelastic, electro=IdealDielectric(ε=ε))\nkm = Kinematics(Mechano, Solid)\nke = Kinematics(Electro, Solid)\nk = (km, ke)\nF, _, _ = get_Kinematics(km)\nE = get_Kinematics(ke)","title":"Constitutive model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E16_ViscoElectroMechanics/#Boundary-conditions","page":"16 · Visco-electro-mechanics: creep of a dielectric bimorph","text":"The clamp is constant. The potential of the mid-plane electrode follows EvolutionFunctions.ramp(t_r), min(tt_r 1): in a TransientModel the evolution functions receive the time.\n\nV0 = 0.008                                                     # voltage of the mid-plane electrode (scaled)\nt_r = 1.0                                                      # s, duration of the ramp\nDu = BoundaryConditions(BoundaryCondition(\"fixed\", [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]))\nDφ = BoundaryConditions(BoundaryCondition(\"bottom\", 0.0), BoundaryCondition(\"mid\", V0, EvolutionFunctions.ramp(t_r)))\nD_bc = MultiFieldBC([Du, Dφ])","title":"Boundary conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E16_ViscoElectroMechanics/#Finite-element-spaces","page":"16 · Visco-electro-mechanics: creep of a dielectric bimorph","text":"order = 2\nreffeu = ReferenceFE(lagrangian, VectorValue{3,Float64}, order)\nreffeφ = ReferenceFE(lagrangian, Float64, order)\nVu = TestFESpace(model, reffeu, D_bc[1], conformity=:H1)\nVφ = TestFESpace(model, reffeφ, D_bc[2], conformity=:H1)\nV = MultiFieldFESpace([Vu, Vφ])\nU = MultiFieldFESpace([TrialFESpace(Vu, D_bc[1], 0.0), TrialFESpace(Vφ, D_bc[2], 0.0)])\nΩ = Triangulation(model)\ndΩ = Measure(Ω, 2 * order)\nprintln(\"free dofs: \", num_free_dofs(U), \" (u: \", num_free_dofs(U[1]), \", φ: \", num_free_dofs(U[2]), \")\")","title":"Finite element spaces"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E16_ViscoElectroMechanics/#Internal-variables","page":"16 · Visco-electro-mechanics: creep of a dielectric bimorph","text":"CellState(physmodel, dΩ) creates one internal variable per viscous branch at every quadrature point, initialised to boldsymbol C_va = boldsymbol I (the plate starts relaxed). With backend=:compiled they are stored as dense arrays that the compiled kernels read directly, and their update runs as a compiled kernel too. A is a tuple with one state per branch.\n\nA = CellState(physmodel, dΩ; backend=:compiled)","title":"Internal variables"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E16_ViscoElectroMechanics/#Weak-form","page":"16 · Visco-electro-mechanics: creep of a dielectric bimorph","text":"A TransientModel passes to the forms a time context ctx: the time t_n+1 (ctx.t), the step (ctx.Δt) and the state of the previous step (ctx.u⁻; ctx[1].u⁻ is its first field, the displacement). With the QuasiStatic scheme the unknown of the step is the field itself, so the forms are those of a static problem with boldsymbol F_n and the internal variables as extra arguments. HyperFEM's helpers build the terms of tutorial 04 with those extra arguments: the mechanical and the electrical residual, and the mechanical, electrical and coupling blocks of the tangent (residual(physmodel, Mechano, k, ...) etc.). The slot after dΩ is the load factor Lambda of the static helpers, which the kinematics of this problem ignore; we pass ctx.t there only because the extra arguments boldsymbol F_n boldsymbol A_1 boldsymbol A_2 come after it. The time step Delta t is not an argument: the model reads it from a reference that the driver sets before every attempt (physics=physmodel below), so a change of Delta t changes no form.\n\nres(ctx) = ((u, φ), (v, vφ)) -> begin\n  Fn = F ∘ ∇(ctx[1].u⁻)'\n  residual(physmodel, Mechano, k, (u, φ), v, dΩ, ctx.t, Fn, A...) +\n  residual(physmodel, Electro, k, (u, φ), vφ, dΩ, ctx.t, Fn, A...)\nend\njac(ctx) = ((u, φ), (du, dφ), (v, vφ)) -> begin\n  Fn = F ∘ ∇(ctx[1].u⁻)'\n  jacobian(physmodel, Mechano, k, (u, φ), du, v, dΩ, ctx.t, Fn, A...) +\n  jacobian(physmodel, Electro, k, (u, φ), dφ, vφ, dΩ, ctx.t, Fn, A...) +\n  jacobian(physmodel, ElectroMechano, k, (u, φ), (du, dφ), (v, vφ), dΩ, ctx.t, Fn, A...)\nend\n\nThe update of the internal variables, called by the driver once per accepted step with the new state xh and the state of the previous step xh⁻ (a failed attempt leaves them untouched). The arguments are those of the energy: boldsymbol F_n+1, boldsymbol E_0n+1 and boldsymbol F_n (the electric field is not used by the update, but it is part of the call: without it, HyperFEM 0.3.0 does nothing and does not complain, and the internal variables keep their initial value, which turns the viscous branches into elastic springs).\n\ninternal(xh, xh⁻) = update_state!(physmodel, A, F ∘ ∇(xh[1])', E ∘ ∇(xh[2]), F ∘ ∇(xh⁻[1])'; backend=:compiled)","title":"Weak form"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E16_ViscoElectroMechanics/#Solver-and-computational-model","page":"16 · Visco-electro-mechanics: creep of a dielectric bimorph","text":"TransientModel with scheme=QuasiStatic() is the time loop of a problem without inertia. physics receives update_time_step!(physics, Δt) before every attempt; internal is the update above. With backend=:compiled the forms are compiled once; at every step they are only rebound to the new previous state and internal variables (we check it at the end).\n\nnls = NewtonRaphson(LUSolver(); maxiter=20, atol=1e-12, rtol=1e-9, verbose=1)\ncreep = TransientModel(res, jac, U, V, D_bc; scheme=QuasiStatic(), nls=nls, physics=physmodel, internal=internal,\n                       backend=:compiled)","title":"Solver and computational model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E16_ViscoElectroMechanics/#Post-processing","page":"16 · Visco-electro-mechanics: creep of a dielectric bimorph","text":"After every accepted step: the time, the voltage, the mean deflection u_z of the free end and the wall-clock time (to measure the cost of a step); the VTK files every few steps. The post-processing runs before the update of the internal variables.\n\nΓtip = BoundaryTriangulation(model, tags=\"tip\")\ndΓtip = Measure(Γtip, 2 * order)\nez = VectorValue(0.0, 0.0, 1.0)\nramp_voltage = EvolutionFunctions.ramp(t_r)\ntimes, voltage, tip_uz, wall = Float64[], Float64[], Float64[], Float64[]\nfunction record(post)\n  t = post.Λ[post.iter]\n  xh = get_state(creep)\n  push!(times, t)\n  push!(voltage, V0 * ramp_voltage(t))\n  push!(tip_uz, sum(∫(xh[1] ⋅ ez)dΓtip) / (W * H))\n  push!(wall, time())\n  if post.iter % 5 == 1\n    get_pvd(post)[t] = createvtk(Ω, joinpath(outdir, \"creep_$(post.iter).vtu\"), cellfields=[\"u\" => xh[1], \"φ\" => xh[2]])\n  end\nend\npost = PostProcessor(creep, record; is_vtk=true, filepath=outdir)","title":"Post-processing"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E16_ViscoElectroMechanics/#Solve","page":"16 · Visco-electro-mechanics: creep of a dielectric bimorph","text":"The time scales go from the ramp and tau_1 (about a second) to lambda_2 (40 s), and the hold must last several times lambda_2. Equal steps small enough for the ramp would need thousands of steps; TimeStepping grows the step by the factor grow after every step that converged easily, up to Δtmax, and tstops makes a step end exactly at the end of the ramp. The voltage increments of the ramp are small and the response is nearly linear in V^2, so Newton's method converges without the Dirichlet projection of tutorial 02 (ProjectDirichlet=true gives the same results with fewer iterations, but compiles and factorises a second operator: here it costs more than it saves).\n\nλ₁, λ₂ = let b = μ∞ * (τ₁ + τ₂) + μ₁ * τ₁ + μ₂ * τ₂, c = τ₁ * τ₂ * (μ∞ + μ₁ + μ₂)   # retardation times (above)\n  sort([(b - sqrt(b^2 - 4μ∞ * c)) / (2μ∞), (b + sqrt(b^2 - 4μ∞ * c)) / (2μ∞)])\nend\n@printf(\"relaxation times τ = %.2f s, %.2f s; retardation (creep) times λ = %.2f s, %.2f s\\n\", τ₁, τ₂, λ₁, λ₂)\nt_end = FAST ? 250.0 : 400.0                                   # s: about 6 λ₂ (FAST) and 10 λ₂\nΔtmax = 10.0\nstepping = FAST ? TimeStepping(; Δt=0.1, t_end, grow=1.3, Δtmax, tstops=(t_r,), maxbisec=4) :\n                  TimeStepping(; Δt=0.05, t_end, grow=1.1, Δtmax, tstops=(t_r,), maxbisec=4)\nHyperFEMKernels.reset_counters!()\nt_solve = @elapsed x, flag = solve!(creep; stepping, post=post)\nwork = HyperFEMKernels.counters()\nnsteps = length(times)\nt_step = sort(diff(wall))[cld(nsteps - 1, 2)]                  # median wall-clock time of a step (the first compiles)\n@printf(\"%d steps in %.1f s (%.1f ms per step, median)\\n\", nsteps, t_solve, 1e3 * t_step)","title":"Solve"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E16_ViscoElectroMechanics/#Long-time-limit:-the-equilibrium-branch-alone","page":"16 · Visco-electro-mechanics: creep of a dielectric bimorph","text":"A static electro-mechanical problem (tutorial 04) with only the neo-Hookean branch, at the final voltage, on the same mesh: the deflection that the creep tends to. It has its own conditions (the voltage follows the load factor) and trial spaces; the test spaces are the same.\n\nequilibrium = ElectroMechModel(mechano=NeoHookean3D(λ=λ, μ=μ∞), electro=IdealDielectric(ε=ε))\nDφ_eq = BoundaryConditions(BoundaryCondition(\"bottom\", 0.0), BoundaryCondition(\"mid\", V0, Λ -> Λ))\nD_eq = MultiFieldBC([Du, Dφ_eq])\nU_eq = MultiFieldFESpace([TrialFESpace(Vu, D_eq[1], 0.0), TrialFESpace(Vφ, D_eq[2], 0.0)])\nres_eq(Λ) = (x, y) -> residual(equilibrium, k, x, y, dΩ)\njac_eq(Λ) = (x, dx, y) -> jacobian(equilibrium, k, x, dx, y, dΩ)\nstatic = StaticNonlinearModel(res_eq, jac_eq, U_eq, V, D_eq; nls=NewtonRaphson(LUSolver(); maxiter=20, atol=1e-12, rtol=1e-9),\n                              backend=:compiled)\n_, flag_eq = solve!(static; stepping=LoadStepping(nsteps=10, maxbisec=4), ProjectDirichlet=true)\ntip_eq = sum(∫(get_state(static)[1] ⋅ ez)dΓtip) / (W * H)","title":"Long-time limit: the equilibrium branch alone"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E16_ViscoElectroMechanics/#Results","page":"16 · Visco-electro-mechanics: creep of a dielectric bimorph","text":"The deflection at the end of the ramp, and then along the hold, against the long-time limit.\n\ni_r = findfirst(t -> t ≥ t_r - 1e-12, times)                  # the step that ends the ramp\nfor i in unique([1:min(nsteps, 3); i_r; i_r+3:5:nsteps; nsteps])\n  @printf(\"t = %8.3f s   V = %.4f   tip u_z = %.4f mm   (%.1f %% of the long-time value)\\n\",\n          times[i], voltage[i], 1e3 * tip_uz[i], 100 * tip_uz[i] / tip_eq)\nend\n@printf(\"equilibrium branch alone at V0: tip u_z = %.4f mm\\n\", 1e3 * tip_eq)","title":"Results"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E16_ViscoElectroMechanics/#Checks","page":"16 · Visco-electro-mechanics: creep of a dielectric bimorph","text":"Long-time limit. After the hold, the deflection is that of the equilibrium branch alone. What is left of the viscous stresses decays like e^-tlambda_2 (a little more slowly with the backward-Euler update and large steps): after t_end it is below 0.5 % (FAST) and 0.05 %.\n\nrel_limit = abs(tip_uz[end] - tip_eq) / abs(tip_eq)\nprintln(\"long-time limit: |u_z(t_end) − u_z,eq| / |u_z,eq| = $(rel_limit)\")\n@assert flag == 1 && flag_eq == 1 \"a time loop or the load stepping did not finish\"\n@assert rel_limit < (FAST ? 5e-3 : 5e-4) \"the deflection after the hold is not the equilibrium deflection ($(rel_limit))\"\n\nCreep time. During the hold the distance to the limit, r = u_zeq - u_z, decays with the longest retardation time. With steps of constant size Delta t the backward-Euler update multiplies it by 1(1 + Delta tlambda) per step, so ten steps of size Δtmax give a measured lambda, which we compare with lambda_2 of the small-strain model (5 %: the deflection is about a tenth of the length, and the electric load changes a little with the deformation).\n\nr = tip_eq .- tip_uz\niconst = findall(d -> abs(d - Δtmax) < 1e-9, diff(times)) .+ 1   # steps of size Δtmax\nia = first(iconst)\nib = min(ia + 10, last(iconst))\nλ_measured = Δtmax / ((r[ia] / r[ib])^(1 / (ib - ia)) - 1)\n@printf(\"creep time measured between t = %.1f s and %.1f s: %.2f s (small-strain λ₂ = %.2f s)\\n\",\n        times[ia], times[ib], λ_measured, λ₂)\n@assert ib - ia ≥ 5 && times[ib] - times[ia] ≥ 50 \"the hold is too short to measure the creep time\"\n@assert isapprox(λ_measured, λ₂; rtol=0.05) \"creep time $(λ_measured) s away from λ₂ = $(λ₂) s\"\n\nCreep and the instantaneous response. After the ramp the voltage is constant and the deflection grows monotonically towards the limit, from below. The compliance u_zV^2, relative to that of the equilibrium branch, grows during the whole run, ramp included: the plate is stiffest at the start, where the viscous branches have not relaxed and the response is close to the instantaneous one, mu_infty(mu_infty + mu_1 + mu_2) = 0135 in the small-strain model (the first step already lets them relax a little).\n\nhold = findfirst(t -> t ≥ t_r - 1e-12, times):nsteps\ncompliance = (tip_uz ./ voltage .^ 2) ./ (tip_eq / V0^2)\nc_inst = μ∞ / (μ∞ + μ₁ + μ₂)\n@printf(\"relative compliance: %.3f after the first step, %.3f at the end of the ramp (instantaneous %.3f)\\n\",\n        compliance[1], compliance[first(hold)], c_inst)\n@assert tip_uz[end] > 0 \"the plate does not bend upwards\"\n@assert all(diff(tip_uz[hold]) .> 0) \"the deflection does not grow monotonically during the hold\"\n@assert all(tip_uz[hold] .< tip_eq) \"the deflection goes beyond the long-time limit\"\n@assert all(diff(compliance) .> 0) \"the compliance does not grow in time\"\n@assert compliance[1] < 1.25 * c_inst \"the response of the first step is not close to the instantaneous one\"\n\nEfficiency. HyperFEMKernels.work_summary lists the groups of integrals of the compiled operator, the path each takes (:linear_maps: the coefficients come from linear maps of the state inputs, the fast path) and whether a part falls back to Gridap. The counters since reset_counters!() show that the forms were only rebound to the new previous state and internal variables at every step (update_fast, one per step) and never compiled again (update_full). The forms were generated when the model was built; during the loop the functions generated (codegen) are two per step: the update of the internal variables of each branch is generated again at every call from the same expression, which finds the compiled code already there (about 1 ms per step, against tens of milliseconds for the Newton iterations). HyperFEM 0.3.0 has no accessor for the operator of a TransientModel: creep.caches.op.inner is an internal field and may change.\n\nws = HyperFEMKernels.work_summary(creep.caches.op.inner)\nforeach(g -> println(\"  \", g.kind, \" group \", g.group, \": \", g.cells, \" cells × \", g.points, \" points, path \", g.path), ws.groups)\nprintln(\"  Gridap fallbacks: residual $(ws.fallback_residual), Jacobian $(ws.fallback_jacobian); \",\n        \"updates: $(work.update_fast) fast, $(work.update_full) full; functions generated: $(work.codegen)\")\n@assert ws.fallback_residual == 0 && ws.fallback_jacobian == 0 \"part of the forms is assembled by Gridap\"\n@assert work.update_full == 0 \"the compiled forms were compiled again during the time loop\"\n@assert work.update_fast == nsteps \"the compiled forms were not rebound once per step\"\n\nRegression (HyperFEM 0.3.0, HyperFEMKernels 0.2.6, Gridap 0.20.8, Julia 1.13.0, macOS arm64): deflection at the end of the ramp and at the end of the hold, and the equilibrium deflection.\n\nprintln(\"regression: tip u_z(t_r) = $(tip_uz[i_r]), u_z(t_end) = $(tip_uz[end]), u_z,eq = $(tip_eq), steps = $(nsteps)\")\ntipr_ref, tipend_ref, tipeq_ref, nsteps_ref = FAST ?\n  (0.0009741162955541829, 0.00543470810431621, 0.005448188282398284, 41) :\n  (0.000979340936078761, 0.005767556235774467, 0.00576801014451426, 87)\n@assert nsteps == nsteps_ref \"$(nsteps) time steps instead of $(nsteps_ref)\"\n@assert isapprox(tip_uz[i_r], tipr_ref; rtol=1e-5) \"tip deflection at the end of the ramp $(tip_uz[i_r]) differs from $(tipr_ref)\"\n@assert isapprox(tip_uz[end], tipend_ref; rtol=1e-5) \"tip deflection at t_end $(tip_uz[end]) differs from $(tipend_ref)\"\n@assert isapprox(tip_eq, tipeq_ref; rtol=1e-5) \"equilibrium tip deflection $(tip_eq) differs from $(tipeq_ref)\"","title":"Checks"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/E16_ViscoElectroMechanics/#Going-further","page":"16 · Visco-electro-mechanics: creep of a dielectric bimorph","text":"Hold the voltage for a short time and switch it off (BoundaryCondition(\"mid\", V0, t -> …) with any function of time): the plate does not come back at once, it recovers with the same relaxation times.\nRaise V_0: the equilibrium deflection grows as V_0^2 up to V_0 approx 0012 (about 13 mm), and beyond that the plate curls up as it creeps (with V_0 = 002 the tip of the equilibrium plate rises by about 30 mm, 60 % of its length). More voltage steps (a smaller Δt or grow) may be needed for the large rotations.\nAdd a third branch with a long relaxation time (the HyperFEM test of a viscoelastic bar uses tau_3 = 500 s), or use ViscousIncompressible(NeoHookean3D(...); τ) branches.\nSolve the two fields one after the other (StaggeredModel, tutorial 09) instead of together.\nReferences: Reese & Govindjee, Int. J. Solids Struct. 35 (1998) 3455–3482, doi:10.1016/S0020-7683(97)00217-5 (finite viscoelasticity with internal variables); Hossain, Vu & Steinmann, Comput. Mater. Sci. 59 (2012) 65–74, doi:10.1016/j.commatsci.2012.02.027 (experiments and a generalised Maxwell model of the acrylic elastomer VHB 4910).","title":"Going further"}]
}
